To implement a transactional increment, open the target file, read its current content, parse the integer value, sleep to hold the read set open, write the incremented value back, and close the handle. If a concurrent write invalidates the read set during the sleep, the system automatically re-runs the script, allowing the transaction to succeed on the updated snapshot.
```sol
open @/tmp/sol-counter.txt;
(s, h2) = readAll h;
n = Str.parse s;
u = sleepMs 300;
h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";
close h3;
```

The expression `(s, h2) = readAll h` returns a tuple containing the file's contents as a string `s` and a new file handle `h2`. The expression `h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}"` writes the string representation of `n` plus one to the file and returns the file handle `h3` (which is then closed).

Example:
> (s, h2) = readAll h;
> h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";

The script uses transactional semantics where `readAll` snapshots the file into a read set, and `sleepMs` keeps the transaction open to allow concurrent writers to invalidate it. If a conflict is detected during the `commit` phase (implied by the re-run logic), the script automatically re-executes.

```sol
u = sleepMs 300;
h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";
close h3.
```

