The script uses a `pending` object to store incoming values from `Live.Emit` events until their target DOM elements are created. When an event arrives, the value is stored in the `pending` object rather than being applied immediately. A `MutationObserver` monitors the document for new elements; once an element with the matching `data-input` attribute is found, the script retrieves the stored value and assigns it to the element, triggering an `input` event to update any dependent UI components.

```sol
(function () {
  var pending = {};
  function fill() {
    Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
      var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
      if (!els.length) return;
      els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
      delete pending[n];
    });
  }
  document.addEventListener('live:set', function (e) {
    var vals = JSON.parse(e.detail);
    Object.keys(vals).forEach(function (n) { pending[n] = vals[n]; });
    fill();
  });
  new MutationObserver(fill).observe(document.getElementById('lv-root'), { childList: true, subtree: true });
})();
```

The `fill` function iterates through a `pending` object containing values awaiting assignment and updates the corresponding DOM elements identified by their `data-input` attributes. It resolves timing issues by detecting when an element exists via `MutationObserver` and immediately dispatching an `input` event, which allows the UI to react to data that was sent by the server before the input field was actually created.

```sol
function fill() {
  Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
    if (!els.length) return;
    els.forEach(function (el) { 
      el.value = pending[n]; 
      el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); 
    });
    delete pending[n];
  });
}
```

The `fill` function is called directly inside the event listener registered for the `'live:set'` event. When an event occurs, the handler parses the `e.detail` JSON string into a `vals` object, iterates through its keys to store values in a `pending` object, and then immediately executes `fill()`. This function queries the DOM for elements matching the `[data-input="n"]` selector and sets their values, triggering an 'input' event on each.

```sol
document.addEventListener('live:set', function (e) {
  var vals = JSON.parse(e.detail);
  Object.keys(vals).forEach(function (n) { pending[n] = vals[n]; });
  fill();
});
```

The system allows commands to arrive before the corresponding input element is rendered; the value is stored in a `pending` object until the element appears in the DOM. Once the element exists, the `fill` function iterates through pending values, assigns them to the input fields, and dispatches an `input` event to trigger the UI update.

```fprisc
pending = {}
function fill() {
  Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
    if (!els.length) return;
    els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
    delete pending[n];
  });
}
document.addEventListener('live:set', function (e) {
  var vals = JSON.parse(e.detail);
  Object.keys(vals).forEach(function (n) { pending[n] = vals[n]; });
  fill();
});
```

