The ARC table initially uses a fixed 1024-slot array. When the combined count of live and tombstone slots exceeds 70% of the current capacity, the table rehashes into a buddy block of twice the size to accommodate growth. This expansion process occurs under the ARC lock on the insert path, prior to the probe operation.

```fprisc
// Example of an actor sending a shared value that may trigger ARC growth
actor main = {
  for i = 0 to 3000 do
    send (shared_tuple, recipient)
  drain
}
```

The ARC table starts as a fixed 1024-slot array and grows by rehashing into a buddy block of twice the size whenever the count of live and tombstone slots exceeds 70% of the current capacity. This growth occurs under the ARC lock on the insert path, before the probe step, and the system tracks the high-water mark and growth count for monitoring purposes.

```sol
// Example illustrating the growth mechanism conceptually
// When live + tombstone slots > 0.7 * current_size, ARC rehashes to 2x size
if (live_slots + tombstone_slots) > (0.7 * current_arc_size) {
    rehash_arc_table(); // Triggers growth from 1024 -> 2048 -> 4096, etc.
}
```

