The example first generates a range of numbers from 1 to n and maps them to integers using `toZ`. It then calculates the sum via a JIT fold with the `plus` function and divides by n to get the mean (mu). Next, it performs a second JIT fold using a captured `mu` and a `sqDev` function to compute the sum of squared deviations. Finally, it divides this sum by n and applies `Num.sqrt` to derive the standard deviation (sd).

```sol
n = 120000;
raw = Vec.map toZ (Vec.range 1 n);
(s0, raw1) = Vec.fold plus 0 raw;
mu = s0 / n;
(sq, raw2) = Vec.fold (sqDev mu) 0 raw1;
sd = Num.sqrt (sq / n);
```

These lines compute the sum of all elements in the `raw` vector and then derive the mean (`mu`) by dividing that sum by the total count `n`. The expression `(s0, raw1) = Vec.fold plus 0 raw;` returns a tuple containing the accumulated sum `s0` and the reduced vector `raw1` (which is empty after folding). The subsequent line `mu = s0 / n;` calculates the arithmetic mean using the sum `s0`.

```sol
(s0, raw1) = Vec.fold plus 0 raw;
mu = s0 / n;
```

The `Vec.fold` function is used to reduce a vector by applying a binary operation cumulatively. To calculate the mean, it is called with `plus` as the fold function, `0` as the initial value, and the input vector to sum all elements. To calculate the standard deviation, it is called with `sqDev mu` (a function that squares the difference from the mean) as the fold function, `0` as the initial value, and the previously summed vector to compute the sum of squared deviations.

```sol
mu = s0 / n;
(sq, raw2) = Vec.fold (sqDev mu) 0 raw1;
```

