This design ensures that lists of any length cost no stack space, preventing stack overflow errors regardless of input size. By forcing loops to be tail calls that pass an accumulator, the language guarantees that the stack frame is not retained between iterations, making memory usage constant relative to loop count.

```sol
// Standard iterative approach (inefficient due to stack growth)
let sum = 0
for i <- 1 to 1000000 do {
  sum <- sum + i
}

// Tail-call optimized approach (constant stack usage)
let sum (acc, i) = 
  if i > 1000000 then acc else sum (acc + i, i - 1)
sum (0, 1000000)
```

THP significantly increases allocation costs by causing every pool reset to hand pages back and fault them in again, where each fault zeroes a 2 MiB page. This results in a 27% overhead on allocation when THP is set to `always`. To align with standard behavior and reduce this cost, the kernel configuration `CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE=y` should be enabled in the image.

```sol
