A Sol function can implement tail recursion with an explicit memory termination counter by defining a recursive function that reads from a specific memory address, decrements the value stored there, and writes the result back before making the recursive call. This approach uses the memory location as a stateful termination counter, ensuring the recursion stops when the value reaches zero. The following Sol code demonstrates this pattern using `Mem.read32`, `Mem.write32`, and a recursive call to `again`:

```fprisc
again = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
n = Word.toInt (Mem.read32 p); 
case n == 0 of True -> Unit | False -> 
    _ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt (n - 1)); 
    again;
main = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
_ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt 10000); 
again;
```

To define a nullary function with tail recursion and an explicit memory counter, declare a recursive function (e.g., `again`) that reads a 32-bit word from a specific address, decrements the value, and writes it back to the same address before calling itself again. The `main` function initializes this address with a starting count and invokes the recursive function. This pattern ensures termination is controlled by explicit memory writes rather than stack depth.

```fprisc
again = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
n = Word.toInt (Mem.read32 p); 
case n == 0 of True -> Unit | False -> 
  _ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt (n - 1)); 
again.

main = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
_ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt 10000); 
again.
```

