The `openDoc` function loads a document by name, updates the UI to highlight the corresponding tab, and retrieves the document body from an API endpoint. It then renders the content into the `#doc` element and optionally scrolls the window to a specific line based on the `scrollLine` parameter, calculating the scroll position as a fraction of the total document lines.

```fprisc
async function openDoc(name, scrollLine) \{
  CUR = name;
  document.querySelectorAll('.tab').forEach(t =>
    t.classList.toggle('on', t.dataset.n === name));
  const d = await j('/api/doc?n=' + encodeURIComponent(name));
  $('#doc').innerHTML = render(d.body);
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);
  if (scrollLine) \{
    const frac = scrollLine / Math.max(1, d.body.split('\\n').length);
    window.scrollTo(0, frac * document.body.scrollHeight * 0.9);
  }
  j('/api/visit', \{ method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(\{ doc: name }) })
    .then(loadRecent);
}
```

This expression returns the total number of lines in the document body. It splits the `d.body` string by newline characters and counts the resulting array elements. This count is used to calculate a scroll fraction, which determines how far down the page to scroll when a specific line number is requested.

```fprisc
const lineCount = d.body.split('\\n').length;
const scrollFraction = scrollLine / Math.max(1, lineCount);
```

When a scroll line is provided, the code calculates a fractional position based on the total number of lines in the document body. It divides the requested line number by the maximum of 1 and the document's line count, then multiplies this fraction by 90% of the document's total scroll height to ensure it stays within bounds. The `window.scrollTo` function is then called with this computed vertical offset.
```sol
const frac = scrollLine / Math.max(1, d.body.split('\\n').length);
window.scrollTo(0, frac * document.body.scrollHeight * 0.9);
```

