FP-RISC establishes a concurrent service scope by loading typed configuration, handling requests, maintaining state, performing bounded concurrent work, logging failures, and shutting down cleanly. The system relies on explicit resource management and failure logging to ensure clean shutdowns, with concurrency constrained to bounded workloads to maintain stability.

```sol
// Example: Bounded concurrent work in a service
spawn {
  load_config typed_config;
  while true {
    handle_request req;
    maintain_state state;
    if fail log_failure;
  }
}
```

You implement a bounded concurrent loop by defining a fixed number of worker threads or processes that process requests from a typed configuration queue. Each worker maintains its own state and logs failures locally before the system performs a global shutdown sequence that gracefully terminates all threads and flushes logs. For example, in FP-RISC, you would define a `ConcurrentService` struct with a `start` function that initializes a bounded pool of workers and a `shutdown` function that signals completion and waits for the pool to drain:

```fp-risc
struct ConcurrentService {
    config: TypedConfig,
    workers: Pool<Worker>,
    shutdown: bool
}

fn start(service: &mut ConcurrentService) {
    service.workers = Pool::new(service.config.max_workers);
    for _ in 0..service.workers.len() {
        service.workers.spawn(handle_request);
    }
}

fn shutdown(service: &mut ConcurrentService) {
    service.shutdown = true;
    service.workers.join();
    flush_logs();
}
```

This ensures bounded concurrency and clean termination without speculative expansion of the specification.

