Promotion moves an object into the ARC.qa pool, where a dedicated manager actor handles Increment/Decrement messages to ensure atomicity; when the count reaches zero, the object is deallocated and the manager exits. This replaces the old lock-based model where multiple actors held raw pointers, with a single owner per memory block where holders read frozen data directly without messaging.

```fprisc
// Example of a manager handling a promoted object
actor MemoryManager {
    count: u32 = 0
    ptr: *u8

    decr() {
        count := count - 1
        if count == 0 {
            dealloc(ptr)
            exit()
        }
    }
}
```

When an object is promoted, it is deep-copied once and moved into the ARC.qa pool. Subsequently, ARC.qa spawns a dedicated manager actor for that object. Increment and decrement operations are sent as messages to this manager, ensuring structural atomicity through mailbox linearization. When the count reaches zero, the object is deallocated and the manager actor exits.

```fprisc
// Pseudo-code illustrating the promotion flow
obj = promote(source_obj)
// Deep copy happens here
manager = ARC.qa.spawn_manager(obj)
// Future operations are messages to manager
ARC.qa.send_inc(obj, manager)
ARC.qa.send_dec(obj, manager)
```

A panic causes a total abort, meaning all effects from the current script run are unwritten and no partial state persists. The runtime reverts to the snapshot taken at the script's entry, ensuring that if a phase fails, none of the previous phases' writes are visible.

```sol
// Example demonstrating total abort
fpr sol abort_test.sol
> phase1: file.write("data.txt", "value")
> phase2: if (1 / 0) { panic() }
// Result: "data.txt" does not exist after the script runs.
```

To generate the list of 32-bit words, first define the message as a string, then call `padWords` with that string. The function internally pads the message to a 64-byte boundary, appends the original length in bits as a big-endian 64-bit integer, and splits the resulting byte string into a list of 32-bit integers.

```fprisc
s = "example message";
wordList = padWords s;
```

The Matched-Set Law dictates that `plugsyms` embeds the absolute symbol addresses of the specific shell image into the plugin, creating an ABI-bound dependency. If the running app differs from the image the plugin was built against, any access to global or fuel variables will point to incorrect memory locations, causing memory corruption. To ensure stability, plugins must be regenerated using the current shell image before deployment, as the running application contract assumes it is the last-built version.

```sol
// Example of a plugin accessing a global symbol that requires correct addressing
// This code assumes the plugin was built against the current shell image.
let x = global_counter;
```

The `diffKeyed` function first attempts an optimized fast path using identity checks (`==`) on the lists; if elements differ, it checks if the difference is a single insertion or removal by comparing the rest of the lists. If the difference is more complex, it falls back to the `byKey` function, which uses a map to efficiently identify and update all changed keys in the database log.

```fprisc
byKey db keyOf one ek os ns =
  old = M.fromList S.compare (L.map (fn o -> (keyOf o, o)) os);
  new = M.fromList S.compare (L.map (fn n -> (keyOf n, True)) ns);
  _ = L.each (fn n -> (case M.get (keyOf n) old of
        O.Some o -> (if o == n then Unit else KV.post (ek (keyOf n)) (one n) db)
      | O.None -> KV.post (ek (keyOf n)) (one n) db)) ns;
  L.each (fn o -> if M.member (keyOf o) new then Unit else KV.post (ek (keyOf o)) J.Null db) os.
```

The `Vec.fold` function fused with `Vec.map` collapses a three-map chain into a single pass that writes the mapped element `g(el)` back into the column while simultaneously folding the accumulator `f` over it. This optimization ensures the map's writes remain program-visible through the returned vector, allowing the subsequent fold to re-fold the data without separate intermediate steps. The code demonstrates this by folding `addA` over the result of `Vec.map` operations on a range vector, verifying both correctness against a reference function and low memory usage.

```fpr
v0 = Vec.range 1 1000;
v3 = Vec.map mneg (Vec.map dbl (Vec.map inc v0));
(s1, _) = Vec.fold addA 0 v3;
```

