An operation belongs in L when implementing it as an ordinary library abstraction cannot preserve its intended meaning or guarantees. This typically includes syntax, evaluation rules, ownership checks, and primitive effect mechanisms. In contrast, S provides the canonical types, operations, laws, and minimal environmental interfaces that complete the language into an immediately usable system, ensuring programmers do not have to redefine fundamental constructions.

