You must write your code in `std/esp` because the ESP-IDF system uses the rv32 ISA and lacks standard Unix processes, files, or command lines, rendering most of `std/os` unavailable. Instead of POSIX calls, you use the `Esp.*` primitives provided in `std/esp` to access hardware features like the second core, GPIO, and the radio via ESP-Hosted or NimBLE. Blocking IDF calls are executed as jobs off the harts.

