```

C code does not consume fuel; instead, its execution time is treated as a constant black box bounded by WCET models for the specific hardware and QOS version. Consequently, any unbounded loops or blocking operations within a HAL function will hold the hart outside the fuel budget. To remain compliant, HAL functions must either guarantee bounded execution or yield control via the actor machinery (such as syscalls or mailbox patterns) rather than spinning.

```fprisc
// Example: A HAL function that correctly yields to avoid WCET violations
// instead of spinning an unbounded loop.
@hal
fn infinite_worker(actor: Actor) -> Unit {
    let mut counter = 0u64;
    while true {
        counter = counter + 1u64;
        // Yield to allow preemption and stay within fuel bounds
        actor.yield(); 
    }
}
```

