The `boundary` keyword defines a point in code where temporary resources created before it must be explicitly cleaned up to prevent memory leaks. Without it, a long-lived loop would keep every temporary it ever made until the boundary was reached. For example, a loop creating actors must call `drop` on them before the boundary:

```fpr
