The WCET bound is the sum of instruction costs across all basic blocks, plus the cost of fuel checks for every entry, plus the worst-case entry point bounds for all function calls, plus the total cost of sends multiplied by the promotion bound and receives multiplied by the decref bound. This calculation yields the segment time T(segment) in both the FPRISC and hardware views.

```fprc
// Example: T(segment) = sum(instruction costs) + entries * fuel_check
// + sum(C calls * C-entry-point bound) + sends * promotion_bound + recvs * decref_bound
```

FUEL_QUANTUM is a constant value used to calculate scheduler-level latency by multiplying it with the worst-case number of instructions per fuel unit for each runnable peer. This product determines the latency contribution of the scheduler based on instruction density relative to available fuel.

```sol
// Example illustrating the calculation of scheduler-level latency
let worst_instr_per_fuel = 5;
let fuel_quantum = 10;
let runnable_peers = 2;

let scheduler_latency = fuel_quantum * worst_instr_per_fuel * runnable_peers;
```

The calculated worst-case execution time (WCET) will be significantly underestimated because the formula fails to account for the cumulative overhead of scheduling multiple runnable peers. According to the provided decomposition, the scheduler-level latency is defined as FUEL_QUANTUM multiplied by the worst instructions per fuel unit for each runnable peer. Omitting the fuel-check constant associated with basic blocks removes the cost of the periodic fuel checks required to manage this scheduling overhead, leading to a model that does not reflect the actual resource consumption of the system.

    // Incorrect model: Missing fuel-check cost for basic blocks
    // This assumes instructions run without periodic fuel validation overhead.
    T_wrong = sum(instruction cost) + sum(C-entry-point bound) + sends * promotion + recvs * decref;

    // Correct model: Includes fuel-check constant per entry
    // This accounts for the scheduler-level latency impact on each basic block.
    T_correct = sum(instruction cost + fuel-check constant) + sum(C-entry-point bound) + sends * promotion + recvs * decref;

