To create a staggered wave pattern, calculate a unique phase offset for each unit using the `phaseOf` function, which combines the unit index, group ID, and clock time into a distinct value. This offset is then used to shift the input time variable into the `tri` or `sweep` functions, ensuring neighbors move asynchronously.

```sol
phaseOf s z c = (s * 7 + z * 3 + c * 11) * 397;
tri p t = x = ((t % p) + p) % p * 2000 / p; 1000 - Int.abs (x - 1000);
sweep p t = x = ((t % p) + p) % p * 1000 / p;
  case x < 350 of True -> 0 - 1000 | False -> case x < 500 of True -> (x - 425) * 1000 / 75 | False -> case x < 850 of True -> 1000 | False -> (925 - x) * 1000 / 75;
```

The `phaseOf` function generates a large integer hash to ensure neighbors never move in step, but FP-RISC requires all hash values to fit within the machine's native integer width. If the calculation `(s * 7 + z * 3 + c * 11) * 397` overflows the 32-bit limit (common on QOS's default architecture), the resulting value wraps around or truncates, causing the phase calculation to produce incorrect, non-unique values. This leads to adjacent units synchronizing their motion instead of maintaining the intended staggered wave pattern.

```fprisc
s = 10000; z = 10000; c = 10000;
phaseOf s z c = (s * 7 + z * 3 + c * 11) * 397;
```

