The fuel mechanism enforces a cooperative execution quantum where every unbounded control path must pass a safepoint. This allows the runtime to detect and handle resource exhaustion or deadlock conditions. The fuel is consumed at specific sites defined in the code, and if it reaches zero, execution is interrupted to allow recovery or termination.

```fprisc
// Example of fuel consumption at a safepoint
fuel: 100;

loop {
    // Work consumes fuel
    fuel := fuel - 1;

    // This is a safepoint where fuel is checked
    if fuel == 0 {
        break; // Exit the loop safely
    }
}
```

Fuel is consumed exclusively at safepoints, which are the only locations where an unbounded control path may pass without violating the cooperative execution quantum. The fundamental invariant is that every unbounded control path must traverse at least one fuel safepoint. This mechanism ensures that the runtime can preemptively reclaim resources when a task exceeds its allocated fuel quota.

Example Sol code demonstrating a safepoint:
```sol
