The runtime automatically handles paging for actors that have YIELDED or are blocked under memory pressure; it does not require an explicit manual call like `pageOut()`. The system will copy the actor's stack and heap as a single chunk to the reserved partition at `/actorchunks` once these conditions are met. To fix the code, remove the manual paging call and ensure the actor is allowed to yield or remain blocked naturally; the garbage collector and paging mechanism will then manage the memory reclamation based on the per-url liveness predicate and the index.

```sol
// Incorrect: Manual paging call on a blocked actor
actor blockedActor = spawn {
    waitForStorageResponse()
    pageOut(self) // This call is unnecessary and incorrect
}

// Correct: Rely on automatic paging when the actor yields or blocks
actor blockedActor = spawn {
    waitForStorageResponse() // If this blocks, the runtime will page the actor if memory is low
}
```

When an actor yields or is blocked, QOS can copy its stack and heap as a single chunk to a reserved partition of the log mounted at `/actorchunks` before reclaiming the memory. This paging mechanism is designed to occur automatically under memory pressure.

```fprisc
// Example demonstrating the concept of actor chunking to disk
// Note: Actual implementation details are internal to the QOS runtime.
actor MyActor = 
  let chunkSize = 1024 * 1024 in
  let reservedPartition = "/actorchunks" in
  when {
    // Logic that triggers yielding or blocking
    yield()
  } {
    // QOS runtime handles copying this actor's memory to 'reservedPartition'
    // and reclaiming it automatically.
  }
```

When a process yields or becomes blocked under memory pressure, its stack and heap are copied out as a single chunk to a reserved partition of the log mounted at `/actorchunks`, after which the original memory is reclaimed. This paging mechanism is managed by `URActors` (actors of System.qa), which handle file management with non-blocking forward discipline, while the Global Collector (GC) utilizes an in-memory index to identify live records and perform sweeps without reference tracing.

```sol
// Example: Simulating the concept of an actor managing a chunked memory region
// Note: This is a conceptual Sol script illustrating the actor management pattern
// described in the documentation, not a direct implementation of the GC or paging logic.

import System.qa;
import Qos.vfs;

// Create an actor that manages a specific URL/file context
let actor = new System.qa.URActor(url="my_file_url");

// The actor will handle requests non-blocking
actor.handleRequest(request) ->
    if request.type == "yield" then
        // Under memory pressure, the runtime would copy this actor's
        // stack/heap to /actorchunks and reclaim memory.
        print("Actor yielding; memory may be paged to /actorchunks");
    else
        print("Processing request");
    end;
```

The `/actorchunks` partition is a reserved area of the log used for paging actors to disk. When an actor or loaded process yields or is blocked under memory pressure, its stack and heap are copied out as a single chunk to this partition, allowing the memory to be reclaimed.

```fpr
// Example: Paging an actor to /actorchunks
actor PagedActor {
  yield() ->
    copyHeapToDisk("/actorchunks", self.heap) ->
    freeHeap()
}
```

