In the LiveView runtime, the poller operates without blocking and backs off to 5 ms between polls, causing the first message after a quiet spell to wait for the next tick before crossing parked harts. In contrast, the QOS kernel can utilize a poller that blocks without blocking a hart; this approach is designed to deliver readiness into the actor system via a thread outside the hart pool, specifically using the kernel's irq path.

```sol
// QOS Kernel Poller Example (Conceptual)
// Delivers readiness via an external thread without blocking harts
defn kernel_poller() {
  let thread = spawn_external_thread()
  thread.poll_and_deliver_readiness()
}
```

The poller (`std/poller`) operates without blocking but backs off to a 5 ms interval between polls. Consequently, the first message received after a quiet period incurs a latency of 9.5 ms because it must wait for the next poll tick before crossing parked harts and entering the kernel, whereas active operations take only 3.2 ms. To fix this, the architecture proposes a poller that blocks in the kernel without blocking a hart, utilizing a thread outside the hart pool to deliver readiness into the actor system via the runtime's irq path.

```sol
// Conceptual example of the proposed irq path delivery
when (ready) {
  // The irq thread delivers readiness directly into the actor system
  // without waiting for the main poller tick
  actor.receive! { msg }
}
```

