The `loadProf` function first checks if a disk is present by examining the `fs` argument. If no disk is available (`fs` is not provided or effectively zero), it prints a message indicating that games are not recorded and returns a modified model with `fsOn` set to 0. If a disk is available, it compacts the file system, replays the profile to generate statistics, and loads the latest game state to update the model's profile and enable disk writing.

```fpr
loadProf m me 0 =
  _ = print "[terra2] profile: no disk, games are not recorded";
  { m | fsOn = 0 }.
loadProf m me fs =
  c = FS.compact me fs;
  _ = drop c;
  r = FS.replay me fs profUrl;
  p = profOf r;
  _ = drop r;
  _ = print "[terra2] profile: {p.played} games ({p.won} won, {p.lost} lost, {p.gone} abandoned)";
  g = FS.readLatest me fs gameUrl;
  m2 = gameOf { m | prof = p, fsOn = 1 } g;
  _ = drop g;
  _ = announce m2;
  m2.
```

When a game is loaded, the system first reverts the profile to its initial state by replaying the compacted file and parsing the resulting log to extract play, win, and loss counts. This updated profile is then merged with the current game metadata to create a new game object, which is finally announced to the user. The transition of the game state flag occurs within the `flush` function, which checks if the game is dirty and idle; if so, it triggers a save operation, otherwise it retains the current state.

```sol
p = profOf (FS.replay me fs profUrl);
m2 = gameOf { m | prof = p, fsOn = 1 } g;
flush env m = case m.dirty == 1 and idleNow m of True -> saveGame m | False -> m;
```

