Bulk data, such as vectors and frames, uses the linear regime where each item has exactly one owner, is mutated in place, and carries no reference counts; it is freed at a single consume point. In contrast, tree or list data uses the Rc regime, which is restricted to sharing within a single actor and relies on reference counting at existing safepoints, where an Rc-zero call triggers a deallocation.

```fprisc
// Linear regime: single owner, no refcount, freed at one point
let vec = Vec.alloc(10);
vec.push(1);
// vec is freed here (scope exit) or via Vec.free()

// Rc regime: multiple owners within one actor, freed when count hits zero
let node = Node.make();
let child = node.link(node); // Rc increment
// child is freed here when last owner drops (Rc-zero)
```

Reference counting (Rc) is strictly confined to intra-actor sharing and will not cross an actor boundary. Attempting to share data across actors using Rc is incorrect because the Rc mechanism assumes a single-actor context where inc/dec are plain stores without concurrency; consequently, the reference count logic will not function as intended when applied outside a single actor.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: Trying to share 'shared_data' across two actors using Rc
actor A {
    let shared_data = Rc::new(Vec::new());
    // ...
}

actor B {
    let shared_data = shared_data; // This is an error or undefined behavior
    // ...
}
```

You cannot share tree data between actors; the Rc regime is strictly intra-actor only. To share within a single actor, use `ref` at safepoints and `unref` to drop references. When the last reference count reaches zero, the `dealloc` command is invoked automatically.

```sol
actor main() {
  let node = Node.new();
  ref node; // Shared within actor
  // ... manipulate node ...
  unref node; // Drops reference; dealloc called if count is 0
}
```

No. Reference counting in FP-RISC is strictly intra-actor; the Rc regime never crosses an actor boundary. Inc/dec operations are plain stores that occur only within the single owning actor, and when the reference count reaches zero, the structure is deallocated.

```sol
