The code will fail because `receiveFrom` requires the target actor to be on the same core (hart) as the sender; attempting to receive from a remote actor triggers a core mismatch error. To fix this, the message must be sent to the same actor instance used for sending, or the cross-core communication must be handled via a dedicated message queue or explicit core-to-core messaging mechanism rather than direct `receiveFrom` on a remote target.

Corrected logic example:
```fpr
bounce me other k = if k == 0 then 0 else
  _ = send me (me, k);
  r = receive me;
  _ = drop r;
  bounce me other (k - 1).
```

You use the `spawnOn` function with a core index derived from `Sys.harts` to distribute jobs across available harts. For example, to run `n` jobs across two cores, you can spawn them with indices `k % Sys.harts Unit` and then collect results using a `receive` loop that tracks execution time with `Esp.ms`.

```fpr
t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
_ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
```

Using `receiveFrom` on the local hart causes the actor to block indefinitely or fail to receive the intended message because `receiveFrom` is designed to block until a message arrives from the specified remote hart. In the `bounce` function, the line `r = receiveFrom me other` attempts to receive from `other` (the remote hart). If `other` were incorrectly identified as the local hart, the actor would never receive the ping it sent, breaking the cross-core communication loop. Correct usage requires passing the actual remote hart identifier to `receiveFrom`.

```fpr
// Correct usage: 'other' is the remote hart
r = receiveFrom me other;

// Incorrect usage: passing self instead of 'other'
// r = receiveFrom me self; // This would hang or fail as expected
```

