The file `qos/docs/BOUNDS.md` documents the QOS side of the bounds register rules, which dictate that limits must originate from the machine, protocol, or programmer, while any other constraint must grow until memory is the bound; reaching such a limit must never be silent. It details specific fixes from September 19, 2026, such as changing `qa_t.perms` from a fixed 32-entry array to a heap-allocated array that doubles in size, ensuring no permissions are silently dropped. For example, instead of truncating a capability path, the system now uses owned strings of whatever length the manifest provides:

```sol
// Old behavior (dropped permissions silently)
qa_t.perms[32] = ... 

// New behavior (growable heap array)
qa_t.perms = new_heap_array();
while (grants_needed > size_of(perms)) {
    double(perms);
}
```

To implement a dynamic capability manifest, use the `qa_t.perms` heap array which doubles in size, replacing the fixed 32-entry limit. For strings, utilize owned strings of whatever length the manifest provides, avoiding the previous truncation of URLs and modes. This ensures all granted permissions and paths are fully preserved.
```sol
