To implement a dynamic ring buffer that maintains message order and expands the ARC table, use the `Dynamic` actor from the standard library and the `drainT` function to process heap values (tuples). The `drainT` function iterates through `n` items, receiving tuples `(a, b)` from the input channel, and only retains them if they are strictly greater than the previous value (`a > last`), ensuring strict ordering. This approach allows the system to manage thousands of shared values in flight, bypassing the static 1024-entry limit.

```fpr
sink2 main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainT me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.

drainT me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered).
drainT me main k last ordered =
  (a, b) = receiveFrom me main;
  drainT me main (k - 1) a (ordered and a > last).
```

To implement a dynamic ring buffer that grows past its old ceiling while maintaining order, you define a `sink` actor that receives a count from a gate and uses the `drainN` function to process messages from the main input channel. The `drainN` function recursively consumes messages, tracking the last value to ensure strict ordering, and returns the count and ordered status to the main channel. This approach allows the ARC table to expand with thousands of shared values in flight without refusing a burst that outruns the buffer.

```fpr
# Dynamic ring buffer implementation
sink main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainN me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.

drainN me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered);
drainN me main k last ordered =
  v = receiveFrom me main;
  drainN me main (k - 1) v (ordered and v > last).
```

