The `doNeq` function determines inequality by first marking the current substitution state using `marks`, then attempting to unify the two terms. If unification succeeds, the system undoes the bindings created by `marks` to restore the original state, effectively treating the terms as distinct because they were not unified. If unification fails, the function proceeds to solve the goal with the modified substitution, indicating the terms are indeed unequal. This mechanism ensures that `!=` succeeds only when the terms are not unifiable at the moment of the call.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `marks` function extracts the current unification history (bindings) from the memory state `m` to enable the detection of cycles or non-termination during negation checks. In `doNeq`, it captures these marks before attempting unification; if unification succeeds (`ok` is `True`), the marks are used to undo the bindings via `undoTo`, ensuring that the negation succeeds only if the terms are not currently unifiable.

