In FP-RISC, socket primitives are non-blocking and never block a hart. When a socket operation cannot complete immediately, it returns the status `"again"`. To handle this, you must use `std/stream`, which automatically puts the actor to sleep for a brief duration (backing off from 0.1 ms to 4 ms) and then retries the operation until success. This mechanism allows servers and clients to run concurrently within the same process without halting the execution thread.

```fprisc
import std/stream

actor server() {
  var s = std/stream.open(socket, address);
  while (true) {
    var result = s.read();
    if (result == "again") {
      // std/stream handles the sleep and retry automatically
      continue;
    }
    // Process received data
  }
}
```

A hart is a thread that is shared among multiple actors. Because a hart can be shared, socket primitives within this architecture are non-blocking and return "again" instead of blocking execution. To handle this, the `std/stream` module places the actor to sleep with a backing-off strategy starting at 0.1 ms and increasing up to 4 ms before retrying.

```sol
// Example of a hart-based socket primitive that returns "again"
socket.send(msg)
// If the socket is busy, this returns "again" without blocking the hart
```

When a socket primitive returns "again", indicating the socket is not ready, the `std/stream` module places the associated ACTOR into a sleep state. This sleep mechanism includes a backing-off delay that starts at 0.1 milliseconds and increases up to 4 milliseconds before the actor retries the operation.

```sol
// Example demonstrating the retry loop logic handled by std/stream
while (socket_read_result == "again") {
    // std/stream automatically puts the actor to sleep with backing off
    // and retries the socket_read operation
    socket_read_result = socket_read();
