The QOS design treats a panic in any actor as a fatal system event that ends the whole program immediately. This strict behavior prevents a library from masking individual task failures as `Err` results, ensuring that no failed task can be silently ignored or transformed without crashing the entire application. Consequently, error handling must occur at the actor level before any message is sent to `receiveRes`, as a bare tuple or any unhandled panic will cause the program to enter a deadlock state or terminate.

```fprisc
-- Example of a fatal panic in an actor
actor panicActor() {
  panic("Critical failure");
  // The entire program ends here; no Err can be returned.
}
```

The `paintLocals` function synchronizes HTML elements with local variables by iterating through attributes like `data-text`, `data-show`, `data-on`, and `data-input` to set text content, visibility, active classes, and input values respectively. In Sol, this is achieved by defining a function that maps these attributes to their corresponding variable lookups and DOM manipulations:

```sol
fn paintLocals {
  root.querySelectorAll('[data-text]').forEach(el -> el.textContent = locals[el.getAttribute('data-text')] ?? '')
  root.querySelectorAll('[data-show]').forEach(el -> {
    var spec = el.getAttribute('data-show').split(':')
    var v = locals[spec[0]]
    var on = spec.length > 1 ? String(v) === spec[1] : (v && v !== '0' && v !== 'false')
    el.style.display = on ? '' : 'none'
  })
  root.querySelectorAll('[data-on]').forEach(el -> el.classList.toggle('is-on', String(locals[el.getAttribute('data-on').split(':')[0]]) === el.getAttribute('data-on').split(':')[1]))
  root.querySelectorAll('[data-input]').forEach(el -> if (el !== document.activeElement) el.value = locals[el.getAttribute('data-input')] ?? '')
}
```

The `applySet` function updates a value in the `locals` object based on the provided name and value string. It first retrieves the current value for the specified name. If the new value is the string "toggle", it flips the current value between "0" and "1". If the new value starts with "+" or "-", it performs arithmetic addition on the current numeric value (defaulting to 0 if the current value is not numeric) and the new value. Otherwise, it simply assigns the new value directly to the `locals` object.

```fprisc
applySet(name, val) \{
    var cur = locals[name];
    if (val === 'toggle') locals[name] = (cur === '1' ? '0' : '1');
    else if (val[0] === '+' || val[0] === '-') locals[name] = String((+cur || 0) + (+val));
    else locals[name] = val;
}
```

