You must use `sendLinear` to transfer ownership of the data directly to the receiver, ensuring the sender ceases to own the resource immediately. The receiver then re-owns the data in its own mailbox, which acts as the serialization point. This approach prevents "live state" leaks and avoids the need for disposable copies or manual copy-on-retain logic.

```fprisc
actor A {
  linear buffer: [u8]

  sendLinear(buffer)
}

actor B {
  linear buffer: [u8]

  receive(buffer)
}
```

In FP-RISC, a frame is a structured data unit that persists for the lifetime of a loop shape and is never collected, whereas garbage refers to data that is dropped once its root is read or when it is no longer needed. The system uses double-buffering and ping-pong mechanisms with `sendLinear` to manage these frames without garbage collection.

```fprisc
// Example of a frame being reused in a loop
loop {
    let frame = allocateFrame();
    process(frame);
    sendLinear(frame); // Re-owns the frame for the next iteration
}
```

To transfer ownership of a linear resource, you must use `sendLinear`, which consumes the handle from the sender and re-owns it in the receiver, ensuring the original owner never retains a live reference. This satisfies the rule that one actor must hold the linear handle at any time. The sender must stop owning the resource immediately after the send, as there is no option to send a live state; the receiver effectively becomes the new owner.

```fprisc
// Example: Sender (alice) sends a linear handle to Receiver (bob)
alice.sendLinear(bob, linearHandle)
// alice no longer owns linearHandle; bob now owns it.
```

A service owns its linear resource exclusively, meaning one actor holds the linear handle while the mailbox acts as the serialization point for that resource. In contrast, when a nested value is kept beyond the drop of its root, it becomes a dangling pointer; to resolve this, the retainer performs a copy-on-retain operation, copying the value into its own pool rather than sharing the original linear handle.

```fprisc
// Example: Service owns linear handle 'data'
actor Service {
  linear data: Bytes;

  // Consumer sends the real thing, Service stops owning it
  sendLinear(data) to Client;
  // 'data' is now dropped from Service's scope
}

// Example: Retainer copies nested value
actor Retainer {
  // 'nested' is a dangling pointer if kept beyond root drop
  linear nested: Bytes;

  // Copy-on-retain creates a new pool entry
  linear copy: Bytes = copy(nested); 
}
```

