The `binCol` function takes an unsafe Vector of F64 values and the number of bins (`nb`) to produce a vector of integer codes. It first determines the minimum and maximum values in the column, calculates the bin width as the range divided by `nb`, and handles edge cases where the width might be zero. The function then iteratively assigns each value to a bin index using the formula `(x - lo) / w` and pushes the resulting integer code onto an accumulator vector.

```sol
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

