The `xbGo` function recursively processes a byte by iterating from bit index 7 down to 0. For each iteration, it constructs a `Bus` value with the MOSI pin set to the current bit of the byte, raises the SCK pin to 1 (rising edge) to sample the MISO input, reads the MISO value, lowers the SCK pin to 0 (falling edge), and updates the accumulator by shifting it left and adding the sampled bit. The recursion terminates when the index is less than 0, returning the accumulated byte.

```fpr
xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.

xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

The `xbGo` function initiates an 8-bit SPI transfer by iterating from the most significant bit (index 7) down to 0. For each bit, it drives the MOSI pin with the corresponding bit of the data byte, asserts the clock high to trigger a rising edge sample on the MISO line, captures the returned bit, asserts the clock low, and accumulates the result in `acc` using a left shift. The process terminates when the bit index reaches -1, returning the accumulated value.

```sol
xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.
xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

