The `balance` function ensures the tree remains balanced by comparing the heights of the left (`hl`) and right (`hr`) subtrees. If the left subtree is taller than the right by more than one level (`hl > hr + 1`), it performs a right rotation. Otherwise, if the right subtree is taller by more than one level, it performs a left rotation; otherwise, it constructs a new node. The `node` function calculates the new height as `1 + max(hl, hr)`, where `hl` and `hr` are the heights of the left and right children respectively.

```fprisc
balance l k v r =
  hl = height l;
  hr = height r;
  case hl > hr + 1 of
    True -> rotRight l k v r
  | False -> (case hr > hl + 1 of True -> rotLeft l k v r | False -> node l k v r).

node l k v r =
  hl = height l;
  hr = height r;
  Node (1 + (case hl > hr of True -> hl | False -> hr)) l k v r.
```

The file `qos/programs/mods/dungeonrules.fpr` defines the core movement and combat logic for the dungeon game mode within the QOS operating system. It implements a heuristic search algorithm to determine the optimal next step for a player, prioritizing paths that minimize distance to the goal while avoiding blocked cells and enemies. Additionally, it handles the consequences of enemy attacks, such as reducing the player's hit points and triggering death if health reaches zero, and manages the progression of turns by advancing time and executing enemy actions.

```fpr
bestStep m dist x y flee =
  here = distAt m dist x y;
  c1 = (x - 1, y);
  c2 = (x + 1, y);
  c3 = (x, y - 1);
  c4 = (x, y + 1);
  b1 = pick m dist (x, y) here c1 flee;
  b2 = pick m dist b1 (scoreOf m dist b1 flee) c2 flee;
  b3 = pick m dist b2 (scoreOf m dist b2 flee) c3 flee;
  pick m dist b3 (scoreOf m dist b3 flee) c4 flee.
```

The `doAfter` function calculates the absolute deadline (`dl`) by adding the requested delay in milliseconds (`ms`) to the current clock value, using the constant `ticksPerMs` (10,000) to convert milliseconds to clock ticks. It then inserts this new deadline into the sorted pending list (`pend`) using the `insert` function, which maintains the list in ascending order of deadlines to ensure the oldest due timer is processed first.

```fprisc
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
```

