The PLIC implements a CLAIM-THEN-MASK strategy where the interrupt drain loop masks the source immediately upon claiming it, preventing the level line from re-pending the same interrupt while the actor is being scheduled. The actor responsible for servicing the device must explicitly re-arm the source after completion; if the line remains high, the gateway re-pends, delivering the interrupt exactly once per acknowledge.

```fpr
// Pseudo-code illustrating the claim-mask-ack cycle
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    // Mask the source to prevent re-pending
    plic_set_mask(source_id, 1);

    // Deliver to actor
    send(Int, source_id);
}

// Inside the actor after servicing
Sys.irqAck(source_id) {
    // Re-arm the source if the line is still high
    plic_set_mask(source_id, 0);
}
```

In the QOS-x86_64 target (`--target=qx64`), the three thread-local cells normally used by hosted lowering (`fpr_posix_hart`, `fpr_x64_a6/a7`) are replaced with plain globals loaded RIP-relative. This occurs because a loaded app in this design is single-hart (meaning one global is correct), and the fixed-slot image lacks a dynamic loader to register a TLS block, making link-time `@tpoff` constants invalid as they would index the host's TCB.

```sol
