The `release` function initiates a garbage collection cycle by enqueuing a block into the worklist, which is then processed by the `drain` function. The `drain` function traverses the block's fields, visiting each address to determine if it is live. Crucially, before freeing the block's payload, `drain` decrements the reference count of the block itself and sets it to 1, ensuring the block remains allocated until all its fields have been fully visited and their reference counts adjusted.

```fpr
release v = drain (enqueue v (Block.null Unit))

drain work = case Block.isNull work of
    True -> Unit
  | False ->
    b = work;
    rest = Block.pending b;
    rest2 = visit b (Addr.add (payload b) 8) 0 (fields b) rest;
    _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
    _ = free (payload b);
    drain rest2.
```

The `nestedProbe` function returns the sum of the elements in the inner vector, which is 6. It first creates an inner vector containing `[1, 2, 3]`, pushes a unit value onto it to create an outer vector, retrieves the inner vector via `Vec.get`, and finally folds the inner vector's elements to compute the sum.

```sol
nestedProbe u =
  inner = Vec.fromList [1, 2, 3];
  outer = Vec.push inner (Vec.new Unit);
  (back, outer2) = Vec.get 1 outer;
  (s, back2) = Vec.fold (fn a x -> a + x) 0 back;
  u1 = Vec.free back2;
  u2 = Vec.free outer2;
  s.
```

