Device interrupts are routed to the last live hart, which acts as the interrupt handler, while timer interrupts are managed exclusively by the timer service actor. This design ensures that the prime hart (hart 0) remains free for critical tasks, taking device interrupts only if it is the sole core. The timer service arms a single deadline and delivers a bare interrupt to the designated hart, allowing blocked callers to move from the backlog to the ready zone upon wake-up.

```fprisc
// Example: Registering the timer service to manage CLINT deadlines
Sys.timerBind(actor: TimerService);
Sys.timerArm(deadline: nearest_deadline);
```

Device interrupts are routed to the last live hart (the IRQ hart), which is typically an auxiliary hart, whereas timer interrupts are handled by a dedicated timer service actor that arms deadlines on the CLINT and delivers a bare interrupt to the IRQ hart. Prime hart 0 does not handle device interrupts unless it is the only core, while the timer service manages all CLINT driver work and wakes blocked callers via ordinary messages rather than direct hart preemption.

```fprisc
// Example of the timer service binding and arming a deadline
Sys.timerBind(actor);
Sys.timerArm(deadline); // Arms the nearest deadline; IRQ hart delivers the bare Int
```

