// This script relies on the Sol profile to guarantee transactional effects
// and resource ownership, regardless of internal interpreter dispatch.
import sol;

fn main() {
  > let x = 1;
  > use x; // Consumes x per the value/ownership model
}
```

The Sol contract defines the specific surface, library environment, and execution guarantees that a program expects, rather than merely enabling arbitrary flags. It covers aspects such as script entry, default imports, the value/ownership model, transaction boundaries, and standard host facilities. While the implementation can choose internal techniques like JIT thresholds or memory management, it must preserve these observable behaviors.

```sol
# Example demonstrating the Sol contract's handling of linear resources
import sol.prelude

@linear
fn consume(x: u32) {
  x.use();
  x.free();
}

fn main() {
  let val = 42;
