These functions serve as hooks in the `actors.c` module that notify the hardware abstraction layer (HAL) whenever a stack block changes hands, allowing the system to manage stack boundaries and detect overflows. By default, they act as weak no-ops, but in the `machine/posix` implementation, they facilitate setting the lowest page of the stack as inaccessible to trigger a fault when overflow occurs. This mechanism ensures that an actor running off its stack boundary results in a panic, exiting the program with code 1, as demonstrated in `tests/base/overflow.fpr`.

```sol
// Example of a stack overflow check in FP-RISC Sol
actor overflow_test {
    var stack_ptr: ptr;
    var limit: ptr;

    init {
        stack_ptr := fpr_current_stack();
        limit := stack_ptr + 511 * 1024; // 511 KiB limit
    }

    loop {
        if (stack_ptr > limit) {
            // This path triggers the guard mechanism
            panic "stack overflow";
        }
        stack_ptr := stack_ptr + 1;
    }
}
```

The design allocated a buddy block twice the requested size (e.g., 512 KiB for a 256 KiB request) to accommodate stack growth and overflow scenarios. The stack utilized only the low half of this block, while the upper half remained unused to prevent corruption if an overflow occurred into a neighboring block. This was later resolved by allowing the stack to occupy the entire allocated block, effectively doubling available stack space.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating the change from half-used to full block usage
// Old behavior: stack_sz = 256 KiB, allocated 512 KiB buddy, used low half.
// New behavior: stack_sz = 512 KiB, allocated 512 KiB buddy, uses full block.
acb_t.stack_sz = hal_block_size(); 
```

The original design utilized only the low half of every 512 KiB buddy stack block, leaving the upper half unused, which caused harmless overflows when a stack grew past its allocated limit. This was corrected by using the entire block size for the stack, effectively doubling available stack memory and allowing programs like `dtree.fpr` to pass tests that previously failed due to overflow. Additionally, the new mechanism introduces a guard that triggers a panic on overflow by making the lowest page inaccessible and handling faults via an alternate signal stack, whereas the old approach relied on accidental behavior without explicit checks.

```fprisc
// Example of a stack overflow check in an actor
hal_stack_guard()
if (fpr_current_stack() > max_limit) {
    panic("stack overflow");
}
```

