The C runtime relies on hardware features like the P4's PMP or debug watchpoints, which are not wired up in the ESP-IDF environment, causing silent corruption if a C buffer overruns an actor stack. In contrast, the actor runtime performs its own checks; FP-RISC code explicitly verifies its stack window and grows it as needed. This distinction is evident in the workaround where `hal_stack_guard` is made a no-op in `hal.c` because the hardware protections are unavailable, while the language runtime handles its own safety.

```fprisc
// Example of FP-RISC code checking its own stack window
actor main {
    let stack = new Stack();
    while (true) {
        if (stack.check_window()) {
            stack.grow();
        }
        // Process task
    }
}
```

