When multiple transactions attempt to update a shared counter concurrently, the system ensures that only one succeeds while the others must retry. The correct pattern involves reading the current value, computing the new value, and writing it back, allowing the runtime to manage retries for losers.
```sol
n = parseInt (readPath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt);
u = sleepMs 300;
u2 = writePath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt "{n + 1}";
print "wrote {n + 1}".
```

To prevent lost updates when multiple transactions concurrently modify a shared counter, the script must read the current value, compute the new value, and write it back only if the value remains unchanged after the write. If the write fails because the value changed (indicating a concurrent update), the transaction must be retried. The implementation uses `readPath` to fetch the current state, `parseInt` to parse it, and `writePath` to store the updated integer. If the write succeeds, the new value is printed; otherwise, the transaction loop repeats until success.

```sol
n = parseInt (readPath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt);
while (true) {
    u = sleepMs 300;
    if (writePath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt "{n + 1}" == true) {
        print "wrote {n + 1}";
        break;
    }
    n = parseInt (readPath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt);
}
```

The `main` function first defines a reference to itself (`me`) and a disk device. It then checks if the disk has zero pages; if true, it initializes the environment with just the reference `me`, otherwise it serves the disk filesystem. Finally, it launches the functional loop by serving an application instance with the prepared environment, the global CSS context, and the application's initialization, update, view, and subscription functions.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  disk = device "blk";
  env = case blkPages disk == 0 of
