The `!=` operator acts as a negation for equality, returning `yes` if the operands differ and `no` if they are identical. This applies consistently to both tuples and lists; for instance, `(1, 2) != (3, 4)` evaluates to `yes`, while `(1, 2) != (1, 2)` evaluates to `no`. The same logic holds for lists constructed via `mkList`, where `mkList 1 != mkList 9` yields `yes` because the lists contain different elements.

```fprisc
yes "!= on tuples" ((1, 2) != (3, 4));
no "!= on equal tuples" ((1, 2) != (1, 2));
yes "!= on lists" (mkList 1 != mkList 9);
```

Use the `!=` operator to check if two values are not equal. This operator returns `yes` if the values differ and `no` if they are identical, working consistently across tuples and lists. For example, `(1, 2) != (3, 4)` evaluates to `yes`, while `(1, 2) != (1, 2)` evaluates to `no`. Similarly, `mkList 1 != mkList 9` returns `yes` because the lists contain different elements.

```fprisc
yes "Inequality works on tuples and lists" ((1, 2) != (3, 4));
no "Inequality fails on equal tuples" ((1, 2) != (1, 2));
yes "Inequality works on lists" (mkList 1 != mkList 9);
```

The `!=` operator performs element-wise negation of equality. On tuples, it returns `yes` if the components differ, such as `(1, 2) != (3, 4)`, and `no` if they are identical, like `(1, 2) != (1, 2)`. For lists, it evaluates to `yes` when the lists have different lengths or contents, as seen with `mkList 1 != mkList 9`. Regarding booleans, `True == True` yields `yes`, while `True == False` yields `no`, implying `True != False` is `yes`. The operator also treats the `Unit` value as equal to itself, so `Unit != Unit` is `no`.

```fprisc
  _ = yes "Tuples differ" ((1, 2) != (3, 4));
  _ = no "Tuples equal" ((1, 2) != (1, 2));
  _ = yes "Lists differ" (mkList 1 != mkList 9);
  _ = yes "True != False" (True != False);
  _ = no "Unit != Unit" (Unit != Unit);
  0.
```

