You can implement a live-reloading watcher by defining a configuration with a list of files to monitor and a rebuild function, then running a loop that checks file modification times against a stored snapshot. If a change is detected, the system sleeps briefly, retrieves the current file states, and if they differ from the last known state, executes the rebuild function and sends a reload signal.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
  else
    _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
    (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
```

The `live` module provides a `watcher` function that monitors a list of file paths. It periodically checks file metadata (size and modification time) to detect changes. If a change is detected, it executes the `rebuild` configuration; if the rebuild succeeds with a return code of 0, it sends a `Reload` signal to the client to serve the updated program.

