To perform an atomic compare-and-swap, the program must use the `amo_cmpxchg` command on a naturally aligned eight-byte location in ordinary RAM. This operation uses an LR/SC retry loop with an acquire read on the comparison and a release write on a successful swap; if the comparison fails, it performs an acquire read. Ordering across memory and I/O domains requires explicit use of fences, as calls alone do not provide hardware memory barriers.

```sol
// Example: Atomic compare-and-swap with fence
let val = Mem.load8(addr)
if val != expected {
    Mem.fence iorw, iorw
    val = Mem.load8(addr)
}
if val == expected {
    Mem.store8(addr, new_val)
    Mem.fence iorw, iorw
}
```

When a loop re-runs a non-idempotent operation, the transactional semantics fail because the system expects operations to be safe to retry on conflict. This results in incorrect data states or unexpected side effects, as the model relies on idempotency to safely handle retries without duplicating effects. For example, attempting to append a string to a file inside a retry loop without using a unique marker or atomic write will cause the file to contain duplicate content if the transaction retries after a conflict.

```sol
-- Incorrect: Non-idempotent append inside a loop
loop {
  fileAppend("log.txt", "entry\n")
  if (fileRead("log.txt") == "expected") break
}
-- If a conflict forces a retry, "entry\n" is appended twice.

-- Correct: Idempotent append using a marker
loop {
  let marker = fileWrite("log.txt", "entry\n", "marker")
  if (marker == "done") break
}
```

