An actor's pool is reclaimed only by a reset, meaning a loop running for a day in one pool would retain every string it ever made. To prevent this, each turn sends its whole state to itself, resets the pool so that every temporary of the turn dies, and reads the state back into the fresh pool. This process can be implemented as follows:

```fprisc
boundaryR : unsafe Int -> Rs -> Rs .
boundaryR me st =
  _ = send me st;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  st2 = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  st2.
```

An actor's pool is reclaimed only by a `reset`, meaning a long-running loop without an explicit reset will accumulate all temporary strings created during execution. To fix this, the code must explicitly call `Sys.poolReset` at the end of each turn to free temporary values before reading the state back into a fresh pool. For example:

```sol
loopTurn = unsafe Int -> Rs -> Rs .
loopTurn me st =
  _ = send me st;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  st2 = keep m;
  st2.
```

The `boundaryR` function ensures that an actor's memory pool is reclaimed by sending its state to itself, resetting the pool via `Sys.poolReset 0`, and then receiving the state back into a fresh pool. This process guarantees that temporary values created during the turn are discarded, preventing memory accumulation over time.

```fprisc
boundaryR me st =
  _ = send me st;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  st2 = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  st2.
```

