The `splice` function first determines the longest common prefix between the old and new strings. It then calculates the number of characters to remove by subtracting the length of the common suffix (found by reversing the remaining parts of the strings and finding their common prefix) from the length of the remaining old string. The number of characters to insert is the difference between the length of the remaining new string and the common suffix. The resulting string is formatted as a JSON-like object containing the prefix, the count of removed characters, and the inserted characters.

```fprisc
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

The `splice` function calculates the edit distance by first finding the longest common prefix and suffix between two lists, then determining the number of elements to remove from the first list and the number to insert from the second. The result is a string describing the transformation, including the prefix, the count of removed items, and the list of inserted items.

```sol
