The loop termination condition must use the less-than operator (`<`) to check if the current face index is strictly less than the total number of faces, ensuring the loop executes exactly `nFaces` times. Using a greater-than-or-equal-to (`>=`) comparison as the guard for a recursive function that increments the counter will cause the function to skip the final iteration instead of terminating after it, effectively baking one fewer neighbor than intended. The corrected function uses `| f < nFaces =` to allow the recursive call to proceed for the last face, after which the base case `f >= nFaces` triggers the return.

```sol
rebakeNbrs : unsafe Vector -> Vector -> Int -> Int -> (Vector, Vector) .
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1).
