The PLIC implements a CLAIM-THEN-MASK discipline where the interrupt source is masked immediately upon being claimed by the drain loop, preventing re-pending if the device line remains high. The actor servicing the interrupt re-arms the source only after successfully acknowledging it via Sys.irqAck. This ensures exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment without storms.

```fpr
# Example logic for claim and ack in actors.c
hal_irq_claim(hart, source) {
    # Mask the source immediately to prevent re-pending
    plic_mask(hart, source);
}

hal_irq_ack(hart, source) {
    # Re-arm the source only after successful service
    plic_unmask(hart, source);
}
```

The PLIC implements a CLAIM-THEN-MASK strategy where the drain loop masks the interrupt source immediately upon claiming it, preventing re-pending while the actor is being scheduled. The bound actor re-arms the source only after successfully servicing the device via Sys.irqAck; if the interrupt line remains high, the gateway re-pends the interrupt, ensuring exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment without storms.

```sol
// Pseudocode representing the drain and ack sequence
if (irq_claimed) {
    irq_mask_source();
}

// Inside the actor
Sys.irqAck(irq_source);
```

The PLIC prevents infinite loops by masking the interrupt source immediately upon claiming it; if the source remains enabled (line still high), the gateway will re-pend the interrupt, allowing a fresh delivery only after the actor re-arms it. The actor must re-arm the source via the `Sys.irqAck` call after servicing the device to ensure the interrupt is delivered exactly once per acknowledge.

```fpr
# Example: Claim, service, and re-arm a level-triggered IRQ
hal_irq_open 0x1000;
hal_irq_claim 0x1000;
# ... service device ...
Sys.irqAck 0x1000;
```

CLAIM-THEN-MASK is a strategy used to prevent interrupt storms from level-triggered sources, such as the 16550 UART, by masking the interrupt source immediately when it is claimed by the drain loop and re-arming it only after the actor successfully services the device. This ensures that a level-triggered line does not re-pend the interrupt while the actor is busy, which would cause the drain loop to spin indefinitely. The mechanism guarantees exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```fpr
// Example of the drain masking logic
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    // Mask the source to prevent re-pending while draining
    plic_set_pending(source_id, 0);
    // Perform drain logic here
}

// Example of the actor re-arming logic
Sys.irqAck(source_id, bound_actor) {
    // Re-arm the source if the device line is still high
    if (device_line_is_high(source_id)) {
        plic_set_pending(source_id, 1);
    }
}
```

