To implement a game loop in QOS, you define a recursive function that receives the current frame data, polls for new keys, calculates the new player position using a movement function, and then renders the updated scene. The loop must send the new frame back to the parent process to trigger the next iteration.

```sol
frameWorker : unsafe Int -> Int -> Unit .
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys = message;
  nextKeys = pollKeys keys 32;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The `frameWorker` function establishes a message-passing loop between a parent process and itself to manage the game state. It receives a `Frame` message containing the current position and keys, calculates the next position using the `move` function which applies the `axis` helper to determine velocity based on key presses, and renders the scene via `renderFrame`. The function then sends the updated `Frame` message back to the parent, effectively creating a recursive loop that polls keys every 32 milliseconds and updates the display until termination.

```sol
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys = message;
  nextKeys = pollKeys keys 32;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

