}
// Once True is returned, the slot is released and the loop exits.
```

The 24-watcher cap is an implementation limit rather than an API rule, and `Os.watchClose` initiates an asynchronous teardown where the caller yields and retries until the function returns True. Only upon returning True is the slot released, allowing descriptor buffers to be freed, synchronization objects to wake, and detached workers to exit. Because abandoned handles are not collected by the system, reusing a handle before the handshake completes risks violating the 24-watcher cap or leaving resources in an inconsistent state.

```sol
// Example of completing the handshake before reuse
while (Os.watchClose(handle) == False) {
    // Yield and retry until teardown finishes
    yield();
}
