The provided test demonstrates how to trigger software interrupts via the CLINT, manage a shared flag to verify execution order, and handle CPU state saving and restoring. To replicate this behavior in Sol, you define a recursive function that writes to the interrupt control register, waits for the completion flag, checks the counter, and decrements the remaining count.

```sol
fire n = 
  if n == 0 then Unit
  else
    flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
    Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
    Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
    waitFor flag;
    xs = [n, n + 1];
    check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
    fire (n - 1);

waitFor flag = 
  if Word.toInt (Mem.readWord flag) == 0 then waitFor flag
  else Unit;

main = 
  CPU.csrWrite 772 (Word.fromInt 8);
  CPU.irqEnable Unit;
  fire 1000;
  CPU.irqSave Unit;
  print "INTERRUPTS RESUME";
```

The `fire` function simulates a sequence of software interrupts by incrementally clearing the interrupt flag and setting the Machine Status Interrupt Pending (MSIP) register. It works by recursively decrementing a counter `n` while performing memory operations: it resets the interrupt flag address to 0, writes 1 to the MSIP address to trigger the pending state, waits for the flag to be cleared by the CPU, and verifies that the system resumes execution at the expected instruction count.

```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

The `fire 1000` expression triggers a software interrupt by writing to the machine interrupt pending status register (MSIP) and then loops 1000 times, checking and clearing the interrupt flag on each iteration until the interrupt is fully processed. It performs no direct print or return value operation; instead, it executes side effects to simulate interrupt handling.

```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1);
```

