FP-RISC prioritizes simple, elegant code with linear vectors and refcounting, whereas Sol explicitly violates this ethos by leaving its core interpreter quadratic while growing acceleration tiers on top. In FP-RISC, this approach ensures maintainability, while in Sol, the quadratic core is masked by optimizations that contradict the "no premature optimization" principle.

```sol
-- Sol example illustrating the quadratic core vs acceleration tiers
-- The core interpreter processes every pair, violating the linear ethos
fn quadratic_interpreter(txn: Transaction) {
    let result = 0;
    for i in 0..txn.len() {
        for j in 0..txn.len() {
            result += txn[i] * txn[j];
        }
    }
    return result;
}

-- Acceleration tiers are layered on top, but the core remains quadratic
fn optimized_interpreter(txn: Transaction) {
    let result = quadratic_interpreter(txn); // Core violation
    return result;
}
```

