The function `serGo`, which is invoked by `pSeries`, will fail because its base case expects the `named` list to be empty to terminate the recursion, but the initial call passes an empty list while the pattern matching logic for processing series items (`s :: r = named`) is unreachable immediately. In FP-RISC, this results in a runtime error or non-termination depending on the specific compiler version and how the empty list pattern is handled before the base case guard is evaluated, as the recursive step attempts to destructure an empty list without first checking the termination condition in the correct order.

```sol
pSeries title named =
  bx = allBounds (map (fn s -> snd2 s) named);
  b = axes bx (svgOpen title (BStr.new Unit));
  b2 = serGo bx named 1 b;
  svgClose (legend (map (fn s -> fst2 s) named) b2).
```

The issue arises because `serGo` is defined with a guard: `serGo bx named i b | named == [] = b`. If the compiler or interpreter does not evaluate the guard before attempting the pattern match `s :: r = named`, it will try to match the empty list against `s :: r`, causing a failure.

The `pSeries` function generates a chart with multiple data series by first calculating the bounding box of all points across all series using `allBounds`, then initializing the SVG context with axes and the chart title. It iterates through the provided series list using `serGo` to draw each series as a polyline with a specific color and append it to the current buffer. Finally, it closes the SVG and appends a legend containing the names of all series. The `serGo` function acts as the recursive iterator: it takes the first series from the list, draws its corresponding polyline using `polyline`, and then recursively calls itself with the remaining series and an incremented index to assign the next color from the fixed-order slot colors.

```sol
pSeries title named =
  bx = allBounds (map (fn s -> snd2 s) named);
  b = axes bx (svgOpen title (BStr.new Unit));
  b2 = serGo bx named 1 b;
  svgClose (legend (map (fn s -> fst2 s) named) b2).

serGo bx named i b =
  s :: r = named; (nm, pts) = s;
  serGo bx r (i + 1) (polyline bx (colAt i) pts b).
```

