When `collect` is applied to a list containing a failing `Result`, the entire operation fails immediately, and the specific error from the first failure is propagated up the chain, discarding any subsequent results. In contrast, the `oks` function filters out all failures, keeping only the values from the successful `Result`s in the list.

```fprisc
// If rs contains an Err, collectGo returns Err e immediately.
// CollectGo: unsafe List (Result a e) -> List a -> Result (List a) e
collectGo rs acc = case rs of
    Nil -> Ok (rev acc Nil)
  | r :: rest -> (case r of Ok x -> collectGo rest (x :: acc) | Err e -> Err e)

// oksGo ignores Err values and continues processing the rest of the list.
// oksGo: unsafe List (Result a e) -> List a -> List a
oksGo rs acc = case rs of
    Nil -> rev acc Nil
  | r :: rest -> (case r of Ok x -> oksGo rest (x :: acc) | Err _ -> oksGo rest acc)
```

