The `sol` language supports interoperability between CSV and JSON formats using the `C` (CSV) and `J` (JSON) modules. To convert records to JSON, map the records to JSON objects using `J.ofRecord` and wrap them in a JSON array with `JArr`. To parse the result back, render the JSON array, parse it with `J.parse`, extract the items, and map over the fields to convert JSON text values into strings while preserving the field names.

```sol
rows = unwrap (C.parse orders);
recs = List.map trim (C.records rows);
asJson = JArr (List.map J.ofRecord recs);
hdr :: body = rows;
back = J.parse (J.render asJson) |>? J.items
  |> mapOk (List.map (fn o -> List.map (fn p -> (k, v) = p; (k, unwrap (J.text v))) (unwrap (J.fields o))));
```

Layouts are syntactic sugar that expands at parse time into empty `Type` signatures and primitive definitions over `Mem`/`Addr`/`Word`, meaning downstream components like inference and ARC see no special layout metadata. The cast between `Addr` and nominal types is implemented as an internal primitive `$cast` which is erased before Core reaches any backend, ensuring the result costs nothing.

```fprisc
// Example of a layout definition expanding to primitives
struct Point {
  x : Int
  y : Int
}

// These are free; they expand to primitive operations over Addr/Word
@Point.at = Addr
@Point.sizeOf = Unit -> Int
```

FP-RISC layouts are syntactic sugar that expands at parse time into empty types, signatures, and ordinary definitions over `Mem`/`Addr`/`Word`. They incur zero runtime cost because downstream components like modules, inference, and ARC are unaware of them, and the only cast between `Addr` and a nominal type is an erased primitive that disappears before Core generation.

```fprisc
// Example of a layout definition that costs nothing at runtime
layout MyStruct {
    x: Word
    y: Word
}
```

