Host adapters are implementations that allow Sol programs to run on different operating systems by adhering to the same contracts. The initial direction is to start with existing POSIX facilities and then implement other hosts against these same contracts, ensuring that shared programs establish agreement on ordinary values and effects across different environments.

```sol
// Example concept: A host adapter interface stub (pseudocode)
// This shows the contract rather than a specific implementation.

interface HostAdapter {
  func init(host: string) -> bool;
  func execute(bytecode: bytes) -> Result;
  func get_value(type: Type) -> Value;
  func set_value(value: Value) -> Result;
}
```

Because the instruction set support is only part of the work; the runtime must also supply required value behavior, transactional services, and other imports. Silently replacing unsupported requirements would violate the contract of the host interface, potentially breaking the program's logic or safety guarantees. The design mandates that any missing or incompatible feature be reported explicitly to ensure shared programs establish agreement on effects and failures across different hosts.

```sol
