When a request from nobody (sender 0) is received by `serveLog`, the system does not reply to it; instead, it only logs a failure message to standard error if the request results in a `Failed` response. This behavior is implemented by checking if the reply type `rt` is 0, and if the result `r` is `Failed`, it prints an error indicating the write failed.

```fprisc
msg = receive self;
(rt, q) = msg;
(m2, r) = step path m q;
_ = (if rt == 0 then (case r of
        Failed why -> _ = Sys.stderr "kvlog: {path}: a posted write failed: {why}\n"; Unit
      | _ -> Unit)
     else _ = send rt r; Unit);
_ = drop msg;
serveLog self path m2;
```

The `serveLog` function continuously receives messages from a sender, extracts the request type (`rt`) and query (`q`), and updates the state map using `step`. If the request type is 0 (indicating a posted write from nobody), it checks for failures and logs an error to standard error; otherwise, it sends the response to the correct receiver. After processing, the function drops the message and recurses to handle the next request.

```sol
serveLog self path m =
  msg = receive self;
  (rt, q) = msg;
  (m2, r) = step path m q;
  _ = (if rt == 0 then (case r of
          Failed why -> Sys.stderr "kvlog: {path}: a posted write failed: {why}\n"
        | _ -> Unit)
       else send rt r);
  _ = drop msg;
  serveLog self path m2
```

The `binCol` function discretizes a floating-point column into integer bin codes by first calculating the range (lo, hi) and bin width (w) of the input data. It then iterates through the column using `binGo`, which recursively processes elements until all are handled. For each element, `binPut` retrieves the value, computes its normalized position within the bin width, and pushes the resulting integer code onto an accumulator vector before recursing.

```sol
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

FP-RISC optimizes chained `Vec.map` operations by fusing the outer pair, allowing all three maps to execute as a single pass, whereas a `Vec.fold` applied after a `Vec.map` prevents fusion because the fold requires visible intermediate results. Consequently, in a chain like `map f3 (map f2 (map f1 h))`, the computation is fused, but in a sequence like `fold (fn a x -> a + x) 0 (map f1 i)`, the map must write its results visibly before the fold can consume them. This distinction ensures correctness when verifying sums or accessing specific elements after transformation.

```fprisc
# Chained maps fuse; single map + fold does not.
h2 = Vec.map f3 (Vec.map f2 (Vec.map f1 h1));
(hs, h3) = Vec.fold (fn a x -> a + x) 0 h2;

# Fold requires visible map results; fusion is disabled.
(is, i2) = Vec.fold (fn a x -> a + x) 0 (Vec.map f1 i1);
(iv, i3) = Vec.get 100 i2;
```

