    struct acb *next; // Linked list pointer stored directly here
};
```

In Tier 1, the implementation cannot allocate memory, so unbounded structures like run queues are achieved intrusively by linking them through fields inside the actor control block (acb) rather than using heap allocation. In contrast, Tier 2 handles memory as the only bound, where unbounded-by-default structures like `Vec` or mailboxes are bounded only when the programmer explicitly declares a limit using `Static n` or `Dynamic n`, never by the implementation itself.

```sol
// Tier 1 example: Intrusive linked queue in the acb (no allocation)
