The file `fprisc/std/store.fpr` defines the `serveShard` function, which implements a key-value store shard capable of handling insertion, deletion, pagination, filtering, and retrieval operations. It manages state by maintaining an entry map, line count, and file path, while ensuring efficient data serialization and disk appending. For instance, when inserting a key-value pair, the function encodes the value, appends the record to the file path, and updates the internal entry map with the new key-value association.

```fprisc
serveShard cfg q st = case st of Sh entries lines path -> (case q of
    SPut x ->
      k = cfg.key x;
      _ = append path (J.render (J.object (("k", J.Integer k) :: ("v", one cfg x) :: Nil)));
      entries2 = M.insert k (keep x) entries;
      lines2 = compactIf cfg path entries2 (lines + 1);
      (Sh entries2 lines2 path, SCountIs (M.size entries2))
    | ...
```

The `serveShard` function processes a `SPut` request by extracting the key and value, appending a JSON representation of the key-value pair to the log path, inserting the new entry into the key-value map, and incrementing the line count. It returns the updated shard state and a count reflecting the new number of entries.

```fprisc
serveShard cfg q st = case st of
    Sh entries lines path ->
      case q of
        SPut x ->
          k = cfg.key x;
          _ = append path (J.render (J.object (("k", J.Integer k) :: ("v", one cfg x) :: Nil)));
          entries2 = M.insert k (keep x) entries;
          lines2 = compactIf cfg path entries2 (lines + 1);
          (Sh entries2 lines2 path, SCountIs (M.size entries2))
        _ -> st
```

The `node` function returns a new balanced tree constructed by combining the left and right subtrees with the given key and value, calculating the new height based on the taller of the two sides plus one. The `balance` function returns a rebalanced tree by either performing a left rotation, a right rotation, or creating a new node, depending on whether the heights of the left or right subtrees exceed the other by more than one.

```fprisc
balance l k v r =
  hl = height l;
  hr = height r;
  case hl > hr + 1 of
    True -> rotRight l k v r
  | False -> (case hr > hl + 1 of True -> rotLeft l k v r | False -> node l k v r).

node l k v r =
  hl = height l;
  hr = height r;
  Node (1 + (case hl > hr of True -> hl | False -> hr)) l k v r.
```

