MVU is the exclusive application framework where every application is defined by a `serve` function containing `init`, `update`, and `view` callbacks, returning a scene or `None`. FPRLive is a remote execution mechanism that pairs with MVU to enable client/server apps, live sessions, and telemetry. While MVU dictates the internal logic and state management of the application, FPRLive handles the network layer to run that application remotely.

```sol
// Local MVU example (returns a scene)
MVU.serve {
  init = { ctx = {} },
  update = { ctx, input = { key = "a" } -> ctx.key = "a" },
  view = { ctx -> Scene2D("Hello", ctx.key) }
}

// Remote MVU + FPRLive example (returns None to indicate remote service)
FPRLive.serve {
  init = { ctx = {} },
  update = { ctx, input = { key = "b" } -> ctx.key = "b" },
  view = { ctx -> None } // Remote service does not render locally
}
```

The design separates immutable content from mutable metadata to enforce the 409 invariant that "bindings are immutable." By storing deduplicated content blobs at SHA256 hashes in the store and managing only the (name, version, hash) bindings in the append-only versions.db, the system ensures that reusing a hash for a new version is impossible without publishing a new binding entry. This prevents accidental overwrites of existing versions while allowing efficient content sharing across different packages and versions.

```sol
// Example of a version binding in the index.db
INSERT INTO versions.db (name, version, hash) VALUES ("mylib", "1.0.0", "abc123...");

// Attempting to rebind the same hash to a new version fails due to the 409 invariant
// INSERT INTO versions.db (name, version, hash) VALUES ("mylib", "1.0.1", "abc123..."); -- ERROR
```

