A warm free list is a state achieved by a recycler after it has acquired enough reusable blocks to handle a steady workload, whereas bigfree is the specific per-pool, exact-fit LIFO recycler tier designed for blocks larger than the bucket ceiling (8 KiB). The distinction lies in the temporal state of the allocator rather than its structural definition; bigfree is the mechanism itself, while being "warm" describes when that mechanism is ready for production use. For example, in `tests/bigfree.fpr`, the system performs three warm-up cycles to build the warm free list before measuring memory usage stability across forty additional iterations.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating the transition to a warm free list in bigfree
// Before warm-up: Reclaiming blocks may cause fragmentation or latency spikes.
