The Base contract requires explicit specification of acquisition, permission, absence, failure, ordering, and resource lifetime to ensure portability across different machines that may have varying environmental inputs, timing, and failure modes. Relying on Unix paths or descriptors ties the implementation to a specific reference host, whereas the contract mandates that implementations provide required interfaces while obeying their specified capability and error semantics, ensuring an unsupported result is only conforming where the language explicitly allows it.

