The test guarantees safety by publishing a `Watch` to the poller before announcing registration, ensuring the owner waits for both announcements before stopping. It then spawns two identical waiter processes, waits for both to complete, and verifies that the poller stop was successful. The test explicitly checks that both waiters are cancelled, confirming that the mechanism prevents new registrations from racing the stop contract even when duplicate waits occur.

```fpr
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

When `n` is 0, the `cycles` function returns `Unit`. When `n` is greater than 0, it starts a poller, spawns two waiter processes to register for a file descriptor, stops the first poller, and then recursively calls itself with `n - 1`. This pattern repeats for the specified number of cycles, effectively exercising the poller's start and stop mechanisms multiple times.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = P.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = P.start Unit;
  _ = P.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

