The `marchSun` function performs a fixed-step ray march toward the sun direction by incrementing coordinates by half the sun vector components (sunX/2, sunY/2, sunZ/2) for up to 40 steps. It first checks if the starting coordinates are negative using `negPos`; if so, it returns `(0, w)`. If the current cell is out of bounds (`inWorld`), it also returns `(0, w)`. Otherwise, it retrieves the neighbor cell via `wGet` and delegates the actual marching logic to `marchAt`.

```sol
marchSun w px py pz 0 = (0, w).
marchSun w px py pz k =
  nx = px + sunX / 2;
  ny = py + sunY / 2;
  nz = pz + sunZ / 2;
  marchAt w nx ny nz (k - 1) (nx / fx) (ny / fx) (nz / fx).
```

