QEMU virt builds install a fatal fallback trap vector that prints diagnostic registers (`mcause`, `mepc`, `mtval`) and exits QEMU with failure, while ARC builds use a named `machineInterrupt` function to install a checked resumable handler ABI. The ARC handler saves integer/floating-point state, uses a dedicated stack, and returns via `mret`, whereas synchronous faults remain fatal in both.

```text
// QEMU virt style (fatal fallback)
fatal_trap =
  print "mcause", "mepc", "mtval";
  exit QEMU failure;

// ARC style (resumable handler)
machineInterrupt =
  save state;
  use dedicated stack;
  mret;
```

The `fly` function implements semi-implicit Euler integration by first calculating the magnitude of the current velocity vector using `Numeric.sqrt`, then deriving the acceleration components `ax` and `ay` based on quadratic drag proportional to the velocity magnitude. It updates the position and velocity using the current accelerations before recursively calling itself with the new state, continuing until the vertical position `y` drops below zero.

```sol
fly x y vx vy | y < 0 = x.
fly x y vx vy =
  v  = Numeric.sqrt (vx * vx + vy * vy);
  ax = 0 - cD * (v * vx);
  ay = 0 - g - cD * (v * vy);
  fly (x + vx * dt) (y + vy * dt) (vx + ax * dt) (vy + ay * dt).
```

Modifying state after a `boundary` causes undefined behavior because the heap pool has been reset, meaning all pointers to temporary values created in the previous turn are now dangling. The `boundary` function explicitly drops the temporary message received (`_ = drop m;`) and returns only the deep-copied state (`fresh`), discarding any reference to the old heap. If code attempts to access or modify data that relied on the old pool, it will crash or produce incorrect results.

```fprisc
boundary me state =
  _ = send me state;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  fresh = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  fresh.

-- Incorrect usage: modifying data that was in the old pool
boundary me state =
  _ = send me state;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  fresh = keep m;
  -- BAD: Attempting to use 'state' or its internal pointers here
  state.field = "modified"; 
  fresh.
```

The file `fprisc/std/actor.fpr` defines the mechanism for managing actor heap boundaries to ensure long-lived actors maintain a consistent size across turns. It implements a `boundary` function that forces a deep copy of the actor's state out of the shared pool, resets the entire heap pool, and then restores the copied state, effectively discarding all temporary allocations from the current turn. To optimize performance for actors with large states, the code also provides a `tidy` function that automatically triggers this boundary only when the heap usage exceeds a specified slack limit relative to the actor's live state.

```fprisc
boundary me state =
  _ = send me state;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  fresh = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  fresh.
```

An actor enforces a heap boundary by sending its current state to itself, resetting the entire heap pool via `Sys.poolReset`, and then receiving the state back as the only surviving object. This ensures that all temporary values created since the last boundary are discarded when the pool is reset.

```fprisc
boundary me state =
  _ = send me state;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  fresh = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  fresh.
```

