The `frame` function constructs a WebSocket frame by first calculating the payload length `n` and then building the header using a specific bit pattern for the opcode and length fields. If the length is less than 126, it uses one byte for the length; if less than 65536, it uses a continuation byte followed by two bytes; otherwise, it uses a continuation byte followed by an 8-byte big-endian representation. The final frame is the concatenation of this head and the payload string.

```fprisc
frame opcode payload =
  n = strlen payload;
  head = (if n < 126 then strcat (chr (128 + opcode)) (chr n)
          else if n < 65536 then strJoin "" (chr (128 + opcode) :: chr 126 :: chr (n / 256) :: chr (band n 255) :: Nil)
          else strJoin "" (chr (128 + opcode) :: chr 127 :: be64 n));
  strcat head payload.
```

