A HAL function containing an unbounded loop or spin will block or hold the hart outside the fuel budget, violating the requirement that such functions must be O(bounded) or yield via the actor machinery. C code does not consume fuel; instead, every C call is treated as a black box with a fixed duration constant that the WCET model must supply per entry point.
```fprisc
// Incorrect: unbounded loop in HAL blocks the hart
hal_error:
    while (true) {
        // No yield, no bounded execution
        spin();
    }
```

The `qos/programs/mods/docsdata.fpr` file documents the calling conventions and resource models for HAL (Host Application Layer) functions in the FP-RISC ecosystem. It specifies that while the machine layer uses a native C ABI, the generated code must manage fuel between safepoints, treating every C call as a black box with a fixed duration determined by the WCET model rather than consuming fuel itself. Consequently, any HAL function that blocks or spins must do so outside the fuel budget, typically by yielding via the actor machinery, to prevent holding the hart indefinitely.

```sol
// Example of a HAL function adhering to the fuel model
// by yielding instead of spinning in an unbounded loop
hal:spawn_task(task_id) {
    // Promote the task_id to ensure ownership rules are followed
    let task_ptr = ref(task_id);

    // Instead of an infinite loop that consumes the hart, yield
    yield(); 

    // Continue processing in the next actor segment
    // This ensures the WCET model can bound the time between safepoints
}
```

The generated code must not spin or block indefinitely within the fuel budget; instead, it must yield control via the actor machinery (e.g., sending a message or spawning an actor) before the fuel expires. This ensures cooperative preemption, where the hart switches actors only at safepoints, preventing the actor from holding the hart during unbounded HAL calls.

```sol
fn safe_hal_wrapper(actor: Actor, data: &Ref) -> Result {
    // Check fuel before calling HAL
    if !fuel_available() {
        // Yield via mailbox pattern to release the hart
        actor.send(Message {
            payload: data,
            action: Action::Process,
        });
        return Ok(());
    }

    // Call HAL; callee is a black box with a fixed WCET
    let result = unsafe { hal_operation(data) };

    // Promote any returned references to actor slab ownership
    result.map(|r| r.promote(actor))
}
```

C code does not consume fuel; instead, every C call is treated as a black box with a fixed duration determined by the WCET model. Consequently, if a HAL function contains an unbounded loop that blocks or spins, it executes outside the fuel budget while holding the hart, violating the requirement that HAL functions must be bounded or yield via the actor machinery.

```sol
// Example: Correct pattern using actor yield
hal:spawn_actor(1, msg, actor_fn)
// HAL function must not spin here; it yields control to the mailbox.
```

Such a call consumes no fuel itself, but the duration is a constant black box; however, if the HAL function blocks or spins, it does so outside the fuel budget and holds the hart, violating the WCET model. A HAL function must be O(bounded) or yield via the actor machinery (e.g., using the syscall mailbox pattern) rather than spinning.

```fprc
