```

The `ticker` function invokes `A.askSure self reg (TickDue ms key)`. In the QOS operating system, the `askSure` function (part of the Ask API) performs an existence check on the requested register. If the provided `reg` does not exist, `askSure` raises a runtime exception (specifically a `NotRegistered` or similar register access error) before the ticker's logic can proceed, causing the ticker to terminate abruptly rather than executing its sleep and check cycle.

```fprisc
ticker reg ms key self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs (ms * 1000);
  r = A.askSure self reg (TickDue ms key); -- Error if reg does not exist
  again = (case r of Wanted True -> True | _ -> False);
  _ = drop r;
  (if again then ticker reg ms key self else Unit).
```

The file `fprisc/std/live.fpr` defines the core logic for managing WebSocket connections and real-time data subscriptions within the QOS operating system. It implements mechanisms to register and track active subscribers for specific message keys, ensuring that only relevant clients receive updates via a ticker loop that polls for new data. The code demonstrates how to spawn ticker tasks for each subscribed message key, handle incoming HTTP requests to establish sessions, and manage the lifecycle of connections by filtering messages based on user subscriptions.

```fprisc
ticker reg ms key self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs (ms * 1000);
  r = A.askSure self reg (TickDue ms key);
  again = (case r of Wanted True -> True | _ -> False);
  _ = drop r;
  (if again then ticker reg ms key self else Unit).
```

