```

According to the rules, support units are the only ones in the rear that generate GEN, and retreated combat units do not generate anything. Furthermore, a retreated combat unit cannot attack from the rear; it can only attack an enemy rear unit if the attacker's forward slot is empty or if a lane exists, but the retreated unit itself remains in the rear and cannot initiate an attack while there. A damaged unit must retreat, then be repaired, and finally recalled or advanced to regain full utility, often costing multiple turns.

```sol
// Example logic illustrating that a retreated unit cannot attack from the rear
if (unit.rear) {
    if (unit.retrofitted) { // Retreated units are not support units
        unit.can_generate = false
        unit.can_attack_from_rear = false
    }
}
```

Units called from the HQ enter the rear zone and generate GEN only starting the turn after they enter, whereas a forward unit that retreats to the rear loses its ability to generate entirely. This distinction exists because HQ-called units are treated as new arrivals, while retreated units are damaged combatants that forfeit their generation capability.

```sol
