To calculate the dimensions of a `Box` widget, you call the `measure` function with the box type, its padding and gap properties, and a list of its children. The function first computes the width and height of each child, sums the along-axis dimensions while taking the maximum of the cross-axis dimensions, and adds the appropriate gaps and padding based on whether the box is a row or a column.

```fprisc
measure (Box a kids) =
  p = padOf a; g = gapOf a;
  (mw, mh, n) = msum (isRow a) kids;
  gaps = case n > 1 of True -> g * (n - 1) | False -> 0;
  inner = case isRow a of
    True -> (mw + gaps, mh)
  | False -> (mw, mh + gaps);
  (iw, ih) = inner;
  sized a (iw + 2 * p, ih + 2 * p).
```

The `measure` function for a `Box` first calculates the dimensions of each child using `measure`, then aggregates these results into a single bounding box. The `msum` function performs a single traversal to sum the widths along the row axis, find the maximum height across all children, and count the total number of items. Finally, `measure` applies the calculated inner dimensions, adds the gaps between children based on the count, and adds the padding to produce the final size.

```sol
Box a kids =
  p = padOf a; g = gapOf a;
  (mw, mh, n) = msum row kids;
  gaps = case n > 1 of True -> g * (n - 1) | False -> 0;
  inner = case isRow a of
    True -> (mw + gaps, mh)
  | False -> (mw, mh + gaps);
  sized a (inner.1 + 2 * p, inner.2 + 2 * p).
```

