The `Vec.filter` operation performs eager compaction in place, meaning it shrinks the vector length without allocating new heap memory. During this process, two cursors slide within the same vector to move kept elements forward, ensuring the result is a dense, mask-free vector. This behavior is demonstrated by the `fillI` and `keepEven` functions, where `Vec.filter` produces a compacted vector of length 500 from an initial size of 1000 with negligible heap usage increase.

```fprisc
keepEven x = x - (x / 2) * 2 == 0.
a1 = fillI (Vec.new Unit) 1 1000;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a2 = Vec.filter keepEven a1;
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
```

