The server responds to invalid frames with specific RFC close codes while continuing to serve other clients. For binary data or non-text opcodes, it sends code 1003; for fragmented frames, continuation frames, set RSV bits, or unmasked frames, it sends code 1002; for frames exceeding 4096 bytes, it sends code 1009; for client-initiated closes, it sends code 1000; and for server shutdowns (MQuit), it sends code 1001 to all peers.
```fprisc
# Example server logic for frame validation
if (opcode == BINARY) {
    close(1003)
} else if (is_fragmented() or rsv_set() or unmasked()) {
    close(1002)
} else if (frame_size > 4096) {
    close(1009)
} else if (client_close) {
    close(1000)
} else if (mquit) {
    close(1001)
}
```

The `doCmd` function is called recursively by `doCmds` to process a list of commands (`cs`) starting with a query (`q`). When the command `c` is `MLog`, `doCmd` invokes the `logGo` function, passing the remaining command list, the current query, and the string to log. The `logGo` function then executes `Sys.logAt` with the string and continues processing the rest of the command list.
```fprisc
doCmd r q c = case c of
    MLog s -> logGo r q s;
    ...
logGo r q s =
    _ = Sys.logAt 0 s;
    doCmds r q;
```

