The compiler fuses multiple sequential `Vec.map` operations into a single pass when possible, reducing heap allocations and improving cache locality. This optimization is demonstrated by the `fused-scalar-nested` test, where `Vec.map shiftDn (Vec.map scaleUp a1)` produces the same result as a single fused operation, confirming that intermediate vectors are not materialized separately. Additionally, the compiler can optimize these operations to be in-place, as verified by the `fused-scalar-inplace` check, which ensures that the heap usage difference (`h1 - h0`) remains below 4096 bytes despite processing 4000 elements.

```fprisc
a1 = fillI (Vec.new Unit) 1 4000;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a2 = Vec.map shiftDn (Vec.map scaleUp a1);
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
(s1, a3) = sumI a2;
_ = Vec.free a3;
u0 = verdict "fused-scalar-nested" (s1 == refI 1 4000);
u1 = verdict "fused-scalar-inplace" (h1 - h0 < 4096);
```

