let w = Word.fromInt(-1)
let result = w.and Word.fromInt(0xFF)
```

The `Word` type is an opaque 64-bit type that sign-extends negative `Int` values when converted via `Word.fromInt`. All bitwise and arithmetic operations on `Word` (such as `and`, `or`, `xor`, `add`, `sub`, and shifts) perform modulo 2^64 arithmetic.

```sol
let w = Word.fromInt(-1);
let result = w.and(w);
-- result is a Word with all 64 bits set to 1
```

The `Word.fromInt` operation converts an `Int` to a `Word` by sign-extending the 63 payload bits of the integer to fill the full 64-bit width of the `Word`. This ensures that negative numbers are preserved correctly during conversion.

```sol
let x = Word.fromInt(-1)
// x is now a 64-bit Word with all bits set to 1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)
```

