The FP-RISC fold dualizer is restricted to scalar accumulators; it explicitly declines record-accumulator folds. To fix this, you must restructure your fold to use a scalar accumulator (e.g., summing fields into separate variables) rather than accumulating the entire record state in a single variable.

Example:
Incorrect (declines):
  raStep a p = {sx = a.sx + p.x, sy = a.sy + p.y};
  Vec.fold raStep {sx = 0, sy = 0} v;

Correct (scalar accumulator):
  raStep a p = (a.sx + p.x, a.sy + p.y);
  Vec.fold raStep (0, 0) v;

When `Vec.fold` is applied to a named function like `sxy`, the fold dualizer accepts the zero-test body and generates a shape-checked, in-place SoA column loop that avoids boxing and per-row apply, provided the function body contains a zero-test. Conversely, a record-accumulator fold like `raStep` still declines because fold duals are strictly scalar-accumulator; they do not support record-accumulating steps. Consequently, `sxy` executes efficiently with minimal heap usage, while `raStep` falls back to the generic tier, which is correct but less optimized.

```fpr
sxy a p = a + p.x * p.y.
raStep a p = {sx = a.sx + p.x, sy = a.sy + p.y}.

main =
  v = Vec.new Unit;
  (s1, v2) = Vec.fold sxy 0 v;
  (r, v3) = Vec.fold raStep {sx = 0, sy = 0} v2;
  "sxy: in-place, raStep: generic tier".
```

The `driver` function is designed to be nearly allocation-free by spinning on the `clint` mtime register to avoid frequent memory pool bumps, and it explicitly drops the roots of the previous frame before processing the new one. It forwards the request root (`Rq`) to the worker, which increments it on send, ensuring that dropping the root here and there balances the reference count.

```sol
driver me mt tP n rB rM rS lat =
  t = waitTick mt tP;
  (Bk wv lm ar ag ab fr fg fb) = rB;
  (Vw q0 yaw pit sw sh lv) = rS;
  wk = spawn frameWorker;
  _ = send wk (me, wv, lm);
  _ = send wk (ar, ag, ab);
  _ = send wk (fr, fg, fb);
  _ = send wk rM;
  _ = send wk (St yaw pit n sw sh lat lv);
  _ = drop rB; _ = drop rM; _ = drop rS;  -- Drop last frame's roots
  r1 = receive me;
  r2 = receive me;
  r3 = receive me;
  driveNext me mt t n r3 r2 r1 (read mt - t).
```

The driver measures latency by calculating the difference between the current `mtime` register value and the timestamp (`tP`) when the worker was spawned. This calculated value, stored in the variable `lat`, is included in the message sent to the worker alongside the frame state and timing parameters. The worker then receives this `lat` value to record the performance metric for the subsequent frame.

```sol
lat = read mt - t;
...
send wk (St yaw pit n sw sh lat lv);
```

