The `tcStep` function processes a clause by first extracting its head instruction and body, then allocating new variables and instantiating the head to generate a memory address. It attempts to unify this address with the current goal stack; if unification fails, execution stops. Upon successful unification, the function recursively invokes `tcBody` to execute the instruction body while decrementing the fuel counter by one. Finally, it undoes variable allocations from the previous step and recurses with the updated state.

```sol
(m2, haddr) = inst hd vbase0 m1;
(m3, ok) = unify haddr g m2;
(m4, fuel2, sols2) = tcBody db syms m3 ok body vbase0 gs (fuel - 1) qvars sols;
```

The `splice` function takes two lists of strings and returns a single string describing the edit operation. It first finds the longest common prefix (`prefix`) and the longest common suffix (`suffix`) of the two lists. The number of characters to remove is calculated as the length of the first list minus the length of the suffix. The string to insert is derived by taking the portion of the second list that lies between the prefix and the suffix. The function formats this information into a string with the prefix, the count of removed characters, and the rendered inserted strings.

```fprisc
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

The `splice` function first determines the longest common prefix between the old and new strings using the `common` function. It then calculates the number of characters to remove by finding the length of the remaining part of the old string after the prefix. Similarly, it calculates the number of characters to insert by finding the length of the remaining part of the new string after the prefix, excluding the common suffix found by reversing the remainder of the old string and comparing it to the remainder of the new string. The function then constructs the result by concatenating the prefix, the removed portion (rendered as a JSON array of strings), and the inserted portion.

```fprisc
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

To find the edit distance, use the `splice` function, which calculates the common prefix, the number of removed elements, and the inserted elements. To generate a detailed delta list, use the `delta` function, which iterates through the new list and compares each element to the corresponding element in the old list, recording changes as "i:new" pairs.

Here is a complete example in FP-RISC:

```fprisc
old = ["a", "b", "c"];
new = ["a", "x", "c"];
result = splice old new;
deltaList = delta old new 0 Nil;
```

The `splice` function returns a string describing the transformation, while `deltaList` returns a list of strings representing only the differences, such as `["1:x"]`.

The system uses a last-unit race condition where exactly one session commits the sale while the other receives a rejection toast. This ensures data consistency without requiring manual intervention. In Sol, this is handled implicitly by the MVU state machine; the conflicting transaction is rejected by the core logic rather than the application code needing to detect the race.

```sol
// Example of a checkout function that relies on the core's race handling
checkout(item-id) {
  let session = this.session;
  if (session.last-item == item-id) {
    // The core will either commit this or reject it if another session won the race
    return session.commit();
  }
  return "No stock available";
