The `cycles` function takes a file descriptor, a self-identifier, and a cycle count as arguments. It recursively decrements the counter, starting a poller with `P.start`, executing the `one` function to register waiters, stopping the poller with `P.stop`, and then recursing until the count reaches zero.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = P.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = P.start Unit;
  _ = P.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

The test guarantees safety by publishing a Watch before announcing registration, causing the owner to wait for both announcements before stopping. This prevents new registrations from racing the stop contract. The code demonstrates this by spawning two waiters (`a` and `b`) after starting the poller, receiving their acknowledgments, and then calling `P.stop p`. Finally, it checks that both waiters were cancelled, ensuring no registration occurred after the stop signal was sent.

```fpr
waiter p fd boss me = case p of P.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

To prevent race conditions, the poller owner must publish a Watch before announcing registration, and the owner must wait for both the Watch announcement and the registration announcement before stopping the poller. This ensures the stop contract is respected. The implementation spawns two waiters, sends a Watch to the poller, then sends the registration to the boss. Only after receiving both confirmations does the code call `P.stop p`. If a waiter is cancelled prematurely, the system checks for this via `P.Cancelled` signals and reports an error if the race occurred.

```fprisc
waiter p fd boss me = case p of P.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

To prevent race conditions during poller shutdown, the program must publish a Watch to the poller before announcing its registration, and the owner must wait for both announcements before issuing the stop command. This ensures no new registration races the stop contract. The implementation spawns multiple waiter threads, sends them a Watch, receives confirmation from each, and only then calls `P.stop`. The code then verifies that all waiters received a cancellation signal, confirming safe shutdown.

```fprisc
waiter p fd boss me =
  _ = need (send p (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.

one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

The training data is generated recursively by `mkData`, which creates a sample with two random inputs in the range (-1, 1) and a target output equal to their product, then recursively appends the remaining samples. To prepare the data for the network, the `augRows` function is applied to the input vectors, adding a constant column of 1s to the hidden layer activations.

```sol
mkData s n | n <= 0 = ([], []).
mkData s n =
  s1 = Rand.next s; s2 = Rand.next4 s1;
  x1 = Rand.unit s1; x2 = Rand.unit s2;
  (rows, ys) = mkData (Rand.next4 s2) (n - 1);
  ([x1, x2, Numeric.inexact 1] :: rows, x1 * x2 :: ys).

augRows rss = map (fn r -> r + [Numeric.inexact 1]) rss.
```

The `pretty` function uses a helper `prettyAt` to recursively traverse JSON structures, adding two spaces of indentation for nested arrays and objects. Scalars and empty containers are rendered inline without newlines or indentation.

