To measure a direct service round-trip latency, an application reads the CLINT timer register (address 49144) before sending a request, then reads it again after receiving the response. The difference between these timestamps represents the round-trip time. The `pollRt` function automates this process for a loop of `k` iterations, accumulating the total time spent in the service loop by subtracting the timer value before sending from the value after receiving, and adding the result to a running statistic.

```sol
pollRt me inp mt k st | k == 0 = st.
pollRt me inp mt k st =
  t0 = read mt;
  _ = send inp (me, Poll 0);
  r = receiveFrom me inp;
  t1 = read mt;
  _ = drop r;
  pollRt me inp mt (k - 1) (statAdd st (t1 - t0)).
