The simulation uses a recursive Euler-Cromer integrator that advances time by 0.05 units per call, updating velocity based on the force equation $v_{new} = v + (-4x - 0.3v) \times 0.05$ and position based on the new velocity $x_{new} = x + v_{new} \times 0.05$. The recursion terminates when the measure $k$ reaches zero, collecting pairs of time and state into lists. This approach relies entirely on pure arithmetic operations, avoiding any trigonometric functions.

```sol
spring k t x v | k <= 0 = ([], []).
spring k t x v =
  v2 = v + (0 - 4) * x * 0.05 - 0.3 * v * 0.05;
  x2 = x + v2 * 0.05;
  (pxs, pvs) = spring (k - 1) (t + 0.05) x2 v2;
  ((t, x) :: pxs, (t, v) :: pvs).
```

You can generate the animation by defining a recursive Sol function that implements an Euler-Cromer integrator for the damped spring equation $x'' = -4x - 0.3v$. The function iterates the state $(x, v)$ over time steps, accumulating position and velocity data into lists, which are then passed to the plotting backend. Running the script with `fpr sol plotdemo.sol` produces the `plot-spring.svg` file containing the rendered chart.

