The `alloc` function first validates the requested size against a 63-bit limit to prevent overflow, then calculates the required size by adding an 80-byte alignment padding and ensuring a minimum of 96 bytes. If the allocation fails, the function returns an address to an error label.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function requests a specific number of bytes, aligns them to a 64-byte boundary, and adds a 64-byte header overhead to determine the total block size. It enforces a strict upper limit of 96 bytes; if the requested size plus overhead exceeds this limit, the function raises an error. The implementation uses the `firstFit` strategy to locate the first suitable block in the free list that can accommodate the required size.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function enforces safety by checking the requested byte size against overflow thresholds (bits 62 and 63) and aligns the size to a minimum of 80 bytes, adjusting to 96 bytes if the calculated size equals the minimum. It manages fragmentation through a first-fit strategy that searches the free list starting from the head block (`headB Unit`), splitting a block only if the remaining space is at least 96 bytes. Once a suitable block is found, the `claim` function marks it as used, initializes its metadata fields (such as size, capacity, and reference count), and zeroes the payload before returning the address.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

