The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if the terms are currently unifiable but have been explicitly undone, effectively checking for inequality by attempting unification and then restoring the original state if successful. It first marks the initial environment, attempts to unify the two terms, and if unification succeeds, it undoes the bindings to return the original state; if unification fails, it proceeds to solve the goal.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if and only if the two terms are not unifiable at the moment of the call. It achieves this by first recording the current variable bindings using `marks`, attempting to unify the terms, and then undoing those bindings via `undoTo`. If the unification fails, the predicate proceeds to solve the goal with the modified state; otherwise, it returns the state after the attempted unification and binding undo.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

