The parser requires that any `case` expression located within a non-final arm of another `case` must be parenthesized. Without parentheses, the arms following the inner `case` incorrectly attach to the inner expression, leaving the outer `case` non-exhaustive or causing an unreachable arm in the inner one. This structural requirement is enforced at compile time.

```fprisc
// Correct: inner case is parenthesized
x = case y of
  A -> case (z) of
    B -> 1
    _ -> 2
  _ -> 3

// Incorrect: inner case lacks parentheses
// x = case y of
//   A -> case z of
//     B -> 1
//     _ -> 2
