When hardware stack guards are disabled, FP-RISC code checks its own stack window and grows it, but C code that overruns an actor stack corrupts the block below, which happens silently. This occurs because the P4's PMP or debug watchpoint tools are not wired up in the absence of an MMU.

```sol
// Example of unsafe C behavior in FP-RISC without hardware guards
proc unsafe_c_overflow() {
    let stack_ptr = actor_stack_ptr;
    // C code writes past the allocated actor stack
    *stack_ptr = 0xDEADBEEF; 
    // FP-RISC runtime detects the FP-RISC stack window violation and grows it,
    // but the C overflow corrupts adjacent memory silently.
}
```

Since there is no MMU and hardware protections like PMP or debug watchpoints are not wired up, the `hal_stack_guard` function in `hal.c` is implemented as a no-op. Consequently, FP-RISC code checks its own stack window and grows it dynamically, but C code that overruns an actor stack will silently corrupt the memory block below it. To mitigate this, developers must ensure C code respects the FP-RISC-managed stack boundaries.

```fp-risc
// Example of FP-RISC checking its own stack window
actor my_actor() {
    stack_check(); // Checks if the stack is still within bounds
    // If this returns false, the stack has grown beyond the limit
    // and FP-RISC may handle the overflow or terminate the actor.
}
```

