The file `fprisc/std/poller.fpr` defines the `stop` operation for a Poller, which is used to gracefully terminate an existing wait. The owner of the Poller must ensure that all registration sends have completed and that no new waits or concurrent stops occur before invoking this function. Upon execution, the function sends a Stop message to the underlying Poller, waits for an acknowledgment, discards the response, and returns a Unit value.

```fpr
stop p = case p of Poller a ->
  me = myself 0;
  if A.sendSure me a (Stop me) then
    m = receiveFrom me a;
    _ = drop m;
    Unit
  else Unit.
```

The `stop` operation ensures all registration sends are complete by using `sendSure` to guarantee delivery, then discards any resulting cancellation message via `receiveFrom`. The implementation creates a temporary channel, sends a `Stop` message, and ignores the response.

```fprisc
stop p = case p of Poller a ->
  me = myself 0;
  if A.sendSure me a (Stop me) then
    m = receiveFrom me a;
    _ = drop m;
    Unit
  else Unit.
```

The `stop` function ensures all registration sends have completed and prevents new waits or concurrent stops before returning. It works by creating a new message channel `me`, attempting to send a `Stop` message to the Poller via `sendSure`, and then receiving a corresponding `Stop` message from the Poller to confirm the operation. Upon successful receipt, the message is discarded and the function returns `Unit`.

```fpr
stop p = case p of Poller a ->
  me = myself 0;
  if A.sendSure me a (Stop me) then
    m = receiveFrom me a;
    _ = drop m;
    Unit
  else Unit.
```

