```

A `Proc.runNow` process executes immediately before any queued `Proc.afterCommit` effects within the same transaction. This occurs because queued effects are only processed at the commit phase, whereas `Proc.runNow` attempts to run synchronously but refuses to proceed if any effects are still pending in the queue. The `Proc.runNow` function returns a reference indicating the current state of the queue.
```sol
u = Proc.afterCommit (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf queued"] "" [] "" 2000);
r = Proc.runNow (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf ranNow"] "" [] "" 2000);
```
In this example, the process outputting "ranNow" will appear in the log before the one outputting "queued".

