```fpr
  _ = T.after svc 40;
  _ = T.after svc 10;
  g1 = receiveFrom me svc;
  g2 = receiveFrom me svc;
```

A Sol program handles this by scheduling tasks with `T.after` to create pending deadlines, receiving them via `receiveFrom`, and using `isFire` to check their status. The code schedules a task 40 units and another 10 units later, receives both, reads the clock, and uses a boolean expression to verify if both tasks fired. If the clock difference indicates they fired within the expected window, it returns "ok"; otherwise, it reports the status.

```fprisc
_ = T.after svc 40;
_ = T.after svc 10;
g1 = receiveFrom me svc;
g2 = receiveFrom me svc;
t2 = read clk;
ook = case and2 (isFire g1) (isFire g2) of
    True -> (case t2 - t0 >= 900000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "EARLY")
  | False -> "WRONG";
```

QOS loaders avoid ELF machinery to eliminate relocation, dynamic linking, and symbol resolution at load time. Instead, they consume two-byte ranges, an entry, and a W^X boundary, reconstructing loads by parsing headers designed for a different purpose. This approach allows QAR2 to state these five numbers directly, enabling the archive parser to use the system's existing tokenizer and fail with named refusals such as "image outside the window" rather than encountering malformed-header surprises.

```sol
// Example of a loader utilizing the W^X boundary rule
load_segment {
    start: 0x400000
    end:   0x4000FF
    w_x:   true  // Enforces write-execute exclusion at this boundary
}
```

