The `close` keyword explicitly consumes a linear file handle, ensuring that the resource is properly released and the file is committed to disk. In Sol, every operation involving a linear handle rebinds it, so forgetting to close a handle results in a compile error. This discipline prevents resource leaks and guarantees transactional integrity.

```sol
h = open @/tmp/sol-demo.txt;
h2 = writeAll h "written transactionally\n";
close h2;
```

Forgetting to close a linear handle results in a compile error because the language enforces strict linear discipline where every operation rebinds the handle and `close` consumes it. This ensures that resources are never left dangling.
```sol
> h = open @/tmp/sol-demo.txt;
  h2 = writeAll h "written transactionally\n";
  // close h2.  -- Compile error: handle 'h2' not closed.
```

