The ARC table initially uses a fixed 1024-slot array to prevent panics when full. It transitions to a buddy block system whenever the number of live and tombstone slots exceeds 70% of capacity. This growth mechanism not only expands storage but also compacts tombstones, optimizing memory usage for shared values.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating ARC growth logic
if (live_count + tombstone_count) > (current_size * 0.7) {
    grow_arc_table(); // Triggers rehash into a buddy block of double size
}
```

The ARC table starts with a fixed size of 1024 slots and automatically rehashes into a buddy block of twice the size whenever the count of live plus tombstone slots passes 70%. This growth occurs under the ARC lock on the insert path, before the probe, and the high-water mark and growth count are maintained for tracking.

```fprisc
# Example demonstrating ARC growth behavior
let arc = create_arc()
# Sending enough shared tuples to trigger the 70% threshold
for i in range(3000) {
    send_shared_tuple(arc, tuple(i))
}
# The ARC table has now rehashed to double its size
```

The ARC table, which counts shared values, begins as a fixed 1024-slot array and dynamically rehashes into a buddy block of twice the size when live plus tombstone slots exceed 70%, a process protected by the ARC lock. In contrast, an actor handle is an immortal ACB with no allocation preheader; when sent as a message, it is shared raw without being counted in the ARC table, as the system no longer reads the word preceding the handle.
