The `again` function implements a nullary tail-recursive loop that uses an explicit memory address as a termination counter, ensuring the loop runs exactly 10,000 times without relying on implicit stack frames for recursion depth. It initializes a 32-bit word at a specific memory address with the value 10,000, then repeatedly reads the counter, decrements it, writes the new value back to the same address, and branches back to itself. The loop terminates only when the counter reaches zero, at which point the `Unit` case is executed.

```fpr
again = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
n = Word.toInt (Mem.read32 p); 
case n == 0 of True -> Unit | False -> 
  _ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt (n - 1)); 
again.
```

