The `apply` function in `livejs.fpr` processes server-to-client commands by checking for navigation, events, or shape updates. If the response contains a page shape (`res.p` set to 2 or 3), it calculates the new state using `splicedAll` for full history or `spliced` for incremental updates, validates the view shape consistency, updates the global state `S`, and calls `build()`.

```sol
function handle_live_shape(res) \{
    if (res.p !== 2 && res.p !== 3) throw new Error('unsupported live protocol');
    var next = res.p === 3 ? \{s: splicedAll(S.s, res.s), d: splicedAll(S.d, res.d)\}
                           : \{s: spliced(S.s, res.s), d: spliced(S.d, res.d)\};
    if (next.s.length !== next.d.length + 1) throw new Error('invalid view shape');
    S = next;
    build();
}
```

The `apply` function handles server-to-client commands by routing them to specific actions, including updating the internal state `S` when the `p` property is defined. If `p` is 2 or 3, it constructs a new state object by splicing the current state arrays (`S.s` and `S.d`) with the incoming data (`res.s` and `res.d`), validates that the new state's slot count matches the data count plus one, updates `S`, and triggers a rebuild.

```fpr
if (res.p !== 2 && res.p !== 3) throw new Error('unsupported live protocol');
var next = res.p === 3 ? {s: splicedAll(S.s, res.s), d: splicedAll(S.d, res.d)}
                       : {s: spliced(S.s, res.s), d: spliced(S.d, res.d)};
if (next.s.length !== next.d.length + 1) throw new Error('invalid view shape');
S = next;
build();
```

