The `main` function in `timerroute.fpr` demonstrates how to spawn a timer actor, establish a capability-gated write to the `/services/timer` path, and handle the response. It uses `spawn TM.actor` to create the timer service, constructs a capability set including the timer actor, and executes `Svc.write caps "/services/timer" (IInt duration)` to schedule the fire. The result is captured in the variable `wok`, which holds the string "ok" if the write succeeds or an error string otherwise.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  tsvc = spawn TM.actor;
  granted = ("/services/timer", "rw") :: Nil;
  caps = Caps con me clk granted "timerroute" { has = False, act = 0, uart = 0, timer = tsvc };
  r = Svc.write caps "/services/timer" (IInt 30);
  wok = case r of IUnit -> "ok" | e -> "ERR";
  wok
```

The `TM.actor` spawns a timer service instance that listens for fire requests. When the main program writes an integer value (30 milliseconds) to the `/services/timer` service, the timer service processes this request, waits for the specified duration, and then sends a `TFire` message directly to the mailbox of the caller (the main program). This mechanism moves the caller from the backlog into the ready zone under the scheduler's admission bounds.

