The file `/etc/.sol-lock` is a lock file created in the directory of the target path to acquire a write-lock, which is required for every path touched during a transaction commit. If a non-root user attempts to read `/etc/hostname`, the system must lock the `/etc` directory to commit the transaction; however, because the user lacks permission to create a file in `/etc`, the lock acquisition fails. This failure is indistinguishable from a lock contention error, causing the script to enter a spin loop for 5 seconds before dying with a "could not lock" message, even though the read operation itself was not blocked by another process.

```sol
// Example of the lock acquisition logic that causes the failure
acquire("/etc/.sol-lock")
// If this throws EACCES (due to missing directory creation permission),
// the code treats it as contention and spins.
```

A TCP server is implemented by first listening on a specified address and port using `Tcp.listen`, retrieving the port number with `Tcp.port`, and spawning a server thread that calls `Tcp.serve` with a custom handler function. The handler, `echoGo`, reads lines from the connection stream; if a line is not "bye", it writes the uppercase version of that line back to the client using `Stream.write` and recursively processes the next line. If the line is "bye", it writes "goodbye\n" and terminates the connection.

```fprisc
Tcp = use "std/tcp";
Stream = use "std/stream";

echoGo r conn = case Stream.readLine r of (res, r2) ->
  (case res of
      Err _ -> Unit
    | Ok line -> (case line == "bye" of
          True -> _ = Stream.write "goodbye\n" conn; Unit
        | False -> _ = Stream.write "{String.toUpper line}\n" conn; echoGo r2 conn)).

main =
  l = (case Tcp.listen "127.0.0.1" 0 of Ok x -> x | Err e -> error e);
  port = Tcp.port l;
  _ = spawn (fn self -> send self (Tcp.serve l echoGo));
```

