The logic handles a `FServeFront` request by calculating the shard index from the key, retrieving or spawning the shard, executing the operation via `A.askSure`, updating the count map, and sending a final response. Here is the Sol implementation for the specific case where the shard exists:

```sol
serveFront self from q st = case st of
    Fs cfg shards counts ->
      case q of
        FPut x ->
          idx = (cfg.key x) / cfg.span;
          (sh, shards2) = (case M.get idx shards of
              O.Some s -> (s, shards)
            | O.None -> s = spawnShard cfg idx; (s, M.insert idx s shards));
          r = A.askSure self sh (SPut x);
          n = (case r of SCountIs c -> c | _ -> 0);
          _ = drop r;
          counts2 = M.insert idx n counts;
          _ = A.sendSure self from (FDone (total counts2));
          Fs cfg shards2 counts2
```

The `serveFront` function manages the front-end of a distributed store by routing incoming operations to the correct shard based on a key's index. When a `FPut` operation arrives, it calculates the target shard index by dividing the key by the configuration span, retrieves or spawns the corresponding shard, and then asks that shard to perform the put. After receiving a confirmation of the count from the shard, it updates the local shard counts map and sends a final done signal back to the client.

```fpr
idx = (cfg.key x) / cfg.span;
(sh, shards2) = (case M.get idx shards of
    O.Some s -> (s, shards)
  | O.None -> s = spawnShard cfg idx; (s, M.insert idx s shards));
r = A.askSure self sh (SPut x);
n = (case r of SCountIs c -> c | _ -> 0);
counts2 = M.insert idx n counts;
```

