The scheduler uses a deterministic aged tier that admits the oldest ready actor if its wait time exceeds `tau` admissions, followed by a default tier that performs a single-pass weighted reservoir selection. This logic is implemented in `runtime/core/actors.c` using constants defined at the top of the scheduler section for WCET calculation.

```sol
// Example illustrating the admission check logic
// Note: Actual implementation is in runtime/core/actors.c
if (g_adm - ready_at > g_tau) {
    // Aged tier: admit oldest actor deterministically
    admit_actor(oldest_ready_actor);
} else {
    // Default tier: weighted random selection via LCG
    admit_actor(weighted_reservoir_sample);
}
```

The scheduler maintains a per-hart backlog of ready actors and uses a dual-tier admission mechanism. The first tier is an aged tier that deterministically selects the oldest actor if it has waited longer than a tunable threshold (`tau`), ensuring fairness for long-pending tasks. The second tier is a default tier that uses a weighted reservoir sampling algorithm to select actors based on their assigned weights (`acb->weight`), driven by a linear congruential generator seeded with the hart ID for reproducibility.

```fpr
// Example: Pseudocode representation of the admission logic within runtime/core/actors.c
if (g_adm - ready_at > g_tau) {
    // Aged tier: Deterministic oldest-first selection
    admit_oldest_backlog_actor();
} else {
    // Default tier: Weighted random selection via LCG
    seed = hart_id;
    candidate = weighted_reservoir_sample(acb->weight, seed);
    admit(candidate);
}
```

These primitives preserve other `mstatus` bits and do not disable non-maskable events because they are designed to be manual interrupt management tools rather than automatic context protectors. They require the caller to explicitly configure interrupt sources and handlers before enabling them. Furthermore, restoring interrupts can deliver a pending interrupt before the call returns, necessitating manual control over the critical section boundaries.

```sol
let token = CPU.irqSave()
let val = CPU.csrWrite(MSTATUS, MIE | token)
let result = CPU.csrRead(MSTATUS)
CPU.irqRestore(token)
```

To save the current MIE bit, call `CPU.irqSave`, which clears the machine interrupt enable and returns the previous value (0 or 8). To restore it, call `CPU.irqRestore` with the saved integer token. Note that `CPU.irqRestore` only accepts the saved MIE value; any other integer will be rejected. Additionally, these functions do not automatically disable non-maskable events, configure individual interrupt sources, or create a lexical critical section.

```sol
let mie_val = CPU.irqSave()
// ... critical section ...
CPU.irqRestore(mie_val)
```

Unlike automatic lexical critical sections, `CPU.irqSave` and `CPU.irqRestore` do not create a lexical critical section automatically. They only manipulate the machine interrupt enable bit (MIE), preserving other `mstatus` bits, and restoring interrupts can deliver a pending interrupt before the call returns.
```sol
let saved = CPU.irqSave()
// ... critical code ...
CPU.irqRestore(saved)
```

The call will reject the invalid value and fail to restore interrupts. The documentation specifies that `CPU.irqRestore` only accepts the MIE bit and explicitly rejects any other values.

```sol
let saved = CPU.irqSave()
// saved is 0 or 8
CPU.irqRestore(5) // Error: rejects non-MIE value
```

This rule ensures that frames are treated as structure rather than garbage, where the lifetime of the data is strictly the loop shape and nothing is ever collected. By dropping the root immediately upon reading, the system avoids the need for a garbage collector, eliminating pauses and keeping the Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) compositional. If a value must be kept beyond the drop-of-root, the retainer must perform a copy-on-retain into its own pool to avoid dangling pointers, as there is no third option between sending a disposable copy or sending linear data.

```fprisc
// Example: Dropping the root immediately after reading
actor main {
  let x = recv(); // x is dropped here
  // x cannot be used again; if needed, copy it before dropping
  let y = x; 
  // y is now a copy in the new pool; x is dropped
}
```

Use the `sendLinear` command to transfer ownership of a linear handle directly to the receiver, which consumes the resource and stops the sender from owning it. This ensures no live state is sent; the sender receives a disposable copy or the handle is transferred entirely.
```fprisc
actor A {
  linear x;
  sendLinear(x) to B; // A no longer owns x; B owns it and consumes it
}
```

The `loadProf` function loads a game profile from a file system, compacts and replays the stored data to generate a `prof` object, and then merges this profile with the current game state to create a new game model `m2`. If the profile indicates games were not recorded (no disk), it simply returns the modified state with `fsOn` set to 0.

```fpr
loadProf m me fs =
  c = FS.compact me fs;
  _ = drop c;
  r = FS.replay me fs profUrl;
  p = profOf r;
  _ = drop r;
  _ = print "[terra2] profile: {p.played} games ({p.won} won, {p.lost} lost, {p.gone} abandoned)";
  g = FS.readLatest me fs gameUrl;
  m2 = gameOf { m | prof = p, fsOn = 1 } g;
  _ = drop g;
  _ = announce m2;
  m2.
```

