The exchange operation uses a single `amoswap.d.aqrl` instruction, whereas compare-exchange implements a retry loop using load-reserve (LR) and store-conditional (SC) instructions. In the compare-exchange loop, an acquire fence is applied to the read, and a release fence is applied only upon a successful store-conditional; if the conditional fails, an acquire read is performed.

```sol
// Exchange uses a single atomic instruction
Mem.exchange(addr, val); // Emits amoswap.d.aqrl

// Compare-exchange uses a retry loop with LR/SC
while (!Mem.compareExchange(addr, expected, new_val)) {
    // Failure path performs an acquire read
    expected = Mem.load(addr);
}
```

The `sol` profile is restricted to the `posix` target because the FP-RISC build system refuses to link a Sol program on any target other than `posix`. This enforcement is part of the profile matrix, where the `sol` profile is explicitly defined to run only on the `posix` host, while the `builtin` profile is reserved exclusively for the `bare-metal` system.

```sol
# This Sol program will fail to link if built for bare-metal or esp-idf
# because the profile matrix enforces sol -> posix only.
@sol
fn main() {
    let x = 10;
}
```

You cannot write a Sol program for any profile other than POSIX; the build system refuses to link a Sol program if the target is not POSIX. To create a POSIX-targeted Sol program, you must specify the `sol` profile and ensure the target is set to `posix` in your build configuration.

```sol
// Example Sol program targeting POSIX
@profile sol
@target posix

fn main() {
    // Sol code here
}
```

The `builtin` profile is restricted to bare-metal systems and is refused on any other target, whereas the `sol` profile is exclusively for `posix` targets and is refused if a Sol program is compiled for non-POSIX environments. While `builtin` relies on the system's HAL for Base builtins, `sol` programs are not linked into the final image; instead, they run directly via `fpr run` without linking.

