The `unsafe` keyword indicates that the function is intended for internal use and bypasses certain safety checks or type verifications to allow for direct memory manipulation or low-level operations within the QOS kernel. It is typically used for critical system loops like `frameWorker` where performance and direct control over the frame buffer are required.

```sol
frameWorker : unsafe Int -> Int -> Unit .
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  _ = drop message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The `frameWorker` function serves as the main game loop, continuously processing input and rendering frames to maintain the interactive scene. It receives messages from the parent process to initialize the frame state, polls for 64 milliseconds of keyboard and mouse input to update the player's position (`x`, `z`) and camera orientation (`yaw`, `pitch`), and then calls `renderFrame` to draw the updated scene. After rendering, it sends the new frame state back to the parent and resets the system pool before repeating the cycle indefinitely.

```fprisc
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The system outputs "SHORT(<value>)", where <value> is the actual duration in microseconds that elapsed. This occurs because the portable backend spawns sleeper children that may finish before the requested time if the underlying system clock resolution or scheduling overhead is insufficient, causing the comparison `d1 >= 500000` to fail. For example:
```fpr
