_ = T.sleep svc 50;
d1 = read clk - t0;
s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";
```

The file `qos/tests/timer.fpr` serves as an end-to-end test suite for the FP-RISC timer functionality, validating behavior across both native and portable operating system backends. It verifies that the native backend correctly arms the CLINT hardware timer and handles IRQs via the MTIP, while confirming the portable backend successfully spawns sleeper children to simulate time passage. The test suite checks three specific assertions: that a blocking sleep operation consumes the requested amount of CLINT time, that fire-and-forget deadline messages are delivered to the caller's own mailbox, and that multiple pending deadlines are processed in the correct order.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  cl = device "clint";
  clk = reg32 cl 49144;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;
  mode = T.ping svc;

  t0 = read clk;
  _ = T.sleep svc 50;
  d1 = read clk - t0;
  s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";

  _ = T.after svc 20;
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

The system distinguishes between backends by hardware capabilities: the native backend uses the CLINT mtimecmp to arm a hardware timer and expects `mode=1`, while the portable backend lacks this hardware and spawns sleeper children over `Sys.sleepUs`, expecting `mode=0`.

```sol
main =
