The `doArm2` function configures the Peripheral Interrupt Controller (PLIC) to associate the UART's interrupt request with a specific actor and enables the interrupt by setting the corresponding bit in the Interrupt Enable Register (IER). It updates the state to indicate that the transmit queue is armed, allowing the hardware to generate interrupts when the transmit FIFO is full.

```sol
_doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1 = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  _ = irqSet st;
  reply me from UOk st.
```

To bind an actor to the UART IRQ and enable the transmit interrupt, use the `doArm` function. This function checks if the caller is the owner of the device; if so, it binds the IRQ and sets the interrupt state to enable transmission completion interrupts based on the current transmit queue length.

```sol
doArm : unsafe Int -> St -> Int -> a .
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.

doArm2 : unsafe Int -> St -> Int -> a .
doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.
```

The `rebakeNbrs` function iteratively processes a cell and its neighbors by recursively calling `rebakeAt` for each neighbor's coordinates until all faces are processed. It first retrieves the six neighbors of the current cell using `nbrOf`, then calls `rebakeAt` on each neighbor's x, y, and z coordinates, updating the world and lightmap vectors as it progresses. The recursion stops when the face index exceeds the total number of faces.

```sol
rebakeNbrs : unsafe Vector -> Vector -> Int -> Int -> (Vector, Vector) .
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1);
```

