The `xbGo` function in `bbspi.fpr` implements this transfer by iterating from bit index 7 down to 0. It drives the MOSI pin with the current bit of the input byte, pulses the SCK pin to trigger sampling on the rising edge, reads the MISO pin, and shifts the result into the accumulator. The loop terminates when the index is less than 0, returning the accumulated value.

```sol
xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.

xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

The `xbGo` function recursively transfers individual bits of a byte over the SPI bus by iterating from the most significant bit (index 7) down to 0. It drives the MOSI pin with the current bit value, toggles the SCK pin high to sample the MISO input, reads the echoed bit, and shifts the accumulator left while adding the sampled bit. The recursion terminates when the bit index drops below zero.

```sol
xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.

xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

