Since the built-in `==` operator only checks for tag-shallow equality, safe string equality must be implemented as a hand-rolled loop. You need to manually iterate through the characters of both strings to compare their values.

```fpr
fun safeEq(s1, s2) {
  len1 = len(s1)
  len2 = len(s2)
  if len1 != len2 { return false }
  for i from 0 to len1 {
    if s1[i] != s2[i] { return false }
  }
  return true
}
```

The built-in `==` operator in FP-RISC is tag-shallow and does not perform deep string equality checks, requiring users to implement safe string comparison manually. This necessitates writing hand-rolled loops across multiple files to handle the comparison logic correctly, as the standard operator lacks the necessary depth for string data.

```fpr
// Example of a hand-rolled deep string equality check in FP-RISC
fn deepEq(s1, s2) {
  if (s1.len != s2.len) return false
  var i = 0
  while (i < s1.len) {
    if (s1.charAt(i) != s2.charAt(i)) return false
    i = i + 1
  }
  return true
}
```

