The `heapInit` function initializes a heap by calculating aligned boundaries for the start and end addresses, validating that the resulting size is at least 96 words, and then allocating a zeroed memory block with a specific size. It ensures validity by checking that the end address is greater than the start address and that the difference between them is sufficient to hold the minimum required data. The function sets the heap's head pointer to a new empty block and records the calculated low and high addresses.

```fprisc
heapInit start end =
  lo = alignUp (Addr.toWord start);
  hi = Word.and (Addr.toWord end) (Word.not (w 15));
  _ = case i hi <= i lo of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case i (Word.sub hi lo) < 96 of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
  h = Block.at (Addr.fromWord lo);
  _ = zeroWords (Block.addr h) 8;
  _ = Block.setSize h (Word.sub hi lo);
  _ = Heap.setHead (st Unit) h;
  _ = Heap.setLow (st Unit) lo;
  Heap.setHigh (st Unit) hi.
```

