CLAIM-THEN-MASK is a strategy used to prevent interrupt storms from level-triggered sources by masking the interrupt source immediately after the interrupt is claimed, and only re-arming it after the actor has serviced the device. If the source line remains high during re-arming, the PLIC will pend a fresh interrupt, ensuring exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```fpr
# Pseudocode illustrating the CLAIM-THEN-MASK flow
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    # Claim the interrupt and mask the source immediately
    pend_source(source_id, false); 
    mask_source(source_id);

    # Actor services the interrupt
    Sys.irqBind(source_id, actor_function);

    # After servicing, re-arm only if the line is still high
    if (line_is_high(source_id)) {
        unmask_source(source_id);
        pend_source(source_id, true);
    }
}
```

The file `qos/hal/virt/plic.fpr` defines a QEMU virtual Platform Level Interrupt Controller (PLIC) specifically designed for the WFI-wake execution model, where the machine does not trap but instead services interrupts synchronously. It operates on a CLAIM-THEN-MASK discipline to prevent infinite loops caused by level-triggered sources that re-pend interrupts after service; the drain loop masks the source upon claiming it, and the actor re-arms it after servicing. This implementation functions as a raw library unit with no dynamic allocation, providing only aligned 32-bit load and store operations for the C actors that invoke it.

```sol
// Example usage pattern in actors.c
hal_irq_open(&irq_handle, source_id);
hal_irq_claim(irq_handle); // Masks source, claims ownership
// ... service device ...
hal_irq_ack(irq_handle);  // Re-arms source if line is still high
```

The PLIC implements a CLAIM-THEN-MASK discipline where the drain loop masks the interrupt source immediately upon claiming it, preventing re-pending. The actor servicing the device must re-arm the source via Sys.irqAck; if the line remains high, the gateway re-pends and a fresh delivery occurs exactly once per acknowledgment, avoiding storms.

```sol
# Pseudo-code illustrating the claim-mask and re-arm cycle
# (Based on hal_irq_claim and Sys.irqAck behavior)

func hal_irq_claim(source) {
    # Mask the source immediately to prevent re-pending
    plic_mask(source)

    # Drain loop checks if pending, claims, then masks (already done)
    if (plic_pending(source)) {
        plic_claim(source)
        # Source is now masked, drain loop exits or waits for re-arm
    }
}

# Actor logic (conceptual)
func actor_service(source) {
    # ... process interrupt ...
    # Re-arm the source; if line is still high, gateway re-pends
    Sys.irqAck(source)
}
```

The app image must contain no libc and no syscalls because isolation is achieved through build-time design where the codegen never emits raw syscalls and the table's entries serve as the runtime capability surface. Attempting to use a dynamic loader or raw syscalls would violate the fixed-slot loading design, which relies on the slot address being a published constant rather than an address extracted from a system symbol table, ensuring the app loads correctly under any qosp without relocation or external dependencies.

```fpr
// Incorrect: Attempting to use a syscall directly in a QOS app
// This will fail because the codegen does not emit raw syscalls and the table
// replaces external references.
syscall read(fd, buf, count);

// Correct: Using the table for all effects
table_entry read_entry = get_table_entry("read");
call read_entry(fd, buf, count);
```

The `fpr_arc_incref` function is invoked exactly twice at promotion sites: when a value is sent (making the message shared) and when a value is spawned (pinning the entry closure into a child actor. It increments the reference count and pins the owning slab to prevent premature reclamation.

```fpr
send(v) {
    fpr_arc_incref(v);
    // send v over the channel
}

spawn(v) {
    fpr_arc_incref(v);
    // spawn new actor with v as entry closure
}
```

The ARC invariant dictates that `fpr_arc_incref` is invoked exclusively at two promotion sites: `send` (when a value becomes shared) and `spawn` (when an entry closure is pinned into a child actor). Any value escaping an actor's scope triggers an increment at these specific points, while ordinary computation remains free of reference counting.

```fprisc
fn main() {
  // spawn pins the entry closure, triggering fpr_arc_incref
  fpr_arc_incref; 
  fpr_spawn arg1 arg2;

  // send makes the value shared, triggering fpr_arc_incref
  fpr_send arg1 arg2;

  // Ordinary computation below does zero RC work
  add 1 2;
}
```

The largest size served by fixed-size-class recycler buckets is 8 KiB, known as the bucket ceiling. Any freed block of this size or smaller returns to the appropriate bucket, while larger blocks are directed to the `bigfree` exact-fit LIFO recycler. For instance, a 4 KiB block returns to a bucket, whereas a larger vector column goes to `bigfree`.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating the bucket ceiling rule
if (block_size <= 8 * 1024) {
    // Returns to fixed-size-class bucket
    Sys.returnToBucket(block);
} else {
    // Sent to bigfree
    Sys.returnToBigfree(block);
}
```

