The file `qos/tests/sysdisk.fpr` serves as a proof-of-concept demonstrating that the disk image itself acts as the system state, persisting live application configurations across reboots without requiring recompilation or explicit loading calls. It verifies that a "hot swap" of application modules survives a system reboot by persisting the live attachment chain in the log, allowing a fresh process to reconstruct the previous runtime state. The test script initializes a file system and loader, replays the disk state, and executes two boot scenarios: the first boot fails to apply changes because the live chain remains intact, while the second boot successfully reconstructs the live set from the disk records, confirming the persistence of the hot-swapped modules.

