```sol
// Example of handling the empty read in the socket tier
if (netRead.len == 0) {
  return EGone; // Session terminated, connection ID recycled safely
}
```

`Map.equal` compares the internal tree structures of two maps, including their shape and key ordering, rather than just their contents. Consequently, two maps containing the same key-value pairs can be unequal if they were constructed from lists with different insertion orders, resulting in different tree shapes. To compare maps based solely on their contents, one must use `Map.equalBy` with a custom equality function.

Example:
```sol
use "std/map";
use "order";

let m1 = Map.update "b" 2 (Map.update "a" 1 Map.strings);
let m2 = Map.update "a" 1 (Map.update "b" 2 Map.strings);

# m1 and m2 have the same entries but different tree shapes due to insertion order.
# Map.equal m1 m2 evaluates to false.
# Map.equalBy (==) m1 m2 evaluates to true.
```

