The `askPerm` function handles interactive permission checks for applications by prompting the user via standard input. It reads a line from the input, verifies it is not empty, and checks if the first character is 'y' (ASCII 121) or 'Y' (ASCII 89). If the input matches these criteria, the function returns `True`, granting the permission; otherwise, it returns `False`. This boolean result is then used by the `gate` function to decide whether to allow the requested permission or terminate the application if the permission is marked as required.

```fpr
askPerm man kind p =
  (url, mode) = p;
  who = case M.name man == "" of True -> M.id man | False -> M.name man;
  _ = Sys.stderr "{who} \"{M.id man}\" requests {mode} {url} ({kind}) -- allow? [y/n] ";
  saidYes Unit.
```

To configure idle burn, you must modify `machine/posix/park.c` so that an idle hart naps for 200 microseconds before checking for wake sources. The hart should park on a condition variable triggered by a doorbell (`hal_ipi_send`) or a deadline (`hal_timer_arm`), with a maximum wait time of 20 milliseconds to prevent hanging. This reduces idle power consumption from 5.0% to 0.4% of a core.

```c
// Example structure for park.c logic
while (idle) {
    hal_nap(200_us);
    hal_check_wake_sources(); // Checks doorbell and timer
}
```

The `posix` system treats the host OS as the board, providing a standard executable that interacts with the OS via processes, files, and sockets without requiring QOS installation. In contrast, the `qos-portable` system treats the host OS merely as an implementation detail, bundling a copy of the QOS runtime with the application to expose a unified HAL and services regardless of the underlying host.

Example:
```fpr
// In posix, you use standard OS abstractions
