FP-RISC uses a worklist-based comparison for tuples that grows the worklist as it descends through the first field of a boxed payload, preventing premature termination and ensuring deep structural equality. This mechanism requires the accumulator to carry the entire nested structure, meaning two tuples are only considered equal if all corresponding fields match at every level.

```sol
main =
  _ = yes "(1, 2) == (1, 2)" ((1, 2) == (1, 2));
  _ = no "(1, 2) == (3, 4)" ((1, 2) == (3, 4));
  _ = no "(1, 2) == (1, 9)" ((1, 2) == (1, 9));
  _ = yes "a tuple built twice is still equal" (mkPair 3 == mkPair 3);
  _ = no "and not to its neighbour" (mkPair 3 == mkPair 4);
```

The implementation fails to meet the platform conformance contract because the Base specification explicitly prohibits requiring Unix paths, descriptors, or processes merely because the reference host uses them. A conforming implementation must provide a portable interface that specifies acquisition, permission, absence, failure, ordering, and resource lifetime without tying to specific OS artifacts. If a program mandates these OS-specific features in its Base contract, it violates the portability requirements of the `(L_n, S_n)` conformance pair, as the contract requires the interface to be independent of the underlying environment's physical behavior.

```sol
-- Incorrect: Requires Unix path
Base {
  open: File("/var/log/app.log"),
  ...
}

-- Correct: Uses abstract file interface with capability semantics
Base {
  open: File {
    acquire: Capability,
    permission: Read,
    failure: NotFound,
    ...
  },
  ...
}
```

