When the compiler cannot prove that the stack pointer (`sp`) remains within the current segment's bounds (`sp - stk_lo < stk_span`), the runtime invokes `fpr_stack_grow` to link in a new segment with double the size of the previous one, effectively doubling the mailbox ring's capacity. Because function frames are addressed via `s0` and the epilogue restores `sp` from `s0`, the function returns directly into the old segment without requiring a trampoline or moving live data.

```fprisc
// Pseudocode illustrating the stack grow and return mechanism
if (sp - stk_lo >= stk_span) {
    fpr_stack_grow(); // Links in a new segment
}
// Frame setup via s0
s0 = &frame;
// ... function body ...
// Epilogue restores sp from s0, returning to the old segment
sp = s0->sp;
```

To call a function with more than 8 parameters, you must use a tuple type where the arity is encoded in the type definition (e.g., `T_TUPN + n`) rather than relying on fixed-size types like `Tup2` through `Tup8`. The compiler handles the spill of the wide tuple into the hart's spill cells, allowing the function to accept up to 63 parameters plus the spilled tuple, totaling 64 parameters for C exports. You can define such a type and pass it as an argument to the function, as demonstrated in test cases with 100 parameters.

```fprisc
