`std/store` keeps a keyed collection in SHARD actors where each shard holds its part in memory and appends changes to its own file, rewriting the file only when superseded lines outnumber live ones. During compaction, a shard tidies itself one small shard at a time. Any data built inside an actor that must survive a tidy must be stored IN the tidied value, as external references can become dangling after a reset.

```fprisc
// Example of a shard value that survives tidying
shard_state = {
  entries: [key1: "val1", key2: "val2"],
  path: "/shard/data"
}
```

The previous logbook held the entire log in a single register, whereas `std/store` distributes data across SHARD actors where each shard manages its own file. When a shard is superseded, it rewrites only its specific file if the number of new lines exceeds the live ones, allowing for incremental compaction of one small shard at a time. This contrasts with the old model by isolating file operations per shard and ensuring that any state surviving a tidy must be contained within the tidied value itself.

```fprisc
# Example of a shard managing its own file path within its tidied state
shard {
  key: "data"
  file: "/path/to/shard/data"
  # The file path is part of the tidied state, so it survives resets correctly
  compact() {
    if (new_lines > live_lines) {
      rewrite(file)
    }
  }
}
```

