The `split` function divides a memory block (`b`) into two parts: a new block of size `size` and a remaining block containing the rest of the original memory. It is used to allocate a specific amount of memory from an existing free block. If the remaining space after allocation is less than 96 bytes, the function returns `Unit` instead of creating a new block, effectively wasting the small remainder. Otherwise, it updates the block pointers and sizes to link the new block into the free list and reduces the size of the original block.

```fpr
split b size = case i (Word.sub (Block.size b) size) < 96 of
    True -> Unit
  | False ->
    n = Block.at (Addr.add (Block.addr b) (i size));
    _ = zeroWords (Block.addr n) 8;
    _ = Block.setSize n (Word.sub (Block.size b) size);
    _ = Block.setPrev n b;
    _ = Block.setNext n (Block.next b);
    _ = linkBack (Block.next b) n;
    _ = Block.setNext b n;
    Block.setSize b size.
```

The file `fprisc/machine/builtin/heap.fpr` defines the core memory management logic for the FP-RISC heap, implementing functions to allocate, deallocate, and track memory blocks. It utilizes a linked list of blocks managed by `headB` and `Unit` to track live allocations and determines memory usage by counting used blocks. The primary allocation strategy is `firstFit`, which searches for the first suitable block that can satisfy a requested size, splitting larger blocks if necessary to fit the allocation while preserving the remainder for future use.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size;
```

The `alloc` function first calculates a base size by adding an 80-byte overhead to the requested bytes and aligning it upward. It ensures the final size is at least 96 bytes if the calculated base is exactly 80. To find a suitable block, the function performs a linear search starting from the head of the block list (`headB Unit`) using the `firstFit` helper, which checks if the current block is free and large enough to satisfy the request. If a match is found, it claims the block by splitting it if necessary and marking it as used.

```fprisc
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `claim` function reserves a specific block of memory by splitting an existing free block to accommodate the requested size and then initializing the new allocation metadata. It first calls `split` to carve out the required space, ensuring the remaining free block is at least 96 words in size; if the split fails, the original block is returned unmodified. Following the split, it marks the new block as used, clears its layout and pending fields, sets its capacity to the total size minus a 64-word overhead, initializes its reference count to one, and finally zero-fills the payload area to ensure memory safety before returning the address.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

`Vec.iota` generates a vector of integers from 1 to N, which is useful for creating column indices or loop counters. It is often combined with `Vec.dup` to create a constant column that can be added element-wise to the iota vector. For example, `Vec.dup 64 10` creates a 64-element vector where every element is 10, and `Vec.zipAdd` adds this to the output of `Vec.iota 64` to produce values from 11 to 74.

