The `doNeq` function implements a negated equality check that succeeds if and only if the terms are not unifiable right now, relying on the `marks` and `undoTo` functions to restore the original state if unification fails. It first captures the marks of the current environment, attempts to unify the terms, and if unification succeeds (meaning they are equal), it undoes the bindings to return a state where they are effectively unequal for the purpose of the clause. If unification fails, it proceeds to solve the goal with the modified environment.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `placeUnit` function fails to define a valid storage zone because it only handles `:unit` cards by using `p.fwd` and assumes all other cards (like `:tactic`) use `p.rear`. Consequently, the variable `zone` is assigned `p.rear` for non-unit cards, which is incorrect since tactics should occupy the forward lane. When `firstEmpty 1 zone` is called on `p.rear`, it searches the rear lane for an empty slot. If the rear lane is full, the function returns 0, triggering the error log "zone full". This prevents the card from being placed in the correct lane and causes the placement to fail, whereas the logic should have checked the card's kind first to select the appropriate lane.

```sol
placeUnit i c g =
  p = me g;
  zone = case c.kind == :unit of True -> p.fwd | False -> p.rear;
  k = firstEmpty 1 zone;
  case k == 0 of
    True -> addLog "zone full" g
  | False -> commitPlace i c k g.
```

