```

The buddy actor serves as the serialization point for memory allocations, but forcing all requests to queue behind a lock would cause unnecessary stalls, especially when Hart 0 is occupied with long C sections or register copying. By allowing lock-free allocation via `buddy_alloc_try` when the lock is free, the system avoids serializing every block behind a single hart, which improves throughput and prevents stalls during high-contention periods.
```sol
if (buddy_alloc_try()) {
    // Lock-free allocation; requester stores result in its ACB
    // and wakes up immediately without queuing.
} else {
    // Lock held; request queues on the buddy actor.
}
```

To allocate memory, the requester sends a message to the memory actor (Memory.qa) with an integer request value. The format encodes the operation and size using the bit pattern `bytes<<1|1`, where `bytes` is the number of bytes to take. The memory actor responds by storing the resulting pointer into the requester's actor context buffer (acb) and waking the requester, ensuring the allocation happens without draining open borrows.

```sol
// Example: Request 1024 bytes from the memory actor
let req = 1024 << 1 | 1;
mem_actor.send(req);
```

A buddy is a specific actor owned by the memory component that serializes all memory allocation requests, whereas a requester is any other actor or context that initiates an allocation by sending a message to the buddy. The buddy acts as the serialization point for memory operations, managing the pool of slabs and stacks, while requesters may attempt to allocate inline using lock-free paths like `buddy_alloc_try` if the buddy is not currently holding the lock. If the lock is held, the requester queues its request behind the buddy actor on hart 0's queue.

```sol
// Example of a requester attempting inline allocation
buddy_alloc_try(size); 
// If the buddy is free, allocation happens here without queuing.
// If the buddy is busy, execution queues until the buddy wakes up.
```

The requester that cannot acquire the buddy lock will queue on the memory actor in hart 0's queue, creating a serialization point that stalls all subsequent allocations until the hart becomes free. This is problematic because a round trip through one actor on one hart is more expensive than the allocation itself, and stalls occur whenever that hart is busy executing a long C section (such as register copying for a big model), causing every other actor's allocations to serialize behind it.

```sol
// Example of a blocked allocation path
buddy_alloc_try {
    // If lock is held, execution drops here and queues on the actor
    // while the hart processing the lock is stalled by a C section.
}
```

