The `big x` definition is a large-scale arithmetic chain that initializes a variable `x` (implicitly as 0 or a base value) and iteratively computes 101 sequential values (`v1` through `v101`). Each variable `v_n` is calculated by adding the integer `n` to the immediately preceding variable `v_{n-1}`. This creates a linear accumulation where `v_n` equals the sum of all integers from 1 to `n`.

```fprisc
big x =
  v1 = x + 1;
  v2 = v1 + 2;
  v3 = v2 + 3;
  ...
  v101 = v100 + 101;
```

