The `cycles` function recursively invokes the `one` function 40 times, spawning two waiter processes for each iteration to test waiter cancellation. In each iteration, it starts a poller (`p`), spawns the waiters, receives their messages, stops the poller, and then immediately starts a second poller (`q`) to stop it, ensuring the system handles 80 total poller stops. The function decrements its counter until reaching zero, at which point it returns `Unit`.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = PL.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = PL.start Unit;
  _ = PL.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

The CPU saves the current interrupt source mask to a local variable using `CPU.irqSave`, which returns the current value of CSR 772. To restore the state, `CPU.irqRestore` is called with the previously saved value. This mechanism allows for nested interrupt handling, as demonstrated by saving a state, modifying the mask, saving another state (which returns 0 if no new interrupts are pending), and then restoring the original state.

```sol
original = CPU.irqSave Unit;
sources = CPU.csrRead 772;
_ = CPU.csrWrite 772 (Word.fromInt 0);
_ = CPU.irqEnable Unit;
enabled = CPU.irqSave Unit;
_ = CPU.irqRestore nested;
_ = CPU.irqRestore original;
```

