When two transactions execute concurrently on a shared counter, the first transaction to read the value performs its increment and write, but the second transaction reads the same initial value, increments it again, and overwrites the first transaction's result. This causes the final counter value to be less than expected because the first write is lost. To fix this, the code must check if the current value has already been updated before proceeding with the increment and write. The corrected logic reads the current value, checks if it matches the expected value, and only writes the new incremented value if it is still valid:
```sol
