The `histB` function takes four arguments: a history vector `h`, a count vector `cs`, a data vector `ys`, and a list of integers `k` (representing bin row thresholds), along with an optional integer `k` parameter in the recursive step. It processes the list of thresholds to update the history vector. In the base case where the threshold list is empty (`Nil`), it returns the current history, count, and data vectors unchanged. When a threshold is present (`i :: r`), it retrieves the count and data at index `i+1`, calculates a position `pos` using the count, threshold, and data value, retrieves the old count at that position, increments it, and recursively calls itself with the updated history and the remaining thresholds.

```fprisc
histB h cs ys Nil k = (h, cs, ys);
histB h cs ys (i :: r) k =
  (b, cs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) cs;
  (y, ys2) = Vec.get (i + 1) ys;
  pos = b * k + y + 1;
  (old, h2) = Vec.get pos h;
  histB (Vec.set pos (old + 1) h2) cs2 ys2 r k;
```

The `histB` function recursively processes a list of integers, using a binning scheme defined by the multiplier `k` and the value `y` from the input vector. For each element, it retrieves the current count from the histogram vector at the calculated position (`b * k + y + 1`), increments that count, and updates the histogram vector before recursing on the remaining list. The function returns the final tuple of the modified histogram, the sorted bin counts, and the processed list when the input list is empty.

```fprisc
histB h cs ys Nil k = (h, cs, ys).
histB h cs ys (i :: r) k =
  (b, cs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) cs;
  (y, ys2) = Vec.get (i + 1) ys;
  pos = b * k + y + 1;
  (old, h2) = Vec.get pos h;
  histB (Vec.set pos (old + 1) h2) cs2 ys2 r k.
```

The FP-RISC server burns 5.1% of a core IDLE due to a dedicated poller process that renders its entire page on every change, whereas LiveView processes render only what their change tracking indicates moved in parallel. This architectural difference makes FP-RISC's CPU cost per action approximately 7 times higher than LiveView's, though FP-RISC still wins on startup time (8 ms), memory usage at small sizes, and code size.

```sol
// Example of a simple FP-RISC app demonstrating the model-based rendering approach
defn main() {
  model = new Model();
  view = new View(model);
  app = new App(view);
  app.run();
}

class Model {
  data = [];
  change() {
    data.push(new Item());
  }
}

class View {
  model = null;
  render() {
    // Renders only changed parts based on change tracking
    print "Rendering changes";
  }
}

class App {
  view = null;
  run() {
    loop {
      model.change();
      view.render();
      poll(); // Polls for changes
    }
  }
}
```

