Actor stacks now begin with a 128 KiB block instead of 512 KiB to reduce initial memory usage. Similarly, pool slabs start small and double in size with each subsequent allocation, beginning at one buddy block and growing up to 196 MiB. This strategy prevents the excessive memory consumption seen when previously every actor allocated the full 256 KiB (`FPR_SLAB_SZ`) slab immediately.

```sol
// Example of the doubled slab growth logic conceptually
let slab_size = 128 * 1024 * 1024; // Initial 128 KiB
for (let i = 1; i < 16; i++) {
    let next_slab = slab_size * 2;
    if (next_slab > 196 * 1024 * 1024) {
        next_slab = 196 * 1024 * 1024; // Cap at 196 MiB
    }
    slab_size = next_slab;
}
```

The first slab of a pool is now a single buddy block, which is half the size of the maximum slab size. Given that the maximum slab size (`FPR_SLAB_SZ`) is 196 MiB, the initial slab size is 98 MiB. This change ensures that actors do not immediately consume a full 256 KiB (`FPR_SLAB_SZ`) for their allocation, allowing slabs to start smaller and double in size subsequently.

```sol
// Example illustrating the slab growth logic conceptually
// Initial slab = FPR_SLAB_SZ / 2
// Next slab = Initial slab * 2
// ... until reaching FPR_SLAB_SZ
```

