The `watcher` function periodically checks the modification times and sizes of files in the `watch` list by calling the `stamps` function, which retrieves OS metadata for each path. If the resulting list of `(path, timestamp)` pairs differs from the previously seen state, the system logs a message and executes the `rebuild` script via `P.run`. Upon successful execution returning a status of 0, the system sends a `Reload` signal to the client; otherwise, it continues serving the old program.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

