The `binCol` function converts an unsafe Vector of F64 numbers into a Vector of Int codes by binning the data into `nb` equal-width intervals. It requires three arguments: the unsafe Vector of floating-point data, the number of bins (`nb`), and it returns a tuple containing the resulting codes and updated internal state. The function first calculates the minimum and maximum values in the column, determines the bin width, and then recursively processes each element to assign it to the nearest integer bin index.

```fprisc
binCol : unsafe Vector -> Int -> _ .
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

