The operations `u0` through `u4` are deferred mutating tool calls that buffer their effects; they do not immediately touch the disk but instead queue for atomic commit. The `scaffold` function returns a string confirming the atomic completion of the operation: "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic". This ensures that if any of the write or mkdir operations fail during validation, none of the changes are applied to the filesystem.

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

The system ensures consistency by buffering all mutating tool calls, including `writePath`, and applying them atomically only after the entire script validates successfully. If the script fails after `u2`, the filesystem remains in its state before any writes occurred; the partial changes are discarded and no concurrent processes are affected because deferred effects never fire. To verify this behavior, consider a script that attempts to write a file and then remove it; if the script aborts midway, the file will not be created or removed.

```sol
# Example of atomic failure: if this script fails after the write, the file is gone.
safe_scaffold =
  u0 = writePath "/tmp/test.txt" "hello";
  u1 = removePath "/tmp/test.txt";
  print "done";
# If the script crashes here, /tmp/test.txt is deleted and never created.
```

In Sol, use the `shq` tool calls for mutating operations like `mkdirp` and `writePath`, which buffer changes until the script completes successfully. This ensures that no file touches the disk until the entire transaction validates. For example, you can scaffold a project by queuing directory creation and file writes together:

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n";
  print "scaffolded {root}";
```

