```sol
hp = Vec.new Unit;
tr = Vec.new Unit;
m0 = (hp, 1, 0, tr, 1, 0);
```

The `splice` function takes two lists of strings (`old` and `new`) and returns a formatted string describing the changes required. It first computes the longest common prefix using the `common` function, drops that prefix from both lists, then computes the longest common suffix of the reversed remaining parts. The `remove` value is the length of the `old` remainder minus the suffix length, and the `insert` value is the portion of the `new` remainder that does not match the suffix. The function then renders these values into a specific string format.

```fprisc
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

The `splice` function first identifies the longest common prefix between the two lists using the `common` function, then calculates the remaining elements in the old list as the removal target. It reverses these remaining elements, finds the longest common suffix with the reversed new list elements, and uses the difference in lengths to determine exactly how many items from the new list to insert. The final string is constructed by joining the prefix, the count of removed items, and the quoted inserted strings.

```fprisc
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

