To configure the UART, the actor must first verify ownership before binding the interrupt source and setting the interrupt enable register. If the actor is not the owner, the operation returns an error. If it is the owner, the system binds the `uartIrq` to the actor's message port, sets the interrupt enable register to `1` (which triggers the `irqSet` logic to write RDA bits to the IER if armed), and replies with the updated state.

```fpr
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.

doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.
```

To minimize computational cost, you should avoid a full scene rebake and instead perform an incremental update by calculating the specific cells affected by the edit. These affected cells include the edited cell itself and its six neighbors for exposure, as well as all cells along the anti-sun ray for shadow rays. The implementation uses a recursive function to walk through these specific coordinates.

```fpr
rebakeAt w lm x y z | inWorld x y z == False = (w, lm);
rebakeAt w lm x y z = rebakeCell w lm (cellOf x y z);

