To use the SPI bus, you first define a `Bus` value by assigning specific pins to the `pinW` (write), `pinR` (read), `sck` (clock), `mosi` (master out), and `miso` (master in) fields. Once the bus is configured, the `xferByte` function is called with the bus and the data byte to transfer; it returns the echoed byte. For example:

```fpr
bus = Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso =
  _ = pinM sck 1;
  _ = pinM mosi 1;
  _ = pinM cs 1;
  pinM miso 0;
echoedByte = xferByte bus 0x55.
```

The `xbGo` function iterates from bit index 7 down to 0, driving the MOSI pin with the corresponding bit of the byte while the clock is low. On each rising clock edge, it samples the MISO pin to echo the bit back and accumulates the result in a running total. The process stops when the index reaches -1, returning the reconstructed byte.
```sol
xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.
xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

