The `gl_init` function requests the display first because `eglInitialize` opens the primary DRM node, which grants Mesa the master device. If EGL is initialized first, Mesa becomes the master, leaving the scanout subsystem unable to modeset the screen, resulting in an `EBUSY` error and no frame being displayed. The correct sequence is to call `gl_init` (which opens the display and explicitly claims the master) before requesting an EGL context.

