An actor that must wait yields by calling `Sys.sleepUs`, which parks the hart's sleeper on the hart loop's list rather than nanosleeping the thread, ensuring the actor never holds a core. For concurrent access, any CAS retry loop backs off exponentially (capped) before retrying to prevent livelocks and degrade bandwidth only under contention. This is implemented via `Sys.sleepUs` for yielding and `fpr_lock` for the transitional CAS backoff mechanism.

```sol
// Example: Yielding without holding a core
Sys.sleepUs(1000);

// Example: Exponential backoff in a CAS loop (transitional)
fpr_lock(&shared_data);
// ... critical section ...
fpr_unlock(&shared_data);
```

When an actor needs to wait, it yields control by calling `Sys.sleepUs` with a deadline. This parks the sleeper on the hart's sleeper list and wakens it only when the hart loop processes it, ensuring the actor never holds a core. This prevents deadlocks in the detector and allows other actors on the same hart to proceed immediately, replacing previous nanosleep mechanisms that caused all sharing actors to wait out the sleep.

