Deterministic work stealing preserves an actor's age by donating its oldest entry (with the global clock stamp intact) to a machine-wide FIFO under a single lock, ensuring the migrated actor maintains its relative priority without resetting its age. The worst-case wait time for a READY actor is bounded by the sum of its age threshold multiplied by the slot time and the number of resident actors on the hart multiplied by the slot time, expressed as `Wait(a) <= tau * T_slot + A * T_slot`. The slot time `T_slot` is further decomposed into the fuel quantum, segment maximum times hardware cost, cumulative C-entry bounds, and scheduling overhead including drain, backlog scan, and steal lock operations.

```fpr
// Example of an actor yielding fuel to become READY
actor main = 
  fuel_yield()
  receive(m) {
    // This entry is READY and will be served based on age/stamp
    process(m)
  }
```

Work stealing tightens the worst-case wait time bound because donated actors preserve their original stamps and are served oldest-first in the machine-wide FIFO, preventing age resets that would otherwise occur during migration. Consequently, the bound for an actor $a$ is calculated as $Wait(a) \leq \tau \times T_{slot} + A \times T_{slot}$, where $A$ represents ready actors resident on the hart plus any stolen ones, and $\tau$ is the threshold at which aging outranks un-aged actors. This discipline ensures that migration never increases the effective wait time compared to a scenario without stealing.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating the admission constraint based on the bound
actor my_actor {
    stamp = 100;
    age = 0;

    // If age exceeds tau, this actor outranks un-aged actors
    // and can only be admitted if an earlier stamp is available
    on receive(msg) {
        if (stamp < current_hart_earliest_stamp) {
            // Admission allowed
            process(msg);
        } else {
            // Must wait for an earlier stamp or age update
            yield();
        }
    }
}
```

