FP-RISC uses a guard mechanism where the HAL intercepts stack changes via `hal_stack_guard` and `hal_stack_unguard`. When a stack overflow occurs, the system triggers a panic (e.g., "stack overflow -- the actor ran off its 511 KiB stack") and exits. This guard incurs a cost of approximately 0.9 microseconds per actor lifetime due to two `mprotect` calls, which is slightly higher than the cost of spawning and dying actors (1.7 microseconds) but ensures safety.

```sol
// Example of a stack overflow scenario in FP-RISC
actor main() {
    // This actor will eventually overflow its 511 KiB stack
    // The HAL will catch this via hal_stack_guard
    while (true) {
        // ... intensive computation ...
    }
}
```

FP-RISC protects the lowest page of the stack to make it inaccessible, and the HAL catches the resulting fault on an alternate signal stack. When this occurs, the system panics with a message indicating the actor ran off its stack and exits. For example, a POSIX actor can trigger this by growing its stack beyond the protected limit, resulting in a panic like `*** FPRISC PANIC [actor 2]: stack overflow -- the actor ran off its 511 KiB stack`.

```fprisc
-- Example: An actor that overflows its stack in a POSIX environment
actor overflow_test {
  var stack: stack
  var i: int

  init {
    stack = create_stack(511 * 1024) -- Request 512 KiB (256 KiB used + 256 KiB guard)
    i = 0
  }

  loop {
    stack.push(i)
    i = i + 1
    if i > 1000000 then
      -- This will cause a stack overflow on POSIX due to the mprotect guard
      break
    end
  }
}
```

