The `renderWorker` function establishes a frame boundary by calling `Sys.poolReset 0`, which triggers a "soft death" of the current frame context. Immediately following this reset, the function explicitly drops references to both the request root (`m`) and the response (`r`) to release resources, ensuring no memory leaks occur after the frame is processed.

```fprisc
_sys.poolReset 0;
_drop r;
_drop m;
```

The line `Sys.poolReset 0` marks the frame boundary, signaling the soft death of the current frame to allow the operating system to reclaim resources and prepare for the next one. If this line were removed, the frame would not be properly terminated, potentially causing resource leaks or preventing the system from advancing to the next rendering cycle. The code relies on this explicit reset to enforce the frame boundary logic described in the comments.

```fprisc
renderWorker parent render env vl self =
  m = receiveFrom self parent;
  Rq st model vw vh sc fr = m;
  vs = vl self env (vw, vh) model;
  r = svcCall self render (Render st vs sc fr);
  _ = send parent r;
  _ = drop r;
  _ = drop m;
