The buddy system is owned by the Memory actor (Memory.qa), which acts as the serialization point for all memory blocks including pool slabs, stacks, and channel-block carves. Requests are sent as messages to this actor using an Int encoding where the low bit distinguishes a take (`bytes<<1|1`) from a give (`ptr>>1`), and the response is stored directly into the requester's acb followed by a wake, ensuring no mailbox messages are lost. If the buddy lock is free, the allocation happens inline without queuing; otherwise, the request queues on the Memory actor, though it is admitted ahead of the backlog to prevent stalls.

```sol
