```

The buddy actor acts as the serialization point to ensure that every block the runtime needs, such as pool slabs, message slabs, stacks, and the ARC table's next size, is managed safely through a single queue on hart 0. While a requester can attempt to allocate inline if the lock is free, only those requests that find the buddy lock held must queue, preventing stalls caused by long C sections on the host hart. This design ensures that queued requests never wait on a reservoir draw because the actor is admitted ahead of the backlog, and no spinlock is ever held across a queued request.

```sol
// Example of a request path that queues on the buddy actor
// when the lock is held, incurring a round-trip cost.
buddy_alloc_try(ptr) -> {
    if (lock_free) {
        // Inline allocation, no queueing
        store(acb, ptr);
        wake();
    } else {
        // Queueing on the buddy actor
        queue_to_buddy(ptr);
    }
}
```

