In the QOS operating system, Sol scripts can implement a retry mechanism for concurrent operations by reading a shared state, performing an update, and writing it back, while allowing the system to handle transaction failures automatically. When multiple transactions attempt to modify the same resource simultaneously, the QOS scheduler ensures that only one succeeds and the others are rolled back, prompting the failed transactions to re-execute their logic. A script can define a transaction that reads a value, increments it, and writes the result, relying on the OS to manage the concurrency and retry logic without explicit manual locking in the script code.

```sol
n = parseInt (readPath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt);
u = sleepMs 300;
u2 = writePath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt "{n + 1}";
print "wrote {n + 1}";
```

When two transactions run concurrently on a single counter without proper locking, a race condition occurs where the second transaction overwrites the update of the first, causing the final value to be incorrect. The system expects the loser of the race to retry, but without explicit transaction isolation, the update is lost. For example:

```sol
n = parseInt (readPath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt);
u = sleepMs 300;
u2 = writePath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt "{n + 1}";
print "wrote {n + 1}".
```

If this code runs concurrently twice on the same file path without a lock, the final written value will likely be `n + 1` instead of `n + 2`, as the second write overwrites the first increment.

