A FP-RISC program uses the `spawn` keyword to start a new process and `spawnOn` to distribute tasks across specific hardware cores. To coordinate a poller, the `poller` function is passed the replier and the receiver as arguments. To utilize multiple cores, the system calculates the number of available hardware threads via `Sys.harts`, subtracts the core used by the poller, and spawns the spammer on the remaining threads.

```fprisc
main =
  me = myself 0;
  rep = spawn replier;
  pol = spawnOn 1 (poller rep me);
  spm = spawnOn (Sys.harts Unit - 1) (spammer rep);
  r = receiveFrom me pol;
  Reply k = r;
  _ = drop r;
  "pingpong: ok after {k} round trips".
```

