A Sol program can verify correct timer ordering by scheduling multiple `T.after` events, receiving them via `receiveFrom`, and comparing the current clock against a reference point using `read clk` within a conditional expression. The logic must explicitly check if both events have fired (`isFire`) and then validate the time delta to ensure the later deadline was not processed prematurely.

```sol
  t0 = read clk;
  _ = T.after me 100000;
  _ = T.after me 200000;
  g1 = receiveFrom me svc;
  g2 = receiveFrom me svc;
  t2 = read clk;
  res = case and2 (isFire g1) (isFire g2) of
    True -> case t2 - t0 >= 100000 of True -> "OK" | False -> "EARLY"
    | False -> "WRONG";
  _ = kill svc;
  res
```

