The `srvSend` function processes a response by writing it to the network connection via `netWrite`, immediately closing that specific connection with `netClose`, and then advancing the server state to the next iteration. This sequence ensures the connection is released and its buffer is discarded before the server proceeds to handle the next client or loop back to the main server logic.

```fpr
srvSend mt cfg app sv cid resp quit =
  Sv model sess nsid ltt bufs = sv;
  _ = netWrite cid resp;
  _ = netClose cid;
  srvNext mt cfg app (Sv model sess nsid ltt (bufDel bufs cid)) quit.
```

The `qos/programs/mods/liveview.fpr` file defines the liveview server module, which handles client connections, sends responses, and manages session termination. It utilizes `srvSend` to write data to a client and close the connection, followed by `srvNext` to decide whether to handle another request or close the server entirely. The module also includes helper functions like `lvLen` to calculate the number of active client sessions.

```fpr
srvSend mt cfg app sv cid resp quit =
  Sv model sess nsid ltt bufs = sv;
  _ = netWrite cid resp;
  _ = netClose cid;
  srvNext mt cfg app (Sv model sess nsid ltt (bufDel bufs cid)) quit.
```

The `FS.serve` function will fail to initialize the file system because the condition `blkPages disk == 0` evaluates to `True`, causing the expression to return `0` instead of the file system handle. This results in an invalid file system object being passed to the game configuration, leading to runtime errors when the game attempts to use the non-existent file system.

```fprisc
disk = device "blk";
fs = case blkPages disk == 0 of True -> 0 | False -> FS.serve disk;
```

In FP-RISC, a configuration object is constructed using the `case` expression to conditionally initialize the file system based on device state, then wrapped into a record with the required fields. The `MV.game` function accepts this record along with a game loop tuple to start the simulation.

```fprisc
cfg = {
  env = (me, fs),
  mt = mt,
  tick = 160000,
  input = gl,
  render = gl
};
```

The `srvPage` function orchestrates a complete HTTP request-response cycle by first extracting the client's session identifier from the incoming cookie, defaulting to a new session ID if the cookie is missing. It then renders the current model using the view (`vw`) and the model itself, generates the necessary HTML with styling and timing data, and finally constructs a response that includes the updated session state and a fresh session ID for the next request.

```fpr
srvPage mt cfg app sv cid req =
  App ini upd sbs vw = app;
  Sv model sess nsid ltt bufs = sv;
  sid0 = cookieSid req;
  sid = case sid0 > 0 of True -> sid0 | False -> nsid;
  nsid2 = case sid0 > 0 of True -> nsid | False -> nsid + 1;
  tree = vw model;
  (s, d) = G.render tree;
  html = page cfg.css tree s d (tickMsOf (sbs model));
  srvSend mt cfg app (Sv model (sessPut sess sid s d) nsid2 ltt bufs)
          cid (httpRespC "200 OK" "text/html" sid html) 0.
```

When an actor is killed using `kill`, any subsequent `receive` calls on that actor's mailbox return immediately with a value of `0`. This allows the system to detect that the target actor no longer exists without raising an error or blocking. For example, after spawning a counter and killing it, sending a message to the dead actor is a silent no-op, but calling `receive` on the same actor yields `0`:

```fprisc
c = spawn counter;
_ = kill c;
_ = receive c;  -- Returns 0
```

The `kill` keyword terminates an actor, removing it from the system so that any subsequent messages sent to it are silently dropped without triggering an error. This is demonstrated in the code where `kill c` stops the `counter` actor; the following `send c (me, 100)` results in no action, as the actor no longer exists to receive the message.

```fprisc
_ = kill c;
_ = send c (me, 100);
```

