  i = case hasB of True -> 1 | False -> 0;
  ...
```

The `Sys.growLog` function records system events, such as actor spawns and stack grants, into a lock-free ring buffer that avoids traditional locking, printing, or dynamic memory allocation at the write site. This design adheres to the instrumentation-poison law, ensuring that the ring buffer can be accessed by ordinary code via `Sys.growLog` without disrupting the performance-critical path of the system. As demonstrated in the `growlog.fpr` example, a program can spawn an actor, retrieve the event log, and analyze its length or contents using standard functional constructs like list traversal.

```fpr
main =
  a = spawn child;
  evs = Sys.growLog 0;
  n = lgLen evs;
  "Event count: " ++ show n
```

