The `splicedAll` function takes an initial array and a list of patches, applying them sequentially in order. Each patch is a triplet defining a start index, an end index, and a new array of strings to insert. The function iterates through the patches, updating a running copy of the array (`cur`) with each splice operation until the final modified array is returned.

```fprisc
function splicedAll(old, patches) \{
    if (!Array.isArray(patches)) throw new Error('invalid structural patch list');
    var cur = old;
    for (var i = 0; i < patches.length; i++) cur = spliced(cur, patches[i]);
    return cur;
}
```

The `splicedAll` function iterates through a list of patches, applying each one sequentially to the current array state without modifying the original input. Each patch consists of three elements: a start index, a deletion count, and an array of strings to insert. The function initializes a copy of the old array and updates it by calling `spliced` for every patch in the provided list.

```fprisc
function splicedAll(old, patches) {
    if (!Array.isArray(patches)) throw new Error('invalid structural patch list');
    var cur = old;
    for (var i = 0; i < patches.length; i++) cur = spliced(cur, patches[i]);
    return cur;
}
```

