The `measure` function calculates a `Box`'s dimensions by first determining the total width and height of its children using the `msum` function, which performs a single traversal to sum along-axis values, take the maximum of cross-axis values, and count the items. It then adds the padding and gaps to these calculated inner dimensions. Specifically, `msum` iterates through the children, accumulating widths and heights based on whether the box is a row or column, while `measure` wraps this logic to return the final sized tuple including padding.

```fpr
measure (Box a kids) =
  p = padOf a; g = gapOf a;
  (mw, mh, n) = msum (isRow a) kids;
  gaps = case n > 1 of True -> g * (n - 1) | False -> 0;
  inner = case isRow a of
    True -> (mw + gaps, mh)
  | False -> (mw, mh + gaps);
  sized a (iw + 2 * p, ih + 2 * p)
```

