The system pins worst-case behavior entirely to the deterministic tier, which manages aging and FIFO stealing, while the randomized tier only shapes expected fairness weights. To demonstrate this, one can define a Sol function that configures the scheduler with a specific aging bound (`schedSetTau`) and verifies liveness and stealing metrics against the hardware triple.

```sol
fun demo_pin_tier(
    fprc_v: fprc,
    qos_v: qos,
    hw_v: hw,
    tau: u32
) -> (liveness: bool, max_wait: u32, steals: u32) {
    // Configure deterministic aging bound
    schedSetTau(tau);

    // Simulate burst phase to trigger deterministic stealing
    burst_flood();

    // Retrieve metrics maintained by the admission path
    let max_wait = schedMaxWait;
    let steals = schedSteals;

    // Verify bounds: max_wait <= tau + residents
    let liveness = ping_pong_complete(8, 200);

    return (liveness, max_wait, steals);
}
```

