The previous harness design leaked server ports because its cleanup logic, a `finally` block, could not survive if the harness itself was killed by a sweep timeout or user interruption. The improved design places the server in its own process group, allowing it to handle termination signals (SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP) and perform cleanup on exit, while also sweeping a pidfile to handle any remaining cases where those signals do not cover the cleanup.

```fprisc
// Conceptual example of the improved harness structure
process-group {
  server {
    // Server logic here
  }
}

// Cleanup triggered on termination signals
on-sigterm, on-sigint, on-sighup {
  cleanup()
}

// Fallback for cases not covered by signals
on-exit {
  sweep-pidfile()
}
```

The server leaves a port open on the QOS server because the cleanup does not survive the harness being killed. The updated implementation places the server in its own process group to handle SIGTERM, SIGINT, and SIGHUP for cleanup, and it sweeps a pidfile on startup to handle the remaining case where those signals do not cover the termination.

```fprisc
// Example of the old behavior causing a leak
harness: run {
  finally {
    // This block is skipped if the harness process dies
    // e.g., via sweep timeout or user interrupt
    close_port(port)
  }
}
```

The harness now runs in its own process group, allowing it to clean up on SIGTERM, SIGINT, and SIGHUP signals, and it sweeps a pidfile on startup to handle cases where those signals are not received.

