The `advance` function first checks the `cfg.journal` flag; if true, it calls `journal` with the application codec and database to record the event before updating the model. This ensures the event is journaled first, followed by the application update, persistence of the new state, and finally broadcasting the version increment to all sessions.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
  _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
  (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      quit = perform me sessions cmds;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      (if quit then _ = settle app.fields db model2 saved2; _ = goodbye (M.values sessions); O.None
       else O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2)))).
```

The `live.fpr` file defines the core state transition logic for the QOS operating system's live model update mechanism. It orchestrates the sequence where an event is first journaled, its effects are applied to the model, and then the updated state is broadcast to all active sessions. If the update triggers a quit condition, the system settles the state and terminates; otherwise, it restarts the tickers with the new version number and told state.

```sol
advance : Int -> Ev m -> Rs -> O.Option Rs .
advance me ev st = case st of Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
  _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
  (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      quit = perform me sessions cmds;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      (if quit then _ = settle app.fields db model2 saved2; _ = goodbye (M.values sessions); O.None
       else O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2)))).
```

The `advance` function first journals the event if the configuration requires it, then updates the application model to generate commands. It persists the new model state, broadcasts the incremented version to all sessions, and finally either settles the state or initiates tickers for the next cycle.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of
    Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
        _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
        (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
            saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
            quit = perform me sessions cmds;
            told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
