The `writeBuf` function recursively processes a list of pages, terminating immediately if the list is empty (`Nil`) by returning the current page count, otherwise it calls `writeBuf2` to perform a bulk write of a specific page followed by a recursive call to write the remaining pages. The `compact` function manages the log structure by scanning records, determining which entries to keep or delete, and then using `cpDo` to update the device's metadata and rebuild the root, ensuring the log remains consistent and space-efficient.

```sol
compact dev head =
  ris = scanRIs dev head;
  ld = lastDels ris;
  kept = kGo ris ld (kaGo ris ld Nil) Nil;
  cpDo dev (metaOr dev head) (bufPages dev kept Nil) (mkRoot (lvGo dev kept Nil)).
```

The `qlog.fpr` file defines the core logic for managing the write-ahead log, including the `writeBuf` and `writeBuf2` functions for persisting data and the `compact` function for reclaiming space by removing deleted records. It also implements the boot-scan index mechanism, which creates an incremental index of live log chunks to optimize reads from sequential disk walks into index walks.

```fpr
writeBuf : unsafe dv -> Int -> List String -> Int .
writeBuf dev p pages = case pages of Nil -> p | pg :: r -> writeBuf2 dev p r pg.
writeBuf2 dev p r pg =
  _ = blkWrite dev p pg;
  writeBuf dev (p + 1) r.

compact dev head =
  ris = scanRIs dev head;
  ld = lastDels ris;
  kept = kGo ris ld (kaGo ris ld Nil) Nil;
  cpDo dev (metaOr dev head) (bufPages dev kept Nil) (mkRoot (lvGo dev kept Nil)).
```

