A program can use the `watcher` function to continuously monitor a list of file paths defined in a configuration. The `watcher` checks the modification time and size of these files using `OS.stat`. If a change is detected, it executes the `rebuild` function; if successful, it sends a `Reload` signal to the client. Here is a minimal example:

```fprisc
import Std; import Live;

cfg = { watch = ["src/main.sol"], rebuild = [Sol.compile], restart = Nil };
watcher (Live.new "127.0.0.1" 8080) cfg Nil (Live.new "127.0.0.1" 8080);
```

The file `fprisc/std/live.fpr` defines the `live` module, which implements a hot-reloading system for FP-RISC programs. It monitors a list of source files using the `stamps` function to detect changes, and upon detecting a modification, it executes a user-provided `rebuild` script to update the running program. The `watcher` function orchestrates this process by sleeping for 300 milliseconds, comparing the current file timestamps against the last seen state, and triggering a rebuild if a change is detected or if the previous rebuild failed.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

