When the font size (`fsOf a`) is positive, `textSize` calculates width and height using proportional metrics derived from the advance table (`propW`, `propH`), while a non-zero character cap (`ellipOf a`) may override the width if the calculated width exceeds the cap. If the font size is zero or negative, it falls back to fixed-size dimensions based on the script scale (`scOf a`). The function also enforces the cap constraint by returning the cap value for the width if the proportional width exceeds it.

```sol
textSize a n t =
  e = fsOf a;
  (w, h) = case e > 0 of
    True -> (propW t e, propH e)
  | False -> (textW n (scOf a), textH (scOf a));
  cap = ellipOf a;
  case cap > 0 and w > cap of True -> (cap, h) | False -> (w, h).
```

