A client connects using `Tcp.connect`, writes a message string containing "bye" to the connection, reads the entire response with `Stream.readAll`, and calculates the byte length of the result string. This pattern mirrors the `client` and final `main` logic in the reference, where a connection is established, data is sent, echoed data is read back, and its size is printed after the server stops listening.

```fprisc
client port n =
  conn = (case Tcp.connect "127.0.0.1" port of Ok c -> c | Err e -> error e);
  _ = Stream.write "hello {n}\nsecond line\nbye\n" conn;
  all = Stream.readAll conn;
  _ = print "received length: {(case all of Ok b -> String.length b | Err _ -> 0)}";
  _ = Stream.close conn;
  all.
```

The server is created by calling `Tcp.listen` with the address and port, extracting the port number via `Tcp.port`, and spawning a process that sends the connection handler to itself. The handler processes each line from the client stream, converting it to uppercase unless it is "bye", which terminates the connection.

```sol
main =
  l = (case Tcp.listen "127.0.0.1" 0 of Ok x -> x | Err e -> error e);
  port = Tcp.port l;
  me = myself 0;
  _ = spawn (fn self -> send me (Tcp.serve l echo));
  ...
```

