The acceptor actor is responsible for polling the socket tier. Upon first contact, it spawns a connection actor on one of the harts above 0 and hands each connection's bytes to that specific actor. Once the connection actor posts its connection id to the close queue, the acceptor closes the socket.

```sol
// Pseudocode illustrating the acceptor's polling and spawning logic
actor acceptor {
  on poll_socket() {
    let conn_bytes = read_from_socket();
    if (conn_bytes is new_connection) {
      spawn_connection_actor(conn_bytes);
    }
    hand_bytes(conn_bytes, target_connection_actor);
  }
}
```

The acceptor actor is the sole entity interacting with the socket tier, responsible for polling connections, spawning a dedicated connection actor upon first contact, and managing socket closure. In contrast, each connection actor independently owns its session data, parses frames, and pushes deltas to the central register.
```sol
// Example of a connection actor receiving bytes
actor conn = new {
  receive bytes {
    // Process bytes and parse frames
    self.update(frame_boundary)
  }
}
```

The system will fail to scale because the number of sessions is limited by available memory, not by the number of sockets or harts. If an actor is spawned on every poll, the memory footprint grows linearly with the number of polls rather than the number of unique peers, causing the system to exhaust memory quickly. This violates the architectural requirement that "sessions scale with memory, not with a table," as each unique peer should own exactly one actor regardless of how many times the socket is polled.

```sol
// Incorrect: Spawning on every poll leads to O(poll_count) actors per peer
actor bad_handler {
  on poll() {
    spawn ConnectionActor() // Creates a new process for every poll
  }
}

// Correct: Spawn only once per connection
actor good_handler {
  on poll() {
    if (!connected) {
      connected = true
      spawn ConnectionActor() // Creates exactly one process per peer
    }
  }
}
```

