To create a Sol script for a QOS application, you initialize the system by defining a device, loading an FP-RISC file into the memory system, and spawning a driver function that communicates through a port. You then configure the application with the loaded file, port, and memory settings, and run it using the MV.run function. Finally, you clean up resources by killing the port.

```sol
me = myself 0;
fs = FS.serve (device "blk");
ld = LD.serve fs;
r0 = LD.load me ld "edit1";
_ = case r0 of Ok u -> 0 | Err e -> error "__NAME__ load edit1: {e}";
clint = device "clint";
mt = reg32 clint 49144;
port = spawn MV.port;
_ = spawn (driver port);
cfg = { env = (ld, port), mt = mt, tick = 3000, input = 0 };
r = MV.run me cfg;
_ = kill port;
r.
```

The `case` expression performs pattern matching on the result type returned by `LD.load`. It distinguishes between two possible outcomes: a successful load returning an integer (`Ok u`) or an error (`Err e`). If the result matches `Ok u`, the code executes `0`; otherwise, it executes `error "__NAME__ load edit1: {e}"` to handle the failure case explicitly.

```sol
r0 = LD.load me ld "edit1";
_ = case r0 of Ok u -> 0 | Err e -> error "__NAME__ load edit1: {e}";
```

When `LD.load` fails, it returns an `Err` value containing the error message, which is caught by the `case` expression in the `main` script to trigger a specific error handling routine. The script defines the error message as `"__NAME__ load edit1: {e}"`, where `{e}` is replaced by the actual error details from the `LD.load` result.

```sol
ld = LD.serve fs;
r0 = LD.load me ld "edit1";
_ = case r0 of Ok u -> 0 | Err e -> error "__NAME__ load edit1: {e}";
```

The `notesRun` function initializes a new process by opening a console and checks for storage write capabilities, then sets up a TUI screen with a custom buffer. It calls `notesHello` to display a status message indicating whether storage is available and, if so, the count of existing notes loaded from the storage device.

```fpr
notesRun con stg =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = P.putLn con "";
  _ = P.putLn con "  == QOS Notes ==   (a loaded process)";
  _ = notesHello con stg;
  _ = TUI.enterScreen (P.puts con);
  nFrame con me stg Nil 0 "" (TUI.invalidBuf 80 24).
```

The `notesRun` function establishes a process identity, clears the console, displays a header, and enters the TUI screen using the provided console and storage capability. It then calls `notesHello` to report whether the storage capability was granted; if denied, it indicates the notes are session-only, otherwise it replays key-value data to display the current note count on disk.

```sol
notesRun con stg =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = P.putLn con "";
  _ = P.putLn con "  == QOS Notes ==   (a loaded process)";
  _ = notesHello con stg;
  _ = TUI.enterScreen (P.puts con);
  nFrame con me stg Nil 0 "" (TUI.invalidBuf 80 24);
```

The `acceptor` actor polls a clock register, drains its port, and processes incoming connections via `netPoll`. When a connection arrives, it reads the data, spawns a new actor using `actorFor`, and sends the data to that actor. If the received data is empty, the slot is deleted via `mDel` to free the connection. The actor then increments its slot counter and loops back to check for new connections or idle ticks.

```fpr
accPoll me st cid =
  As reg port cfg app map k ltt mt tickMs = st;
  bytes = netRead cid;
  (a, map2) = actorFor reg port cfg app map cid k;
  _ = send a (Data bytes);
  map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
  accNext me (As reg port cfg app map3 (k + 1) ltt mt tickMs).
```

