A panic in a worker causes the entire program to panic immediately, rather than returning a list of results. Since work that can fail must answer a `Result` to avoid crashing the system, the `worker` function must ensure it never panics; instead, it should send an `Err` value via `A.sendSure` if an error occurs. For example, if the function `f` fails on input `x`, the worker must handle this condition and send an error result instead of letting the exception propagate.

```fpr
worker parent f i x self = 
  try_res = try (f x) with panic;
  _ = A.sendSure self parent (Ok (i, try_res));
```

