The `xbGo` function implements SPI mode 0 by iterating through bits of the input byte, where the clock idles low, data is valid while the clock is low, and both sides sample on the rising edge. For each bit, it writes the data bit to `mosi`, sets `sck` to 1 (rising edge) to sample the `miso` line, reads the result, sets `sck` to 0 (falling edge) to prepare for the next bit, and accumulates the received value. The correct sequence for one byte is: write `mosi`, set `sck` high, read `miso`, set `sck` low, repeating until all bits are processed.

```sol
bus = Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso;
b = 0b10110001;
acc = 0;
i = 7;
while (i >= 0) do
  pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  pinW sck 0;
  acc = acc * 2 + r;
  i = i - 1;
end;
```

The `xferByte` function initializes the SPI bus configuration and executes a recursive bit-transfer loop. It drives the MOSI pin with the inverted bit value (active low), toggles the SCK pin to trigger sampling on the rising edge, reads the MISO pin, and accumulates the result by left-shifting the accumulator and adding the sampled bit.

```sol
xferByte bus b = xbGo bus b 7 0.

xbGo bus b i acc =
  case i < 0 of
    True -> acc
  | False -> xbBit bus b i acc.

xbBit bus b i acc =
  Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso = bus;
  _ = pinW mosi (bitOf b i);
  _ = pinW sck 1;
  r = pinR miso;
  _ = pinW sck 0;
  xbGo bus b (i - 1) (acc * 2 + r).
```

