The `doAfter` function calculates the absolute deadline by adding the requested delay in milliseconds to the current clock tick, then inserts this new deadline into a sorted priority queue to maintain the oldest-first order. If the timer is the nearest event (`nat == 1`), it arms the CLINT hardware to fire immediately; otherwise, it schedules a child sleep. The `fireDue` function subsequently checks the queue and processes any entries with zero remaining time (expired deadlines) instantly, bypassing the normal wait for the next hardware interrupt.

```sol
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
case nat == 1 of True -> armNearest clk pend2 | False -> sleepChild me ms;
fireDue me clk nat pend2;
```

The deadline is calculated by adding the message duration in milliseconds to the current clock value, scaled by the system's 10 MHz tick rate (10,000 ticks per millisecond). This deadline is then inserted into the deadline-sorted pending list to maintain oldest-first ordering, ensuring that entries with zero duration or passed deadlines are fired immediately upon the next system poke.

```sol
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
```

