CLAIM-THEN-MASK is a strategy used to prevent infinite loops caused by level-triggered interrupts where the signal remains high after the initial service. When a source is claimed, the PLIC immediately masks it to stop re-pending the interrupt. After the actor servicing the device completes its work, it re-arms the source via Sys.irqAck; if the device signal is still high, the gateway re-pends the interrupt, ensuring the interrupt is delivered exactly once per acknowledgment without causing storms.

```fpr
// Pseudo-code illustrating the claim then mask flow
hal_irq_claim(source) {
    // Drain masks the source at claim time
    plic_mask(source); 
}

// After servicing the device
Sys.irqAck(source) {
    // Actor re-arms the source if the line is still high
    plic_unmask(source);
}
```

If a level-triggered source remains high during servicing, the drain loop would spin delivering the same interrupt forever because the source re-pends immediately upon completion, preventing the actor from being scheduled again. To prevent this storm, the system employs a CLAIM-THEN-MASK discipline: the drain masks the source at the moment of claim, and the actor must re-arm it via Sys.irqAck after servicing. Only if the source line is still high at re-arm time does the gateway pend again, ensuring exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```fpr
# Example of the drain masking logic during claim
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    # Source is masked here to prevent re-pending while draining
    Sys.irqMask(source_id);
}

# Example of the actor re-arming logic after service
Sys.irqAck(source_id) {
    # Source is re-armed here; if still high, gateway will pend again
    Sys.irqUnmask(source_id);
}
```

A QOS-x86_64 application accesses resources exclusively through the effect table, which serves as its runtime capability surface, replacing any need for libc or syscalls. Thread-local cells are implemented as plain globals loaded RIP-relative, ensuring correctness in a single-hart environment where the image is fixed and loaded at a published constant address rather than extracted from a symbol table. Consequently, the application code compiles to bare-metal-like instructions that interact solely with these static tables and globals, guaranteeing isolation and portability across different QOS builds.

```fpr
# Example: Accessing a capability via the effect table and a global
@effect_table[0] := 0x1000; # Load capability from table entry
@my_global := @effect_table[0]; # Access as a plain global
```

The `fpr_g_` import set remains the static one exactly as on bare metal because the app image contains no libc and no syscalls; instead, all effects are routed through a capability table whose entries define the app's runtime capability surface.

```sol
// Example of a static import set entry matching the FPR_QOSAPP declaration
import fpr_g_init : @fpr_init;
```

An app image built for QOS contains no libc and no syscalls; all effects must go through the capability table, and link-time imports remain static as on bare metal. Consequently, codegen never emits raw syscalls, and the app cannot access the host's TLS block via `%fs` or rely on dynamic relocation because fixed-slot loading publishes a constant slot address (0x40000000) rather than extracting one from a symbol table. If an app attempts to use thread-local storage or expects addresses to change, it will fail because the three thread-local cells are lowered to plain globals loaded RIP-relative, assuming a single-hart environment with no dynamic loader.

```fpr
// Example: Correct usage of global constants in a QOS app
const QOS_SLOT_BASE = 0x40000000;
let my_ptr = load_global(QOS_SLOT_BASE, offset);
```

The `fpr_arc_incref` function is called exactly twice for every escaped value: once during a `send` promotion where the message becomes shared, and once during a `spawn` promotion where the entry closure is pinned into a child actor. Crucially, this promotion also pins the owning slab itself by incrementing an `escaped++` counter; if the last escapee of an orphaned slab is dropped, the entire slab is freed.

```fpr
// Conceptual example of incrementing the slab escape counter
fn promote_to_shared(msg: Value) -> Result<(), Error> {
    fpr_arc_incref(msg)
    // The slab owning 'msg' is automatically pinned here via escaped++
    send(msg)
}
```

According to the ARC invariant, ordinary computation performs zero reference counting; values are slab-allocated and owned by their actor. `fpr_arc_incref` is invoked exclusively at promotion sites, specifically when a value is sent to become shared. The following Sol function illustrates the `send` promotion site where the reference count is incremented before the message is dispatched.

```sol
fun fpr_arc_send(value: Any, target: Actor) -> Unit {
  // Promotion site 1: send
  fpr_arc_incref(value);
  // Dispatch the message to the target actor
  target.send(value);
}
```

The `bigfree` tier is an exact-fit LIFO recycler designed exclusively for blocks larger than the bucket ceiling of 8 KiB, whereas the bucket recycler handles allocations up to that 8 KiB limit using fixed size classes. When a freed block exceeds 8 KiB, it bypasses the bucket system and returns directly to the `bigfree` list; conversely, smaller blocks return to their specific bucket. For example, in `tests/bigfree.fpr`, building large vectors returns blocks to `bigfree` to maintain flat arena usage, while smaller allocations utilize the bucket system.

```fprisc
if (block_size > 8192) {
    return_to_bigfree(block);
} else {
    return_to_bucket(block);
}
```

