fn actors() : void { ... }
```

`machine/virt/devtable.h` defines the boundary between the machine layer and higher-level HALs by resolving device names against the machine's two built-in entries and any externally defined `hal_devtable_ext` entries. It provides no registry or capacity management, ensuring that if a HAL is not provided via `EXTRA_RT`, the resulting image lacks drivers and interrupts.

```sol
// machine/virt/devtable.h
// Resolves "device" names against machine entries and optional HAL extensions.
// No registry or capacity is managed here.

def hal_devtable_ext = empty;

def device "name" = 
    lookup machine_entries(name)
    || lookup hal_devtable_ext(name);
```

The image fails at link time because its imports serve as its capability manifest; any reference to a primitive supplied by none of these layers results in a failure on the `fpr_g_` name. For example, if a module declares `blkRead` but the current tree does not implement it (relying on QOS instead), the build will fail:
```fpr
module BadApp {
    import blkRead; // Error: blkRead is not defined in this tree
}
```

A program reaches external hardware by declaring needed primitives as signatures with no definition in its code, while the `fpr build --with driver.c` command links the C implementation of those primitives. If a program references a primitive that no module supplies, the build fails at link time with an error on the `fpr_g_` name.

```sol
