The test ensures safety by publishing a Watch to the poller before announcing registration, forcing the poller to wait for both announcements before processing a stop. This mechanism prevents new registrations from racing past a stop contract. The test spawns two waiters, verifies both are cancelled, then stops the poller and checks that neither waiter was prematurely cancelled, proving the ordering constraint holds.

```fpr
waiter p fd boss me = case p of P.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

The error "first waiter not cancelled" or "second waiter not cancelled" occurs because the stop sequence requires the `waiter` function to receive both the Watch announcement and the boss registration message before it can return `Unit`. If the poller is stopped before these messages are delivered, the waiter threads remain blocked on `receiveFrom`, and the subsequent `check` assertions fail. The `waiter` function explicitly waits for `receiveFrom me a` and `receiveFrom me b` before proceeding to send the cancellation signal to the boss.

```sol
check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled";
```

The `one` function spawns two identical waiter processes, waits for both to complete their registration logic, and then stops the poller. It subsequently verifies that both spawned waiters were successfully cancelled by checking their return values, ensuring no race conditions allow new registrations to slip past the stop contract.

```sol
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

