The new automatic ARC subset lifts lambdas to named functions with captured values made explicit as arguments, whereas the existing general runtime supports partial application via a PAP containing supplied arguments where saturation calls the function and over-application applies remaining arguments to its result. The ARC mode does not yet support ownership-aware PAPs and maintains partial applications without introducing a separate closure mechanism.

```sol
// Existing general runtime behavior (conceptual)
let f = fpr_applyN (lambda (x y) (+ x y)) 5
// PAP contains 5; if called with 2 args: (5 + 10) = 15

// New ARC subset behavior (conceptual)
let add = lift_to_named (lambda (x y) (+ x y))
let add_explicit = add 5 10
// Lambda is lifted; captured values are explicit arguments
```

The program will fail to pass because the example relies on normal library imports and a real readiness watcher, not on fake process implementations. Since `std/os` currently pulls in wrappers for every facility including unsupported processes, omitting real implementations prevents the system from functioning correctly.
```fpr
