The QOS HAL distinguishes these implementations by providing a unified interface defined in `qos_abi.h` while sourcing the actual function pointers from different directories depending on the target platform. For native systems, the interface is backed by drivers located in `hal/virt/` such as `plic.fpr`, `net.c`, and `blk.c`. Conversely, for portable systems running on a host OS, the interface is backed by raw drivers in `hal/unix/` like `gfx.c` and `net_raw.c`. This separation allows the same application code to link against the same ABI table (`qos_hal_t`) regardless of whether it is executing on a virtual board or a host machine.

```fpr
// Conceptual representation of the dual backing structure
// Native (virt) vs Portable (host) drivers mapping to the same ABI table

hal/virt/net.c      // Virtio net driver
hal/unix/net_raw.c  // Host raw net driver

// Both feed into the same table structure
qos_hal_t hal_table; 
```

The program fails at link time with an error on the `fpr_g_` name. This happens because the compiler is handed the signature from `core/foreign.fpr` as `FPR_FOREIGN` and treats the types as external; if the corresponding implementation is missing from the HAL table for the target system, the linker cannot resolve the symbol.

```fpr
// Example: Attempting to use a graphics primitive on a system
