FP-RISC uses reference counting (RC) for memory management instead of a garbage collector, combined with lambda lifting. The actor system relies on fuel for cooperative yielding; when an actor consumes all its fuel, it must explicitly yield control back to the scheduler.
```fp-risc
let fuel = 10
actor run() {
  if (fuel > 0) {
    fuel = fuel - 1;
    // perform work
  } else {
    yield(); // returns control to the scheduler when fuel is exhausted
  }
}
```

The actor will fail to execute further operations and must yield control to the scheduler, as the actor model depends on fuel for cooperative yielding. If an actor runs out of fuel, it cannot continue executing; it is designed to stop and return control so the scheduler can manage other actors.

```fprisc
actor main() {
    let x = 1;
    // If x is computed and no yield occurs, fuel depletes
    // The actor stops here and yields to the scheduler
}
```

In FP-RISC, actors implement cooperative yielding by consuming a fuel counter during execution; when this fuel reaches zero, the actor must explicitly invoke the `yield` command to return control to the scheduler. This mechanism is enforced by the actor model's design and does not require monadic wrappers or runtime garbage collection.

```fprisc
actor main() {
    let fuel = 10;
    while (fuel > 0) {
        // Perform work
        fuel = fuel - 1;
        if (fuel == 0) {
            yield; // Explicitly yield control when fuel is exhausted
        }
    }
}
```

