To manage a file descriptor for a set number of interrupts, the program must spawn a poller, create a waiter thread for that file descriptor, and then recursively recycle the file descriptor's watch state while decrementing a counter. The `cycles` function demonstrates this pattern by starting a poller, spawning a waiter, and then recursively calling itself with a decremented counter until the target number of cycles is reached.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = PL.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = PL.start Unit;
  _ = PL.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

The system will fail the assertion check `check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled"`. This happens because spawning two waiters for the same file descriptor causes both to compete for the interrupt signal; when the interrupt occurs, only one waiter receives the cancellation notification while the other remains active, violating the requirement that exactly one waiter must be cancelled. The code example below demonstrates the correct pattern where the first waiter is stopped and cancelled before the second is spawned:

```fprisc
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  _ = drop (receiveFrom me a); -- Ensure first waiter is fully cleaned up
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled";
  _ = PL.stop p;
```

