When the `depth` parameter is greater than or equal to `md`, the `build` function immediately returns a `Leaf` node containing the column with the maximum gain (`argmaxL counts`), without performing any further recursive splits. This is because the pattern `case depth >= md of True -> ...` triggers first, forcing the function to construct a terminal leaf node and return the remaining vectors (`c0`, `c1`, `ys2`) directly. Consequently, the tree construction stops prematurely at that depth, ignoring any potential need to split further even if the index list length (`n`) or other conditions would normally justify deeper recursion.

```fprisc
build ps bn c0 c1 ys depth idxs =
  P bins md mg ml k = ps;
  n = List.len idxs;
  (counts, ys2) = ccounts ys idxs k;
  case depth >= md of
    True -> (Leaf (argmaxL counts), c0, c1, ys2)
  | False -> ...
```

In the proposed design, `Result` is used when a failure operation has something meaningful to say about the error, whereas `Option` is used when absence is ordinary. `Option` is unified as a single prelude type `Option (Some x | None)`, while `Result` remains distinct for cases requiring specific error details. Panic is reserved exclusively for unwrapping operations that should not fail, such as `unwrap` or `!`.

