The system fails because every SoA operation returns a tuple, and the object pool is only returned to the main thread upon actor death. If an actor survives past its frame, it retains references to objects from the pool, causing memory leaks or undefined behavior in subsequent frames. To ensure stability, each frame must run in a fresh actor that dies immediately after completion, while the driver retains only stable Vec handles and scalar model data exchanged as flat pinned message roots.

```fpr
actor main {
    // Correct: Actor dies after frame, pool released on death
    poll(); update(); physics(); render(); draw();
    die(); // Pool returned here, safe for next frame
}

// Incorrect: Actor lives across frames, pool not released
actor main {
    poll(); update(); physics(); render(); draw();
    // If this loop repeats without 'die()', the pool is never returned
    poll(); update(); physics(); render(); draw();
}
```

