Fuel serves as a cooperative execution quantum that ensures every unbounded control path passes a safepoint. It is consumed at specific sites within the generated code, runtime, and HAL layers to enforce these safepoints, thereby preventing infinite loops and enabling precise Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) modeling.

```fprisc
// Example of a function that consumes fuel at a safepoint
def loop_safe(fuel: u64) -> u64:
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < 1000:
        if fuel == 0:
            break
        fuel := fuel - 1
        i := i + 1
    return i
```

The `fuel_safepoint` instruction is the mandatory gate for all unbounded control paths; a function that never reaches it is considered to have leaked fuel and violates the cooperative execution invariant. In the Sol scripting profile, a function body is typically wrapped in a loop that calls `fuel_safepoint` before any potentially infinite logic, ensuring the runtime can reclaim resources.

```sol
// Example: A valid infinite loop that passes the safepoint
while (true) {
    fuel_safepoint; // Required here before any unbounded work
    // ... perform work ...
}
```

