The `watcher` function implements a hot-reloading mechanism that periodically checks if any files in the `watch` list have changed. It uses a 300-millisecond sleep interval and compares the current file modification times (captured via `stamps`) against a previously recorded state (`seen`). If no changes are detected, it recursively calls itself with the same state; if a change is detected, it executes the `rebuild` script, sends a reload signal to the server upon successful execution, and updates the recorded state to the new modification times to prevent redundant rebuilds.

```fpr
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

