The `orbit` function defines a recursive tail-call pattern where the base case occurs when the iteration counter `k` is less than or equal to zero, returning an empty list. For subsequent iterations, it calculates the next position and velocity by applying a gravitational force model based on the current distance from the origin, then recursively calls itself with the decremented counter and updated state. The function reconstructs the final list by prepending a point to the recursive result only when the current iteration index is a multiple of the `every` parameter.

```sol
orbit k every x y vx vy | k <= 0 = [].
orbit k every x y vx vy =
  r2 = x * x + y * y;
  r3 = r2 * Num.sqrt r2;
  vx2 = vx - x / r3 * 0.02;
  vy2 = vy - y / r3 * 0.02;
  x2 = x + vx2 * 0.02;
  y2 = y + vy2 * 0.02;
  rest = orbit (k - 1) every x2 y2 vx2 vy2;
  case (k % every) == 0 of
    True -> (x, y) :: rest
  | False -> rest.
```

