Autodrop is a compiler feature that automatically discards message roots once they have been read, provided they match common shapes. This enforcement of the "drop what you receive" law ensures that linear resources are not retained longer than necessary without manual intervention.

```fprisc
# Example: The compiler automatically drops the root of this message
# after the actor reads the value, adhering to autodrop rules.
actor A {
  receive (msg) {
    let x = msg.value; // Compiler discharges root here
    // x is dropped at the end of the scope automatically
  }
}
```

This rule exists because FP-RISC is a non-GC language where the runtime does not manage memory collection, ensuring zero pauses and keeping Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) compositional. The compiler discharges common shapes via autodrop, while the ARC.qa auditor provides a backstop for complex types.
```fprisc
// Example: The root of this message is dropped here.
msg = receive();
use(msg); // Compiler discharges msg; no GC needed.
```

