The `waking` function serves as the recursive core of the interrupt handling loop. It receives a message from the OS watcher, checks if the message is `0` (indicating the interrupt has cleared), and if so, unbinds the IRQ and signals the main actor that the waker has stopped. If the interrupt is still active, it acknowledges the IRQ and re-sends a `Woke` message to keep the loop running.

```sol
waking : unsafe Int -> Int -> Int -> Unit .
waking poller irq self =
  m = receive self;
  done = m == 0;
  _ = drop m;
  if done then
    _ = unbind irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller WakerStopped;
    Unit
  else
    _ = Sys.irqAck irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller Woke;
    waking poller irq self.
```

The `waking` function detects that the interrupt is no longer active by checking if the received message equals `0`. If this condition is met, it unbinds the interrupt using `Sys.irqUnbind`, sends a `WakerStopped` message back to the actor to signal completion, and returns the unit value `Unit` to exit the loop. If the message is not `0`, it acknowledges the interrupt and loops back to wait for the next wake event.

```sol
m = receive self;
done = m == 0;
if done then
  _ = unbind irq;
  _ = A.sendSure self poller WakerStopped;
  Unit
```

