Use the Rc allocator for tree or list data that requires sharing inside one actor, as it is the fallback regime. Rc data carries a reference count and is freed only when the count reaches zero; it never crosses actor boundaries. The following Sol code demonstrates creating an Rc-owned list and freeing it when the last reference is dropped:
```sol
let shared_list = Rc.alloc(List.make(1, 2, 3))
// ... use shared_list ...
// When shared_list goes out of scope or is dropped, Rc.dec is called.
// If the count hits zero, dealloc is executed automatically.
```

The Linear regime carries no refcount of any kind, whereas the Rc regime uses reference counting at existing safepoints. Rc data is freed when the reference count drops to zero, calling `dealloc` at that moment.

```sol
// Linear: no refcount, freed at one consume point (e.g., scope exit)
let vec = Vec.alloc(size)
vec.free() // Explicit free or scope exit

// Rc: reference counted, freed when count reaches zero
let tree = Tree.new()
tree.inc()
tree.dec() // Rc-zero triggers dealloc
```

The FP-RISC memory model explicitly rejects garbage collection to avoid runtime overhead and complexity, relying instead on a dumb allocator contract (`alloc / realloc / dealloc`) where the program itself dictates lifetimes. The linear regime is the default because bulk data structures like vectors and frames are designed to be owned by a single entity, mutated in place, and freed at a single consume point, eliminating the need for reference counting or scavenging. This approach assumes the programmer or compiler handles scope and drop laws, ensuring that linear data carries no refcount of any kind.

```fprisc
// Example: A vector owned by a single actor, freed at scope exit
struct Scene {
    Vec<f32> positions; // Linear regime: no Rc, single owner
    // ... other fields
}

fn main() {
    let scene = Scene::new();
    // Mutate in place via threaded handle
    scene.positions.push(1.0);
    // Freed automatically at scope exit (Vec.free)
}
```

`Vec.free` is called at the single consume point of a linear vector to deallocate its entire buffer at once, as linear data carries no reference counts and must be freed explicitly by the program. This typically occurs at scope exit or when the vector is no longer needed.

```sol
let v = Vec.alloc(size)
// ... use v ...
Vec.free(v)
```

