The `frameWorker` function implements a recursive game loop by repeatedly polling for new input and rendering the updated scene. It receives a message containing the current frame state, polls for 64 milliseconds of new input to determine movement, calculates the next player position using the `move` function, renders the scene via `renderFrame`, and sends the updated frame back to the parent process. The function then resets the system pool and recursively calls itself to continue the loop.

```fprisc
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  _ = drop message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The `mkRow` function generates a single data sample for the decision tree training set by creating two Gaussian random features (`x1`, `x2`), applying a deterministic axis-aligned rule to compute a true label (`ytrue`), and then introducing 5% label noise via a uniform random flip. It returns a `Numeric` record containing the features, the noisy label, and the state seed for reproducibility.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20; # 5% label noise
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

The `mkRow` function generates a single data point by first initializing a random state, then drawing Gaussian values for features `x1` and `x2`. It calculates a true label `ytrue` based on an axis-aligned rule (y=+1 if x1>0.25 and x2>-0.5) and introduces 5% noise by randomly flipping the label if a uniform random number is less than 20. Finally, it returns a record containing the features, the noisy label, and the updated random state.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20; # 5% label noise
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

The expression `d1 >= 500000` evaluates to `True` if the blocking sleep waited at least 50 milliseconds of CLINT time, resulting in the string `"ok"`. The expression `case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG"` evaluates to `"ok"` if the fire-and-forget deadline fires successfully and is collected from the mailbox, indicating the deadline was delivered to the caller's own mailbox rather than a different one.

```fpr
s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";
aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

To verify that a fire-and-forget timer event was delivered correctly, you must schedule the event using `T.after` and then retrieve it from your own mailbox using `receiveFrom` with the same service actor. The returned value must be the `TFire` type; any other value indicates a failure.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;

  _ = T.after svc 20;
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

Using `receiveFrom` to collect a fire-and-forget deadline is unnecessary and potentially incorrect because `T.after` schedules the event and returns immediately without blocking, whereas `receiveFrom` is intended for blocking collection of messages sent via `Sys.send`. The code in the example avoids this by assigning the result of `T.after` to a variable `f` and then using `case f` to check if the returned value is `TFire`, thereby correctly handling the immediate return value of the scheduling function rather than attempting to block on a mailbox receive.

```fpr
# Correct approach shown in timer.fpr
f = receiveFrom me svc;
aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";

# Incorrect approach (conceptual)
_ = T.after svc;
_ = receiveFrom me svc; # This blocks waiting for a message that was never sent
```

