The `accPoll` function acts as the polling loop for the acceptor actor. When a connection ID (`cid`) is received, it reads incoming data using `netRead`, spawns a new actor to handle that connection via `actorFor`, and sends the received data to it. If the data length is zero, indicating an end-of-file, the function deletes the corresponding slot in the map using `mDel` before incrementing the counter and proceeding to the next iteration.

```fpr
accPoll me st cid =
  As reg port cfg app map k ltt mt tickMs = st;
  bytes = netRead cid;
  (a, map2) = actorFor reg port cfg app map cid k;
  _ = send a (Data bytes);
  map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
  accNext me (As reg port cfg app map3 (k + 1) ltt mt tickMs).
```

The function `map2` returns the result of `closeAll` applied to the output of `drainPort`, which closes all sockets in the provided map. The function `map3` returns either the map with the specific CID (connection ID) removed if the received bytes length is zero (indicating EOF), or the original map unchanged otherwise.

```fpr
map2 = closeAll map (drainPort me port);
map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
```

The `accPoll` function is called as part of the `accLoop` iteration in the `acceptor` actor to handle incoming network connections. It takes three arguments: the `me` identifier, the current state `st`, and a connection identifier `cid`. If `cid` is 0, the function calls `accIdle` to wait for the next tick; otherwise, it reads bytes from the connection, spawns a new actor, sends the data, manages the connection map based on whether the message was an EOF, and increments the index before looping back.

```fpr
accPoll me st cid =
  As reg port cfg app map k ltt mt tickMs = st;
  bytes = netRead cid;
  (a, map2) = actorFor reg port cfg app map cid k;
  _ = send a (Data bytes);
  map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
  accNext me (As reg port cfg app map3 (k + 1) ltt mt tickMs).
```

