The `fib` function defines a standard recursive Fibonacci sequence calculation, serving as a CPU-bound task to measure performance across cores. The `worker` actor utilizes this function to compute a specific Fibonacci number (`fib n`) and then communicates the result along with core identifiers to a central `boss` actor via a message of type `R`. This mechanism allows the system to spawn multiple workers on different harts (cores) to execute the same computationally intensive job concurrently, enabling the measurement of parallel execution efficiency.

```fpr
fib n = if n < 2 then n else fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2).

worker boss k n me =
  c0 = Esp.core Unit;
  v = fib n;
  _ = send boss (R k c0 (Esp.core Unit) v);
  0.
```

To spawn actors on specific cores, use the `spawnOn` command with a core index derived from the `Sys.harts` type, which returns the number of available harts. You can spawn a list of jobs by iterating through a range and applying `spawnOn` to each index. To measure time, record the elapsed milliseconds using `Esp.ms` before and after the spawning and collection logic, then subtract the start time from the end time.

```fpr
t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
_ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
```

The `timed` function spawns multiple instances of the `worker` actor, each assigned to a specific core using `spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit)`, allowing the system to execute multiple CPU-bound jobs concurrently. After spawning, it collects the results from all workers and calculates the total elapsed time by subtracting the start timestamp from the current timestamp. This approach verifies that two cores can finish two jobs in approximately the time one core takes to finish one job.

```fpr
timed me label jobs n =
  t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
  _ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
  lines = collect me jobs Nil;
  ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
  _ = print "{label}: {jobs} x fib {n} in {ms} ms";
  ms.
```

