Page-out operations append a chunk record to the QLOG containing the number of pages, length, and the actor's chunk directory path. Page-in operations occur exclusively during the wake path when a sender's CAS operation transitions a paged actor from BLOCKED to READY; the hart's dequeue loop detects this PAGED status, re-reads the latest chunk from the specified directory, and switches the actor in before processing the message. Chunks are considered dead immediately upon reactivation or exit, making garbage collection a simple latest-wins sweep without compaction.

```sol
// Example of a page-out chunk record in QLOG grammar
QCHK <npages> <len> actorchunks/<actor-id>

// Example of a page-in trigger in the hart loop
if (sender.cas(actor, BLOCKED, READY) == OLD_BLOCKED) {
    // Hart loop detects PAGED status
    chunk = read_latest_chunk(actorchunks, actor.id);
    switch_in_actor(chunk);
}
```

When an actor is page-in, the system reads the latest chunk record for that actor's ID from the QLOG and appends nothing, leaving the old chunk dead. If the actor subsequently exits, that chunk is immediately considered dead and reclaimed by the ordinary latest-wins garbage collection sweep. Because chunks are short-lived and stored as whole records, no compaction is required; only pages are reclaimed.

```fprisc
// Example of a page-in operation that reads the latest chunk
// and appends nothing to the log
QCHK <npages> <len> actorchunks/<actor-id>
```

In QOS, the memory model uses per-actor slabs where the actor's heap is a copyable extent list. Chunks are only reclaimed after the actor is re-resident or exits, but the wake path must ensure the chunk is re-read before the hart switches in. To prevent a crash, you must ensure the actor is in a READY state before attempting to switch it in; page-in triggers are strictly limited to the wake path when a sender's CAS changes the actor from BLOCKED to READY. If the actor is blocked, the sender's CAS will ship the wake protocol to the owner hart, which will then re-read the chunk before switching in, ensuring consistency without needing write-back ordering.

```sol
// Example of correct blocking behavior
actor blockedActor {
  msg:
    on Receive {
      // This will trigger a page-in if the actor was paged out
      reply {
        // Sender's CAS(BLOCKED->READY) handles the wake path
      }
    }
}
```

