Previously, permissions beyond the 32nd were silently dropped from the manifest while required ones were included. The system now uses a heap array for `qa_t.perms` that automatically doubles to accommodate any number of permissions, ensuring no data is lost. This is implemented in `qos/portable/qa.h` where `qa_t.perms` is managed as a dynamic structure rather than a fixed-size array.

```sol
// Example of dynamic permission management in qos/portable/qa.h
struct qa_t {
    // A heap array that doubles to hold any number of permissions
    perms_t* perms; 
};
```

Instead of dropping permissions past the 32nd without notification, the `qa_t.perms` field is now implemented as a heap-allocated array that automatically doubles in size to accommodate the full set of granted permissions. When serializing these grants, the buffer is sized exactly to the number of grants rather than returning early upon filling a fixed limit.

```c
// Example of accessing the expanded permissions array
qa_t manifest;
// The perms array grows dynamically to hold all granted permissions
for (int i = 0; i < manifest.perms.size; i++) {
    if (manifest.perms[i].url.length > 0) {
        // Process the full URL without truncation
    }
}
```

A heap array is a dynamically allocated block of memory that grows as needed, rather than using a fixed static size. In this context, `qa_t.perms` was changed from a static limit of 32 permissions to a heap array that doubles its capacity whenever more permissions are required, ensuring no valid permissions are dropped without a message. This allows the system to handle an arbitrary number of permissions safely.
```sol
// Example conceptually showing dynamic growth vs static limit
// Old approach (static, limited to 32):
// perms: [0, 1, 2, ..., 31] (size fixed at 32)

// New approach (heap, doubles on growth):
// perms: [0, 1, 2, ..., 31]
// After 33rd permission: perms -> [0, 1, ..., 31, 32, 33] (size doubles to 64)
// After 65th permission: perms -> [0, 1, ..., 64] (size doubles to 128)
```

The previous fixed limit of 32 permissions caused any grants beyond that count to be silently dropped from the manifest, which violated the rule that reaching a limit must never be silent. The new design uses a heap array that doubles to ensure all required permissions are included and visible to the application.

```sol
// Example of the new dynamic behavior
qa_t.perms = new array(0);
while (needed_perms > 0) {
    append(perms, current_perm);
    if (size(perms) >= 32) {
        resize(perms, size(perms) * 2); // Doubles to fit more grants
    }
}
```

