The first line calculates the average latency in tenths of milliseconds by dividing the sum of latencies (`lsum`) by the number of samples (`n`), the latency ring size (`latN`), and 1000 to normalize the 10 MHz tick count. The second line constructs a new model (`m0`) representing the current frame's position, velocity, and selection state, initializing it with the provided coordinates and orientation while setting the gravity flag based on the input `g` value.

```fpr
ms10 = lsum / minI n latN / 1000;
m0 = Mdl (Bd px py pz vx vy vz (g == 1)) yaw pit sel "";
```

To calculate the average latency, you must first use `Vec.set` to update a specific slot in a vector handle `lv` with the current latency value, ensuring the handle remains mutable for the next frame. Then, apply `Vec.fold` with the `sumF` combinator and an initial value of 0 to the vector to compute the sum of latencies in that window. Finally, divide the resulting sum by the number of slots (`n`) and 1000 to convert the 10 MHz tick count into tenths of milliseconds.

```fpr
(lsum, lv2) = Vec.fold sumF 0 lv1;
ms10 = lsum / minI n latN / 1000;
```

The frameWorker calculates the average latency by first writing the spawn-to-reply time (measured in 10 MHz clint ticks) into a specific slot of a latency ring vector using modulo arithmetic. It then folds the vector to sum all entries, divides the sum by the number of samples and 1000 to convert ticks to tenths of milliseconds.
```fprisc
lv1 = Vec.set (imod n latN + 1) lat lv;
(lsum, lv2) = Vec.fold sumF 0 lv1;
ms10 = lsum / minI n latN / 1000;
```

