The `binCol` function converts a raw floating-point vector column into integer codes by dividing the data into equal-width bins. It first calculates the minimum (`lo`) and maximum (`hi`) values in the column, then determines the bin width (`w0`) by dividing the range (`hi - lo`) by the number of bins (`nb`). If the calculated width is non-positive, it defaults to 1.0 to ensure valid binning. The actual width (`w`) used for encoding is the maximum of this default and the calculated width. A helper function, `binGo`, recursively iterates through the column, computing the bin index for each value using the formula `(value - lo) / w` and clamping the result to the valid range `[0, nb - 1]`.

```sol
w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
```

The `binCol` function first determines the number of bins (`n`) by taking the length of the input column. It then calculates the range of the column by finding the minimum (`lo`) and maximum (`hi`) values. The bin width (`w0`) is derived by dividing the range (`hi - lo`) by the number of bins (`nb`). If the calculated width is non-positive, it defaults to 1.0 to ensure valid binning; otherwise, it uses the computed width.

```fprisc
w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
```

