// Example adapter logic required for FP-RISC os.c
clock_time_t get_gmtoff(time_t t) {
    // Fallback implementation when tm.tm_gmtoff is unavailable
    return 0; 
}
```

The `poller` actor manages state by tracking a counter `k` and performing actions only when `k` reaches specific thresholds. When `k` is a multiple of 64, the `pollLoop` invokes the `boundary` function to reset the system state via `Sys.poolReset`. The `boundary` function sends the current state `st` to itself, triggers a pool reset, and then reads the updated state back to ensure consistency before continuing the loop.

```fpr
pollLoop me st =
  ...
  case k % 64 == 0 of
    True -> pollLoop me (boundary me st)
  | False -> pollLoop me st2.

boundary me st =
  _ = send me st;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  m = receiveFrom me me;
  st2 = keep m;
  _ = drop m;
  st2.
```

The FPRISC project has been restructured to support Homebrew installation by moving per-program outputs outside the source tree and adding a dedicated install mode. To install the dungeon game, you would use the standard Homebrew command `brew install fprisc`, which triggers the new install mode. Once installed, the game is accessible via the `fpr sol` wrapper command, which serves as the entry point for running Sol programs compiled by the FPRISC toolchain.

