The QAR2 format removes ELF because the toolchain consumes the ELF at build time, extracting loadable bytes into a flat image. This results in a container that requires only one copy, one zero-fill, and one protection flip for the loader, eliminating the overhead of header chasing, program-header walking, and relying on externally written linker formats.

```sol
// Example Sol script verifying the flat image structure
// The loader expects IMAGE bytes starting immediately after the blank line,
// with no ELF header or section headers present.

@load "app.qa"
@read "app.qa" as stream

// Skip the header lines (QAR2\n, MANIFEST, LOAD, IMAGE)
// until the blank line that precedes the payload.
skip 4 lines

// The remaining bytes constitute the flat IMAGE.
// No ELF magic bytes (0x7f454c46) or section headers are expected here.
assert stream.starts_with 0x7f  // Just a random byte check, as ELF is gone
```

