The `portFlush` function handles the draining of the message port by sending the reversed queue of accumulated strings to the sender, dropping the current message `m`, and clearing the entire queue before looping back to receive the next message. This ensures that queued roots are dropped after the drain reply's deep copy.

```fprisc
portFlush me m from q =
  _ = send from (revK Nil q);
  _ = drop m;
  _ = portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

To drain a queue, you call `portFlush` with the port identifier, the message to be drained, the sender identifier, and the queue list. This function sends the reversed queue to the specified sender, drops the drained message, and drops all remaining items in the queue before looping back.

```fprisc
portFlush portId msgFrom senderId queueList =
  _ = send senderId (revK Nil queueList);
  _ = drop msgFrom;
  _ = portDropAll queueList;
  portLoop portId Nil;
```

