The `cmd` function selects the command mode by setting the DC pin to 0 before transferring the byte stream, whereas the `dat` function sets the DC pin to 1 for data mode. Both functions share the same underlying SPI bus configuration but differ only in the DC pin state and the sequence of chip select and transfer operations.

```fprisc
cmd pinW pinR pins miso bytes =
  Pins sck mosi cs dc = pins;
  _ = pinW dc 0;
  _ = S.csLow pinW cs;
  _ = S.xfer (S.Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso) bytes;
  _ = S.csHigh pinW cs;
  pinW dc 1.
```

The mistake is setting the DC pin to 1 (data mode) inside the `cmd` function, which causes the SPI bus to transmit the command bytes as data instead of commands. According to the driver logic, the DC pin must be low (0) when sending commands to the display controller. The code should be corrected by ensuring `pinW dc 0` is present before the chip select assertion and before the `xfer` call, as shown in the corrected `cmd` function below:

```fprisc
cmd pinW pinR pins miso bytes =
  Pins sck mosi cs dc = pins;
  _ = pinW dc 0;
  _ = S.csLow pinW cs;
  _ = S.xfer (S.Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso) bytes;
  _ = S.csHigh pinW cs;
  pinW dc 1.
```

