The `serve` function implements the core service loop by receiving a message from a client, processing it with a pure handler, sending a reply, and recursively continuing with the updated state. It ensures safety by dropping the received message after the reply is sent, preventing the same message from being processed again.

```fprisc
serve self h st =
  m = receive self;
  (rt, q) = m;
  (st2, r) = h st q;
  _ = send rt r;
  _ = drop m;
  serve self h st2.
```

The `serve` function is used to define the handler logic for an actor, taking a state, a request, and returning a new state and a reply. It requires three arguments: an unsafe integer identifier for the actor, a pure handler function that transforms the state and request into a new state and reply, and the initial state. The runtime manages the infinite loop of receiving messages, applying the handler, sending the reply, and dropping the message.

```fprisc
serve self h st =
  m = receive self;
  (rt, q) = m;
  (st2, r) = h st q;
  _ = send rt r;
  _ = drop m;
  serve self h st2;
```

The `serve` primitive encapsulates the entire request-response cycle into a single pure handler function `(st -> q -> (st, r))`. It receives a request via `receive`, applies the handler to produce a new state and a reply, sends the reply to the caller, drops the consumed message to prevent memory leaks, and recursively invokes itself with the updated state. This ensures the handler is pure while the runtime manages the message lifecycle and deep-copies request parts before dropping.

```fprisc
serve self h st =
  m = receive self;
  (rt, q) = m;
  (st2, r) = h st q;
  _ = send rt r;
  _ = drop m;
  serve self h st2.
```

