A QOS FPRLive session consumes approximately 50 MiB when idle and grows to about 550 MiB with 1,000 sessions. Performance is flat while idle, utilizing only one core's 4% for polling, and resumes when sessions leave. To efficiently broadcast an event to all sessions, the system now broadcasts a VERSION token, and a writer pulls the model only when ready; busy sessions automatically skip to the newest version. This avoids the previous inefficiency of pushing the entire model to every session on every event.

```sol
// Example of efficient event broadcasting in FP-RISC/Sol
// The writer pulls the model only when ready; busy sessions skip to the newest.
event broadcast() {
    // Broadcast version to all sessions
    version = 1;
    // Writers pull the model when ready
    pull_model();
}
```

To prevent memory leaks, avoid using `Os.read` in loops that wait for data without a guaranteed tidy point, as it allocates a 64 KiB String immediately upon blocking. Instead, use `Os.ready` to check for readiness without allocation, and only allocate the String when `Os.read` successfully retrieves data. This ensures that idle sessions do not accumulate gigabytes of garbage, keeping heap usage stable.

```sol
while (true) {
    if (Os.ready) {
        let data = Os.read;
        // Process data
    }
}
```

`Os.ready` returns a static boolean indicating readiness without allocating memory, whereas `Os.read` allocates a String containing the exact data received. A leak occurred because the code waited for `Os.read` in a loop before checking if data was available, causing the allocator to reserve 64 KiB for a String even when no data arrived. Using `Os.ready` first prevents this unnecessary allocation.

```sol
// Correct pattern using Os.ready to avoid allocation
actor stream {
  var buffer: String = ""

  poll() {
    if (Os.ready(socket)) {
      buffer = Os.read(socket) // Only allocates if data exists
    }
  }
}
```

