To achieve this, you first parse the input string into a JSON document using `J.parse`. You then extract the specific array path (e.g., `["customers"]`), filter the items using `List.filter` with a lambda function that checks a boolean field (e.g., `"active"`), and map the result using `mapOk` to transform each item (e.g., via `reshape`). Finally, construct a new JSON object containing the count and the transformed array using `JObj`, and format it with `J.pretty`.

```sol
doc = unwrap (J.parse customers);
active = J.path ["customers"] doc |>? J.items |> mapOk (List.filter (fn c -> (J.get "active" c |>? J.bool) == Ok True));
out = active |> mapOk (fn cs -> JObj [("count", JNum (List.len cs)), ("customers", JArr (List.map reshape cs))]);
