The `claim` function is the core operation that finalizes a memory block selection by splitting the block if necessary, marking it as in use, and zeroing out its payload. It first calls `split` to ensure the block has enough contiguous space for the requested size plus alignment padding; if the remaining space is less than 96 bytes, it creates a new block to hold the payload. Subsequently, it sets the block's usage flag to true, clears its fields, pending operations, and layout description, and calculates the remaining capacity. Finally, it initializes the payload area to all zeros.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

The `claim` function allocates a block of memory by first splitting the block if necessary, then setting internal flags (such as `used`, `pending`, and `rc`) to indicate the block is in use and ready for payload storage. The expression `p.`, which is the payload address extracted from the block header, returns an `Addr` value representing the start of the usable memory area within that block.

```fprisc
p = payload b;
_ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
p.
```

The `alloc` function first validates that the requested size fits within a 64-bit word by checking bits 62 and 63, returning an error if they are set. It then calculates the required size by aligning the input up to the nearest 8-byte boundary and adding an 80-byte header offset. If the resulting size equals 80, it is adjusted to 96 bytes to satisfy minimum allocation constraints. Finally, it delegates the search for a suitable free block to the `firstFit` function, which traverses the block list to find the first block large enough to satisfy the request.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

