The system uses a two-step process for non-equality constraints where it first attempts standard unification to check if terms are identical, then undoes any bindings created if unification succeeds. If unification fails (terms are not unifiable), the system proceeds to solve the goal with the original state. Conversely, if unification succeeds, the system explicitly undoes the marks (bindings) and returns the modified state without solving, effectively asserting the terms are distinct.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

