FP-RISC allocates a stack equal to the full buddy block size provided by the HAL, utilizing the entire memory block rather than just the lower half. If an actor overflows its stack, the HAL triggers a panic via the `hal_stack_guard` mechanism, which is implemented in `machine/posix` by making the lowest page inaccessible and catching the fault on an alternate signal stack. This results in an exit code of 1, as demonstrated by the following FP-RISC test case that verifies the panic behavior:

```fprisc
@ tests/base/overflow.fpr
import hal

actor overflow_test() {
    var stack_ptr = fpr_current_stack();
    // Attempt to grow stack beyond allocated bounds
    while (true) {
        stack_ptr = stack_ptr - 4;
        if (stack_ptr < 0) break;
        // Accessing invalid memory triggers the guard
        var _ = stack_ptr * 0;
    }
    // This line is unreachable due to panic
    print("Stack overflow handled");
}
```

When an actor exceeds its allocated stack, the lowest page of its stack is made inaccessible, triggering a fault handled by an alternate signal stack. This causes a panic with exit code 1. The `actors.c` module uses weak HAL functions `hal_stack_guard` and `hal_stack_unguard` to manage this, while `machine/posix` registers `fpr_current_stack` to identify the offending actor.

