@define PIN_TRACE_CAP 8192

// Inside the pin handler loop
if (pin_trace_count >= PIN_TRACE_CAP) {
    // Report truncation or handle overflow
    panic("Pin trace buffer full, truncating");
}
pin_trace_record(pin_id, event_data);
```

No, the compiler rejects such handlers to prevent entering non-reentrant allocators or shared argument-spill areas. An interrupt handler must acknowledge its source, preserve registers and status, and strictly avoid allocation, managed construction, printing, indirect calls, CSR writes, and interrupt re-enabling.

```fprisc
// This code would be rejected by the compiler if used in an interrupt handler
fn badHandler (cause: Word, pc: Word, value: Word) -> Unit {
    let x = new Int(42) // Allocation rejected
    print "Hello"       // Printing rejected
}
```

Checked resumable interrupt handlers must run in a non-reentrant environment where the non-reentrant allocator and shared argument-spill area cannot be entered. Managed operations like construction and projection rely on the allocator, while string printing may trigger indirect calls or CSR writes, both of which violate the strict constraints required for safe, synchronous interrupt handling.

```fprisc
machineInterrupt cause pc value {
    // This code is rejected by the compiler if it contains:
    // - Managed construction (e.g., new String)
    // - Managed projection (e.g., ref.field)
    // - String or print work
    // - Indirect calls
    // - CSR writes
    // - Interrupt re-enabling
    // - Functions exceeding the eight-register ABI
    return unit;
}
```

