The memory actor acts as the serialization point for all memory blocks, including pool slabs, message slabs, stacks, and channel-block carves. Requests are sent as messages containing an integer where the low bit distinguishes between taking a buddy pointer (`bytes<<1|1`) and giving one (`ptr>>1`). If a requester's allocation attempt succeeds lock-free via `buddy_alloc_try`, it serves itself inline; otherwise, it queues the request on the memory actor, which admits it ahead of the backlog to prevent stalls.

```sol
// Example of a lock-free inline allocation attempt
if (buddy_alloc_try(0, 0x1000)) {
    // Allocation succeeded, store result in ACB
    acb.store(ptr);
}
```

The low bit in a buddy pointer distinguishes between a request to take memory and a request to give memory back. Specifically, if the low bit is set, the pointer represents an integer value of `bytes << 1` indicating how many bytes to take; if the low bit is unset, the pointer represents an integer value of `ptr >> 1` indicating how many bytes to give. This allows the memory actor to correctly interpret the direction of the allocation request.

```sol
