Sol uses read-write sets to manage concurrency; when a script reads a file, it snapshots the content into its read set, and any concurrent write attempt to that file will invalidate the read set, causing the transaction to fail. This mechanism forces the script to re-run upon conflict, ensuring eventual consistency. For example, a script can open a file, read its contents, sleep to allow potential concurrent writes, and then write the updated value:
> h = open @/tmp/sol-counter.txt;
  (s, h2) = readAll h;
  n = Str.parse s;
  u = sleepMs 300;
  h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";
  close h3.

The script safely increments a counter by opening a file, reading its current content, parsing the number, sleeping for a duration to hold the transaction open, writing the incremented value, and closing the file handle. The `sleepMs` function is critical because it keeps the read snapshot active; if a concurrent writer modifies the file during this sleep, the subsequent `writeAll` call will detect a conflict, causing the script to re-run automatically until the transaction succeeds.

```sol
h = open @/tmp/sol-counter.txt;
(s, h2) = readAll h;
n = Str.parse s;
u = sleepMs 300;
h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";
close h3.
```

The file at `/tmp/sol-counter.txt` serves as a shared data store to demonstrate transactional concurrency control within the Sol scripting environment. It stores an integer value that is safely incremented by a single thread while blocking concurrent modifications through a read snapshot and a `sleepMs` delay to simulate contention. If a concurrent write invalidates the transaction, the script automatically re-runs to ensure the final count is accurate.

```sol
h = open @/tmp/sol-counter.txt;
(s, h2) = readAll h;
n = Str.parse s;
u = sleepMs 300;
h3 = writeAll h2 "{n + 1}";
