When folding over a vector produced by Vec.map, the write-backs from the map operation must have landed in memory before the fold begins. This ensures that the fold operation sees the updated values. The process is implemented in a single pass where the map's writes are finalized before the fold accumulates the result.

```fprisc
(s2, v5) = Vec.fold addA 0 (Vec.map dbl v4);
(s3, v6) = Vec.fold addA 0 v5;
```

The FP-RISC compiler performs a write-back fold optimization where a `Vec.fold` applied to the result of a `Vec.map` executes in a single pass. During this pass, writes generated by the map operation must "land" (commit to memory) before the fold consumes the vector, ensuring the fold sees the updated values. This eliminates the need to store intermediate results in a separate heap region, allowing the fold to sum the modified elements directly while keeping heap usage low.

```fprisc
  h2 = heapUsed Unit;
  (s2, v5) = Vec.fold addA 0 (Vec.map dbl v4);
  h3 = heapUsed Unit;
  u3 = verdict "wfold-writeback" (s2 == s3);
  u4 = verdict "wfold-inplace" (h3 - h2 < 4096);
```

In FP-RISC, a write-back fold occurs when the reduction operation (in this case, `Vec.fold addA`) processes a vector that was previously constructed by a map operation which modified the heap. The fold must read the updated values from the heap immediately after the map completes, ensuring the reduction operates on the written results rather than the original inputs. This is verified by checking that the sum of the folded vector (`s3`) equals the sum of the doubled elements (`s2`), confirming the writes landed and were visible to the subsequent fold.

```fprisc
(s2, v5) = Vec.fold addA 0 (Vec.map dbl v4);
