The `foldStmts` function recursively processes a list of statements, reversing the order of the database, query list, and symbol table at each step. It distinguishes between clause statements and query statements, delegating their specific transformations to `fsClause` and `fsQuery` respectively. These helper functions build new clause or query structures while updating the symbol table and accumulating results into the respective lists, which are then folded back into the main list.

```sol
foldStmts [] db qs syms = (List.rev db, List.rev qs, syms);
foldStmts (s :: r) db qs syms = fsStep s r db qs syms;

fsStep s r db qs syms = case s of
  SClause hd body -> fsClause hd body r db qs syms
| SQuery gs -> fsQuery gs r db qs syms;

fsClause hd body r db qs syms =
  (c, syms2) = buildClause hd body syms;
  foldStmts r (c :: db) qs syms2;

fsQuery gs r db qs syms =
  (gp, nqv, qnames, syms2) = buildQuery gs syms;
  foldStmts r db ((gp, nqv, qnames) :: qs) syms2;
```

The `Vec.filter` function compacts the resulting vector in-place within the allocated memory of the input vector, rather than allocating a separate buffer for the output. This is demonstrated by the test case where the heap usage after filtering (`h3`) is measured to be only slightly higher than before (`h2`), specifically less than 65536 bytes, confirming that the operation reuses the existing storage.

