To verify correct handling of multiple pending deadlines, you must ensure that all scheduled events fire regardless of the order in which they are requested. The system maintains an internal ordering independent of the `after` call sequence. You can validate this behavior by scheduling a later deadline first, then an earlier one, and checking if both fire before a specific time threshold. If both fire correctly, the result is "ok"; otherwise, it indicates a scheduling error.

```sol
g1 = receiveFrom me svc;
g2 = receiveFrom me svc;
ook = case and2 (isFire g1) (isFire g2) of
    True -> "ok"
  | False -> "WRONG";
```

A QOS timer allows scheduling multiple deadlines using `T.after`, where the system maintains an internal queue regardless of the order of requests. To verify the firing order, the program registers two `receiveFrom` handlers, waits for the clock to advance past the later deadline, and checks if both events fired using `isFire`. If the earlier deadline fired before the later one, the condition is met; otherwise, the system reports "EARLY" or "WRONG".

```fprisc
  _ = T.after svc 40;
  _ = T.after svc 10;
  g1 = receiveFrom me svc;
  g2 = receiveFrom me svc;
  t2 = read clk;
  ook = case and2 (isFire g1) (isFire g2) of
      True -> (case t2 - t0 >= 900000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "EARLY")
    | False -> "WRONG";
  _ = kill svc;
```

The system correctly handles out-of-order deadline registration by maintaining a priority queue based on absolute time rather than registration order. When `T.after` is called with a later time (40) before an earlier time (10), the internal scheduler ensures the event with time 10 fires first. The provided test confirms this behavior: if both deadlines fire correctly within the expected window, the result is "ok". If the earlier deadline fails to fire or fires outside the 900000 clock unit window, the test reports "EARLY" or "WRONG", indicating a violation of the strict timing guarantee.

```fprisc
_ = T.after svc 40;
_ = T.after svc 10;
```

