Attempting to launch a Sol application recursively using LD.launch results in a deadlock or infinite recursion because the application never yields control back to the operating system to process the new launch request. The standard pattern to avoid this is to launch a helper process and then yield, allowing the scheduler to handle the new process before the original process continues.

```fprisc
-- Incorrect: Recursively launching itself
LD.launch me ld "me";

-- Correct: Launch a helper and yield
ha = expectOk "launch helper" (LD.launch me ld "helper");
yield me;
```

Under memory pressure, the QOS runtime copies the stack and heap of a yielded or blocked actor into a single chunk and stores it in a reserved partition of the log mounted at `/actorchunks` before reclaiming the original memory. This mechanism is designed to manage memory usage efficiently without blocking the storage front, as the non-blocking forward discipline ensures replies travel via the original `replyTo` while the log handles the data movement.

```fprisc
// Example demonstrating the concept of moving an actor's memory to disk
// Note: This is conceptual; actual implementation details are internal to the QOS runtime.
actor moveToDisk(actorRef, chunkSize) {
    // The runtime internally copies the actor's stack and heap to /actorchunks
    // if the actor has yielded or is blocked and memory pressure is detected.
    if (isBlocked(actorRef) || hasYielded(actorRef)) {
        copyToDisk(actorRef, chunkSize)
        freeMemory(actorRef)
    }
}
```

The `/actorchunks` partition is a reserved area on the log used to store actor stacks and heaps when memory pressure occurs. When an actor yields or becomes blocked, its memory is copied out as a single chunk to this location to be reclaimed, allowing the system to continue operating without blocking the storage front.

```sol
// Example: Triggering a page-out when memory is low
if (memoryPressure()) {
  pageOutActor(self, "/actorchunks");
}
```

