To trigger a software interrupt, write to the Machine Interrupt Pending Register (MIP) using the `Mem.write32` function, ensuring the corresponding interrupt enable bit is set via `CPU.irqEnable`. Verification is achieved by polling a dedicated flag register until it reaches zero, indicating the interrupt has been serviced and the program resumed. Below is a concise example demonstrating the trigger and check:

```fprisc
machineInterrupt =
  msip = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432);
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.write32 msip (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  Unit

waitFor flag =
  case Word.toInt (Mem.readWord flag) == 0 of
    True -> Unit
  | False -> waitFor flag
```

To trigger a software interrupt, write to the Machine Interrupt Pending Register (MIP) to set the pending bit, then increment a status flag to indicate the interrupt has been processed. Verification involves checking if the flag returns to zero after the interrupt handler completes, confirming the CPU resumed execution correctly.

```sol
flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
_ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 1);
_ = waitFor flag;
_ = check (Mem.readWord flag == Word.fromInt 0);
```

The `fire` function expects a non-negative integer `n` to represent the number of interrupts to trigger. If `n` is negative, the recursive call `fire (n - 1)` continues indefinitely because the argument decreases without ever reaching zero, causing the program to hang. This is because the base case `case n == 0` is never satisfied for negative inputs.

```fprisc
fire (-1) =
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [-1, -2];
  _ = check (xs == [-1, -2]);
  fire (-2);
```

