The `tick` function advances the game state by adding the elapsed time (`tickMs`) to the simulation time (`ms`) of all keys, while simultaneously managing audio and logs. It processes animations based on the `kind` field, either advancing the animation or entering an idle state, and finally plays all queued sound effects and prints the current log to stdout.

```fpr
tick m | frozen m =
  (m2, cs) = takeKey { m | ms = m.ms + tickMs, log = Nil, sfx = Nil };
  _ = say (rev m2.log Nil);
  _ = Sfx.playAllVar m2.ms (rev m2.sfx Nil);
  (m2, cs).
tick m =
  m1 = ageAll { m | ms = m.ms + tickMs, log = Nil, sfx = Nil } tickMs;
  An kind s fz fc tz tc t done = m1.anim;
  (m2, cs) = case kind != 0 of
    True -> (advance m1 tickMs, Nil)
  | False -> idle m1;
  _ = say (rev m2.log Nil);
  _ = Sfx.playAllVar m2.ms (rev m2.sfx Nil);
  (m2, cs).
```

To handle a frozen state, the script checks the `frozen` condition and uses `takeKey` to retrieve the active key while clearing its logs and sound effects. It then executes `say` to print the reversed log history and `Sfx.playAllVar` to replay all associated sounds. Finally, it returns the updated state tuple `(m2, cs)` to resume execution.

```fpr
tick m | frozen m =
  (m2, cs) = takeKey { m | ms = m.ms + tickMs, log = Nil, sfx = Nil };
  _ = say (rev m2.log Nil);
  _ = Sfx.playAllVar m2.ms (rev m2.sfx Nil);
  (m2, cs).
```

If `kind` is 0, the condition `kind != 0` evaluates to `False`, causing the execution to skip the `advance` logic and instead call `idle m1`. This results in the state machine entering an idle state where it processes the queue or takes a key, rather than advancing the animation or simulation by `tickMs`.

```fpr
tick m =
  m1 = ageAll { m | ms = m.ms + tickMs, log = Nil, sfx = Nil } tickMs;
  An kind s fz fc tz tc t done = m1.anim;
  (m2, cs) = case kind != 0 of
    True -> (advance m1 tickMs, Nil)
  | False -> idle m1;
  _ = say (rev m2.log Nil);
  _ = Sfx.playAllVar m2.ms (rev m2.sfx Nil);
  (m2, cs).
```

