The `openDoc` function first updates the UI to highlight the selected tab, then asynchronously fetches the document body from the API and renders it into the `#doc` element. If a `scrollLine` argument is provided, it calculates a fractional scroll position based on the total number of lines in the document and scrolls the window to approximately 90% of that height. Finally, it sends a POST request to the `/api/visit` endpoint to record the visit and triggers the `loadRecent` function.

```fprisc
async function openDoc(name, scrollLine) \{
  CUR = name;
  document.querySelectorAll('.tab').forEach(t =>
    t.classList.toggle('on', t.dataset.n === name));
  const d = await j('/api/doc?n=' + encodeURIComponent(name));
  $('#doc').innerHTML = render(d.body);
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);
  if (scrollLine) \{
    const frac = scrollLine / Math.max(1, d.body.split('\\n').length);
    window.scrollTo(0, frac * document.body.scrollHeight * 0.9);
  }
  j('/api/visit', \{ method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(\{ doc: name }) })
    .then(loadRecent);
}
```

The code calculates a scroll fraction by dividing the `scrollLine` by the maximum of 1 and the document's line count. If `scrollLine` exceeds the line count, the division results in a fraction greater than 1. This causes `window.scrollTo` to attempt to scroll to a position beyond the bottom of the viewport (specifically 90% of the page height plus the extra overflow), which may cause the browser to ignore the command or scroll to the maximum possible position instead of the intended line.
```fprisc
const frac = scrollLine / Math.max(1, d.body.split('\\n').length);
window.scrollTo(0, frac * document.body.scrollHeight * 0.9);
```

