The animation clock applies the midpoint rule only if the current time plus the time step (t + dt) is greater than or equal to half the duration of the specific kind of animation, provided the animation is not finished. If this condition is met, the system calculates a new state with an updated animation time and potentially modifies the snap value. If the condition is not met, the state remains unchanged for the animation logic. Once the time exceeds the full duration of the animation (or zero for kind 4), the animation is forcibly finished.

```fpr
advance m dt =
  An kind s fz fc tz tc t done = m.anim;
  t2 = t + dt;
  m1 = case done == 0 and t2 >= durOf kind / 2 of
    True -> { (midpoint m kind s fz fc tz tc) | anim = An kind s fz fc tz tc t2 1, snap = m.snap + m.auto * bi (kind < 4) }
  | False -> { m | anim = An kind s fz fc tz tc t2 done };
  case t2 >= (case kind == 4 of True -> 0 | False -> durOf kind) of
    True -> finishAn m1 kind s fz fc tz tc
  | False -> m1.
```

The keyword `case` is a conditional operator that evaluates a boolean expression and executes one of two branches based on the result. If the condition is true, it returns the first branch; otherwise, it returns the second. In the `advance` function, it checks if the animation is done and if the time has reached the midpoint to decide whether to apply the midpoint rule or continue normally.
```fpr
case done == 0 and t2 >= durOf kind / 2 of
  True -> { (midpoint m kind s fz fc tz tc) | anim = An kind s fz fc tz tc t2 1, snap = m.snap + m.auto * bi (kind < 4) }
| False -> { m | anim = An kind s fz fc tz tc t2 done };
```

