The `advance` function first journals the event if configured, then updates the model and persists the new state. It subsequently broadcasts the incremented version number to all active sessions and finally settles the state if the session is quitting.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
  _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
  (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      quit = perform me sessions cmds;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      (if quit then _ = settle app.fields db model2 saved2; _ = goodbye (M.values sessions); O.None
       else O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2)))).
```

The `advance` function first conditionally journals the event to the database if the configuration requires it. It then updates the model, persists the new state, and broadcasts a version increment to all active sessions that haven't received the update yet. Finally, it either settles the state if the application quits or starts new tickers to continue the cycle.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of
    Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
      _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
      (model2, cmds) = app.update ev model;
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      if quit then settle app.fields db model2 saved2 else
        O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2));
```

The `advance` function processes a single event within the runtime loop by first journaling the event if configured, then updating the model, persisting the new state, and finally notifying all active sessions of the change. If the event triggers a quit condition, it settles the system and terminates; otherwise, it starts the next tick with updated session information.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
  _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
  (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      quit = perform me sessions cmds;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      (if quit then _ = settle app.fields db model2 saved2; _ = goodbye (M.values sessions); O.None
       else O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2)))).
```

The `advance` function orchestrates a single logical time step in the FPRISC live model by executing a sequence of operations to ensure consistency and state propagation. It first journals the event if configured, then updates the model and persists the new state to the database. Following this, it broadcasts the state change to all active sessions that haven't been notified yet, performs any necessary session quits, and finally settles the database or terminates the session if the update was a quit event. The function returns `O.None` if the update results in a quit, or `O.Some` containing the updated configuration and state for the next tick.

```fprisc
advance me ev st = case st of Rs cfg app model sessions nextSid db saved tickers version told ->
  _ = (if cfg.journal then journal app.codec db ev else Unit);
  (case app.update ev model of (model2, cmds) ->
      saved2 = persist me app.fields db model2 saved;
      quit = perform me sessions cmds;
      told2 = broadcast me (M.values sessions) (version + 1) told;
      (if quit then _ = settle app.fields db model2 saved2; _ = goodbye (M.values sessions); O.None
       else O.Some (startTickers me (Rs cfg app model2 sessions nextSid db saved2 tickers (version + 1) told2)))).
```

The `writeBuf` function recursively processes a list of pages, terminating immediately if the list is empty (`Nil`) by returning the current page count `p`. If the list contains a page (`pg :: r`), it first calls `writeBuf2` to perform the actual block write operation on the device, then recursively calls itself with the incremented page count and the remaining list. This pattern ensures that all pages in the list are written sequentially before the function returns.

```fprisc
writeBuf dev p pages = case pages of Nil -> p | pg :: r -> writeBuf2 dev p r pg;
writeBuf2 dev p r pg =
  _ = blkWrite dev p pg;
  writeBuf dev (p + 1) r;
```

`measure`s are user-defined mechanisms used to provide a well-founded metric for recursion, ensuring each call progresses toward a base case to prevent unbounded resource consumption. In contrast, built-in recursion schemes are pre-defined patterns provided by the language that facilitate common recursive structures while automatically enforcing the constraints imposed by `measure`s.

```fprisc
// Using a built-in recursion scheme that enforces a measure
list_reverse = rec(list, measure: length) {
    case [] -> []
    case [x | xs] -> [x | list_reverse(xs)]
}
```

