```

The `qosp` tool initializes by parsing command-line arguments to determine the target application file, then executes the `launch` function which reads the file and parses the QAR structure. It validates the load mode, gates the required and optional capabilities based on the configuration, and finally loads the image into memory using `Host.loadImage` before executing the specified application ID.

```sol
launch o =
  Opts yes0 trace path = o;
  _ = case Host.init trace of Ok _ -> Unit | Err e -> die e;
  qa = fileRead path;
  man = case M.parseQa qa of Ok m -> m | Err e -> die "{path}: {e}";
  granted = append (gate man yes "required" (M.req man)) (gate man yes "optional" (M.opt man));
  _ = case Host.loadImage path (QI.sha m) img (QI.nums m) of Ok _ -> Unit | Err e -> die "image load failed: {e}";
  case Host.run (M.id man) (M.name man) (M.capsBlob (M.id man) granted) of
    Err e -> die e
  | Ok result -> print "\n[qos] {M.id man} => {result}".
```

The `compact` operation performs a garbage collection sweep by copying live records forward in order and truncating the log by the head, while simultaneously minting a new merkle root over the live chunk hashes. In contrast, `ensure` detects torn writes by checking the commit flag ahead of every append and rolling back any incomplete writes via truncation at the head. While `compact` is idempotent and reconstructs the logical file structure, `ensure` acts as a crash-safety mechanism to validate write integrity without necessarily performing a full sweep.

```sol
// Example of a compact operation
compact() {
    sweep = gc_sweep(head)
    new_root = merkle_root(sweep.live_hashes)
    metadata.merkleRoot = new_root
    metadata.truncate(head)
}

// Example of an ensure operation detecting a torn write
ensure() {
    if (metadata.commitFlag == false) {
        metadata.commitFlag = true
        return false // Write detected as torn, will be rolled back
    }
    return true
}
```

The commit flag is written ahead of every append but remains unchanged until the operation completes. If a torn write occurs, the `ensure` function detects this inconsistency and rolls back the operation by truncating the log at the current head.

```sol
ensure {
    if (commit_flag_torn) {
        truncate_at_head();
        rollback();
    }
}
```

The system detects torn writes by checking the commit flag (`c`) in the metadata header; this flag is set ahead of every append and reset afterward. If a write is incomplete (torn), the `ensure` function triggers a rollback by truncating the log back to the current head. This process is idempotent, meaning re-running compaction with the same head and root state produces no changes.

```sol
ensure {
    if (metadata.commit != true) {
        truncate_at_head();
        return;
    }
    compact_live_records();
    update_merkle_root();
}
```

