```

The `Vec.filter` function performs eager compaction in place, shrinking the vector length without zero allocation. It utilizes two cursors within the same vector to slide kept rows downward, ensuring that subsequent map or fold operations remain dense and mask-free. This behavior is verified by checking that the heap usage delta remains minimal after filtering.

```fpr
keepEven x = x - (x / 2) * 2 == 0.

a1 = fillI (Vec.new Unit) 1 1000;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a2 = Vec.filter keepEven a1;
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
# Verify minimal heap growth
(h1 - h0 < 4096)
```

The `Vec.filter` operation performs eager in-place compaction, shrinking the vector length and eliminating zero allocations to ensure subsequent map or fold operations remain dense and mask-free. This behavior is verified by observing that the heap usage delta after filtering a vector remains negligible, confirming the data stays compact without shifting to a sparse representation.

```sol
main =
  v = Vec.new Unit;
  h0 = heapUsed Unit;
  v' = Vec.filter keepEven v;
  h1 = heapUsed Unit;
  _ = Vec.free v';
  print (h1 - h0 < 4096);
```

The file `fprisc/tests/vfilter.fpr` verifies that the `Vec.filter` operation in FP-RISC performs eager compaction in place, ensuring zero allocation and maintaining a dense, mask-free vector structure for subsequent map or fold operations. This is demonstrated by measuring the heap usage before and after filtering a vector of integers and records, confirming that the vector length shrinks without reallocation. The test suite validates this behavior on both scalar and Structured Array (SoA) vectors by asserting that the sum of filtered elements matches expected values and that the memory delta remains negligible.

```fprisc
fillI v i lim | i > lim = v;
fillI v i lim = fillI (Vec.push i v) (i + 1) lim;
a1 = fillI (Vec.new Unit) 1 1000;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a2 = Vec.filter keepEven a1;
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
