As the number of sessions increases, the per-session process memory grows from approximately 263 KiB to 600 KiB. While a login burst of 64 sessions completes in 0.1 seconds, scaling to 512 sessions causes the login burst to extend to 23 seconds due to the time required for the register to fan out deltas to all actors. This architecture ensures that sessions scale with memory rather than a shared table, as each actor manages its own state and only stalls if its specific peer is slow or hostile.

```sol
// Example of a connection actor handling its own session state
actor Connection(id) {
  init() {
    self.buffer = Buffer();
    self.stats = Stats();
  }
  onFrame(frame) {
    self.parse(frame);
    self.register.update(self.event);
  }
}
```

The server utilizes a BEAM-shaped architecture where each connection spawns its own dedicated actor upon first contact, ensuring sessions scale with memory rather than a central table. As the number of sessions increases, the per-session process memory grows linearly, rising from approximately 263 KiB for 64 sessions to 600 KiB for 512 sessions. While the register broadcasts updates to all active session actors, the latency for the final update to reach the entire group increases significantly with scale, taking 113 milliseconds for 512 sessions.

```sol
// Example of a connection actor spawning on first contact
actor spawn_connection(conn_id) {
    // Spawn the dedicated actor for this specific connection
    spawn_actor(conn_id) {
        // The actor owns its buffer, stats, and parses frames
        self.keep(state_from_std_mvu)
        register.update(self)
    }
}
```

