The scheduler uses a deterministic aged tier first, admitting the oldest actor if its wait time exceeds `g_tau` admissions; otherwise, it uses a weighted random default tier based on per-actor weights. This logic is implemented in `runtime/core/actors.c` using the constants defined at the top of the scheduler section.

```fpr
// Example: Checking the aged tier condition per hart
if (g_adm - ready_at > g_tau) {
    admit_oldest_actor();
} else {
    admit_weighted_random();
}
```

The scheduler guarantees deterministic aging by selecting the oldest actor from the backlog whenever an actor has waited more than `tau` admissions (defined as `g_adm - ready_at > g_tau`). This selection is strictly oldest-first with no randomness, and the `tau` threshold is a runtime-tunable constant (defaulting to `FPR_TAU = 64`).

```sol
actor my_task = {
  ready_at = 100;
  weight = 1;
};

// Assuming g_adm is 170 and FPR_TAU is 64
// Wait time = 170 - 100 = 70.
// Since 70 > 64, my_task is admitted via the Aged tier.
```

The actor will never be admitted by the default tier because the weighted reservoir sampling algorithm ignores actors with a weight of zero, effectively excluding them from the random selection pool. While the aged tier may still admit such an actor if it exceeds the `tau` threshold, the default tier's reliance on the `acb->weight` field means zero-weight actors are invisible to the probabilistic scheduler unless they become aged.

```fpr
// Example of an actor with zero weight that bypasses the default tier
actor bad_actor = {
    name = "zero_weight_task",
    weight = 0,
    // This actor is invisible to the default tier's reservoir
    // until it ages past g_tau
}
```

`CPU.irqSave` and `CPU.irqRestore` do not automatically create a lexical critical section, disable non-maskable events, or configure individual interrupt sources. They merely save and restore the single Machine Interrupt Enable (MIE) bit as a tagged integer token, allowing interrupts to potentially deliver before the restoration call returns. Unlike automatic mechanisms, these functions require manual configuration of interrupt sources and handlers by the caller.

```sol
// Manual interrupt handling pattern
let mie = CPU.irqSave();
// ... perform critical work ...
CPU.irqRestore(mie);
```

The call rejects the invalid value. The documentation explicitly states that `CPU.irqRestore` only restores the MIE bit from a saved token and rejects any other values.
```sol
let saved = CPU.irqSave();
// ...
CPU.irqRestore(1234); // Error: rejects non-token value
```

To temporarily disable machine interrupts, use the `CPU.irqSave` function, which clears the machine interrupt enable bit and returns the previous MIE state as an integer token. To restore the interrupt state, pass that token to `CPU.irqRestore`, which re-enables the saved MIE value. These operations do not allocate boxes and preserve other `mstatus` bits.

```sol
let old_mie = CPU.irqSave();
-- ... perform critical section ...
CPU.irqRestore(old_mie);
```

No, these primitives do not automatically create a lexical critical section. They only manage the machine interrupt enable bit (MIE) and preserve other `mstatus` bits. You must manually configure interrupt sources and handlers before calling `CPU.irqEnable`. Furthermore, restoring interrupts can deliver a pending interrupt before the call returns, and these tokens do not allocate boxes or disable non-maskable events.

