You define a `V2` type struct and implement the operator logic as global functions named `<Struct>.<op>`. The operator site automatically resolves these functions based on the struct's type, so you can use `+`, `-`, and `*` directly on instances of your custom type.

```sol
V2 = Type (V2 Int Int).

v2add a b = V2 ax ay = a; V2 bx by = b; V2 (ax + bx) (ay + by).
v2sub a b = V2 ax ay = a; V2 bx by = b; V2 (ax - bx) (ay - by).
v2mul a b = V2 ax ay = a; V2 bx by = b; V2 (ax * bx) (ay * by).

Vec2 = Struct {
  (+) = fn a b -> v2add a b,
  (-) = fn a b -> v2sub a b,
  (*) = fn a b -> v2mul a b
}.

p = V2 1 2;
q = V2 10 20;
print "p + q = {v2show (p + q)}";
print "p * q = {v2show (p * q)}";
