The error arises because the operator site resolution mechanism relies on the field names of a Struct to automatically map to the corresponding operator functions. If a user defines a field named `+` within a Struct, it overwrites the default addition operator resolution for that type. Consequently, the expression `a + b` will fail to resolve to the intended addition logic and instead attempt to access or invoke the conflicting field definition, breaking the automatic operator site mechanism. For example:

MyStruct = Struct {
  (+) = fn a b -> error "conflict",
  (x) = fn a -> a.x
}.

> p = MyStruct 1 2;
> q = MyStruct 3 4;
> result = p + q.

This code will fail because the `+` field in `MyStruct` is explicitly defined to return an error string, preventing the compiler from finding the correct addition function in the global namespace.

Standard message passing drops the root of every received message immediately upon reading, whereas `sendLinear` allows an actor to retain ownership of a linear resource across the loop shape. In standard passing, the compiler or auditor discharges the root; in `sendLinear`, the sender transfers the handle without dropping it, and the receiver re-owns it for the duration of the loop.

```fprisc
// Standard pass: root dropped after read
msg m = actor1.send(value); 
// m is consumed here, no further access

// Linear pass: ownership transferred, resource retained
val handle = actor1.sendLinear(value); 
// handle remains owned by actor2 for the entire loop iteration
```

The system enforces a strict linear regime where a service cannot send a value it still owns; the only valid options are to reply with a disposable copy or use `sendLinear` to transfer ownership immediately. Attempting to send a live state results in a compiler discharge failure because the runtime does not support a third option, and the linearity checker will report a violation of the "Never send live state" law.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: Attempting to send a live value while retaining ownership
// This will fail the linearity check or be rejected by the runtime.
let x = someLiveState()
send x  // Error: Cannot send live state; must use sendLinear or send a copy
```

When a nested value is kept beyond the drop-of-root, it becomes a dangling pointer because the root is dropped. To resolve this, the retainer must perform a copy on retain, duplicating the nested value into its own pool. This ensures memory safety without a garbage collector, as the original reference is invalidated immediately after the copy.

```fprisc
// Example: Copy on retain pattern
let (root, nested) = receiveLinear(); // root is dropped here
// If 'nested' is needed later, it is copied into the current actor's pool
let safeCopy = copy(nested); 
```

