When an interrupt occurs, the `waker` function binds the IRQ to the actor and sends a `Woke` message. The `waking` function then receives a message; if it is `0`, the OS has already cleared the interrupt, so the code unbinds the IRQ, sends a `WakerStopped` message, and exits the loop. If the message is non-zero, the code acknowledges the interrupt and loops back to wake the actor again.

```fpr
waking : unsafe Int -> Int -> Int -> Unit .
waking poller irq self =
  m = receive self;
  done = m == 0;
  _ = drop m;
  if done then
    _ = unbind irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller WakerStopped;
    Unit
  else
    _ = Sys.irqAck irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller Woke;
    waking poller irq self.
```

If `OS.watchOpen` fails, `begin` logs an error to `Sys.stderr` and falls back to polling on a timer using the `loop` function. Upon successful registration, it spawns a `waker` actor to bind the interrupt, sends a `Woke` message to the main actor, and then enters a `waking` loop that processes messages, acknowledges interrupts, and unbinds the IRQ upon receiving a `WakerStopped` signal.

```sol
begin me = case OS.watchOpen Unit of
    Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "poller: no watcher ({why}); polling on a timer instead\n";
    loop me 0 0 (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100
  | Ok irq -> w = spawn (fn self -> waker me irq self);
              m = receiveFrom me w;
              _ = drop m;
              loop me irq w (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100
end
```

