Validating an artifact at load time involves rejecting malformed or incompatible artifacts with explicit errors based on format versions, profile requirements, and imported runtime operations. However, validation and bytecode execution alone do not establish a security sandbox; the runtime must additionally supply required value behavior, transactional services, and other imports to enforce the boundary. Unsupported requirements must be reported explicitly rather than silently replaced with weaker behavior.

```sol
// Example: A simple Sol artifact that validates its own entry point
// before attempting to invoke host services.

@version 1.0
@profile qos

entry point main:
    // Validation happens here via the loader checking format/version.
    // No sandbox is established yet; host services are only invoked
    // if validation passes and the runtime supports them.
    host_service echo "Hello"
```

The loader rejects the artifact at load time with an explicit error. The runtime must report unsupported requirements explicitly rather than silently replacing them with weaker behavior.

```sol
# Example: An artifact attempting to use an unsupported host operation
# This will fail to load if the host does not provide the required service.
artifact {
    version = 1
    entry_points = ["main"]
    imports = {
        "host_operation" = "unsupported_service_call"
    }
}
```

The loader must explicitly reject the artifact if it cannot understand the required combinations of format, profile, library, or host API versions. It must not silently replace unsupported requirements with weaker behavior; instead, it must report the incompatibility with an explicit error. This ensures that validation and bytecode execution do not falsely establish a security sandbox, as the loader's inability to satisfy the artifact's requirements constitutes a failure condition.

```sol
// Conceptual example of a loader check
if (artifact.format_version > supported_format_version) {
    error("Unsupported bytecode format version");
}
if (artifact.host_api_version > supported_host_api_version) {
    error("Unsupported host API version");
}
```

To define a dynamic frame, you use the `Frame` type constructor with the current coordinates, keys, buttons, and camera angles. Inside the `frameWorker`, the `pollInput` function retrieves the next input state, and `move` calculates the new player position. These values are then packed into a new `Frame` tuple, which is sent to the parent process via `send` to update the rendering loop.

```fprisc
Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
```

The frameWorker function continuously receives messages containing the current player coordinates (x, z) and camera orientation (yaw, pitch). It polls for new input, calculates the next player position by calling the `move` function with the current coordinates and the new keys, and then renders the updated scene using `renderFrame` before sending the new frame state back to the parent.

```sol
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

In the QOS operating system, attempting to send a message to a process handle that refers to a terminated or non-existent parent will result in a communication failure, typically manifesting as a runtime exception or an error indicating the destination process is unavailable. The `frameWorker` relies on the `send parent reply` statement to communicate with its parent; if the parent process has terminated, the underlying IPC mechanism cannot deliver the `Frame` message, causing the worker to halt or crash.

```fprisc
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  _ = drop message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply; (* Failure if 'parent' is dead *)
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The `fill` function recursively builds a vector by calling `mkRow` for each iteration, which generates synthetic data using Gaussian distributions for features and a uniform random number for the noise flag. The `mkRow` function calculates a true label based on the axis-aligned rule, then flips it with a 5% probability if the `flip` random value equals 0, ensuring the resulting dataset contains the specified noise level while maintaining integer labels.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20; # 5% label noise
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

To verify the blocking sleep duration, read the CLINT clock register before the sleep and immediately after, then calculate the difference. If the result is greater than or equal to the requested time in microseconds, the wait was successful. In Sol, this is implemented by reading the register, calling the sleep function, reading the register again, and using a case expression to check the elapsed time:
```sol
clk = reg32 cl 49144;
t0 = read clk;
_ = T.sleep svc 50;
d1 = read clk - t0;
s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";
```

The `T.after` function schedules a deadline that fires through a single armed hardware slot (CLINT) when the time is due, ensuring the nearest deadline fires first. This mechanism is fire-and-forget; the system does not wait for confirmation but relies on the caller to collect the resulting `TFire` message from their own mailbox using `receiveFrom`.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;
  _ = T.after svc 20;
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

The file `qos/tests/timer.fpr` serves as an end-to-end test for the `Timer.qa` module, validating timer functionality across both native and portable backends. It verifies that the native backend correctly handles hardware interrupts via the CLINT timer and that the portable backend accurately simulates time delays using `Sys.sleepUs`. The test confirms that blocking sleeps meet their duration requirements and that fire-and-forget deadlines are processed in the correct order within the same armed slot.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  cl = device "clint";
  clk = reg32 cl 49144;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;
  mode = T.ping svc;

  # blocking sleep: must cost at least the asked-for CLINT time
  t0 = read clk;
  _ = T.sleep svc 50;
  d1 = read clk - t0;
  s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";

  # fire-and-forget: schedule, then collect from our own mailbox
  _ = T.after svc 20;
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

