To bin a raw F64 column, you must first determine the minimum and maximum values in the column, then calculate the bin width based on the number of bins. The `binCol` function automates this process by folding the vector to find bounds, computing the width, and recursively assigning integer codes via `binGo` and `binPut`. For example:

```fprisc
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

