To arm the UART, the owner actor must call `doArm` with the device address, the actor's identity, and the source address. This function checks ownership, binds the interrupt to the actor using `Sys.irqBind`, sets the interrupt enable register (`irqSet`) to enable Receive Data Available (RDA) based on pending bytes in the transmit queue, and replies with the updated state. The `irqSet` function specifically sets the RDA bit if the device is armed.

```sol
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.

doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.

irqSet st =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case armed == 1 of
    False -> Unit
  | True -> write (reg8 dev 1) (rdaBits (strlen txq)).
```

The `doArm` function first verifies that the calling actor owns the UART device, rejecting the request with a `UErr` if ownership is invalid. Upon successful validation, it binds the UART interrupt to the actor's ID using `Sys.irqBind` and initializes the interrupt enable register (IER) by calling `irqSet`. The `irqSet` function then configures the IER based on the `armed` flag: if the UART is armed, it writes the `rdaBits` value (set to 1 for ready-to-drain or 3 if there are pending bytes) to enable the receive data available interrupt, ensuring the interrupt fires when the transmission queue is full or data is ready to be read.

```fpr
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True ->
      _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
      st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
      _ = irqSet st2;
      reply me from UOk st2.
```

The `doArm2` function configures the Peripheral Interrupt Controller (PLIC) to associate the UART device with a specific interrupt request (IRQ) and sets the interrupt enable register (IER) to trigger interrupts when the transmit FIFO is full. It ensures that the system can automatically handle data transmission when the buffer is saturated.

```sol
_doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.
```

