An actor can explicitly yield control by calling `yield me` to return to the hart loop and receive a fresh fuel slice upon resume, provided the actor uses its own handle; using handle 0 will panic because it is not the current actor's handle. The scheduler manages the maximum observed backlog wait, measured in admissions, by maintaining a counter that is bounded by the aging threshold (`schedTau`); if the observed wait exceeds this threshold plus resident admissions, the system triggers an error, indicating the backlog has grown beyond the configured admission limits.

```sol
me = myself 0;
u = yield me;
mw = schedMaxWait 0;
t = schedTau 0;
if mw <= t + 1 then print "within bounds" else error "backlog exceeded";
```

The `yield` function is a cooperative yield that returns control to the hart loop and provides a fresh fuel slice upon resume, intended for politeness in long CPU loops. In contrast, `kill` marks an actor as DEAD, causing the hart loop to reap it off the stack; only the actor's control block (acb) persists, while its stack and slabs are reclaimed.
```
u = yield me;
me = myself 0;
u = kill w;
```

