The `snapshot` represents the stable file view observed during a single transaction attempt, which remains consistent even if other transactions modify the filesystem. In contrast, the `read set` consists of the specific snapshotted paths and directory listings validated at commit time to detect interference; if another writer changes a path in the read set, the script triggers a retry.

```sol
// Example: A read set is validated against the snapshot at commit.
// If a conflict is found, the script retries against a fresh snapshot.
SOL_FORCE_RETRY=1 fpr sol txnretry.sol
```

The `fprisc/NAMING.txt` file defines the core terminology and operational semantics for transactional consistency within the FP-RISC QOS operating system. It establishes critical concepts such as the snapshot (the stable file view during a transaction), the read set (paths validated at commit), and the write journal (buffered intentions), ensuring that transactions remain atomic and isolated. This documentation clarifies how the system handles conflicts through retries or rollback nets, and how receipts serve as evidence of committed effects.

```sol
// Conceptual example illustrating the atomicity defined in NAMING.txt
// A transaction reads a snapshot, modifies a goal state, and commits atomically.
// If a conflict occurs, the write journal is rolled back without leaking partial effects.
SOL {
  readPath "data.txt";
  writePath "data.txt" body "new content";
  commit; // Atomically validates read set against goal state
}
```

The QOS system treats the disk as the system itself, where the Loader service reads records from the log to reassemble a live set of code upon boot. When a process exits, its state is recorded as log entries rather than being lost; a subsequent boot replays these records, allowing the Loader to reconstruct the live application environment exactly as it was left, including any hot-swapped modules.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  fs = FS.serve (device "blk");
  ld = LD.serve fs;
  n = expectOk "replay" (LD.replay me ld);
  case n == 0 of
    True -> boot1 me ld
  | False -> boot2 me ld n.
```

The system verifies persistence by booting twice on the same disk image. In the first boot, a hot swap is performed via the Loader, which records the new attachment chain as `sys/live`. In the second boot, the Loader re-reads `sys/live` from the disk, reassembles the live set, and confirms the swap survived the reboot with no compiler or operator intervention.

```fpr
main =
  fs = FS.serve (device "blk");
  ld = LD.serve fs;
  replay = LD.replay myself ld;
  case replay == 0 of
    True -> boot1 myself ld
  | False -> boot2 myself ld replay.
```

The file `qos/tests/sysdisk.fpr` serves as a proof-of-concept demonstrating that the disk itself acts as the system by persisting state across reboots without requiring recompilation or explicit reloading. It verifies that a hot swap between application modules survives a reboot by replaying the disk's log records, which reassembles the live application set automatically. The test executes two boot cycles on the same disk image: the first boot fails to load a new module because the application chain remains intact, while the second boot successfully reassembles the updated live set from the persisted log, confirming the disk's ability to manage the system state independently of the running process.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  fs = FS.serve (device "blk");
  ld = LD.serve fs;
  n = expectOk "replay" (LD.replay me ld);
  case n == 0 of
    True -> boot1 me ld
  | False -> boot2 me ld n.
```

