The `readLatest` discipline (tag 1) retrieves the single newest live record for a given URL, serving applications that need the current state of a file or blob. Conversely, the `replayAll` discipline (tag 3) concatenates every live record for a URL in chronological log order, treating tombstones as stream resets; this implements an event-source model where the application folds updates over a durable message stream.

```sol
readLatest(url) {
    head = diskfs.head(url);
    return head.payload;
}

replayAll(url) {
    stream = [];
    head = diskfs.head(url);
    while (head != tombstone) {
        stream = stream ++ [head.payload];
        head = diskfs.next(head.url);
    }
    return stream;
}
```

The QOS system ensures durability by writing all persistent data—including archives, key-value streams, and files—into a single append-only page log. This mechanism is managed by the `mods/qlog.fpr` module, which defines the QLOG format and runs a dedicated STORAGE ACTOR spawned by `System.qa`. Access to this durable substrate is gated through capabilities, and while the system defaults to using a disk image, it gracefully degrades to an error state if no disk is present, treating the absence of storage as data rather than a fault.

```fpr
use mods/qlog.fpr;
use System.qa;

// Initialize the storage actor to manage the append-only log.
// This sets up the durable substrate for all application data.
StorageActor = new StorageActor();

// Register the storage actor with the system to gain capability access.
System.qa.registerStorage(StorageActor);
```

The append-only page log is the single durable substrate used by QOS to store all persistent data, including archives, key-value streams, general files, and actor chunks. It is managed by the `qlog.fpr` module and accessed via the `blk.c` HAL, where the system enforces durability by rejecting access if the log is missing rather than treating it as a fault.

```sol
use qos/qlog;
// The storage actor uses this log to persist state.
actor StorageActor = {
  on boot:
    initLog();
  on receive:
    if (isWrite) {
      logAppend(data);
    }
};
```

The `pushOne` threading mechanism addresses buffer corruption by passing unconsumed bytes through the function to handle frames arriving behind an HTTP upgrade in the same segment, whereas the `std.mvu` soft death pattern addresses memory growth by ensuring temporaries are reclaimed at the boundary of each turn. In the former, the loop overwrites the buffer and the fix involves threading data through the function; in the latter, the loop explicitly sends its state to itself, calls `Sys.poolReset`, and reads the state back so that turn-specific temporaries are destroyed, resulting in flat constant-state memory.

```sol
// Example of std.mvu soft death pattern in a socket loop
loop {
  state <- Sys.poolReset(state);
  read state <- read(state);
  // Temporaries created here die at the loop boundary
}
```

The file `runtime/actors.c` implements the actor runtime system, which manages the lifecycle of actors, including the creation of new actors for each connection, the reuse of connection IDs, and the periodic resetting of actor pools to prevent memory growth. It handles state transitions where actors send their state to themselves and trigger pool resets to ensure temporaries are reclaimed at turn boundaries.

```sol
actor loop {
  state = self.state;
  Sys.poolReset(self);
  state = self.read(state);
}
```

The loop must utilize `std.mvu`'s soft death mechanism by explicitly sending its state to itself, invoking `Sys.poolReset`, and then reading the state back at the boundary of each turn. This ensures that temporaries created during the turn are reclaimed before the next turn begins, keeping constant-state memory flat.

```sol
// Pseudocode representing the state management pattern
loop {
  // ... process turn ...
  Sys.poolReset();
  state := readState();
  // ... continue with new state ...
}
```

