Promotion moves an object into the ARC.qa pool where a dedicated manager actor handles its lifecycle via atomic message linearization, whereas the memory actor ownership model assigns a single memory actor to own a buddy block, with all actor-context blocks acting as requests to that owner. This distinction ensures that promotion centralizes control for individual objects while the memory actor model simplifies arena management by delegating block allocation to a single owner per buddy.

```fprisc
// Promotion creates a manager actor for the object
promotion(obj) -> {
    spawn manager_actor(obj)
    send manager_actor, INC(1)
}

// Memory actor owns the buddy; context blocks request from it
arena_request(arena_actor, size) -> {
    send arena_actor, GRANT(size)
}
```

The promotion request fails with an error returned to the sender instead of causing a machine panic. The system treats capacity exhaustion as a refused promotion, where the `Err` is sent back to the caller.

```fprisc
// Example of a refused promotion
let obj = promote(ptr)
if err(obj) {
  // Handle the refused promotion gracefully
}
```

When the ARC.qa pool is exhausted, promotion is refused by returning an error to the sender rather than triggering a machine panic. The system manages this by consolidating the interaction of three refcounts into a single count within one mailbox, where the object is deallocated and the manager exits upon reaching a count of zero.

```sol
# Example: A request that fails gracefully on pool exhaustion
let result = arc_promote(arena, size)
if result == Err {
  # Handle the refused promotion gracefully
  log "Promotion failed due to pool exhaustion"
}
```

When the ARC.qa pool is exhausted, promotion requests are refused rather than causing a machine panic. The allocator returns an error (Err) to the sender, indicating that capacity is unavailable.

```fprisc
try {
    arc_promote(object, count)
} catch err: Err {
    // Handle exhaustion: promotion failed due to pool limits
}
```

The system guarantees total atomicity by ensuring that a panic in any phase unwrites all preceding phases, leaving no partial state. For read-write conflicts, the runtime re-executes the entire script against a fresh snapshot rather than a conflicting partial state.

```sol
fpr sol example.sol
// Phase one writes a file
> fpr write "data.txt" "content"

// Phase two causes a panic
> panic "error"

// Result: "data.txt" does not exist after abort
```

To force a full re-execution of the script during a conflict (demonstrating the retry behavior), set the environment variable `SOL_FORCE_RETRY=n`.

