You can extract this identity by parsing the QAR2 file's LOAD section header. First, verify the file starts with the "QAR2\n" signature, then locate the LOAD entry within the header block. The hash is found in the first line of the LOAD section that begins with "sha ". Here is a Python function that performs this extraction:

```python
def load_sha_of(qa_path):
    with open(qa_path, "rb") as f:
        b = f.read()
    if b[:5] != b"QAR2\n":
        raise SystemExit(f"not a QAR2 archive")
    head, _, _ = b.partition(b"\n\n")
    off = len(head) + 2
    for line in head.decode().split("\n")[1:]:
        if line.startswith("LOAD"):
            load_start = off + int(line.split()[1])
            load_end = load_start + int(line.split()[2])
            load_data = b[load_start:load_end].decode()
            for ln in load_data.split("\n"):
                if ln.startswith("sha "):
                    return ln.split()[1]
    raise SystemExit(f"no LOAD sha found")
```

