To prevent race conditions where new registrations occur after a stop is announced, you must publish the watch before announcing the registration. The owner waits for both announcements before executing the stop, ensuring no new registration races the stop contract. This is implemented by sending a watch and a registration signal to the poller, receiving acknowledgments, and only then calling the stop function.

```fprisc
waiter p fd boss me = case p of P.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.

one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled";
```

The `one` function spawns two identical waiter processes and waits for both to complete by receiving their termination signals. It then explicitly stops the poller and verifies that both processes have been cancelled by checking their return values against expected error messages. This sequence guarantees that no new registrations race the stop contract and that the fallback cancellation mechanism functions correctly even when watcher slots are exhausted.

```sol
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

