A stamp is a global clock value preserved when an actor is donated or stolen, ensuring that its admission order remains strictly tied to its original arrival time. This mechanism guarantees deterministic behavior where actors with earlier stamps are always served before those with later ones, preventing the reset of aging that might occur during migration. For example, when an idle hart steals work, it pops the oldest entry from the machine-wide FIFO, which retains the original stamp:

```fpr
actor steal_work() {
    entry e = pop_oldest_fifo(); // e.stamp is preserved globally
    if (e.stamp < current_time()) {
        // e is served before any newly admitted work
        process(e);
    }
}
```

When an actor is donated or stolen, it preserves its original stamp, meaning its age does not reset and it maintains its relative priority based on that initial age. The stolen actor is placed into a machine-wide FIFO under a single lock, where the oldest donated entry is popped first before the hart sleeps. This ensures that the migrated actor continues to outrank un-aged actors on its new hart and is served before other aged actors with later stamps.

```fprisc
// Example of a donated actor retaining its age (stamp)
actor old_work = spawn(task) ;
if (backlog > DONATE_HI) {
    donate(old_work) ; // Stamp preserved, age not reset
}
// On the receiving hart, old_work remains ahead of newly admitted work
```

