The compiler rejects the code with an error stating that the case is not exhaustive or contains redundant arms. This is because the grammar enforces that every `case` must be exhaustive at compile time, and an arm that overlaps with earlier arms is considered an error. For example, the following Sol code would fail:

```sol
case x {
  1 -> { print "one"; }
  _ -> { print "other"; }
}
```

If the second arm were changed to explicitly handle `1` again, the compiler would flag it as redundant.

This document defines the scheduling policy for the QOS operating system, specifying that it is cooperative rather than preemptive, with device interrupts routed to auxiliary harts and prime hart (0) reserved for timer interrupts unless it is the only core. The scheduler manages ready slots and backlogs by randomly selecting two tasks and prioritizing the higher-priority one, or by using WCET bounds when an ACB threshold is exceeded. Timer service work is centralized in Timer.qa, which arms the nearest deadline and triggers a wake via an ordinary send, while single-hart systems utilize a 30ms deadlock detector for pacing.

```fprisc
// Example of the timer service binding and arming a deadline
Sys.timerBind(actor, Sys.timerArm);
// The actor will arm the nearest deadline in its sorted pending set
// and the irq hart will deliver a bare Int when due.
```

In a multi-core configuration, device interrupts are routed exclusively to auxiliary harts; the prime hart (hart 0) takes no device interrupts unless it is the only core. The prime hart's M context and interrupt drains are gated on the last live hart, meaning auxiliary harts are woken from wait states by armed CLINT deadlines via the MEIP. While bound actors may run on any hart assigned by the scheduler, the interrupt delivery itself is isolated to the auxiliary hart handling the interrupt.

```fprisc
// Example of binding a timer service to an auxiliary hart for interrupt handling
Sys.timerBind(actor_id) where actor_id is bound to an auxiliary hart
// This ensures device interrupts are drained on the auxiliary hart, not hart 0
```

