The implementation uses a recursive function that calculates the new velocity and position based on the current state and a time step, accumulating results into lists. It avoids trigonometry by relying entirely on arithmetic operations and rational bumps where necessary. The function takes the spring constant, time, position, and velocity as arguments, computes the next velocity using the formula $v_{new} = v + (-4x - 0.3v) \times dt$, updates the position with $x_{new} = x + v_{new} \times dt$, and recursively calls itself for the next iteration.

```sol
spring k t x v | k <= 0 = ([], []).
spring k t x v =
  v2 = v + (0 - 4) * x * 0.05 - 0.3 * v * 0.05;
  x2 = x + v2 * 0.05;
  (pxs, pvs) = spring (k - 1) (t + 0.05) x2 v2;
  ((t, x) :: pxs, (t, v) :: pvs).
```

