When an app launches, its full set of required and optional permissions is logged at `sys/perms/<aid>`. On subsequent launches, the system replays these remembered decisions silently, substituting them into the prompt without asking again. A required permission remembered as DENIED will be re-asked to prevent bricking the application, while optional permissions replayed as DENIED do not trigger a prompt. The log only grows when a new prompt is actually shown, and all decisions are cleared by `make wipe-disk` or a QDEL storage tag on the permissions file.

```fprisc
// Example of a runtime module resolution check that relies on remembered permissions
let res = svcModFn caps "hash/clock"
if res == (1, "pad2") {
    // Permission granted previously; use the resolved module
    clock = pad2
} else if res == (0, 0) {
    // Permission denied or not granted; fall back to builtin
    clock = builtin_clock
}
```

The TUIAppLauncher operates as a non-privileged application that requests standard permissions (keyboard, display, clock, apps) just like any other application. When an app is granted read access to `/services/modules`, it can invoke `svcModFn caps "<hash>/<fn>"` to resolve modules at runtime. If the module is found, `svcModFn` returns `(1, fn)`; if it is not found (a miss), it returns `(0, 0)` rather than panicking, allowing the resolver to handle absence gracefully.

```fprisc
// Example: Attempting to resolve a module with optional permissions
let (status, name) = svcModFn caps "tuiclock/now";
if (status == 1) {
    // Module found, use name
    print "Clock loaded: " name;
} else {
    // Module miss, fall back to builtin formatting
    print "Clock not found, using builtin";
}
```

The call returns `(0, 0)`, which is treated as valid data rather than a fatal error. This prevents the application from panicking if a resolver is absent, ensuring stability in environments like disk module stores or remote peers where missing modules are expected.

```fprisc
