A dynamic ring buffer is implemented using the `Dynamic` actor from the standard library, which grows its ARC table beyond the default 1024-entry ceiling to accommodate thousands of shared values in flight. Unlike a static ring that refuses a burst exceeding its capacity, the dynamic ring accepts all messages and preserves their arrival order. The provided `drainN` and `drainT` functions demonstrate how to drain these ordered messages from a main channel into a gate, either by processing individual integers or tuples based on a count `k`.

```fpr
# Example: Dynamic ring buffer using ordered tuple processing
main =
  gate = Dynamic;
  n = receiveFrom main gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainT main gate n 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (n, ordered);
  0.

drainT me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered).
drainT me main k last ordered =
  (a, b) = receiveFrom me main;
  drainT me main (k - 1) a (ordered and a > last).
```

The file `tests/mailbox.fpr` defines the mailbox contract within the FP-RISC standard library, specifically testing both Static and Dynamic ring buffer implementations. It verifies that a Static ring refuses bursts exceeding its capacity while a Dynamic ring grows to accommodate messages in order, even surpassing initial entry limits. The test suite includes a `gate` actor that passes message counts to a `sink`, which drains and reports ordered data, along with `burst` and `burstT` functions that simulate message injection to stress-test these buffer behaviors.

```fpr
gate me =
  s = receive me;
  k = receive me;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie s k;
  0.

sink main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainN me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.
```

