The previous model stored the entire log in a single register, whereas the new `std/store` distributes data across SHARD actors where each shard holds a specific key's part in memory and appends changes to its own file. Compaction occurs incrementally by rewriting only the files of superseded lines when they outnumber live ones, rather than managing a monolithic log. This approach ensures that any structure built inside an actor must survive a tidy by being included within the tidied value itself, preventing path dangling issues.

```fprisc
// Example of a shard handling its own compaction and file management
actor Shard {
  let key = "user_123";
  let memory = Map(); // Holds current state in memory
  let file = File();  // Appends changes and rewrites when superseded

  on write {
    memory.update(key, value);
    file.append(key, value);
    if (superseded_lines > live_lines) {
      file.rewrite(); // Compacts this shard at a time
    }
  }
}
```

