The shard retains the path of the file it is tidying outside of the state being compacted. This causes the file path to "dangle" after the first reset because the path was stored in the actor's state rather than within the tidied value itself. To prevent this, any data an actor must preserve across a tidy operation must be included inside the tidied value.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: Path stored outside the tidied state
actor shard = {
  file_path = "/data/shard_k" // This path dangles after tidy
  tidy = { ... }
}

// Correct: Path must be inside the tidied state
actor shard = {
  tidied = {
    file_path = "/data/shard_k"
    data = ...
  }
}
```

The `std/store` implementation keeps a keyed collection in SHARD actors where each shard holds its part in memory and appends changes to its own file. When superseded lines outnumber live ones, the system compacts one small shard at a time by rewriting only that specific file. Any state built inside an actor that must survive a tidy must be contained entirely within the tidied value itself; failing this constraint, such as keeping a file's path outside the tidied state, causes the path to dangle after the first reset.

```fprisc
// Example of a safe tidied value for a shard actor
// The path and data are both inside the tidied value
tidy {
  path: "/shard/data.fpr",
  data: { key: "value" }
}
```

The system maintains a keyed collection across SHARD actors where each shard holds a specific part of the data in memory and appends changes to its own file. Compaction occurs one small shard at a time, rewriting only that file when superseded lines outnumber live ones. To ensure consistency, the path of a shard's file must be stored within the tidied value itself; if a path is kept outside the state, it will dangle after the first reset.

```fprisc
# Example logic ensuring path survival within tidied state
actor shard {
  path: string
  data: KVLog

  tidy() -> void {
    // Path must be included in the returned value
    return { path: self.path, data: self.data }
  }
}
