C code does not consume fuel, so HAL functions must be bounded or yield via the actor machinery rather than spinning, as any blocking or spinning occurs outside the fuel budget and holds the hart. You must ensure your HAL entry points are O(bounded) or use the syscall mailbox pattern to yield.

```fprisc
// BAD: Spins outside fuel budget, blocking the hart
func bad_hal() {
    while (true) {
        // Busy-waiting here consumes no fuel but holds the hart
        _ = read_status();
    }
}

// GOOD: Yields via actor machinery to respect the fuel model
func good_hal() {
    // Yield allows the scheduler to switch harts before fuel is exhausted
    yield(); 
}
```

Arguments passed to HAL functions are borrowed, meaning the callee must increment the reference count (promote) any data it stores beyond the call boundary to ensure ownership transfer to the caller's actor slab. This prevents use-after-death bugs during actor teardown. Regarding the fuel model, C code does not consume fuel; its execution time is treated as a black box with a constant duration determined by the WCET model, so HAL functions must be bounded or yield via actors rather than spinning, as any blocking occurs outside the fuel budget.

```sol
// HAL function that stores a borrowed pointer
fn handle_resource (r: Ref<Resource>) -> Result {
    // Promote ownership of r to this actor's slab
    r.promote() 
    // Process safely within fuel bounds
    Ok(())
}
```

Arguments passed to HAL/runtime calls are BORROWED, meaning the callee does not decrement the reference count. The callee must incref (promote) any data it stores beyond the call boundary, such as when sending or spawning actors. Return values are owned by the caller's actor slab. Failing to promote a retained pointer constitutes a use-after-death bug during actor teardown.

