The `alloc` function searches for a suitable memory block using the `firstFit` strategy, starting from the head of the free list. It enforces strict size constraints by rejecting any allocation request exceeding 64 bits (bits 62 and 63), and it rounds up the requested size to a minimum of 96 bytes for alignment. If a matching block is found, the function splits it if necessary, marks it as used, zero-fills the payload, and returns the address of the new allocation.

```fprisc
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function first validates the requested size against the system's maximum limit by checking the 62nd and 63rd bits of the input word, returning an error if they are set to prevent allocation overflow. It then calculates the required block size by aligning the request to a 16-byte boundary and adding an 80-byte header overhead, ensuring the resulting size is at least 96 bytes. Finally, it delegates the actual search for a suitable free block to the `firstFit` function, which traverses the linked list of blocks to find the first one large enough to satisfy the adjusted size requirement.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function returns the error string "Builtin: allocation overflow". This occurs because the function checks the 62nd and 63rd bits of the input `bytes` parameter; if either bit is set (True), the function immediately triggers an error before attempting any memory allocation.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

