FP-RISC uses linear types and reference counting for memory management to avoid garbage collection, whereas the actor model uses fuel for cooperative yielding to control actor execution. Linear types ensure values are used exactly once, while fuel acts as a resource limit that forces an actor to yield control when exhausted.

```fprisc
let fuel_actor = actor {
  fuel: 10;
  loop:
    if fuel > 0 then
      println "Running";
      fuel := fuel - 1;
      loop
    else
      yield; // Controlled by fuel exhaustion
};
```

When an FP-RISC actor exhausts its allocated fuel, it must explicitly yield control back to the scheduler. Failure to yield results in the actor being terminated by the system.

```fprisc
actor main {
    fuel = 0;
    while (true) {
        fuel = fuel - 1;
        if (fuel <= 0) {
            yield(); // Required to prevent termination
        }
    }
}
```

