If a Sol function is used recursively without enabling auto-tabling, the VM will eventually exhaust its bounded execution budget (fuel) rather than terminating the recursion gracefully. When the fuel is exhausted, the runtime increments the fuel-preemption count instead of monopolizing execution, which triggers cooperative preemption. This behavior is inherent to the VM's design to prevent infinite loops from blocking the system, as demonstrated by the example where repeated integer calls might hit a bounded LRU table if auto-tabling is active, but would consume fuel if it is not.

```sol
// Example of a recursive function that consumes fuel without auto-tabling
def fib(n):
    if n <= 1: return n
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)

// Without auto-tabling enabled, repeated calls to fib will exhaust fuel
// and trigger preemption, whereas with auto-tabling, eligible pure
// recursive calls would be memoized in an LRU table instead.
```

