To create a multi-threaded server-client loop, you define a `rounds` function that recursively spawns a listener and a client on separate threads, waits for their completion via a `boss` channel, and decrements the iteration count. The server validates the incoming message and sends a reply, while the client sends a request and verifies the response before closing. This pattern utilizes `TCP.listen`, `TCP.connect`, `ST.write`, `ST.readOn`, and `spawnOn` to manage concurrent I/O.

```fpr
rounds me poll n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  listener = need (TCP.listen "127.0.0.1" 0);
  _ = spawnOn 0 (server listener poll me);
  _ = spawnOn 1 (client (TCP.port listener) poll me);
  _ = receive me;
  _ = receive me;
  rounds me poll (n - 1).
```

