To implement a simple TCP server, you must listen on a local address, spawn a worker to handle the connection, and use `Os.accept` to retrieve the file descriptor. The worker should read a fixed number of bytes using `readN`, verify the data, write a response via `writeOne`, and close both the connection and listener descriptors.

```fprisc
server listener boss me =
  _ = check (Probe.tls Unit == 0) "server hart";
  _ = wait listener;
  (fd, peer) = need (Os.accept listener);
  msg = readN fd 12 "";
  _ = check (msg == "hello shared") "request mismatch";
  _ = writeOne fd "shared reply";
  _ = need (Os.close fd);
  _ = need (Os.close listener);
  _ = send boss 1;
  0.
```

If `readN` is called when the file descriptor is at end-of-file, the `Os.read` operation returns an empty string. The subsequent `check` call verifying that the length of this string is greater than zero will fail, raising an error with the message "unexpected EOF". This prevents the recursive call to `readN` from attempting to read further on a closed stream.

```fprisc
readN fd n acc = if strlen acc >= n then acc else
  _ = wait fd;
  s = need (Os.read fd (n - strlen acc));
  _ = check (strlen s > 0) "unexpected EOF";
  readN fd n (acc + s).
```

