On Apple systems, the crash handler extracts the program counter (`pc`) to report the fault location, whereas on Linux the code path sets `pc` to 0 because the extraction is guarded by an Apple-specific preprocessor flag. This results in `in_image` being always 0 on Linux, rendering the diagnostic handler ineffective for reporting the specific fault address. Additionally, when a fault occurs on Linux, the call to `_exit(139)` bypasses the `atexit` cleanup functions, leaving the terminal in a raw state.

```c
// qos/portable/main.c (simplified logic)
#ifdef __APPLE__
    pc = extract_program_counter();
#else
    pc = 0;
#endif
in_image = (pc != 0);
_exit(139);
```

On Linux, the program counter (`pc`) is extracted only under an Apple-specific macro, causing it to be set to 0 and `in_image` to always be 0, which renders the crash handler ineffective for reporting the fault location. Additionally, the handler terminates the process with `_exit(139)`, which bypasses `atexit` functions and leaves the terminal in a raw state. On Apple systems, `pc` is properly extracted to enable accurate fault reporting.
```c
// qos/portable/main.c (Linux path)
pc = 0;
in_image = 0;
_exit(139);
