In QOS, "W^X" refers to the memory protection boundary where a region is either writable or executable, but never both simultaneously. This constraint ensures that when a loader reconstructs an image by parsing headers, it enforces a strict separation between code and data segments. The system refuses execution if an "execsz" (executable size) range overlaps with a "rwoff" (read/write/off) boundary, preventing code execution from modifying its own instructions.

```sol
// Example of a W^X boundary check in Sol
// If the executable size (execsz) reaches the read/write/off boundary (rwoff),
// the loader refuses execution to maintain memory safety.
if execsz >= rwoff {
    refuse "execsz reaches rwoff"
}
```

The QOS loader avoids ELF's machinery to eliminate relocation, dynamic linking, and symbol resolution at load time. Instead, it consumes two byte ranges, an entry point, and a write-execute (W^X) boundary, reconstructing the load by parsing headers designed for a different purpose. This approach allows QAR2 to output five numbers directly, enabling the archive parser to utilize the system's existing tokenizer while defining failure modes as explicit refusals—such as "image outside the window" or "sha mismatch"—rather than relying on malformed-header errors.

```sol
; Example of a minimal FP-RISC image using W^X boundaries
; The loader expects: [range1] [range2] [entry] [w^x_boundary]

; Define the executable range and entry
@entry = 0x1000
@range1 = 0x0000
@range2 = 0x0001

; Set the W^X boundary to prevent code execution from data sections
@w_x = 0x0002

; The loader will refuse if any instruction crosses @w_x
```

