The WCET bound decomposes into the sum of instruction costs across basic blocks, multiplied by the number of fuel-check entries, plus the sum of entry-point bounds for all function calls, and finally adding the costs of sends and recvs based on their respective promotion and decref bounds. Scheduler-level latency is defined as the fuel quantum multiplied by the worst number of instructions per fuel unit for any runnable peer; however, this theoretical value often fails to account for actual system constraints, creating the "gaps" where the model underestimates real-world performance.

```fprc
// Example illustrating the fuel quantum and instruction cost relationship
actor main() {
    while (true) {
        // High instruction density per fuel unit increases scheduler latency
        x = x + 1;
        y = y + 1;
        z = z + 1;
        check_fuel(); // Triggers fuel-check constant overhead
    }
}
```

