ESP-IDF split from FP-RISC due to three fundamental differences: the Instruction Set Architecture (ISA), the absence of a specific process model, and distinct hardware requirements. While FP-RISC relies on a unified ISA and process model, ESP-IDF implements a useful subset of POSIX (including pthreads over FreeRTOS, newlib's clocks, poll, lwIP's BSD sockets, and a VFS) to run on diverse hardware. Consequently, the design evolved toward a single POSIX system capable of hosting multiple ISA types, with hardware-specific implementations moved into platform libraries rather than the core machine layer.

```sol
// Example of hardware-agnostic POSIX behavior in FP-RISC
// The runtime handles the POSIX interface; hardware specifics are abstracted away.
import posix;

func main() {
    var thread = posix::createThread("worker", worker_func);
    var socket = posix::socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    var fd = posix::open("/dev/tty", O_RDONLY);

    // All hardware specifics (ISA, process model, UART controller)
    // are handled by the underlying platform library, not the POSIX layer.
    posix::sleep(1000);
}

func worker_func() {
    var data = posix::read(fd, buffer, 1024);
}
```

