When called, `Os.watchClose` initiates an asynchronous teardown process where the caller yields and retries until the function returns True, indicating the slot is released. During this phase, the system frees descriptor buffers, wakes up descriptors and synchronization objects, and allows the detached worker to exit. It is critical that the caller completes this handshake, as abandoned handles are not automatically collected.

```sol
while (Os.watchClose(handle) == False) {
    yield();
    // Retry until the slot is released
}
```

In this context, "yield" means the calling function pauses its execution to allow other tasks to run while the watch slot is being asynchronously torn down. The caller must repeatedly call `Os.watchClose` until it returns `True`, which signals that the slot has been fully released and resources freed.

```sol
while Os.watchClose(handle) == False:
    yield()
    // Retry the close operation
```

The operation starts asynchronous teardown because the caller must finish the handshake; abandoned handles are not collected. The function returns False while the slot is being freed, waking descriptors and synchronization objects, and only returns True once the detached worker exits and the slot is released.
```sol
while (Os.watchClose(handle) == False) {
    yield();
    // Retry until the slot is released
}
```

