The `fire` function simulates a sequence of software interrupts by incrementing a flag variable until it reaches zero, which signals the CPU to resume execution. It takes an integer `n` representing the number of interrupts to fire; if `n` is zero, it completes immediately, otherwise it writes zero to the flag address, sets the Machine Software Interrupt Pending (MSIP) register to 1 to trigger the interrupt, waits for the flag to become zero (indicating the interrupt handler has finished), verifies the execution counter matches the expected sequence, and recursively calls itself with `n-1`.

```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

The `fire` function returns `Unit` (the void type in FP-RISC) to indicate successful execution of its side effects. It does not return a value that can be printed or used in further calculations; its purpose is to trigger software interrupts and verify their handling. As shown in the definition, the function performs memory writes, waits for a flag, checks a condition, and recursively calls itself, all ending with `Unit`:

```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

The `fire` function triggers a sequence of software interrupts by resetting the interrupt flag to zero, setting the machine interrupt status word to one, waiting for the flag to be set, verifying the execution order, and then recursively calling itself with the decremented count. It accepts a single integer argument representing the initial count of interrupts to fire.

```fprisc
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1);
```

The function implements an exponential backoff strategy for reconnection attempts. When a WebSocket closes unexpectedly (excluding codes 1000 and 1001), it schedules a new connection attempt using `setTimeout` with a duration stored in the `wait` variable. If the reconnection fails again, this duration is doubled up to a maximum cap of 8000 milliseconds.

```fprisc
ws.onclose = function (ev) \{
  status('offline (' + ev.code + ')');
  if (ev.code === 1000 || ev.code === 1001) return;   /* deliberate close: stay down */
  setTimeout(connect, wait);
  wait = Math.min(wait * 2, 8000);
};
```

