Enabling this configuration prevents performance degradation during memory allocation caused by Transparent Huge Pages (THP). When THP is set to `always`, every page fault zeroes 2 MiB of memory, and the subsequent reset of a pool forces these pages back and faults them in again, incurring a 27% buildroot cost on allocation. Setting it to `madvise` aligns THP behavior with the rest of the system, eliminating this specific overhead.

```sol
// Example Sol code demonstrating memory allocation where THP overhead is avoided
// with CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE=y

import qos.kernel;

func main() {
    // Allocating memory for the application pool
    let mem = qos.kernel.alloc_page_pool(1024 * 1024);
    // Without CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE=y, subsequent pool resets
    // could trigger costly zeroing and refaulting of 2 MiB pages.
}
```

When THP is set to `always`, every page fault zeroes 2 MiB, causing Transparent Huge Pages to cost Buildroot 27% on allocation due to page resets and refaulting. In contrast, when using `madvise` (Ubuntu's default), THP costs are level with the rest of the memory allocation.
```sol
// Example of memory allocation behavior difference
let page_size = 2 MiB;
if (thp_mode == "always") {
    // Each fault zeroes page_size
    fault(page);
} else if (thp_mode == "madvise") {
    // Fault cost is standard
    fault(page);
}
```

