When two registers attempt to sell the last unit of an item simultaneously, exactly one register commits the sale and the other is informed that the item is sold out. The badge reads "sold out" for the register that did not commit. This behavior is part of the standard guard logic for quantity clamping and stock management.

```sol
// Example logic for the last-unit race outcome
if (quantity == 1) {
    // Attempt to sell
    if (commit_succeeded) {
        // Register commits the sale
        print("Sold last unit");
    } else {
        // The other register fails
        print("Sold out");
    }
}
```

This design ensures atomicity and prevents overselling when multiple registers attempt to sell the last available unit simultaneously. The system guarantees that only one transaction successfully commits the sale, while the other is immediately rejected with a sold-out status to maintain inventory consistency.

```sol
// Example logic for the last-unit race resolution
if (quantity == 1) {
    if (register1.is_ringed() && register2.is_ringed()) {
        // Exactly one commit, the other is told sold out
        if (register1.id < register2.id) {
            register1.commit_sale()
            register2.set_status("sold_out")
        } else {
            register2.commit_sale()
            register1.set_status("sold_out")
        }
    }
}
```

