Previously, the harness leaked server ports upon being killed by a sweep timeout or manual termination because its cleanup logic could not survive the harness's own destruction. Running the harness inside the sweep ensures proper cleanup on signals like SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP and handles the edge case of abandoned runs via a pidfile sweep on startup.

```fprisc
// Example of the harness running in the sweep context
sweep {
  live {
    // Harness logic here
  }
}
```

The previous harness cleanup relied on a `finally` block that failed to execute if the harness itself was killed by a sweep timeout or manual interruption, leaving orphaned server ports. The updated mechanism places the server in its own process group, ensuring cleanup via SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP and a pidfile sweep, preventing resource leaks during abrupt terminations.

```fprisc
