spawn(server) // Ensures independent lifecycle and signal handling
```

The `apply` function in the live protocol handler checks if the response object contains an `emit` field. If present, it constructs a `CustomEvent` with the name prefixed by 'live:' and the provided details, then dispatches it to the document. This mechanism allows the server to trigger specific actions within the client-side script.

```sol
function dispatchLiveEvent(name, detail) \{
    document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('live:' + name, \{ detail: detail }));
}
```

The `apply` function processes server-to-client commands by inspecting the `res` object to determine the required action, such as navigation, event emission, view state updates, or slot modifications. If `res.nav` exists, it redirects the browser location; if `res.emit` is present, it dispatches a custom DOM event. For view state updates, it validates the protocol version (`p`), splices the state and data arrays accordingly, and rebuilds the view. Finally, if neither navigation nor emission is set, it merges new slot data into the global state `S` and triggers a build if a wire element exists to display timing metrics.

```fpr
if (res.nav) \{ location.href = res.nav; return; \}
if (res.emit) \{ document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('live:' + res.emit, \{ detail: res.detail })); return; \}
```

The `apply` function supports partial updates via the `res.p` parameter, which must be either 2 or 3 to modify the server-side state `S` without a full replacement. If `res.p` is 3, the function splices all elements in the state arrays; if it is 2, it only splices the new data. After updating the state, the function calls `build()` to reflect changes on the client.

```fprisc
var next = res.p === 3 ? \{s: splicedAll(S.s, res.s), d: splicedAll(S.d, res.d)\}
                       : \{s: spliced(S.s, res.s), d: spliced(S.d, res.d)\};
```

The `send` function initializes `busy` to `true` at the start of the function execution to block further requests while the current one is pending. It is set back to `false` in the `.finally` block, ensuring the flag resets even if the request fails or completes successfully, thereby allowing new requests to proceed immediately after the previous one finishes.

```fprisc
if (busy) return;
busy = true;
// ... request logic ...
.finally(function () \{ busy = false; });
```

The `apply` function processes a JSON response containing shape changes (`res.s`) or slot data updates (`res.d`). If the response includes a shape change, it triggers a full rebuild of the application state. Otherwise, it iterates through the updated slots, locates the corresponding DOM elements, and updates their text content and visual feedback state by removing and re-adding a 'lv-flash' class to indicate the update.

```sol
function apply(res) {
  if (res.s) { S = { s: res.s, d: res.d }; build(); return; }
  for (var k in res.d) {
    S.d[k] = res.d[k];
    var el = root.querySelector('[data-slot="' + k + '"]');
    if (el) {
      el.textContent = res.d[k];
      el.classList.remove('lv-flash');
      void el.offsetWidth;
      el.classList.add('lv-flash');
    }
  }
}
```

