FP-RISC prioritizes a strong core with careful analysis engines, whereas Sol emphasizes a great core with inverted priorities by adding acceleration tiers on top of a quadratic interpreter. This ethos violation in Sol contrasts with FP-RISC's approach of avoiding premature optimization in favor of linear vectors and refcounting. For example, a Sol interpreter might be left quadratic while four acceleration tiers are grown on top, whereas FP-RISC relies on string-matched, show-hashed, and comment-asserted diagnostics to maintain its core integrity.

