The `watcher` function continuously monitors a list of file paths to detect changes. It uses a `seen` timestamp to compare against the current file modification times obtained by the `stamps` function. If a change is detected, it executes the `rebuild` function via `P.run`. If the rebuild succeeds with an exit status of 0, it sends a `Reload` signal to the client; otherwise, it logs an error and continues monitoring. The loop sleeps for 300,000 microseconds (300ms) between checks to avoid excessive CPU usage.

```sol
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

