The `placeAt` function attempts to place a block at a specific world coordinate within a voxel model. It accepts the current model, world state, location, selection, color, and a boolean flag indicating whether the placement is successful. If the placement fails (indicated by the `False` argument), the function retrieves the original model components, sets the new block color at the selected location, performs a rebake edit to update the model geometry, and returns a new model with a specific message indicating the failure reason.

```sol
placeAt m w lm sel c False =
  (Mdl bd yaw pit sel2 act) = m;
  w2 = wSet w c sel;
  (w3, lm2) = rebakeEdit w2 lm c;
  (Mdl bd yaw pit sel2 "place {sel} at {c}", w3, lm2).
```

When `placeAt` fails, it invokes `missMsg` to generate a specific error message and returns the original model and vectors unchanged. If the placement is blocked, `missMsg` returns a message indicating the block is occupied, while if the target is missing, it returns a message stating there is no target.
```fpr
placeAt m w lm sel c False =
  (Mdl bd yaw pit sel2 act) = m;
  w2 = wSet w c sel;
  (w3, lm2) = rebakeEdit w2 lm c;
  (Mdl bd yaw pit sel2 "place {sel} at {c}", w3, lm2).

missMsg (Mdl bd yaw pit sel act) 0 = Mdl bd yaw pit sel "destroy (no target)".
missMsg (Mdl bd yaw pit sel act) mode = Mdl bd yaw pit sel "place (blocked)".
```

The first line executes `Tcp.listen` to bind a TCP socket to the specified IP and a randomly assigned port, returning the socket handle `x` if successful (or raising an error otherwise). The second line spawns a new thread that runs `Http.serve` indefinitely, listening on that socket for incoming HTTP requests and dispatching them to the `handle` function.

