`Mem.alloc` creates a new memory block from a specified byte size, returning a fresh address that is valid only for the caller. `Mem.realloc` modifies an existing address to a new size; if the new size is zero, it frees the memory and returns null, whereas `Mem.alloc` returning null indicates a zero-size allocation. Unlike `Mem.alloc`, `Mem.realloc` may move the data to a new address if growth is required, and the caller must use the returned address rather than the old one.

```sol
// Allocate a new buffer of 256 bytes
let addr1 = Mem.alloc 256

// Reallocate the existing buffer to 512 bytes (may move data)
let addr2 = Mem.realloc addr1 512

// Freeing null is a no-op
Mem.free null
```

The `wrDo` function executes a write operation under the write-ahead discipline by first writing the superblock to mark the data as uncommitted, then writing the data pages, and finally updating the superblock to commit the transaction. It ensures atomicity by updating the head pointer only after all data pages are successfully written.

```fpr
wrDo dev head hdr s pl np m =
  Meta h v w c d sw mr = m;
  _ = sbW dev m;
  _ = blkWrite dev head hdr;
  _ = writePages dev (head + 1) s 1 pl;
  h2 = head + 1 + np;
  _ = sbW dev (Meta h2 v 0 1 d sw mr);
  Ok h2.
```

