The ALIGN(0x10000) directive ensures the `execsz` segment is padded to a page boundary, which allows the loader to safely round up `execsz` without exceeding the `rwoff` (write-only) boundary of the subsequent rw segment. This enforces the W^X (Write XOR Execute) security model, where a loader must refuse execution if the page-aligned `execsz` would overlap into the writable region.

```sol
# Example of the alignment effect on segment sizes
segment r-x {
    align(0x10000) # Forces padding here
    load 0x1000..0x1FFF
}
segment rw {
    load 0x2000..0x2FFF # Starts exactly where the aligned r-x ends
}
```

The ALIGN directive ensures that the `execsz` (executable size) rounded up to a page size does not exceed the `rwoff` (read-write-only file offset) boundary. This enforces the W^X (write XOR execute) security model by guaranteeing that the writable section starts strictly after the executable section, preventing execution of code within writable memory regions.

```sol
// Example of the boundary check logic derived from the alignment rule
let execsz_rounded = round_up(execsz, page_size)
if (execsz_rounded >= rwoff) {
    error "W^X violation: executable region overlaps writable region"
}
```

