A destructive semantic change necessitates a new major foundation because it fundamentally alters the language's behavior, rendering existing Base contract claims invalid even if much of the implementation can be reused. Consequently, the Base contract itself must be reconsidered to reflect the new language capabilities, as a breaking Base change cannot maintain an unchanged platform-contract claim.

```sol
-- Example illustrating the need for a new Base contract when Sol semantics change
-- This code relies on Sol features that would break under a destructive semantic change.
-- It requires the new (L_n, S_{n+1}) contract pair instead of the old (L_n, S_n).

import Base;

// This function signature assumes the old Sol semantics.
// If Sol undergoes a destructive change, this function or its import
