A hot-swappable driver is implemented by spawning the function as a separate process that communicates via a port, allowing the application to send control commands like "swap" to trigger a version change. The driver function itself remains stable in signature, while its internal logic can be replaced by a new file (e.g., edit2.fpr) that defines the same entry points. This is demonstrated in the `driver` function, which sends a "swap" command at a step boundary, and the `main` function, which spawns the driver and manages the port lifecycle.

```sol
driver port me =
  _ = send port ("note", "one");
  _ = send port ("swap", "edit2");
  _ = send port ("note", "two");
  _ = send port ("quit", "");
  "driver done".
```

The `driver` function is a high-level Sol script that orchestrates a hot-swappable application lifecycle by sending specific control messages to a communication port. It begins by sending a "note" to initialize the session, followed by a "swap" command to trigger a hot swap at a step boundary, allowing the system to load a new version of the application logic. After processing, it sends another "note" to confirm completion and finally sends a "quit" signal to terminate the driver process. This mechanism enables the `qos` operating system to dynamically replace the implementation of the `editLine` function, as demonstrated by the transition from `edit1.fpr` to `edit2.fpr`, where the second version capitalizes the first letter of input strings.

```sol
driver port me =
  _ = send port ("note", "one");
  _ = send port ("swap", "edit2");
  _ = send port ("note", "two");
  _ = send port ("quit", "");
  "driver done".
```

To load a file, first obtain a local device (LD) by serving a file system (FS), then use the LD.load function with the local device, the memory entity (me), and the filename as arguments. If the load succeeds, the result is an integer representing the loaded data; if it fails, an error value is returned. You must pattern match on the result to handle both cases, executing specific logic for success or error.

```sol
ld = LD.serve fs;
r0 = LD.load me ld "edit1";
_ = case r0 of
  Ok u -> 0
  | Err e -> error "__NAME__ load edit1: {e}";
```

The `notesRun` function creates a process identity, clears the console, prints a header, and enters the TUI screen using `TUI.enterScreen` with the console as the input source and an invalid buffer of 80x24 dimensions.

```fpr
notesRun con stg =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = P.putLn con "";
  _ = P.putLn con "  == QOS Notes ==   (a loaded process)";
  _ = notesHello con stg;
  _ = TUI.enterScreen (P.puts con);
  nFrame con me stg Nil 0 "" (TUI.invalidBuf 80 24).
```

The `notesRun` function is called within the `main` process after verifying that the process has been granted write access to the `/services/display` channel and read access to the `/services/keyboard` channel. It requires two arguments: a console handle (`con`) obtained via `P.openCon Unit`, and a storage capability flag (`stg`) determined by checking the `/services/storage` channel for write permissions. Upon execution, it initializes the process by printing a header, entering the TUI screen, and setting up an invalid buffer.

```fpr
notesRun con stg =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = P.putLn con "";
  _ = P.putLn con "  == QOS Notes ==   (a loaded process)";
  _ = notesHello con stg;
  _ = TUI.enterScreen (P.puts con);
  nFrame con me stg Nil 0 "" (TUI.invalidBuf 80 24).
```

The program first requests a write grant on the display service and a read grant on the keyboard service via the `P.hasGrant` function. If both grants are successful, it opens a console, checks for a storage write grant, and enters the TUI screen. During initialization, it prints a header and then queries the storage capability; if storage is unavailable, it indicates the notes are session-only, otherwise it replays the key-value store to display the current note count.

```fpr
notesRun con stg =
  me = myself 0;
  _ = P.putLn con "";
  _ = P.putLn con "  == QOS Notes ==   (a loaded process)";
  _ = notesHello con stg;
  _ = TUI.enterScreen (P.puts con);
  nFrame con me stg Nil 0 "" (TUI.invalidBuf 80 24).
