The `binCol` function first calculates the range of the input column and divides it into `nb` equal-width bins. It then iterates through the column values, computing the bin index for each value by dividing its difference from the lower bound by the bin width, clamping the result to the valid range of indices. The function returns the resulting vector of integer codes along with the updated lower bound and bin width.

```fprisc
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4)
```

The `binCol` function takes an unsafe Vector of F64 numbers and an integer specifying the number of bins, returning the resulting integer codes and updated vector state. It calculates bin width by finding the minimum and maximum values in the column, then iterates through the data assigning each value to the nearest integer bin index.

```sol
binCol : unsafe Vector -> Int -> _ .
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

The function calculates the binning width `w0` by dividing the difference between the maximum and minimum values in the column by the number of bins. If this calculated width is less than or equal to zero, it is replaced with 1.0 to prevent division by zero or invalid binning. The code implements this logic as follows:

```fprisc
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
```

