The `rebakeNbrs` function performs an incremental rebake by first checking if the face count exceeds the total number of faces to avoid unnecessary work. It retrieves the neighbors of the edited cell, iterates through them sequentially, and recursively calls `rebakeAt` for each neighbor until all are processed. This approach ensures that only the edited cell's neighbors and the cells along the anti-sun ray are updated, reducing the computational load to approximately 1/50th of a full bake.

```sol
rebakeNbrs : unsafe Vector -> Vector -> Int -> Int -> (Vector, Vector) .
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1).
```

The `rebakeNbrs` function implements an incremental rebake strategy to update only the lightmap regions affected by a specific edit, significantly reducing computational cost compared to a full bake. It recursively processes the edited cell and its six neighbors (exposure) and the cells along the anti-sun ray. The function first checks if the face counter `f` has reached the total number of faces `nFaces`; if so, it returns the current state unchanged. Otherwise, it calculates the coordinates of the neighbor cell, invokes `rebakeAt` to update that specific cell, and then recursively calls itself with the updated world and lightmap vectors, incrementing the face counter for the next iteration.

```sol
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1);
```

The system avoids recalculating the entire scene by limiting updates to only the faces of the edited cell, its six neighboring cells (for exposure changes), and cells along the anti-sun ray (for shadow ray intersections). This reduces the computational load to approximately 1/50th of a full bake, as the lightmap remains static until specific edits dirty a named slice of it.

```sol
