The `bestStep` function determines the optimal adjacent cell for a character to move to, either to minimize distance to a goal or maximize distance when fleeing. It evaluates the current position along with the four cardinal neighbors (up, down, left, right) and selects the next best candidate using a recursive `pick` operation. The selection logic depends on the `flee` flag: if fleeing, it chooses the neighbor with the highest distance value (up to 9999), whereas normally it chooses the neighbor with the lowest distance. If a neighbor is blocked or contains an enemy, it is skipped in favor of the previously selected best option.

```fpr
bestStep m dist x y flee =
  here = distAt m dist x y;
  c1 = (x - 1, y);
  c2 = (x + 1, y);
  c3 = (x, y - 1);
  c4 = (x, y + 1);
  b1 = pick m dist (x, y) here c1 flee;
  b2 = pick m dist b1 (scoreOf m dist b1 flee) c2 flee;
  b3 = pick m dist b2 (scoreOf m dist b2 flee) c3 flee;
