The `heapInit` function initializes a heap by calculating the aligned start (`lo`) and rounded-down end (`hi`) addresses from the provided range, then performs three critical checks: it ensures the start address is not greater than the end address, verifies the total size is at least 96 bytes, and creates a `Block` at the aligned start address. It then zeroes out the first 8 words of this block, sets the block size, and registers the block as the heap head along with the low and high boundaries in the global state.

```fprisc
lo = alignUp (Addr.toWord start);
hi = Word.and (Addr.toWord end) (Word.not (w 15));
_ = case i hi <= i lo of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
_ = case i (Word.sub hi lo) < 96 of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
h = Block.at (Addr.fromWord lo);
_ = zeroWords (Block.addr h) 8;
_ = Block.setSize h (Word.sub hi lo);
_ = Heap.setHead (st Unit) h;
_ = Heap.setLow (st Unit) lo;
Heap.setHigh (st Unit) hi.
```

The `heapInit` function allocates a heap region between a start and end address, initializing it with zeroed memory and setting internal heap metadata. It takes two `Addr` arguments: `start` and `end`. Before calling it, ensure the start address is aligned upwards and the calculated size is at least 96 words. The function returns `Unit` on success or raises an error if the region is too small.

```fprisc
heapInit startAddr endAddr =
  lo = alignUp (Addr.toWord startAddr);
  hi = Word.and (Addr.toWord endAddr) (Word.not (w 15));
  case i hi <= i lo of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
  case i (Word.sub hi lo) < 96 of True -> error "Builtin: heap too small" | False -> Unit;
  h = Block.at (Addr.fromWord lo);
  zeroWords (Block.addr h) 8;
  Block.setSize h (Word.sub hi lo);
  Heap.setHead (st Unit) h;
  Heap.setLow (st Unit) lo;
  Heap.setHigh (st Unit) hi.
```

