The PLIC uses a claim-then-mask discipline where the drain loop masks the source upon claiming it, preventing re-pending while the actor is not scheduled. After the actor services the device via Sys.irqAck, it re-arms the source; if the line remains high, the gateway pends again for a fresh delivery, ensuring exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```sol
# Example of the claim-then-mask flow
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    # Drain masks the source immediately to prevent storms
    hal_irq_ack(source_id) {
        # Actor re-arms the source if needed
    }
}
```

The CLAIM-THEN-MASK strategy prevents interrupt storms by masking a level-triggered source immediately upon claim, ensuring the drain loop does not spin endlessly on a single pending event. The actor re-arms the source only after servicing the device; if the line remains high, the gateway re-pends the interrupt for a fresh, exactly-once delivery. This ensures that level-triggered sources like the 16550 do not re-pend an interrupt the instant the actor completes its service.
```fpr
# Pseudo-code illustrating the claim and mask sequence
hal_irq_claim(source_id) {
    # Mask the source immediately to prevent re-pending during drain
    plic_set_mask(source_id, 1);
    # Drain loop processes the pending interrupt
    drain_loop(source_id);
}

# Actor re-arms the source after servicing
Sys.irqAck(source_id) {
    # Re-arm the source; if still high, plic will re-pend
    plic_set_mask(source_id, 0);
}
```

To service an interrupt, call `hal_irq_claim` from the IRQ hart's M context after the source has raised MEIP. This function masks the source to prevent re-pending and returns the source ID. You must then deliver the interrupt to your actor via `Sys.irqBind`. After servicing the device, call `hal_irq_ack` to re-arm the source if necessary.

```fpr
// Inside the IRQ hart's M context
let src = hal_irq_claim()
Sys.irqBind(src, my_actor)
// ... service device ...
hal_irq_ack(src)
```

