The `evalStr` function evaluates a string term located at address `f` by first extracting its cell contents to determine the string's length (`s`) and internal offset (`ar`). It then validates the term's validity by checking if the length `s` is between 2 and 6 (inclusive) and if the offset `ar` equals 2; if either condition fails, it returns `Nope`. If the term is valid, it delegates the actual evaluation to the `evalBin` function.

```sol
evalStr f m =
  (m1, tf, fv) = cellAt f m;
  s = fv / 1024;
  ar = fv % 1024;
  case and (and (s >= 2) (s <= 6)) (ar == 2) of
    False -> (m1, Nope)
  | True -> evalBin s f m1.
```

The `evalStr` function retrieves a heap cell using `cellAt`, then decomposes the cell's value into a sign/scale component (`s`) and an argument component (`ar`) by dividing and modulo-ing by 1024. It validates the argument only if the scale `s` is between 2 and 6 (inclusive) and the argument `ar` equals 2; if both conditions hold, it proceeds to `evalBin`, otherwise it returns `Nope`.

```sol
evalStr f m =
  (m1, tf, fv) = cellAt f m;
  s = fv / 1024;
  ar = fv % 1024;
  case and (and (s >= 2) (s <= 6)) (ar == 2) of
    False -> (m1, Nope)
  | True -> evalBin s f m1.
```

