The `allocAdt` function creates an allocated data structure by first checking layout constraints, then allocating memory, and finally setting the field count. The `retain` function increments the reference count of a block if it is currently in the heap, while `release` drains the block's pending worklist to ensure all fields are visited before freeing the memory.

```sol
def allocRetainRelease : Word -> Unit .
allocRetainRelease n =
  v = allocAdt 16 n;
  retain v;
  release v;
```

When a block's reference count drops to zero, the `drain` function is invoked to process the work queue associated with that block. The mechanism retrieves the pending work, visits the block's fields to propagate ownership changes, and then explicitly deallocates the block's payload memory before draining the remaining work queue. This ensures that memory is only freed after all dependent blocks have been processed.

```fprisc
drain work = case Block.isNull work of
    True -> Unit
  | False ->
    b = work;
    rest = Block.pending b;
    rest2 = visit b (Addr.add (payload b) 8) 0 (fields b) rest;
    _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
    _ = free (payload b);
    drain rest2.
```

The `jobWait` function initiates a blocking operation for an ESP-IDF job by first allocating a unique job slot via `Esp.jobNew` and binding its interrupt handler to the caller. It then spawns a helper task that waits for the job to complete, retrieves the result text via `Esp.jobResult`, and sends the outcome back to the original caller through a message channel. The caller can subsequently retrieve this result using the `job` function, which wraps `jobWait` to handle the spawning and message reception transparently.

```sol
jobWait boss start self =
  slot = Esp.jobNew Unit;
  _ = Sys.irqBind slot self;
  r = (case start slot of
      0 -> m = receive self; _ = drop m; Esp.jobResult slot
    | _ -> _ = Esp.jobResult slot; "error\tthe job queue is full");
  _ = send boss (Ok r);
  Unit.
```

The `job` function returns a string containing the result of an asynchronous ESP-IDF job, handling both success and error cases by wrapping the output in either `Ok` or `Err` constructors before extracting the text. It achieves this by first spawning a `jobWait` task that blocks the caller until the job completes, then receiving the result from that task. If the result is `Ok`, the function keeps the text; otherwise, it keeps the error message.

```sol
job : (Int -> Int) -> String .
job start =
  me = myself 0;
  w = spawn (fn self -> jobWait me start self);
  r = receiveFrom me w;
  out = (case r of Ok text -> keep text | Err why -> keep why);
  _ = drop r;
  out.
```

To initiate a Wi-Fi scan, the user calls `Esp.wifiScan` with an integer argument, which returns a job ID. This ID is then passed to `Esp.jobResult` to retrieve the resulting string. Alternatively, a blocking call can be made using the `job` helper, which spawns a background task to wait for the job completion.

```sol
jobId = Esp.wifiScan 0;
result = Esp.jobResult jobId;

-- Or using the blocking helper
result = job Esp.wifiScan 0;
```

The `job` function spawns a helper task that uses `Esp.jobNew` to queue a blocking operation (like `wifiScan`) and binds its interrupt to the calling actor. It then waits for the result via `receiveFrom` and returns a formatted string. If the job queue is full, the operation fails with an error message.

```sol
job start =
  me = myself 0;
  w = spawn (fn self -> jobWait me start self);
  r = receiveFrom me w;
  out = (case r of Ok text -> keep text | Err why -> keep why);
  _ = drop r;
