When an object is promoted, it is deep-copied once into the ARC.qa pool, and the ARC.qa manager actor spawns a dedicated manager to handle increment/decrement messages via a mailbox, ensuring structural atomicity. Deletion occurs when the count reaches zero, triggering deallocation and the manager's exit.

```fprisc
// Example of promoting an object and managing its lifecycle via ARC.qa
let obj = promote(source_object) // Deep copy into ARC.qa pool
let manager = arc_spawn_manager(obj) // Spawn manager actor

// Increment count via message to manager
manager.inc()

// Decrement count; if count hits zero, manager exits and object is freed
manager.dec() 
```

ARC.qa acts as the central memory actor that owns the buddy pool. It spawns a manager actor for each object, handles promotion by deep-copying the object into its pool, and manages deallocation when an object's count reaches zero.
```sol
// Example of a manager actor spawned by ARC.qa
actor Manager {
  let count = 0
  let obj = <deep_copy_of_original_object>

  message INC {
    count = count + 1
  }

  message DEC {
    if (count == 0) {
      // Dealloc object and exit manager
      deallocate(obj)
      exit()
    } else {
      count = count - 1
    }
  }
}
```

When the grant pool is exhausted, promotion fails by returning an error to the sender rather than causing a machine panic. The capacity is managed by ARC.qa's pool, so the application must handle the rejection gracefully.
```fprisc
try {
    result = memory.promote(object, context)
} catch (Err: "pool_exhausted") {
    // Handle allocation failure
}
```

ARC.qa manages individual object instances by spawning a manager actor per object to handle increment/decrement messages, ensuring structural atomicity via mailbox linearization. In contrast, Memory.qa owns the underlying buddy block, treating every actor-context block as a request to that owner, thereby centralizing capacity management within a single actor. This shift allows capacity exhaustion to result in a refused promotion error rather than a machine panic.

```fprisc
// ARC.qa manages per-object managers for atomic refcounting
arc_lock {
  manager = spawn_manager()
  count = inc()
}

// Memory.qa owns the buddy; actors request blocks from it
memory_request {
  block = memory_arena.grant() // Request from single owner
}
```

