park_on_cv() {
  wait hal_ipi_send;
  wait hal_timer_arm;
}
```

The `hal_ipi_send` condition variable serves as a wake source for an idle hart, allowing the system to interrupt the hart's nap (originally 200 microseconds, now capped at 20 milliseconds) when an Inter-Processor Interrupt (IPI) is sent. This mechanism ensures the core does not remain idle indefinitely while still minimizing energy consumption.

```sol
-- Example of using the condition variable in the idle loop
while (true) {
    park_condition hal_ipi_send;
    -- Check other wake sources like hal_timer_arm if needed
}
```

The idle burn was reduced by changing the napping mechanism from a fixed 200 microsecond interval to a condition variable wait, which limits wake-ups to only 5,000 per second instead of 5,000 per hart. This change caps the maximum wake source at 20 milliseconds to prevent hanging and reduces the core's idle consumption significantly. In the implementation, the hart now parks on a condition variable monitored by doorbell signals (`hal_ipi_send`) and timer deadlines (`hal_timer_arm`), as defined in `machine/posix/park.c`.

```sol
machine/posix/park.c
    // Old: simple loop with 200us sleep
    // New: park on condition variable
    cond_wait(&doorbell, hal_ipi_send);
    cond_wait(&timer, hal_timer_arm);
```

Use the `posix` system profile to compile the application. This builds an ordinary executable that interacts with the host OS as a process, providing access to command-line arguments, environment variables, standard streams, files, and sockets without embedding the QOS runtime.

