The `alloc` function first validates that the requested size does not exceed the maximum limit (bits 62 and 63), then calculates the minimum block size by rounding up to the nearest multiple of 80 bytes, with a minimum of 96 bytes. It then searches for a suitable free block starting from the head of the block list using the `firstFit` strategy, which attempts to use the current block if it is not used and is large enough, otherwise recursively checking the next block.

```fprisc
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `claim` function finalizes the allocation of a specific memory block by splitting it if necessary, marking it as in use, clearing its fields, and zeroing out its payload. It ensures the block is ready for use by setting its usage flag to true, resetting its layout and pending fields to null, calculating the remaining capacity, and initializing the reference count to one. The payload portion of the block is then explicitly filled with zeros to prevent uninitialized data from being accessed.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

The `claim` function returns the payload address (`p`) of the newly allocated memory block. It performs internal operations to split the block if necessary, mark it as used, initialize its fields, and zero out the payload area, but its explicit return value is the address of the start of the usable data within that block.

```fprisc
p = payload b;
_ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
p.
```

