The `doNeq` function determines if two terms are not equal by first marking the current state, attempting to unify the terms, and then undoing the unification bindings regardless of the result. If the unification succeeds (the terms are equal), the function returns the modified state with the `fuel` decremented, indicating the inequality check failed. If unification fails (the terms are not equal), it proceeds to `solveG` to propagate the solution. The `undoTo` function is crucial here as it restores the environment to its pre-unification state when equality is not found, allowing the non-equality check to succeed in the context of the solver.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if the terms are currently unifiable, which effectively means they are not distinct. It first marks the current state of the environment, attempts to unify the two terms using `unify`, and then undoes the bindings if unification fails. If unification succeeds (the terms are equal), the predicate returns the modified environment with fuel and solved goals; if unification fails, it proceeds to solve the goal with the original marked environment, indicating inequality.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

