To define a resumable machine interrupt handler, declare a function named `machineInterrupt` with the signature `machineInterrupt cause pc value = ...`, accepting three `Word` arguments (`mcause`, `mepc`, and `mtval`) and returning `Unit`. The runtime automatically installs an entry stub when this function exists. Inside the handler, acknowledge the hardware source and return to resume the interrupted instruction stream using `mret`.

```fprisc
machineInterrupt cause pc value = {
    // Handle interrupt logic here
    mret
}
```

Ensure `ARC=1` is set to enable this feature. Note that faults occurring inside the interrupt handler are fatal and use an emergency stack, while synchronous exceptions remain fatal regardless.

To define a resusable machine interrupt handler, use the `machineInterrupt` function with three `Word` arguments (`mcause`, `mepc`, and `mtval`) and a `Unit` result. The handler must acknowledge the hardware source and return to resume execution with `mret`. To invoke this functionality, compile the program with `ARC=1` and run it using the command `make bare-metal-builtin-run ARC=1 ARC_CHECK=1 BUILTIN_HEAP_BYTES=16384 PROG=tests/builtin_interrupt.fpr`.

```sol
machineInterrupt cause pc value = {
    // Acknowledge the interrupt source
    // ...
    mret
}
```

Managed language exceptions involve panic termination without unwinding all live objects and rely on automatic fallback ARC, whereas `machineInterrupt` is a raw, non-owning mechanism that requires manual acknowledgment of the hardware source via a `Unit` result to resume the instruction stream with `mret`. Unlike managed code which may use cycles or partial application optimizations, the interrupt handler operates on a dedicated stack preserving specific registers while keeping interrupts disabled, treating synchronous exceptions as fatal rather than resumable.

```fprisc
machineInterrupt cause pc value = {
    // Acknowledge hardware source to resume execution
    return Unit();
}
```

