To define a checked resumable interrupt handler, you must declare a function with the signature `machineInterrupt cause pc value` that takes three raw Words and returns Unit. The compiler enforces strict constraints, rejecting allocation, managed construction, string operations, indirect calls, CSR writes, and any function exceeding the eight-register argument ABI to ensure safety. The handler must acknowledge the interrupt source before resuming execution via `mret`.

```fprisc
machineInterrupt myHandler cause pc value {
    // Acknowledge interrupt source here
    // ...
    mret
}
```

Checked resumable interrupt handlers are compiled as root definitions alongside the main program, but they enforce strict reentrancy and safety constraints that standard ARC compilation does not. The compiler rejects any handler that exceeds the eight-register argument ABI, performs indirect calls, allocates memory, or writes to CSR registers, ensuring the handler never enters the non-reentrant allocator or shared argument-spill area. While standard ARC allows raw field access which may appear to be heap pointers but is treated as scalars, interrupt handlers permit only raw writes, as the compiler cannot prove such writes do not corrupt managed objects or stacks.

```fprisc
machineInterrupt "my_handler" 0x00100000 0x00000000
  // Acknowledge interrupt source
  // ...
  // Must return Unit without exceeding 8 args, no alloc, no indirect calls
end
```

