The strategy evaluates the predicate branchlessly using SIMD lanes to write a mask column, then scans (map/fold/zip) fuse this mask as a lane selector instead of compacting in place per element. Density is restored via an explicit `Vec.compact` call or automatically at the `Sys.poolReset` frame boundary.
```sol
// Example: Filter then compact
let filtered = Vec.filter(data, is_valid);
let compacted = Vec.compact(filtered);
```

The design decision prioritizes branch-light conditionals to preserve the wide execution path; per-element taken/not-taken branches poison this path more than a fused mask read by the fixed-function tier and specialized loops. The policy moved to the program layer, where `Vec.compact` (either explicitly after filter or automatically at `Sys.poolReset`) restores density in a single, branch-light pass at the loop boundary.

```sol
Vec.filter {
  // Predicate evaluated branchlessly (cmov/SIMD lanes)
  mask = evaluate_predicate(lane); 
  // Scan fuses mask as lane select
  result = scan(mask, source); 
  // Density restored later
  compact(result);
}
```

The previous implementation eagerly compacted vectors in place to keep scan operations mask-free, whereas Phase 6 evaluates the predicate branchlessly using SIMD lanes or `cmov` to write a mask column without per-element branches. Scans then fuse this mask as a lane selector, and density is restored later via an explicit `Vec.compact` call or at `Sys.poolReset`, moving the compaction policy from the execution path to the program logic.

```sol
// Phase 6: Filter writes a mask column instead of compacting in place
let v = Vec.filter(v, predicate) // predicate evaluated branchlessly
// Scan fuses the mask as a lane select
let result = Vec.scan(v, func, init) 
// Density restored later
Vec.compact(result)
```

