The `buy k` message instructs the specific browser session (cashier) to ring catalog item `k` into its local cart, provided the shared store model currently has the item in stock. Crucially, this operation is atomic across all sessions; it checks the global stock level, and if the item is available, it deducts from the shared model's inventory immediately while adding the item to the current session's cart. This ensures that no two cashiers can sell the last unit of an item simultaneously.

```fpr
buy k
  // Checks global stock, deducts if available, adds to local cart
  if (stock[k] > 0) {
    stock[k] -= 1
    cart[k] += 1
  } else {
    // Handle out-of-stock logic (e.g., reject or ignore)
  }
```

The disk profile records game progression events (start and end) via an append-only log managed by the QLOG service, persisting aggregate statistics and game history across runs without requiring the game to touch the storage service directly. In contrast, the board state is saved locally at the beginning of a turn to capture the specific game configuration (HQ, supply, deck, hand, units, turn, ENV, counters) but is cleared immediately when an HQ falls or the turn ends, ensuring only the current snapshot persists until the next flush.

```sol
// Disk profile recording (append-only, event-driven via QLOG)
std/fs:write("terra2/profile", "start <seed>\n")
std/fs:write("terra2/profile", "end <seed> win|loss <rounds> <hqMe> <hqEn> <called> <lost> <destroyed> <damage>\n")

// Board saving (state-driven, temporary snapshot)
dirty = true
std/fs:write("terra2/game", "HQ, supply, deck, hand, rows, turn, ENV, counters")
// Board cleared when HQ falls
```

The FP-RISC runtime handles core execution tasks like allocation, actors, scheduling, and vectors, while the QOS machine layer provides system-specific services such as boot, context switching, and device drivers. The runtime does not require QOS to build or run Sol programs, as the HAL it sees is abstracted by QOS. In Sol, a program imports QOS services implicitly, but the underlying implementation of those services resides in the QOS repository rather than the FP-RISC runtime.

```sol
-- Example Sol code demonstrating the separation
-- The program uses QOS services (like console or timer) which are
-- implemented in the QOS machine layer, not the FP-RISC runtime.
import std;
import qos;

let main = () -> {
  qos.console.print "Hello from FP-RISC on QOS";
  qos.timer.sleep 1000;
};
```

The `childActor` function illustrates the deepsend mechanism by constructing a nested data payload containing a list of strings and a tuple, then sending it to a parent actor before the child actor's execution pool is destroyed. This ensures the parent receives a self-contained, deep-copied message that remains valid even though the sender dies immediately after the send operation, preventing the parent from accessing loose or dead cells.

```fpr
childActor self =
  m = receive self;
  (parent, k) = m;
  _ = drop m;
  payload = (mklist 20 Nil, ("nested", k * 2), "tail string");
  send parent payload.
```

The `askPerm` function prompts the user to grant a specific permission by displaying a formatted message containing a URL and mode, then reads a single character from the console to determine if the permission is granted. It evaluates the input character, accepting 'y' or 'Y' as a grant and returning `True`, while any other input results in `False`. The function also echoes the user's input before returning the decision.

```fpr
askPerm io url mode =
  Io4 con me clk st0 = io;
  _ = puts con "  grant ";
  _ = puts con url;
  _ = puts con " (";
  _ = puts con mode;
  _ = puts con ") ? [y/N] ";
  c = getc me con;
  _ = putc con c >> nl con;              # echo
  or2 (c == 121) (c == 89).              # 'y' or 'Y'
```

