The fprc compiler computes the maximum IR-instruction count on any path between consecutive safepoint events (such as entry checks, kernel checks, or calls) by walking the emitted assembly as a Control Flow Graph (CFG). Since all loops are implemented as tail calls, the CFG is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), and the absence of back-edges ensures no unbounded execution paths exist. The fuel unit is defined as one function entry, where the work between entries corresponds to the function body. This analysis is performed pre-lowering IR, meaning the counts represent instruction counts before target-specific lowering factors or cycles-per-instruction are applied.

```fprc
// Example of the output format generated by FPRC_WCET=1
// # wcet: segment_max=221, exit_tail=10, ccall_count=5
```

The FPRC compiler computes the maximum IR-instruction count per function between consecutive safepoint events by treating the control flow graph as a DAG where loops are implemented as tail calls; a back-edge would trigger an UNBOUNDED error, ensuring no unbounded paths exist in the tree. While a fuel unit is defined as one function entry, the work between entries can be arbitrarily long if a function body contains a long straight-line vector kernel or a huge literal fold. Consequently, the WCET model relies on compiler-emitted constants for maximum instructions between safepoints, and kernels must enforce per-invocation lane caps or decrement fuel per N lanes to satisfy timing targets on the hardware variant.

```fprc
// Example of a WCET annotation added by the compiler
// # wcet: segmax=221, exit-tail=10, ccall=5
fn main() {
    // This function body is bounded by the # wcet: comment
    // but a vector kernel inside could be O(n) without lane caps
    vec_kernel(n); 
}
```

