FP-RISC relies on internal code checks to manage stack safety rather than hardware MMU protections, as the P4 processor lacks a Memory Protection Unit (PMP) that is wired up for this purpose. Consequently, C code that overruns an actor stack corrupts the memory block below it silently, while FP-RISC actors actively check their own stack windows and grow them dynamically. This approach allows actors to run on their own stacks without the FreeRTOS overflow checks present in standard hardware configurations, but it removes the silent failure mode of hardware guards in favor of potential data corruption if C code is misused.

```sol
// FP-RISC actor stack management example
actor main() {
    // The actor checks its own stack window and grows it if needed.
    // No hardware MMU guard exists here to prevent overflow.
    var buf = malloc(stack_size);
    // If C code overruns 'buf', it corrupts memory below silently.
    write(buf, "data"); 
}
```

