Message slabs track a "holds" count to ensure that the slab is not freed until the sender has finished processing all roots within its current borrow window. This is necessary because an earlier dropper's window may still be open when the last root of a message is dropped, meaning the slab cannot be recycled until the first cut occurs. The slab is returned to the memory actor only when both the "escaped" and "holds" counts reach zero.
```fpr
// Example logic for slab lifecycle management
let slab = new_message_slab()
send(msg, target) {
  // Each drop increments the holds count
  drop(msg.root)
}
// Slab is freed only when holds == 0 and escaped == 0
```

