The first `readAll` call successfully reads the entire content of the file into variable `a` and returns a handle `h2` that remains open. The second `readAll` call fails because the underlying file handle `h` has already been fully consumed by the first read; attempting to read from an exhausted handle returns an error. To fix this, one should use the handle returned by the first `readAll` call for subsequent operations, or ensure the file is only read once.
