To perform tail recursion with effectful termination, define a nullary function that uses an explicit memory address as a termination counter. The function reads the integer value at that address, decrements it, and writes the new value back before recursing. The main function initializes the memory location with a starting count, such as 10,000. This pattern ensures the stack is released without recursive destruction, as demonstrated by the following Sol code:

```sol
again = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
n = Word.toInt (Mem.read32 p); 
case n == 0 of True -> Unit | False -> 
  _ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt (n - 1)); 
again.
main = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
_ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt 10000); 
again.
```

A QOS program can implement tail recursion that relies on an effectful operation to manage termination by using an explicit memory address as a counter, bypassing the standard stack frame limits. As demonstrated in the reference test cases, a program can define a nullary function that reads from a specific 32-bit memory location, decrements the value, and writes it back, continuing until the counter reaches zero. This approach ensures the function terminates safely outside the managed heap.

```fpr
again = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
n = Word.toInt (Mem.read32 p); 
case n == 0 of True -> Unit | False -> 
  _ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt (n - 1)); 
again; 
main = p = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864); 
_ = Mem.write32 p (Word.fromInt 10000); 
again;
```

