The `splice` function reconstructs a string by first identifying the longest common prefix between the old and new lists using `common`, then dropping that prefix from both lists to isolate the changed sections. It calculates a `suffix` by finding the longest common suffix of the reversed remaining parts, effectively identifying the stable end of the string. The `remove` count is derived from the length of the old middle section minus the suffix, while `insert` captures the new characters between the prefix and suffix. These components are formatted into a structured string representation showing the prefix, the number of removed items, and the inserted items.

```sol
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]";
```

