The `bsc` function first initializes a histogram at the root level using `cvec`, then performs a binary search (`histB`) to find the optimal threshold `h1` within a specified range. It subsequently calls `sweep` starting with `h1` to identify the best split point that maximizes the information gain metric. Finally, it returns the best split value along with the refined column and row indices, while explicitly freeing the intermediate histogram vector to manage memory.

```fprisc
bsc ps parentH cs ys idxs n totalC =
  P bins md mg ml k = ps;
  h0 = cvec (bins * k) 0;
  (h1, cs2, ys2) = histB h0 cs ys idxs k;
  (best, h2) = sweep h1 0 (zerosL k) 0.0 0 0 ps n parentH totalC;
  _ = Vec.free h2;
  (best, cs2, ys2).
```

The best split is found by initializing the sweep with a histogram at zero, then recursively calling `sweep` until the condition `n - nl < ml` fails, indicating the optimal partition point has been reached. The resulting best split value, along with the updated column and row indices, is returned by the `bsc` function.

```fprisc
bsc ps parentH cs ys idxs n totalC =
  P bins md mg ml k = ps;
  h0 = cvec (bins * k) 0;
  (h1, cs2, ys2) = histB h0 cs ys idxs k;
  (best, h2) = sweep h1 0 (zerosL k) 0.0 0 0 ps n parentH totalC;
  _ = Vec.free h2;
  (best, cs2, ys2).
```

