The compiler rejects the program because the case must be exhaustive and no arm may cover cases already handled by earlier arms. This rule prevents ambiguous shapes regardless of the types involved.

```fprisc
actor loop {
  case x of
    1 -> { ... }
    2 -> { ... }
    _ -> { ... }
  end
}
```

In the example above, if the first arm handled values that the second arm would also match, the compiler would flag it as an error. A valid exhaustive case must explicitly handle all possible values without overlap, such as distinguishing between specific integers and the wildcard `_`.

