On multi-core systems, device interrupts are routed to auxiliary harts, while the prime hart (hart 0) does not receive them unless it is the only core available. The `fpr_irq_hart` is determined as the last live hart, and its M context manages the interrupt delivery. The timer service similarly manages CLINT deadlines, where a single-hart machine uses the hart-0 deadlock detector's 30ms pacing to override or ride timer deadlines.

```sol
// Example of binding a timer service that works across hart configurations
Sys.timerBind("my_timer", actor);
Sys.timerArm(deadline); // Arms the nearest deadline; on single hart, 
                        // this interacts with the 30ms deadlock detector pacing
```

An FP-RISC application uses the `std/compile` service to send source code via `CP.qosApp`, which returns an `Ok` result containing the assembly or an `Err` containing diagnostics for syntax failures. The application then uses the `std/fs` service to append both the original source and the returned assembly to specific file paths on the local filesystem. This process ensures that the application remains stable even when the compiler encounters bad source code, as the error handling logic within the `case` statement prevents the program from wedging.

```fprisc
src = "x = 1 + 2;".

main =
  me = myself 0;
  fs = FS.serve (device "blk");
  FS.append me fs "prog.fpr" src;
  r = CP.qosApp src;
  case r of
    Err e -> "Compile failed: {e}"
  | Ok asm -> FS.append me fs "prog.s" asm;
  _ = FS.readLatest me fs "prog.s";
```

