A dynamic ring buffer in FP-RISC can be implemented using the `sink` and `drainN` functions defined in the mailbox contract. The `gate` receives a sink and a count, while the `sink` waits for that count, then uses `drainN` to consume exactly that many values from the main input channel in order, tracking the last value to determine if the stream remains ordered. The result is sent back to the main channel, and the function returns zero to terminate.

```fpr
gate me =
  s = receive me;
  k = receive me;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie s k;
  0.

sink main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainN me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.

drainN me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered).
drainN me main k last ordered =
  v = receiveFrom me main;
  drainN me main (k - 1) v (ordered and v > last).
```

