This file documents the architectural updates to the Live store and view rendering system. It details how `std/store` now manages data across SHARD actors using a compaction strategy that rewrites files only when superseded lines outnumber live ones, ensuring efficient storage and consistency. Additionally, it explains the optimization where every tab page is rebuilt as a composition of five distinct sections, utilizing a `cachedView` to prevent redundant rendering and reduce server CPU usage by approximately 6% per fan-out.

```fprisc
// Example of a shard managing its own file path within its tidied state
actor shard(k) {
  file path = state.path; // Path must be IN the tidied value to survive reset
  compact() {
    if (superseded > live) {
      rewrite(path);
    }
  }
}
```

The actor's state becomes inconsistent because the file's path dangles after the first reset, causing the shard to retain a reference to a path that no longer exists within its current tidied value. This violates the requirement that anything surviving a tidy must be included in the tidied data structure.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: storing a path outside the tidied state
actor Shard {
  tidy: () -> Unit = () -> {
    // ... compaction logic ...
    // Path 'oldPath' is used but not in the returned state
    filePath = "oldPath" 
    return { data: [] } 
  }
}
```

The `std/store` module manages compaction by appending changes to a specific file per shard and rewriting that file only when superseded lines outnumber live ones, compacting one small shard at a time. Data persistence within a shard is constrained such that anything built inside an actor that must survive a tidy must be stored within the tidied value itself; for instance, a shard's file path must be maintained inside the state it tidies to prevent dangling references after a reset.

```fprisc
// Example of a safe compaction operation where the path is preserved in the state
actor shard k {
  // 'tidy' must include the path string in its return value to survive resets
  func tidy(state: map) -> map {
    state["path"] = "/data/shard/k"
    // perform compaction logic here
    state
  }
}
```

The `std/store` system compacts one small shard at a time by rewriting only the file containing superseded lines when they outnumber live ones. To ensure consistency, any data structure built inside an actor that must survive a tidy must be stored within the tidied value itself, as observed when a shard previously kept its file path outside its state, causing the path to dangle after a reset.

```fprisc
// Example of a value that survives a tidy because it is part of the tidied state
actor ShardState {
  file: string; // Path is inside the actor's state, safe to reset
  data: map;
}
```

`std/store` keeps a keyed collection in SHARD actors where each shard maintains its own file. When superseded lines outnumber live ones, the actor compacts only that specific file one shard at a time. This ensures that anything built inside an actor intended to survive a tidy must be stored within the tidied value itself, preventing paths from dangling after a reset.

```fprisc
// Example logic illustrating that state must be internal to the shard's tidied value
actor ShardManager {
  tidiedState: Map<String, Value>

  // Compaction must operate on tidiedState; external paths are forbidden
  func compact(): Result {
    return tidiedState.compact()
  }
}
```

