When a requester cannot serve itself via `buddy_alloc_try`, it queues a message to the memory actor in hart 0. The request is an `Int` where the low bit distinguishes the operation: set to 1 to take bytes (allocated as `bytes<<1`) or set to 0 to give a buddy pointer (freed as `ptr>>1`). The actor serializes these queued requests and responds by storing the result into the requester's ACB and waking the requester, ensuring the allocation happens without holding a spinlock across the queue.

```sol
// Example request format (Int):
// Take 1024 bytes: 0x40000001  (1024 << 1 | 1)
// Free pointer 0x8000: 0x40000000 (0x8000 >> 1 | 0)
```

When a request finds the buddy lock held, it queues on the memory actor in hart 0's queue, positioned second after actor 0. The actor processes this queued request as a round trip through one actor on one hart, which costs more than a lock-free allocation and may cause stalls if that hart is busy. The actor is admitted ahead of the backlog to ensure the queued request does not wait on a reservoir draw.

```sol
// Example of a queued request path when buddy_alloc_try fails
actor mem_requester {
  fun alloc(size: Int): Ptr {
    if buddy_alloc_try(size) {
      return result; // Lock-free path
    } else {
      // Queue on memory actor in hart 0's queue
      return mem_actor.queue_alloc(size);
    }
  }
}
```

