The file `fprisc/tests/procorder.sol` is a test script designed to verify the ordering of process execution when queued effects are present. It demonstrates that `Proc.runNow` refuses to execute immediately if any effects are pending in the queue, ensuring that queued processes (scheduled for commit) are not preempted by immediate execution requests. The script creates a queued effect via `Proc.afterCommit` and an immediate effect via `Proc.runNow`, then logs the state to confirm the refusal behavior.

```sol
u = Proc.afterCommit (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf queued >> /tmp/sol-proc-order.log"] "" [] "" 2000);
r = Proc.runNow (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf ranNow >> /tmp/sol-proc-order.log"] "" [] "" 2000);
print "runNow: {r}".
```

`Proc.runNow` refuses to execute an immediate process if any effect is currently pending in the queue, ensuring that queued effects are processed before immediate ones. This mechanism guarantees that queued effects only execute at commit time, while immediate processes are blocked until the queue is clear. For example, if a process is queued with `Proc.afterCommit`, `Proc.runNow` will not run a subsequent immediate process until that queued effect is committed.

```sol
u = Proc.afterCommit (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf queued"] "" [] "" 2000);
r = Proc.runNow (ProcessSpec ["/bin/sh", "-c", "printf ranNow"] "" [] "" 2000);
```

In the example above, `Proc.runNow` would refuse to execute the `printf ranNow` command if the `Proc.afterCommit` effect was pending, as the immediate process must wait for the queued effect to be committed.

