The design rule states that core_* names are ALWAYS opaque unsafe externs with a cost of ω(name) because unsafety is infectious upward. This means that even if a core_* function is defined internally, the compiler treats it as an external call with unknown runtime cost, ensuring that the Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) equations correctly account for the cost entry point.

```fprisc
@core_*
func core_compute(n : Int) : Int
  return n * 2
end

// The compiler treats core_compute as an opaque unsafe extern
// regardless of its definition above.
func main() : Int
  return core_compute(10)
end
```

A function declared with an all-Int signature is deemed safe because its inputs and outputs are strictly bounded within the interval domain, allowing the compiler to derive a closed-form Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET). In contrast, unproven obligations require dynamic runtime checks at specific sites, and if those checks fail or are not proven, the function is treated as unsafe with a cost of ω(name). This unsafety is infectious upward, meaning the WCET equations must explicitly account for the ω cost at these points.

```sol
// Safe function: all-Int signature ensures interval domain constraints
DECLARES safe_func : (n : Int | n > 0) -> (r : Int | r >= 1)

// Unsafe function: relies on unproven obligations or core_* externs
// core_* names are opaque unsafe externs with cost ω(name)
extern unsafe_core : (x : Int) -> Int
```

