The `fire` function simulates a sequence of software interrupts by resetting a flag to zero, triggering the interrupt via the machine interrupt cause register, and waiting for the flag to increment. It verifies that the interrupt counter resumes sequentially and then recursively fires the remaining count minus one.

```fprisc
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

