```fprisc
_CPU.irqEnable Unit;
_CPU.irqSave Unit;
```

The `fire` function simulates a sequence of software interrupts by initializing the interrupt flag to zero, setting the Machine Software Interrupt Pending (MSIP) register to 1, waiting for the flag to be cleared by the CPU, and verifying that the interrupt counter increments sequentially. It takes a single integer argument `n` representing the starting value of the interrupt counter.

```fprisc
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

