The file `fprisc/examples/logbook.fpr` implements a server-side script that populates input fields within a user interface. It listens for `live:set` events containing JSON data and uses a MutationObserver to detect when DOM elements with specific data attributes appear. Once an element exists, the script fills its value and triggers an `input` event to ensure the client-side framework recognizes the update immediately, handling cases where the data arrives before the corresponding UI element is rendered.

```sol
script = strcat (LJ.src 0) "
(function () {
  var pending = {};
  function fill() {
    Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
      var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
      if (!els.length) return;
      els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
      delete pending[n];
    });
  }
  document.addEventListener('live:set', function (e) {
    var vals = JSON.parse(e.detail);
    Object.keys(vals).forEach(function (n) { pending[n] = vals[n]; });
    fill();
  });
  new MutationObserver(fill).observe(document.getElementById('lv-root'), { childList: true, subtree: true });
})();
".
```

The `fill` function iterates through pending values associated with specific input names, locates the corresponding DOM elements using the `data-input` attribute, assigns the stored values to their `value` properties, and triggers an `input` event to update the UI. It is registered as a callback for a `MutationObserver` that monitors the `lv-root` element for changes in the child list, ensuring that input fields are populated as soon as the DOM structure is updated.

