When `filesFwd` receives a `Read` message, it extracts the file path, retrieves the file data via `fileOf`, and then calls the manager actor (`me`) to forward the request. The response is sent back to the caller via the reply channel, and the reference is dropped to comply with the pshell law before the actor loops back to `filesServe`.

```sol
filesFwd st me act replyTo q path =
  (a, act2) = fileOf st me act path;
  r = call me a q;
  _ = send replyTo r;
  _ = drop r;
  filesServe st me act2.
```

The `fileSpawn` function handles the lazy instantiation of a file actor when the requested service is not currently available. It logs a system event indicating the opening of the file path, constructs a new path string, spawns a fresh actor using the `spawn` primitive, and then updates the system's actor set by prepending the new actor-path pair to the list of active actors. This mechanism ensures that file operations are deferred until actually requested, adhering to the lazy evaluation principle.

```sol
fileSpawn st me act path =
  _ = Sys.logAt 0 "system: opening {path}";
  p2 = strcat path "";
  a = spawn (fileActor st p2);
  (a, (p2, a) :: act).
```

The `filesFwd` function calls `fileOf` to obtain an actor `a` and an updated action list `act2`, then uses `call me a q` to forward the message `q` to that actor. It prints the response `r` to the reply channel, explicitly drops the reference to `r` (adhering to the pshell law), and finally returns `a` (wrapped in a tuple) and `act2` to continue processing the action list.

```fpr
(a, act2) = fileOf st me act path;
r = call me a q;
_ = send replyTo r;
_ = drop r;               # forward then release our ref (pshell law)
filesServe st me act2.
```

