Idle motion is implemented by having every unit run on its own phase, which is a hash of its slot, over waves of the model's clock. These waves are triangle waves that sweep briskly between ends, holding at each end. For specific animations like soldiers shifting weight or tanks scanning turrets, you utilize the clock's sweep pattern; for example, a tank turret pattern is created by summing two sweeps of different periods.
```sol
let phase = hash(slot)
let sweep = triangle_wave(phase)
let rifle_angle = combine_sweeps(sweep1, sweep2)
```

Soldiers shift their weight side to side, square up occasionally, and draw their rifles forward and back, as the rifle is its own part that rises, moves forward, and swings slightly. In contrast, tank turrets scan between actions using two sweeps of different periods summed together so the pattern never repeats, and they snap onto a target only when attacking.

```sol
// Soldier idle logic example
if (clock_phase == 1) shift_weight();
if (clock_phase == 2) square_up();
if (clock_phase == 3) draw_rifle_forward();

// Tank turret idle logic example
turret_scan_period = sweep1_period + sweep2_period;
if (attack_triggered) snap_to_target();
```

The file `fprisc/std/mvu.fpr` implements the Micro Virtual Machine (MVU) port service for handling event-driven communication within the FP-RISC environment. It defines functions to drain subscribed ports into `EMsg` events, manage ASCII character encoding for control codes, and process input streams in arrival order using a poll service. The core logic, `portAsk`, sends a drain command to a port, receives the resulting message stream, converts it into a list of events, and discards the original data to free resources.

```fpr
portAsk me pa =
  _ = send pa (PDrain me);
  ms = receiveFrom me pa;
  evs = portConv ms Nil;
  _ = drop ms;
  evs.
```

