The handshake is entirely implemented in FPRISC using a 63-bit tagged integer to handle all cryptographic operations. The system computes the SHA-1 hash and performs base64 encoding within this single register type, masking every 32-bit intermediate value before each shift to prevent overflow, thereby eliminating the need for any C library calls.

```fprisc
// Simplified logic for the handshake computation
let key = read_reg(key_reg)
let guid = read_reg(guid_reg)
let combined = key ++ guid
let hash = sha1(combined)
let b64 = base64(hash)
let accept = b64
```

The QOS server implements the entire WebSocket handshake, including SHA-1 hashing, base64 encoding, frame parsing, framing, and mask/unmask operations, entirely within FPRISC using 63-bit tagged integers. Intermediate 32-bit values are masked before shifting to prevent overflow, ensuring no C code is involved in the process.

```fprisc
// Example of a 63-bit tagged integer operation
let x = 100;
let y = 1000;
// Masking before shift to stay within 63-bit tag
let z = (x & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) << 1; 
```

