The file `fprisc/std/live.fpr` defines the `live` module, which implements a live-reloading system for FP-RISC scripts. It monitors a list of source files and automatically triggers a rebuild when any file's modification time or size changes, as demonstrated by the `watcher` function which uses `OS.stat` to detect changes and `P.run` to execute the rebuild script. This mechanism ensures that changes to watched files are reflected immediately without manual restarts.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

The `watcher` function continuously monitors a list of file paths defined in the configuration. It calculates a unique stamp for each file by combining its modification time and size, storing these in a `seen` list. If the current stamps match the previously seen ones, the watcher sleeps for 300 milliseconds and repeats the check. If a file has changed, the `rebuild` function is executed via `P.run`. Upon success, a reload signal is sent to the application; if the rebuild fails or the process cannot run, the watcher updates its `seen` list with the new stamps and continues monitoring.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

The `watcher` function accepts a register, configuration, a list of previously seen states, and a self-reference. It sleeps for 300 seconds, retrieves the current state of the files listed in `cfg.watch` using the `stamps` function (which calculates a unique integer key based on file size and modification time), and compares it to the `seen` state. If the states differ, it executes the `cfg.rebuild` command; if the rebuild succeeds with a status of 0, it sends a `Reload` signal to the register.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

The `report` function generates a human-readable summary of a program's manifest by printing its metadata (name, id, load mode, ABI, shell) and counting the required versus optional permissions. It delegates the detailed listing of these permissions to the `showPerms` helper, which iterates through a list of permission tuples and formats them as a string. For example, if a manifest requires a specific capability, `report` will output the tag "required" followed by the specific user and mode details provided in the permission list.

```fpr
report man =
  _ = print "name = {M.name man}";
  _ = print "id = {M.id man}";
  _ = print "loadMode = {M.mode man}";
  _ = print "abi = {M.abi man}";
  _ = print "shell = {M.shell man}";
  _ = print "permissions = {count (M.req man) 0} required, {count (M.opt man) 0} optional";
  showPerms "required" (M.req man);
  showPerms "optional" (M.opt man).
```

The `write` function returns a tuple containing the number of bytes successfully written and the updated `Uart` handle; it enqueues data and returns immediately, or errors if the buffer is full. The `flush` function returns only the updated `Uart` handle; it waits for the outgoing buffer to fully drain before completing.

```sol
write h s =
  svc = uSvc h;
  r = rpc svc (UWrite s);
  n = case r of
    UOkI k -> k
  | _ -> error "uart write failed";
  (n, Uart svc).

flush h =
  svc = uSvc h;
  _ = okCall "flush" svc UFlush;
  Uart svc.
```

