The Typed memory layout defines a `Block` as a nominal pointer type that expands into ordinary definitions at parse time, incurring no runtime cost. This allows the heap implementation to be ported directly to this system, resulting in smaller code size compared to hand-written versions. Additionally, this design enables Tier 1 code to be written to be read, enhancing safety and efficiency.

```fprisc
// Example of a typed memory layout expansion
Block = Layout {
    start: ptr,
    end: ptr,
    size: size_t,
};

// Usage in heap.fpr
heap_alloc(size: size_t) -> Block:
    return new Block {
        start: current_ptr,
        end: current_ptr + size,
        size: size,
    };
```

To enable these features, ensure your system is using the LANDED profile for hosted environments. This involves migrating your heap implementation to `machine/builtin/heap.fpr` (which replaces `heap.c` behind the same C symbols using the `HEAP=fpr` switch) and relying on the compiler to generate equality, show, and copy operations automatically. For stack safety, the compiler will now use guard pages on POSIX and QOS Portable systems, treating a stack overflow as a named panic, while ensuring the stack spans the entire block allocated to it.

Example:
