`Static n` rings hold exactly n messages per sender channel and never grow, providing a fixed bound for reasoning or worst-case execution time. `Dynamic n` rings start at n and double their capacity from a buddy when full, allowing bursts to cost memory rather than messages; the old ring remains reachable until the channel block is reused.

