The `scaffold` function atomically creates a directory tree and associated files by buffering all mutating operations (like `mkdirp` and `writePath`) until a final validation step succeeds. It ensures consistency by preventing partial writes; if any concurrent process modifies a file during execution, the script aborts and re-runs rather than leaving a half-done mess. For example, it creates a `src` and `test` directory under a given root and writes three specific `.sol` files and a `README.md` in a single transactional block:

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

The expressions `u0` through `u4` return the unique identifiers (u0, u1, etc.) of the queued transactional operations: `mkdirp` calls for directories, and `writePath` calls for files. These operations do not immediately touch the disk; instead, they buffer and apply atomically at the end of the script if no errors occur. The final `print` statement executes immediately and outputs a confirmation message stating that the root directory has been scaffolded with four files and two directories, noting that the entire operation was atomic.

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

