The `doArm` function initializes the UART peripheral for communication by binding the specific IRQ handler to the device and configuring the interrupt enable register (IER) to trigger on both receive data available (RDA) and transmit hold error (THRE) conditions. When the caller is the owner, it sets the `armed` flag to 1 and writes the length of the pending transmit queue to the RDA bits field in the IER, ensuring the hardware interrupts fire whenever the FIFO is full or data is ready.

```fpr
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.
doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1 = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  _ = irqSet st;
  reply me from UOk st.
```

To configure the UART actor, you must call the `doArm` function, passing the actor's identifier, the current state, and the target actor ID. This function binds the interrupt source using `Sys.irqBind`, sets the interrupt state with `irqSet` to enable RDA when the transmit queue is non-empty and THRE when bytes are pending, and returns the updated state.

```fpr
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.
doArm2 : unsafe Int -> St -> Int -> a .
doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
