The `lbl` function recursively processes a vector of values based on an index comparison, swapping the output vector if the index is within bounds, or continuing the recursion if not. In Sol, this logic is expressed by checking the condition `i >= n` and branching to either return the current state or invoke the recursive helper `lblOne`.

```sol
def lbl(i, n, xs, zs, ns, ys) =
  if (i >= n) then (ys, xs, zs, ns)
  else lblOne(i, n, xs, zs, ns, ys);

def lblOne(i, n, xs, zs, ns, ys) =
  let (x, xs2) = Vec.get(i + 1, xs)
  let (z, zs2) = Vec.get(i + 1, zs)
  let (nz, ns2) = Vec.get(i + 1, ns)
  let c = if (x > 0.0) != (z > 0.0) then 1 else 0
