The QAR2 format removes ELF to ensure the loader consumes a flat image with exactly one copy, one zero-fill, and one protection flip. This design eliminates the need for header chasing, program-header walking, and reliance on a linker-written format, providing a consistent, minimal container that works identically on both Portable and Native QOS platforms.

```sol
// Example of the flat IMAGE section in a .qa file
// Offsets are relative to the first payload byte
IMAGE 0x1000 0x400
// The payload contains raw bytes of the application code
```

The file `qos/docs/QA-FORMAT.md` documents the QAR2 format, which defines the `.qa` app archive structure used for distributing and loading applications on the QOS operating system. It specifies that the archive is a flat image containing a MANIFEST, a LOAD contract, and an IMAGE payload, eliminating the need for ELF headers or program headers by consuming the linked ELF once at build time. The MANIFEST stores metadata such as the application name and permissions, while the LOAD section provides critical execution parameters like the base address, entry point, and memory protection boundaries required by the loader.

```sol
// Example of a minimal LOAD contract structure as described
LOAD {
    base = 0x1000;
    entry = 0x200;
    execsz = 0x1000;
    rwoff = 0x2000;
    imagesz = 0x4000;
    memsz = 0x8000;
    sha = "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855";
}
```

On x64 (qosp on x86-64), the entire specialization tier is disabled because the SysV ABI has only six callee-saved registers, which is insufficient for the spec loops required by the specialization tier. Consequently, every Vec site runs the generic apply tier, causing the decline scan to report all named-function sites rather than gating it on the tier flag. This behavior differs from a64 and rv64, which retain the specialization tier.

```fprisc
// Example of a site that would be reported as a decline on x64
fn double_row(row: Record) -> Record {
  { field_a: row.field_a * 2, field_b: row.field_b * 2 }
}
```

The QOS operating system defines these primitives because the compiler only declares what the current tree's runtime and machine layer implement, leaving the host facility layer to QOS itself. This separation ensures that the compiler cannot resolve calls to host facilities without explicit implementation in QOS, preventing undefined behavior at link time. For example, a program referencing `blkRead` will fail to link if QOS does not provide its definition, as the image's imports serve as its capability manifest.

```fpr
// Example: A program attempting to use a host facility
// without QOS providing the implementation will fail at link time.
use blkRead
```

To safely stop a poller while ensuring no new registrations race the stop contract, the code must publish a Watch before announcing registration and wait for both announcements before stopping. The provided `one` function demonstrates this by spawning two waiter threads, receiving their completion signals, and then calling `P.stop`. It subsequently checks that both waiters received the cancellation message, confirming the stop contract was respected.

```sol
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

The `cycles` function recursively executes a series of poller start and stop operations to verify the stability of the poller system under repeated stress. It initializes a poller, spawns a waiter to register a file descriptor, and immediately stops the poller before the next iteration begins. This sequence ensures that the system handles rapid registration and cancellation without race conditions.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = P.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = P.start Unit;
  _ = P.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

To manage a loop of poller operations and watch descriptors, you use the `cycles` function to iterate a specific number of times, spawning a poller, registering a waiter, and then stopping the poller in each iteration. For managing watch descriptors, the `reserve` function constructs a list by recursively adding a new watch to the front of the list until a count is reached, and the `releaseAll` function processes the list recursively to close each watch.

```sol
main =
  me = myself 0;
  listener = need (TCP.listen "127.0.0.1" 0);
  fd = case listener of TCP.Listener n -> n;
  _ = cycles fd me 40;
  ws = reserve 24;
  p = P.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  _ = releaseAll ws;
  _ = need (TCP.stop listener);
  print "poller stop: 80 start/stop cycles, duplicate waiters, fallback cancellation passed".
```

