A MATCHED SET is a collection of compiled plugins packaged into a single `.qa` file that contains absolute addresses derived from the specific shell image it was linked against. This ensures compatibility because the host daemon re-derives these addresses from the current build and refuses to attach any plugin whose internal stamp (the shell image's LOAD sha) does not match the hosted image.

```sol
// Example of a MATCHED SET package
module "my_plugin.qa" {
    import "shell_image.qa"; // Matches the exact shell image
    func main() {
        // Uses absolute addresses baked into the package
        call "core.sys" @0x1000; 
    }
}
```

The traditional method relies on a silent, layout-dependent memory corruption when a mismatched module is attached, whereas the modern mechanism uses a named error by stamping every plugin with the shell image's LOAD sha (`shell = ...`) during packaging. The host daemon re-derives plugsyms and refuses any attach whose stamp does not match the hosted image, ensuring the attached module is a matched set with the exact shell image it linked against.

```sol
// Example of a refused attach due to stamp mismatch
CP.plugin slot id src
// The host REFUSES this if the .qa's LOAD sha does not match the current shell
```

The `mMul` function computes the matrix product of two matrices `a` and `b`, returning a new matrix along with the modified versions of the input matrices. It first extracts dimensions and guards against incompatible shapes, then delegates the actual computation to the `Vec.mmul` native function, which handles the triple loop accumulation. The result is a tuple containing the resulting matrix, and the updated input matrices.

```sol
mMul a b =
  (ra, ka, a2) = mDims a;
  (kb, cb, b2) = mDims b;
  u = mMulGuard ra ka kb cb;
  Mat r1 c1 va = a2;
  Mat r2 c2 vb = b2;
  (vo, va2, vb2) = Vec.mmul ra ka cb va vb;
  (Mat ra cb vo, Mat r1 c1 va2, Mat r2 c2 vb2).
```

The `portFlush` function processes a `PDrain` message by flushing the accumulated message queue, dropping the current message, and clearing the queue before returning control to the main loop. It reverses the message list to ensure correct ordering, sends the drained messages to the sender, drops the received message, and drops all remaining queued messages.

```sol
portFlush me m from q =
  _ = send from (revK Nil q);
  _ = drop m;
  _ = portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

To flush the port, call `portFlush` with the actor, the received message, the sender ID, and the current queue. This function sends the reversed queue to the sender, drops the received message, and clears the queue before looping again.

```fprisc
portFlush me m from q =
  _ = send from (revK Nil q);
  _ = drop m;
  _ = portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

The ESP-IDF profile in FP-RISC replaces direct uses of the `tp` register with a hard-coded hart identifier (`fpr_esp_hart`) and performs specific lowering to preserve thread-local storage semantics. The generated code moves the thread pointer to a temporary register (e.g., `t0`) and performs addition with the hart offset, rather than directly accessing `tp`. In contrast, the bare-metal profile retains the `tp` register directly without these transformations.

```sol
// ESP-IDF TLS lowering example
fun fpr_esp_hart_add(a, b) -> u32 {
    mv t0, tp
    addi t0, t0, %tprel_hi(fpr_esp_hart)
    addi t0, t0, %tprel_lo(fpr_esp_hart)
    addi t0, t0, a
    return t0
}

// Bare-metal ABI example (tp used directly)
fun bare_tp_use(a) -> u32 {
    mv t0, tp
    addi t0, t0, a
    return t0
}
```

