\});
```

The function `orbits` recursively simulates the motion of two bodies where the acceleration is defined as $a = -r / |r|^3$, using the Euler-Cromer method to update velocity and position. It terminates when the iteration count $k$ reaches zero, returning empty lists for both trajectories.

```sol
orbits : (k : Int | measure k) -> Int -> Int -> Int -> (List _, List _) .
orbits k i r v | k <= 0 = ([], []).
orbits k i r v =
  a = -r / (r * r);
  v2 = v + a * 0.05;
  r2 = r + v2 * 0.05;
  (trs, trs) = orbits (k - 1) (i + 1) r2 v2;
  ((Numeric.inexact i, r) :: trs, (Numeric.inexact i, v) :: trs).
```

The `spring` function recursively simulates a damped spring where the acceleration is defined by the equation $x'' = -4x - 0.3v$. It uses an Euler-Cromer integrator to update velocity and position in small time steps (0.05). The function returns a list of time-series pairs for position ($x$) and velocity ($v$). Its base case occurs when the measure $k$ is less than or equal to 0, returning empty lists for both position and velocity data.

```sol
spring k t x v | k <= 0 = ([], []).
spring k t x v =
  v2 = v + (0 - 4) * x * 0.05 - 0.3 * v * 0.05;
  x2 = x + v2 * 0.05;
  (pxs, pvs) = spring (k - 1) (t + 0.05) x2 v2;
  ((t, x) :: pxs, (t, v) :: pvs).
```

