```

On Linux, the kernel honors address hints when `mmap` is called without `MAP_FIXED` if the range is free, allowing the standard hint-and-check pattern. FreeBSD does not guarantee this behavior; instead, it returns an arbitrary address, causing fixed-base applications to fail. To replicate the guarantee that the hint is honored, FreeBSD requires `MAP_EXCL` combined with `MAP_FIXED`, which acts as "this address or fail," whereas Linux does not need this combination.

```c
// Example logic for heap reservation in fprisc/machine/posix/hal.c
if (mmap(addr, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_FIXED | MAP_EXCL, -1, 0) == MAP_FAILED) {
    // Allocation failed on FreeBSD due to address conflict or lack of hint honor
    return NULL;
}
```

On Linux, the kernel honors a specific address hint when passed to `mmap` if the range is free. On FreeBSD, this hint is advisory; the kernel returns a different address even if the requested one is free. To achieve the same deterministic behavior as Linux, FreeBSD requires the `MAP_EXCL` flag combined with `MAP_FIXED` to enforce "this address or fail," whereas Linux only needs the hint-and-check pattern without `MAP_FIXED`.

```c
// Linux behavior (hint-and-check)
addr = mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
if (addr != NULL && addr != requested_addr) { /* Hint ignored, continue */ }

// FreeBSD behavior (MAP_EXCL + MAP_FIXED)
addr = mmap(requested_addr, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_FIXED|MAP_EXCL, -1, 0);
if (addr == MAP_FAILED) { /* Address not free, fail */ }
```

