The `alloc` function first checks for overflow by testing bits 62 and 63 of the requested size. It then aligns the size upwards to the nearest multiple of 80 bytes, ensuring a minimum size of 96 bytes. Finally, it invokes `firstFit` to search the free list starting from the head block, allocating the memory if a suitable block is found or recursively searching the next block if the current one is too small.

```fprisc
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `claim` function finalizes a memory block allocation by preparing the block for use. It first calls `split` to adjust the block's size if necessary, then marks the block as used, clears its layout and pending fields, sets its capacity, and initializes its reference count to one. Finally, it zeroes out the payload area to ensure no residual data remains.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

When the remaining block size after subtracting the requested size is less than 96 words, the `split` function returns `Unit`. In this scenario, the function does not create a new block; instead, it performs in-place adjustments on the current block `b` by updating its size, linking the new smaller block into the free list, and zeroing out the payload area of the new block.

```fprisc
split b size = case i (Word.sub (Block.size b) size) < 96 of
    True -> Unit
  | False ->
    n = Block.at (Addr.add (Block.addr b) (i size));
    _ = zeroWords (Block.addr n) 8;
    _ = Block.setSize n (Word.sub (Block.size b) size);
    _ = Block.setPrev n b;
    _ = Block.setNext n (Block.next b);
    _ = linkBack (Block.next b) n;
    _ = Block.setNext b n;
    Block.setSize b size.
```

The `alloc` function takes a single argument representing the desired size in bytes and returns a memory address. It first validates that the requested size does not exceed 62 bits (checking bit 62) and 63 bits (checking bit 63), returning an error if either condition is true to prevent overflow. It then calculates the aligned size by adding 80 bytes and rounding up, ensuring a minimum size of 96 bytes if the calculation results in exactly 80. Finally, it delegates the actual memory search and initialization to the `firstFit` function using this computed size.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

