The `applySet` function handles state updates by checking the operation type specified in the value argument. If the value is 'toggle', it inverts the current local state between '0' and '1'. If the value starts with '+' or '-', it performs arithmetic on the current local value. Otherwise, it assigns the literal value directly.

```sol
fn applySet(name, val) \{
    var cur = locals[name];
    if (val === 'toggle') locals[name] = (cur === '1' ? '0' : '1');
    else if (val[0] === '+' || val[0] === '-') locals[name] = String((+cur || 0) + (+val));
    else locals[name] = val;
}
```

The `applySet` function updates a value in the `locals` dictionary based on the `data-set` and `data-val` attributes of an element. It handles three specific cases: toggling a boolean value between '0' and '1', performing arithmetic addition or subtraction on numeric values, or assigning a literal string.

```fprisc
function applySet(name, val) \{
    var cur = locals[name];
    if (val === 'toggle') locals[name] = (cur === '1' ? '0' : '1');
    else if (val[0] === '+' || val[0] === '-') locals[name] = String((+cur || 0) + (+val));
    else locals[name] = val;
\}
```

The `paintLocals` function iterates through all elements with the `data-show` attribute, parses the attribute value to identify a local variable name and a target state (either a truthy check or an exact string match), and then sets the element's display style to `''` if the condition is met or `'none'` otherwise. This effectively toggles the visibility of UI components based on the current state of specified locals.

```fpr
root.querySelectorAll('[data-show]').forEach(function (el) \{
  var spec = el.getAttribute('data-show').split(':');
  var v = locals[spec[0]];
  var on = spec.length > 1 ? String(v) === spec[1]
                           : (v && v !== '0' && v !== 'false');
  el.style.display = on ? '' : 'none';
\});
```

Fuel is a resource used by actors to enable cooperative yielding within the actor system. As an actor executes, it consumes fuel; when its fuel supply is exhausted, it is required to yield control back to the scheduler to prevent indefinite blocking.

```fprisc
actor main() {
    let fuel = 10;
    while (fuel > 0) {
        // Perform work here
        fuel = fuel - 1;
    }
    // Actor yields here because fuel is 0
}
```

