The `waiter` function spawns a thread that waits for a specific signal from a poller, checks if the poller was cancelled, and notifies the boss of the cancellation status. It works by sending a watch request to the poller, receiving a note from the system, and then sending a cancellation flag back to the boss if the note indicates a cancellation. For example, in the `one` function, two waiter threads are spawned and their cancellation status is checked to ensure both were properly cancelled.

```sol
waiter p fd boss me = case p of PL.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (PL.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of PL.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

The `cycles` function recursively spawns a poller (`PL.start`) and executes the `one` function to manage waiters, decrementing the counter `n` until it reaches 0. Executing `cycles fd me 40` results in 40 iterations where a poller is started, two waiters are spawned, and the poller is stopped, effectively running a test lifecycle 40 times.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = PL.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = PL.start Unit;
  _ = PL.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

