Bounded model checking uses tools like CBMC to verify all possible execution paths up to a specific limit within the existing C code, checking for issues like pointer or buffer overflows. In contrast, model checking a MODEL involves manually writing a formal specification (such as in SPIN or TLA+) to represent the system's behavior, which is then checked for properties like deadlock freedom or message ordering. The former verifies the code directly, while the latter verifies a hand-written abstraction of the protocol logic.

```sol
// Example of a bounded check on a loop (CBMC style)
