    // without memory leaks or corruption, a property too complex for random testing alone.
    if invariant {
        true
    } else {
        false
    }
}
```

Property-based testing validates that invariants hold on thousands of random operation sequences with shrinking to a minimal counterexample, typically taking hours per module and running in seconds. In contrast, Bounded model checking verifies all possible execution paths up to a specific bound, including pointer and bounds checks, which can take minutes to hours of SAT solving per harness and risks blowing up quickly. Property-based testing is recommended as the first step for every sequential module, while Bounded model checking is reserved as a second step for critical modules like parsers and the buddy allocator.

