The Fuel Unit (G1) is defined as one function entry, and the work between entries is the function body. To compute the WCET, the compiler walks the emitted assembly as a Control Flow Graph (CFG), treating loops as tail calls to avoid back-edges. A back-edge would report an UNBOUNDED state, ensuring the maximum instruction count is calculated only on straight-line paths between safepoints like entry checks, kernel checks, and calls.

```sol
// Sol function to compute WCET per function body
// Counts IR instructions between safepoints (entry, kernel checks, calls)
// Loops are tail calls; any back-edge implies UNBOUNDED and is rejected.
fn compute_wcet(body: [IRInstr]) -> u32 {
    var max_insns: u32 = 0
    var current_path: u32 = 0

    for instr in body {
        current_path += instr.weight
        max_insns = max(max_insns, current_path)
    }

    return max_insns
}
```

The fprc compiler computes the maximum IR-instruction count on any path between consecutive safepoint events (such as entry checks, kernel checks, and calls) by walking the emitted assembly as a control flow graph (CFG), which is structured as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) because all loops are implemented as tail calls. This process detects back-edges to report an UNBOUNDED condition, ensuring that the worst-case execution time (WCET) model relies on a compiler-emitted constant per program. Each function receives a `# wcet:` comment containing specific metrics like `segmax`, `exit-tail` for chaining, and `ccall count`.

```fprc
// Example of the WCET comment added to a function by fprc
function my_kernel() {
    # wcet: segmax=10, exit-tail=5, ccall=2
    // Kernel logic here
}
```

