The runtime panics the entire machine instead of degrading gracefully. This occurs because there is no backpressure primitive; if a producer's output rate exceeds the consumer's input rate, the system crashes. A full 64-slot per-sender ring, a ninth distinct sender, or a full 1024-entry ARC table each trigger this panic.

