  @retry
  u = sleepMs 300;
  u2 = writePath @/tmp/sol-txn-race.txt "{n + 1}";
  print "wrote {n + 1}".
  break;
}
```

When two transactions attempt to read and write the same counter concurrently, the system ensures atomicity by having the second transaction retry if it detects a conflict. The first transaction reads the current value, increments it, and writes the new value back. If a concurrent write occurs, the second transaction must re-execute the read-modify-write sequence. Here is an example where a transaction reads a counter, increments it, and writes the result:
