To trigger a software interrupt, the developer writes to the CLINT's MSIP register (address 0x80000000) to set the interrupt pending bit and increments a flag word in the scratch RAM to signal completion. The system resumes execution upon detecting the flag change, allowing the test to verify that the interrupt handler executed sequentially. For example, a developer can use the `fire` function to trigger 1000 interrupts and check that the execution flow resumes correctly:
```fprisc
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

The recursive call fails because the guard `case n == 0` incorrectly evaluates to `False` when `n` is 0, causing the code to attempt to decrement `n` to -1 and enter an infinite loop or undefined behavior. The condition must be `n > 0` to ensure the recursion stops at zero. The corrected logic should be:

```fprisc
fire n = case n > 0 of
  True ->
    flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
    _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
    _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
    _ = waitFor flag;
    xs = [n, n + 1];
    _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
    fire (n - 1)
| False -> Unit
```

