flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
_ = Mem.write32 msip (Word.fromInt 0);
_ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 1);
_ = Mem.readWord flag;
```

The connection attempts to reconnect using an exponential backoff strategy, doubling the wait time after each failed close until it caps at 8000 milliseconds. The connection logic distinguishes between normal closures (codes 1000 and 1001), which are treated as deliberate disconnections and do not trigger a reconnection attempt, versus other error codes that trigger the retry loop.

```fprisc
ws.onclose = function (ev) \{
    status('offline (' + ev.code + ')');
    if (ev.code === 1000 || ev.code === 1001) return;
    setTimeout(connect, wait);
    wait = Math.min(wait * 2, 8000);
\};
```

When `JSON.parse(ev.data)` fails, the code catches the exception and immediately closes the WebSocket connection with code 4002 and the reason string "resync". This is necessary because the live patching protocol relies on ordered frames; a parse failure indicates the data stream is corrupted or out of sync, so the client must disconnect to request a fresh full snapshot from the server rather than attempting to apply invalid data.

```fprisc
try { res = JSON.parse(ev.data); } catch (e) {
  ws.close(4002, 'resync');
}
```

The system automatically reconnects if the WebSocket closes with codes other than 1000 or 1001, which indicate a deliberate disconnection. Upon reconnection, the wait time doubles up to a maximum of 8000 milliseconds. The following FP-RISC code demonstrates the reconnection logic and the handling of connection states:

```fprisc
function connect() {
    var url = (location.protocol === 'https:' ? 'wss://' : 'ws://') + location.host + '/ws?lv=3';
    ws = new WebSocket(url);
    ws.onopen = function () { wait = 500; status('live'); };
    ws.onclose = function (ev) {
      status('offline (' + ev.code + ')');
      if (ev.code === 1000 || ev.code === 1001) return;
      setTimeout(connect, wait);
      wait = Math.min(wait * 2, 8000);
    };
}
```

