When the `tick` function encounters a frozen state (indicated by `frozen m`), it does not simply advance time. Instead, it identifies the oldest memory entry in the queue, modifies its timestamp by adding the elapsed time (`tickMs`), clears its log and sound effect lists, and extracts this specific entry as `m2`. Before returning the updated state and a context list, the function explicitly echoes the cleared log of this entry to the host via `say` and plays all associated sound effects for that entry using `Sfx.playAllVar`. This ensures that frozen events are processed and their output rendered before the system returns control.

```fpr
tick m | frozen m =
  (m2, cs) = takeKey { m | ms = m.ms + tickMs, log = Nil, sfx = Nil };
  _ = say (rev m2.log Nil);
  _ = Sfx.playAllVar m2.ms (rev m2.sfx Nil);
  (m2, cs).
```

The `measure` function for a `Box` first retrieves the padding and gap values from the box's context. It then uses the `msum` function to compute the maximum width and height across all children, accounting for the number of items to calculate gaps. If the box is a row, gaps are added horizontally; otherwise, they are added vertically. Finally, the total width and height are calculated by adding the computed inner dimensions to twice the padding value.

```fprisc
measure (Box a kids) =
  p = padOf a; g = gapOf a;
  (mw, mh, n) = msum (isRow a) kids;
  gaps = case n > 1 of True -> g * (n - 1) | False -> 0;
  inner = case isRow a of
    True -> (mw + gaps, mh)
  | False -> (mw, mh + gaps);
  (iw, ih) = inner;
  sized a (iw + 2 * p, ih + 2 * p).
```

The `QL.appendRec` function adds a new record to the disk at a specific offset, automatically advancing the file pointer to the next available location for subsequent writes. The `QL.countRecs` function determines the current number of records in a specific directory, which is used to calculate the index of the next record to be written. This mechanism ensures that new data is appended sequentially without overwriting existing records.

```fprisc
run dsk n =
  h0 = QL.ensure dsk;
  boot = QL.countRecs dsk h0 "qdisk/boots" + 1;
  h1 = QL.appendRec dsk h0 "QREC" "qdisk/boots" "b";
  ...
```

To write a large payload, use `QL.appendRec` to add a record containing the data to the disk, then use `QL.readLatest` to retrieve it. You can verify integrity by comparing the retrieved string length against the expected size and performing a string equality check.

```fprisc
big = rep "0123456789abcdef" 320;
h2 = QL.appendRec dsk h1 "QREC" "qdisk/big{boot}" big;
bigBack = okOr (QL.readLatest dsk h4 "qdisk/big{boot}") "";
bigOk = case strlen bigBack == 5120 of
          True -> QL.strEq bigBack big
        | False -> False;
```

To verify disk integrity, one must ensure the device is formatted, append records containing a large payload and key-value pairs, then read the latest entry, replay all records, and confirm the payload matches the original. This process validates the storage and retrieval mechanisms of the disk device.

```fpr
run dsk n =
  h0 = QL.ensure dsk;
  h1 = QL.appendRec dsk h0 "QREC" "qdisk/big" (rep "0" 5120);
  h2 = QL.appendRec dsk h1 "QREC" "qdisk/kv" "test";
  lat = okOr (QL.readLatest dsk h2 "qdisk/kv") "(err)";
  all = QL.replayAll dsk h2 "qdisk/kv";
  bigBack = okOr (QL.readLatest dsk h2 "qdisk/big") "";
  bigOk = case strlen bigBack == 5120 of True -> QL.strEq bigBack (rep "0" 5120) | False -> False;
  "qdisk: latest={lat} replay={all} big5120={bigOk}".
```

