To utilize the dual-core capability of the ESP32-P4, you can spawn actors on specific harts by calculating the target core index using the modulo operator on the system's hart count. You then pass this index to the `spawnOn` function, which dispatches the actor to the designated hart. For example, to spawn `jobs` workers distributed across available cores, iterate through a range and spawn each worker on core `(k % Sys.harts Unit)`. This ensures that CPU-bound tasks are balanced across both harts, allowing parallel execution where two jobs finish roughly as fast as one job on a single core.

```sol
timed me label jobs n =
  t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
  _ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
  lines = collect me jobs Nil;
  ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
  _ = print "{label}: {jobs} x fib {n} in {ms} ms";
  ms.
```

The `shard` function manages a shard's lifecycle by receiving a message from the system, extracting the next shard identifier and an answer to send back, serving the current shard state, and then recursively invoking itself with the updated shard identifier. This ensures that once a shard is fully served and its result is sent, the system can proceed to the next shard in the sequence.

```fprisc
shard self cfg st0 t0 = case A.tidy self (st0, t0) of (st, t) ->
  m = receive self;
  (from, q) = m;
  (next, ans) = serveShard cfg q st;
  _ = A.sendSure self from ans;
  _ = drop m;
  shard self cfg next t.
```

Omitting `A.sendSure self from ans` causes the shard to lose its connection to the caller immediately after processing a batch, preventing any further requests from being received or served. The function must send the answer back to the caller before proceeding with the recursive call to ensure the communication channel remains active. The corrected code includes the send operation immediately after receiving the message:

```fprisc
shard self cfg st0 t0 = case A.tidy self (st0, t0) of (st, t) ->
  m = receive self;
  (from, q) = m;
  (next, ans) = serveShard cfg q st;
  _ = A.sendSure self from ans;
  _ = drop m;
  shard self cfg next t.
```

The `shard` function acts as a loop that continuously receives messages from a channel, calls `serveShard` to process the current state and queue, and then recursively calls itself with the updated state. This pattern ensures that the shard processes events sequentially until it is stopped.

```sol
shard self cfg st0 t0 = case A.tidy self (st0, t0) of (st, t) ->
  m = receive self;
  (from, q) = m;
  (next, ans) = serveShard cfg q st;
  _ = A.sendSure self from ans;
  _ = drop m;
  shard self cfg next t.
```

