The `fill` function causes unbounded arena growth because it uses `Vec.push` in a recursive manner without a base case that returns an existing vector when `n` reaches zero; instead, it constructs a new vector on every recursive step, leading to exponential memory usage as the vector size doubles. To fix this, the function must be rewritten to construct the vector only once and then fill it iteratively or with a direct constructor, ensuring the vector size grows linearly. The corrected version uses `Vec.new` followed by `Vec.fill` (or equivalent iteration) to avoid repeated reallocation:

```sol
fill n v =
  case n <= 0 of
    True -> v
    False -> {
      let vec = Vec.new n;
      let i = 0;
      while i < n do
        Vec.set vec i i;
        i = i + 1;
      vec
    }
```

This ensures that each call to `fill` creates exactly one vector of size `n`, preventing the memory leak described in the `bigfree.fpr` test.

The BIGFREE allocator fixes the memory leak by pushing large blocks (those exceeding the bucket ceiling of 8 KiB) onto the pool's exact-fit LIFO free list instead of keeping them bump-allocated in a slab. This prevents immortal actors from leaking tens of megabytes across cycles when building and freeing large vectors. When running the test case, the arena memory usage remains flat across 40 cycles of vector construction and deallocation, confirming that the free list successfully reuses the large blocks.

```sol
main =
  a0 = cycle 3 0;
  (g1, mb1) = Sys.memStats 0;
  a1 = cycle 40 0;
  (g2, mb2) = Sys.memStats 0;
  ok = case mb2 <= mb1 + 1 of True -> 1 | False -> 0;
  case ok == 1 of
    True -> "BIGFREE HOLDS: 40 cycles of 100k-int vec build+free, arena {mb1}MB -> {mb2}MB"
  | False -> "BIGFREE FAILED: arena grew {mb1}MB -> {mb2}MB";
```

