To configure the UART for RDA interrupts, you must call `doArm` with the actor's identifier, the current state, and the target actor that should receive the interrupt. This function checks ownership and then binds the interrupt using `Sys.irqBind` and sets the interrupt flags via `irqSet`, which writes to the RDA register if the actor is armed. The resulting state reflects the armed configuration.

```sol
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.

doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.
```

The module binds the Peripheral Interrupt Controller (PLIC) source to the specific actor using `Sys.irqBind` and enables interrupts via `irqSet` only if the device is armed. The `irqSet` function sets the Interrupt Enable Register (IER) to include the Receiver Data Available (RDA) bit permanently and the Transmitter Holding Register Empty (THRE) bit dynamically based on the transmit queue length. After a successful arm operation, the state reflects the new interrupt configuration with the armed flag set to 1 and the IER updated accordingly.

```fpr
doArm me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  case owner == from of
    False -> reply me from (UErr "not the owner") st
  | True -> doArm2 me st from.
doArm2 me st from =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1 armed = st;
  _ = Sys.irqBind uartIrq me;
  st2 = St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl 1;
  _ = irqSet st2;
  reply me from UOk st2.
```

The system performs an incremental rebake by identifying only the faces of the edited cell and its six neighbors, as well as the cells along the anti-sun ray for directional lighting, rather than recalculating the entire map. This reduces the computational load significantly compared to a full bake. The process is implemented recursively, starting with the edited cell and iterating through its neighbors:

rebakeNbrs : unsafe Vector -> Vector -> Int -> Int -> (Vector, Vector) .
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1).

The incremental rebaking process optimizes performance by limiting updates to only the faces of the edited cell and its six neighbors (for exposure) and the cells along the anti-sun ray (for shadows), reducing the workload to approximately 1/50th of a full bake. This approach treats the lightmap as static until an edit occurs, allowing the system to reuse the allocated lightmap structure efficiently.

```sol
rebakeAt w lm x y z | inWorld x y z == False = (w, lm);
rebakeAt w lm x y z = rebakeCell w lm (cellOf x y z);

rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1);
```

The `rebakeNbrs` function implements an incremental rebaking strategy that avoids recalculating the entire scene. Instead of a full bake, it identifies only the faces of the edited cell and its six immediate neighbors (exposure) as well as the specific cells lying along the anti-sun ray from the edited cell, which are the only regions where shadow rays pass through the modified geometry. This reduces the computational workload to approximately 1/50th of a full bake by recursively calling `rebakeAt` on these limited sets of coordinates until all affected faces are processed.

```sol
rebakeNbrs w lm c f | f >= nFaces = (w, lm);
rebakeNbrs w lm c f =
  (nx, ny, nz) = nbrOf c f;
  (w2, lm2) = rebakeAt w lm nx ny nz;
  rebakeNbrs w2 lm2 c (f + 1);
```

