The system enforces that prices, tax, catalog, and stock are values that can be tweaked live, but the model's shape must remain constant. If an operation attempts to alter the model's structure (e.g., adding new fields or changing the data type of existing fields) during iteration, the system will reject the transaction because the model actor acts as the single source of truth and strictly adheres to the MVU rule where exactly one writer defines the shape. Correctly, updates to these values are handled as new data records or value changes within the existing structure, ensuring the model remains stable while allowing live configuration tweaks.

```sol
// Incorrect: Attempting to change the model shape (e.g., adding a new field 'discount_rate')
// This will be rejected by the model actor.
model.update {
  type = Product
  name = "Widget"
  price = 10.00
  discount_rate = 0.10  // Error: Shape changed; model shape cannot change during iteration
}

// Correct: Updating a value within the existing shape
model.update {
  type = Product
  name = "Widget"
  price = 9.00  // Allowed: Value changed, shape unchanged
}
```

The design enforces atomicity for checkouts by executing a single Sol transaction that contains the stock decrement, receipt generation, and revenue update simultaneously. Because the model is the single source of truth with exactly one writer, this single transaction ensures all changes land together or none do, guaranteeing crash-atomic commits without partial state.

```sol
