The `doAfter` function calculates the next fire deadline by adding the requested delay (`ms`) to the current clock value (`clk`), using a conversion factor of 10,000 ticks per millisecond. It then inserts this new deadline into a sorted list of pending timers (`pend`) using the `insert` function, which maintains order by oldest deadline first. Finally, it arms the nearest timer if the actor is the main clock (`nat == 1`) or sleeps the child actor otherwise, before calling `fireDue` to process any immediately due entries.

```fpr
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
```

The `doAfter` function calculates the absolute deadline by adding the message duration (`ms`) to the current clock value (`clk`), scaled by `ticksPerMs` (10,000 ticks per millisecond). It then inserts this new deadline into the pending list (`pend`) in sorted order, ensuring the oldest deadline is processed first. If the actor is the main loop (`nat == 1`), it arms the nearest deadline; otherwise, it sleeps the child actor.

```fpr
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
```

The `doAfter` function calculates the next fire time by adding the requested delay in milliseconds to the current clock value, using a conversion factor of 10,000 ticks per millisecond. It then inserts this new deadline into a sorted list of pending events, ensuring the oldest deadline is processed first. Finally, it calls `fireDue` to immediately handle any events that have reached their deadline, rather than waiting for the next interrupt.

```sol
dl = read clk + ms * ticksPerMs;
pend2 = insert dl from pend;
_ = case nat == 1 of
      True -> armNearest clk pend2
    | False -> sleepChild me ms;
fireDue me clk nat pend2.
```

