The `bbspi` module uses a software bit-banged SPI interface where the DC (data/command) pin is manually toggled by the driver to select the mode. The `cmd` function sets the DC pin to 0 to send commands, while the `dat` function sets it to 1 for data transfer. This allows the same physical bus to be used for both initialization sequences and display updates without requiring hardware SPI support.

```fprisc
cmd pinW pinR pins miso bytes =
  Pins sck mosi cs dc = pins;
  _ = pinW dc 0;
  _ = S.csLow pinW cs;
  _ = S.xfer (S.Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso) bytes;
  _ = S.csHigh pinW cs;
  pinW dc 1.
```

The `dat` function must explicitly set the `dc` pin to 1 after the SPI transfer to switch the OLED from command mode to data mode. The `cmd` function achieves this by setting `pinW dc 1` after the `xfer` call, which the `dat` function is missing.

Corrected code for `dat`:
```fprisc
dat pinW pinR pins miso bytes =
  Pins sck mosi cs dc = pins;
  _ = S.csLow pinW cs;
  _ = S.xfer (S.Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso) bytes;
  _ = pinW dc 1;
  S.csHigh pinW cs.
```

To send a command, you must configure the DC pin to logic 0, assert the CS line low, transfer the command bytes via the SPI bus, and finally release the CS line. The provided `cmd` function handles this sequence by setting the DC pin to 0, calling `S.csLow` to assert chip select, using `S.xfer` to send the data stream, and calling `S.csHigh` to de-assert chip select.

```fprisc
cmd pinW pinR pins miso bytes =
  Pins sck mosi cs dc = pins;
  _ = pinW dc 0;
  _ = S.csLow pinW cs;
  _ = S.xfer (S.Bus pinW pinR sck mosi miso) bytes;
  _ = S.csHigh pinW cs;
