The function iterates through the desired nodes and retrieves the corresponding existing node from a keyed map of the parent's children. If a match is found and they are compatible, it synchronizes attributes by removing any attributes present on the old node but not on the new one, and setting attributes on the old node only if their values differ.

```fprisc
if (!next.hasAttribute(a.name)) old.removeAttribute(a.name);
Array.from(next.attributes).forEach(function (a) {
  if (old.getAttribute(a.name) !== a.value) old.setAttribute(a.name, a.value);
});
```

Omitting that clause prevents the function from reusing an existing keyless DOM node (`cursor`) that is compatible with the desired child (`next`), forcing the creation of a new node instead of updating the existing one. This results in unnecessary DOM overhead and potential loss of state if the browser manages properties on the original node. The fix is to include the compatibility check within the `else if` block so that `old` is assigned `cursor` before the node is inserted or updated.

```fprisc
// Corrected logic inside the loop:
} else if (cursor && keyOf(cursor) === null && compatible(cursor, next)) {
    old = cursor;
}
```

The function identifies existing nodes by their view key and compares them against incoming changes. If a match is found and the nodes are compatible, it synchronizes attributes by removing those present on the old node but absent on the new one, and setting attributes on the old node if their values differ. This ensures the server-controlled DOM element reflects the exact state of the new input while preserving browser-managed properties like form values.

```sol
// Example synchronization logic extracted from reconcile
if (old.nodeType === 1) {
    // Remove attributes present on old but not on next
    Array.from(old.attributes).forEach(function (a) {
        if (!next.hasAttribute(a.name)) old.removeAttribute(a.name);
    });
    // Set attributes on old if values differ
    Array.from(next.attributes).forEach(function (a) {
        if (old.getAttribute(a.name) !== a.value) old.setAttribute(a.name, a.value);
    });
}
```

The `asReal` function handles this by returning the text representation for `Real` values and converting `Integer` values to their string form.

```sol
use "std/json"; use "string"; use "option"; use "map"; use "sequence"; use "type"; use "list"; use "unsafe";

Json = use "std/json";
M = use "map";
O = use "option";
S = use "string";

Value = Type (Null | Boolean Bool | Integer Int | Real String | Text String | Sequence (List Value) | Object (M.Map String Value));

