The library constructs a matrix using the `mFromRows` function, which takes a list of rows and calculates the number of columns from the first row to determine the matrix dimensions. It enforces row consistency by using the `mFlatten` function, which checks the length of every subsequent row against the width of the first row; if a mismatch is detected, it raises a compile-time panic rather than silently mis-indexing. The resulting structure is a linear `Vector` containing all elements in row-major order.

```sol
mFromRows [[1, 2], [3, 4]] =
  r0 :: _ = [[1, 2], [3, 4]];
  c = List.len r0;
  Mat 2 c (Vec.fromList [1, 2, 3, 4]);
```

To implement a dynamic ring buffer in FP-RISC, you use the `Dynamic` actor from the standard library to handle messages in order. The system can grow past its old 1024-entry ceiling by managing shared values in flight. You define a `sink` actor that receives a count from a gate and drains that many items from its main input channel, maintaining order using a `last` value comparison. For heap values like tuples, you use `drainT` instead of `drainN`, which promotes every send operation.

```fprisc
sink main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainT me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.
drainT me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered).
drainT me main k last ordered =
  (a, b) = receiveFrom me main;
  drainT me main (k - 1) a (ordered and a > last).
```

To implement a dynamic mailbox that processes a burst of messages from a main channel in order, define a `gate` to accept the count and a `sink` function that uses `drainN` for integers or `drainT` for tuples. The `drainN` and `drainT` helper functions recursively consume `k` messages, tracking the last value and an ordering flag, then send the result back to the main channel. This approach ensures messages are processed sequentially while maintaining state about the stream's order.

```fpr
gate me =
  s = receive me;
  k = receive me;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie s k;
  0.

sink main gate me =
  k = receiveFrom me gate;
  (last, ordered) = drainN me main k 0 True;
  _ = AC.sendOrDie main (k, ordered);
  0.

drainN me main 0 last ordered = (last, ordered);
drainN me main k last ordered =
  v = receiveFrom me main;
  drainN me main (k - 1) v (ordered and v > last).
```

The `gfx_render` function receives the application's scene VALUE, which the host walks read-only through the shared layout because both images share a single address space. All value construction, including the (draws, dynBytes) pair and input triples, occurs on the application side using its own allocator, ensuring no allocation crosses the boundary.

```fpr
// Example of the app-side allocation pattern for gfx_render
let result = gfx_render(
  my_scene_value, 
  my_app_allocator
