On FreeBSD, `fprisc` fails because the kernel treats address hints as advisory; without `MAP_FIXED`, the kernel ignores the requested address and returns a different one, causing a check failure. The fix is to use `MAP_EXCL` alongside `MAP_FIXED` to enforce that the specific address must be free, effectively replicating the hint-and-check behavior found on Linux. In `fprisc/machine/posix/hal.c`, this is implemented by passing the hint to `mmap` with `MAP_FIXED` and `MAP_EXCL` flags instead of relying on a post-mmap check.

```c
// Correct approach for FreeBSD/arm64
int fd = mmap(addr, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_FIXED | MAP_EXCL, -1, 0);
if (fd == MAP_FAILED) {
    // Address reservation failed; hint was not honored
}
```

