Appending to the end of the list violates the requirement that the log be ordered "NEWEST FIRST" and that adding is a `cons` (prepend) operation. Since the first page renders the head of the list, appending causes new entries to be buried at the tail, making them invisible on the initial page and requiring pagination to view. Furthermore, because the durability model stores "one record per entry" and writes only what changed, appending a new record as a separate tail node creates a new durable record for every addition, whereas prepending allows the system to potentially reuse or update the existing head record, aligning with the efficient "write what changed" strategy.

```fpr
// Correct: Prepend to maintain NEWEST FIRST order
log := cons (newEntry, log)

// Mistake: Append to the end, burying new data
// log := append (log, newEntry)
```

In FP-RISC, an SoA vector can be reconstructed into an AoS vector by retrieving a record, modifying its fields, and pushing the updated record back onto the vector. The `Vec.push` operation accepts a record with the correct field structure (e.g., `{x, y}`), which the system automatically aligns and stores as a new element in the SoA, effectively creating an AoS-like view of the updated data.

```fprisc
a1 = fillR (Vec.new Unit) 1 100;
(p, _) = Vec.get 7 a1;
a3 = Vec.push {x = p.x + 1000, y = p.y} a2;
```

To convert a Vector of SoA records to a Vector of AoS, use the `Vec.toList` function, which returns a list of individual record values. Once in list form, the `lsum` function can be applied to sum specific fields by projecting them during the recursive accumulation. For example, given a vector `v` of records with fields `x` and `y`, `Vec.toList v` produces a list of records. The `lsum` function, defined to take a list and an accumulator, adds the `x` field of each record to the accumulator, effectively summing all x-coordinates in the transformed list.

```fprisc
v = fillR (Vec.new Unit) 1 10;
l = Vec.toList v;
total_x = lsum l 0;
```

The `==` operator performs a deep structural comparison, recursing through all levels of nesting to verify that both the constructor names and all payload values match exactly. For lists, it checks the entire sequence; for records, it compares field names and their corresponding values. This recursion continues until the deepest leaf nodes are reached.

Example:
```fprisc
_ = yes "nested, same" (mkNest 1 == mkNest 1);
_ = no "nested, one leaf differs" (mkNest 1 == mkNest 2);
_ = no "a difference inside a list inside a tuple"
    ((1, (2, 3 :: Nil)) == (1, (2, 4 :: Nil)));
_ = yes "records by field" ({ a = 1, b = "x" } == { a = 1, b = "x" });
```

FP-RISC compares data structures by recursively checking every field and leaf node until a difference is found or the entire structure is verified identical. The language enforces a depth limit of 64 to prevent infinite loops in cyclic structures; however, this guard only triggers if equality has not been determined by then. Consequently, lists or nested structures differing only beyond the 64th element are incorrectly reported as equal, while structures differing within the first 64 levels are correctly identified as unequal.

```fprisc
# Lists differing only past element 64 are considered equal due to the cycle guard
_ = yes "65-element lists, same" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 7);
_ = no "65-element lists differing only in the last element" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 8);

# Nested structures differing deep inside are correctly compared if the path is shallow enough
_ = yes "nested 100000 deep, same" (leftDeep 100000 7 == leftDeep 100000 7);
_ = no "nested 100000 deep through the first field, innermost leaf differs" (leftDeep 100000 7 == leftDeep 100000 8);
```

The `longList` constructor is used to create lists of arbitrary depth, allowing tests to verify equality behavior on very large data structures. The comments indicate that the equality operator (`==`) includes a cycle guard that terminates recursion after 64 levels of nesting; if two lists differ only beyond this depth, the comparison returns true instead of causing an infinite loop. This prevents the system from hanging on excessively deep structures.
```fprisc
_ = yes "65-element lists, same" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 7);
_ = no "65-element lists differing only in the last element" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 8);
```

To parse such CSV data, use `J.parse` on a string constructed via `JStr` with literal escape sequences like `\t`, `\n`, and `\"`. The parser handles quoted fields containing commas and unescapes sequences like `\"` to actual double quotes. To output compact JSON, apply `mapOk J.render` to the parsed result. For example, parsing the provided orders string yields a JSON object where fields like `note` contain unescaped quotes, which can then be rendered compactly using `J.render`.

```sol
csv = JStr "order,customer,amount,note\n1001, 1 ,19.99,\"first, with comma\"\n1002, 2 ,5,\n1003, 1 ,oops,bad amount\n1004, 3 ,100.01,\"say \"\"hi\"\"\"\n\n1005, 1 ,0.01,";
data = J.parse csv;
out = mapOk J.render data;
writePath "{dir}/output.json" (J.pretty (unwrap out));
