The `alloc` function validates the requested size against overflow conditions by checking bits 62 and 63, returning an error if set. It then aligns the size to at least 80 bytes (or 96 bytes if exactly 80) and initiates a `firstFit` search starting from the head of the free list to find a suitable block. Once a block is found, it is split if necessary to accommodate the request, marked as used, and its payload is zeroed before returning the address.
```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

question:
```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p;
```
What is the specific error in the `claim` function's handling of the `split` operation regarding the block size, and how should it be corrected to ensure the allocated memory is properly sized?

answer:
The `alloc` function requests a specific number of bytes, checks for overflow, and aligns the size to a minimum of 80 bytes (rounded up to the next multiple of 80 if not already 80). It then searches the heap using `firstFit` to find the first free block large enough to satisfy the request. If found, the `claim` function is invoked to mark the block as used, initialize its metadata (setting fields, layout, and reference count), and zero out the payload area.

```fprisc
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function enforces memory safety by checking the 62nd and 63rd bits of the requested size; if either is set, it immediately raises an overflow error. It then calculates the aligned size, rounds it up to at least 96 bytes, and invokes the `firstFit` helper to locate the first suitable free block in the linked list. If a block is found with sufficient space, the `claim` function splits it if necessary, marks it as used, zeros the payload, and returns its address. If no suitable block exists, `firstFit` recursively searches the next block or triggers an out-of-memory error.

```fpr
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

