The `moveBy` function computes the new velocity vector by projecting the forward and right movement inputs onto the current yaw orientation using sine and cosine values. Specifically, it calculates `sw` as the sine of the yaw and `cw` as the cosine of the yaw, then derives the new x and z velocity components (`nvx`, `nvz`) by combining the forward input `f` and right input `r` with these trigonometric factors, scaling the result by the maximum move velocity `moveV` and the inverse of the forward speed `fx`. The resulting velocities are added to the existing `vx` and `vz` components to form the new block state.

```fpr
moveBy (Mdl (Bd px py pz vx vy vz g) yaw pit sel act) f r =
  sw = sinA yaw; cw = cosA yaw;
  nvx = (f * sw + r * cw) * moveV / fx;
  nvz = (f * cw - r * sw) * moveV / fx;
  Mdl (Bd px py pz (vx + nvx) vy (vz + nvz) g) yaw pit sel "move".
```

To calculate the new velocity, you must first determine the sine and cosine of the current yaw angle, then apply the forward and right distances to these trigonometric values to derive the velocity increments. The forward distance is multiplied by the sine and cosine to get the X and Z components respectively, while the right offset is multiplied by the cosine and negative sine. These calculated values are then added to the existing velocity components. The resulting new velocity is used to update the model's state within the `moveBy` function.

```fpr
sw = sinA yaw; cw = cosA yaw;
nvx = (f * sw + r * cw) * moveV / fx;
nvz = (f * cw - r * sw) * moveV / fx;
```

The `moveBy` function calculates new velocity components by decomposing the input forward and right displacements into the model's current yaw-relative axes using sine and cosine of the yaw angle. It then scales these components by a move velocity factor and normalizes them by `fx` to update the x and z velocity components of the bounding data structure while preserving the current yaw, pitch, selection, and action state.

```sol
moveBy (Mdl (Bd px py pz vx vy vz g) yaw pit sel act) f r =
  sw = sinA yaw; cw = cosA yaw;
  nvx = (f * sw + r * cw) * moveV / fx;
  nvz = (f * cw - r * sw) * moveV / fx;
  Mdl (Bd px py pz (vx + nvx) vy (vz + nvz) g) yaw pit sel "move".
```

