The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if and only if the two terms are not unifiable at the moment of execution, utilizing a mechanism to undo any bindings that would have occurred during the unification process. It first marks the current state of the term store, performs a unification check, and if successful, applies `undoTo` to revert the store to its pre-unification state before returning success. Conversely, if unification fails, it proceeds to solve the goal with the modified store.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

