To prevent memory leaks and crashes associated with long-lived loops, a developer must manually manage object lifecycles using the `keep` and `drop` commands while respecting frame boundaries. The `std/actor.fpr` library provides the `boundary` function to identify these points, and the driver automatically performs an amortized `tidy` operation at each boundary to clean up temporary objects. Consequently, application code must explicitly call `drop` on any temporary objects created within a loop to ensure they are released before the next frame boundary is reached.

```fpr
